SST_2020_D_11	SS10_ECO_03	\N	Standard (041)	What is a 'debt trap'? Why does credit turn into a debt trap for small and marginal farmers? Illustrate with a rural example.	{"example": "1.5 Marks", "definition": "1.5 Marks"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	0d543d19ed4f41aadf0d4d9fc051a8a6d1f1e8c0834fcfbd1cdfca0acbda9994	[]	1. Definition: A situation where an existing loan cannot be repaid from crop earnings, forcing the borrower to take another loan and causing indebtedness to multiply.\r\n2. Rural Context: Small farmers borrow to buy seeds, fertilizers, and pesticides. If rains fail or pests ruin the harvest, crop revenue is insufficient to service the loan, forcing asset sales or land transfers.	t	2020	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
M10_CH04_A01	M10_CH04	Word Problems on Speed & Distance	Standard	A motor boat whose speed is 18 km/h in still water takes 1 hour more to go 24 km upstream than to return downstream to the same spot. Find the speed of the stream.	{"steps": [{"marks": 2.0, "step_no": 1, "description": "Let stream speed be x km/h. Form equation: 24/(18-x) - 24/(18+x) = 1"}, {"marks": 2.0, "step_no": 2, "description": "Simplify to quadratic equation x^2 + 48x - 324 = 0"}, {"marks": 1.0, "step_no": 3, "description": "Solve for x to get speed = 6 km/h"}]}	5.0	Cat_A	Word_Problem	HOTS	\N	[]	\N	t	\N	\N	f	MATH	All India
M10_CH04_A02	M10_CH04	Work & Time Quadratic Application	Standard	Two water taps together can fill a tank in 9 hours 36 minutes. The tap of larger diameter takes 10 hours less than the smaller one to fill the tank separately. Find the time in which each tap can separately fill the tank.	{"steps": [{"marks": 2.0, "step_no": 1, "description": "Let smaller tap take x hours. Form equation 1/x + 1/(x-10) = 10/96"}, {"marks": 2.0, "step_no": 2, "description": "Reduce to 8x^2 - 230x + 720 = 0"}, {"marks": 1.0, "step_no": 3, "description": "Solve to find times as 25 hours and 15 hours"}]}	5.0	Cat_A	Word_Problem	HOTS	\N	[]	\N	t	\N	\N	f	MATH	All India
M10_CH04_A03	M10_CH04	Quadratic Equation Reduction	Standard	Find the roots of the equation: 1/(x+4) - 1/(x-7) = 11/30, where x != -4 and x != 7.	{"steps": [{"marks": 2.0, "step_no": 1, "description": "Take LCM and simplify numerator: [(x-7) - (x+4)] / [(x+4)(x-7)] = 11/30"}, {"marks": 2.0, "step_no": 2, "description": "Cross multiply to form standard quadratic equation x^2 - 3x - 10 = 0"}, {"marks": 1.0, "step_no": 3, "description": "Factorize to get roots x = 5 and x = -2"}]}	5.0	Cat_A	Standard	Application	\N	[]	\N	t	\N	\N	f	MATH	All India
M10_CH04_B01	M10_CH04	Nature of Roots	Basic	Find the discriminant of the quadratic equation 2x^2 - 4x + 3 = 0, and hence find the nature of its roots.	{"steps": [{"marks": 1.0, "step_no": 1, "description": "Identify a=2, b=-4, c=3 and calculate D = b^2 - 4ac = 16 - 24 = -8"}, {"marks": 1.0, "step_no": 2, "description": "Since D < 0, the equation has no real roots."}]}	2.0	Cat_B	Analytical	Foundation	\N	[]	\N	t	\N	\N	f	MATH	All India
M10_CH04_B02	M10_CH04	Quadratic Formula Solution	Standard	Solve the quadratic equation by factorization: 2x^2 + x - 6 = 0.	{"steps": [{"marks": 1.0, "step_no": 1, "description": "Split middle term: 2x^2 + 4x - 3x - 6 = 0"}, {"marks": 1.0, "step_no": 2, "description": "Factorize: 2x(x + 2) - 3(x + 2) = 0 -> (2x - 3)(x + 2) = 0, giving x = 3/2, -2"}]}	2.0	Cat_B	Standard	Foundation	\N	[]	\N	t	\N	\N	f	MATH	All India
M10_CH04_B03	M10_CH04	Parameter Value for Equal Roots	Standard	Find the value of k for which the quadratic equation kx(x - 2) + 6 = 0 has two equal roots.	{"steps": [{"marks": 1.0, "step_no": 1, "description": "Expand equation to kx^2 - 2kx + 6 = 0"}, {"marks": 1.0, "step_no": 2, "description": "Set discriminant D = 0 => (-2k)^2 - 4(k)(6) = 0 => 4k^2 - 24k = 0 => k = 6"}]}	2.0	Cat_B	Analytical	Standard	\N	[]	\N	t	\N	\N	f	MATH	All India
