M10_CH14_BAS_501	M10_CH14	Spinning Wheel Arrow Simple	Basic	A game of chance consists of spinning an arrow which comes to rest pointing at one of the numbers 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8. What is the probability that it will point at: (i) 8 (ii) an odd number (iii) a number greater than 2?	{"rubric": "Total = 8. (i) P(8) = 1/8. (ii) Odds {1,3,5,7} => 4/8 = 1/2. (iii) Greater than 2 {3,4,5,6,7,8} => 6/8 = 3/4."}	5.0	Cat_A	Apply	Easy	\N	[]	\N	t	\N	\N	f	MATH	All India
PYQ_2024_CH01_01	M10_CH01	Irrationality Proof	Standard (041)	Prove that $\\sqrt{3}$ is an irrational number by the method of contradiction.	[{"step": "Assume √3 = a/b where a, b are coprime (b ≠ 0)", "marks": 0.5}, {"step": "3b² = a² => 3 divides a² => 3 divides a", "marks": 1.0}, {"step": "Substitute a = 3c => b² = 3c² => 3 divides b", "marks": 1.0}, {"step": "Contradiction established (common factor 3) => √3 is irrational", "marks": 0.5}]	3.0	PYQ	\N	Board Standard	0d5a0afca5096351417a3a9b263843257abeb4d5eb93f14718b61f99bf82bc71	["CBSE Board 2024 (Set 30/1/1)", "Official SQP 2024-25"]	Assume √3 = a/b where a,b are coprime integers. 3b² = a² => 3 divides a. Let a = 3c. 3b² = 9c² => b² = 3c² => 3 divides b. Contradiction to coprimality. Hence √3 is irrational.	t	\N	\N	f	MATH	All India
CBSE_2024_CH01_01	M10_CH01	Irrationality Proof	Standard (041)	Prove that $\\sqrt{5}$ is an irrational number by the method of contradiction.	[{"step": "Assume $\\\\sqrt{5} = a/b$ where $a, b$ are coprime integers ($b \\\\neq 0$)", "marks": 0.5}, {"step": "$5b^2 = a^2 \\\\implies 5$ divides $a^2 \\\\implies 5$ divides $a$", "marks": 1.0}, {"step": "Substitute $a = 5c \\\\implies 5b^2 = 25c^2 \\\\implies b^2 = 5c^2 \\\\implies 5$ divides $b$", "marks": 1.0}, {"step": "Contradiction to coprimality of $a$ and $b$; conclude $\\\\sqrt{5}$ is irrational", "marks": 0.5}]	3.0	PYQ	Procedural	Board Standard	76d029a8d6d7e88450a768f7c8c0b99781ebc39cecefca32569c1ebde249ad88	["CBSE Board 2024 (Set 30/1/1)", "CBSE Board 2023"]	Assume $\\sqrt{5} = \\frac{a}{b}$ where $a, b \\in \\mathbb{Z}$, $b \\neq 0$, and $\\gcd(a, b) = 1$. Squaring gives $5b^2 = a^2$. Since $5$ divides $a^2$, by the Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic $5$ divides $a$. Let $a = 5c$ for some integer $c$. Then $5b^2 = 25c^2 \\implies b^2 = 5c^2$, which means $5$ divides $b$. Hence $5$ is a common factor of both $a$ and $b$, contradicting the assumption that $a$ and $b$ are coprime. Thus, $\\sqrt{5}$ is irrational.	t	\N	\N	f	MATH	All India
CBSE_2024_CH02_01	M10_CH02	Relationship Between Zeroes and Coefficients	Standard (041)	Find the zeroes of the quadratic polynomial $p(x) = 6x^2 - 3 - 7x$ and verify the relationship between the zeroes and the coefficients.	[{"step": "Rearrange as $6x^2 - 7x - 3 = (2x - 3)(3x + 1) = 0$", "marks": 1.0}, {"step": "Determine zeroes: $\\\\alpha = 3/2$ and $\\\\beta = -1/3$", "marks": 0.5}, {"step": "Verify $\\\\alpha + \\\\beta = 7/6 = -b/a$", "marks": 0.75}, {"step": "Verify $\\\\alpha \\\\cdot \\\\beta = -3/6 = -1/2 = c/a$", "marks": 0.75}]	3.0	PYQ	Procedural	Board Standard	bfb8e3d2294a04c858b8d8213799e9bdc1227e0e1d88e61428b4bd99497c0e1b	["CBSE Board 2024", "NCERT Exemplar"]	Rearrange: $6x^2 - 7x - 3 = 6x^2 - 9x + 2x - 3 = 3x(2x - 3) + 1(2x - 3) = (2x - 3)(3x + 1)$. Zeroes are $\\alpha = \\frac{3}{2}$ and $\\beta = -\\frac{1}{3}$. Here $a = 6, b = -7, c = -3$. Sum of zeroes: $\\alpha + \\beta = \\frac{3}{2} - \\frac{1}{3} = \\frac{7}{6} = -\\frac{b}{a}$. Product of zeroes: $\\alpha\\beta = \\frac{3}{2} \\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right) = -\\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{c}{a}$. Verified.	t	\N	\N	f	MATH	All India
CBSE_2024_CH08_01	M10_CH08	Trigonometric Identity Proof	Standard (041)	Prove that: $\\frac{\\sin\\theta - 2\\sin^3\\theta}{2\\cos^3\\theta - \\cos\\theta} = \\tan\\theta$.	[{"step": "Factor numerator and denominator: $\\\\frac{\\\\sin\\\\theta(1 - 2\\\\sin^2\\\\theta)}{\\\\cos\\\\theta(2\\\\cos^2\\\\theta - 1)}$", "marks": 1.0}, {"step": "Substitute $1 = \\\\sin^2\\\\theta + \\\\cos^2\\\\theta$ or convert $\\\\sin^2\\\\theta = 1 - \\\\cos^2\\\\theta$", "marks": 1.0}, {"step": "Show $(1 - 2\\\\sin^2\\\\theta) = (2\\\\cos^2\\\\theta - 1)$ cancel each other out", "marks": 0.5}, {"step": "Conclude LHS $= \\\\frac{\\\\sin\\\\theta}{\\\\cos\\\\theta} = \\\\tan\\\\theta = \\\\text{RHS}$", "marks": 0.5}]	3.0	PYQ	Recall & Proof	Board Standard	9fd9ef0e3e0f7ab9c25659c8bb03ec105c6a3f52aef8d7ad6301f8ba22ecbca5	["CBSE Board 2024", "Official SQP 2024-25"]	$\\text{LHS} = \\frac{\\sin\\theta(1 - 2\\sin^2\\theta)}{\\cos\\theta(2\\cos^2\\theta - 1)}$. Using identity $\\sin^2\\theta = 1 - \\cos^2\\theta$ in numerator: $1 - 2\\sin^2\\theta = 1 - 2(1 - \\cos^2\\theta) = 1 - 2 + 2\\cos^2\\theta = 2\\cos^2\\theta - 1$. Substitute back: $\\text{LHS} = \\frac{\\sin\\theta(2\\cos^2\\theta - 1)}{\\cos\\theta(2\\cos^2\\theta - 1)} = \\frac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta} = \\tan\\theta = \\text{RHS}$. Hence proved.	t	\N	\N	f	MATH	Sample
CBSE_2024_CH03_01	M10_CH03	System of Linear Equations Condition	Standard (041)	Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations has infinitely many solutions:\n$$kx + 3y = k - 3$$\n$$12x + ky = k$$	[{"step": "Condition for infinite solutions: $a_1/a_2 = b_1/b_2 = c_1/c_2$", "marks": 0.5}, {"step": "Set up equality: $k/12 = 3/k = (k-3)/k$", "marks": 1.0}, {"step": "Solve $k^2 = 36 \\\\implies k = \\\\pm 6$", "marks": 0.5}, {"step": "Substitute into $(k-3)/k$ to select common consistent root $k = 6$", "marks": 1.0}]	3.0	PYQ	Problem Solving	Board Standard	a2f873107f853a0efcc68dfc0848d6749a2775ae95f2043a6b2eacec5a419ce7	["CBSE Board 2024 (Set 30/2/1)", "CBSE Board 2020"]	For infinitely many solutions: $\\frac{a_1}{a_2} = \\frac{b_1}{b_2} = \\frac{c_1}{c_2} \\implies \\frac{k}{12} = \\frac{3}{k} = \\frac{k-3}{k}$. From $\\frac{k}{12} = \\frac{3}{k}$, $k^2 = 36 \\implies k = \\pm 6$. From $\\frac{3}{k} = \\frac{k-3}{k}$, $3 = k - 3 \\implies k = 6$. The value satisfying both conditions is $k = 6$.	t	\N	\N	f	MATH	All India
CBSE_2024_CH05_01	M10_CH05	Arithmetic Progressions Sum Formula	Standard (041)	If the sum of the first $14$ terms of an A.P. is $1050$ and its first term is $10$, find the $20^{\\text{th}}$ term.	[{"step": "State formula $S_n = \\\\frac{n}{2}[2a + (n-1)d]$ with $n=14, a=10, S_{14}=1050$", "marks": 1.0}, {"step": "Solve $1050 = 7[20 + 13d] \\\\implies 150 = 20 + 13d \\\\implies d = 10$", "marks": 1.0}, {"step": "Calculate $a_{20} = a + 19d = 10 + 19(10) = 200$", "marks": 1.0}]	3.0	PYQ	Procedural	Board Standard	63de76fb65150d2200117648067dd77f804bc2b640b7d0242da85cd3756f256f	["CBSE Board 2024", "CBSE Board 2019"]	Given $a = 10$, $n = 14$, $S_{14} = 1050$. $S_{14} = \\frac{14}{2}[2(10) + (14-1)d] \\implies 1050 = 7(20 + 13d) \\implies 150 = 20 + 13d \\implies 13d = 130 \\implies d = 10$. The $20^{\\text{th}}$ term is $a_{20} = a + 19d = 10 + 19(10) = 200$.	t	\N	\N	f	MATH	All India
CBSE_2024_CH07_01	M10_CH07	Section Formula	Standard (041)	Find the ratio in which the $y$-axis divides the line segment joining the points $A(5, -6)$ and $B(-1, -4)$. Also find the point of intersection.	[{"step": "Assume ratio is $k:1$ and point on $y$-axis is $P(0, y)$", "marks": 0.5}, {"step": "Apply section formula for $x$-coordinate: $0 = \\\\frac{k(-1) + 1(5)}{k+1}$", "marks": 1.0}, {"step": "Solve for $k$: $-k + 5 = 0 \\\\implies k = 5$, ratio is $5:1$", "marks": 0.5}, {"step": "Calculate $y = \\\\frac{5(-4) + 1(-6)}{5+1} = -\\\\frac{13}{3}$, point is $(0, -13/3)$", "marks": 1.0}]	3.0	PYQ	Procedural	Board Standard	f942a9c44dfb60f97a36a2e03ff6a2622c6939a72658019bd710e4c80f867c80	["CBSE Board 2024", "CBSE Board 2023"]	Let the point of division on the $y$-axis be $P(0, y)$, and let it divide segment $AB$ in the ratio $k:1$. By section formula: $x = \\frac{k(-1) + 1(5)}{k+1} = 0 \\implies -k + 5 = 0 \\implies k = 5$. Thus the ratio is $5:1$. Now find the $y$-coordinate: $y = \\frac{5(-4) + 1(-6)}{5+1} = \\frac{-20 - 6}{6} = -\\frac{26}{6} = -\\frac{13}{3}$. The point of intersection is $(0, -\\frac{13}{3})$.	t	\N	\N	f	MATH	All India
CBSE_2024_CH09_01	M10_CH09	Heights and Distances Double Angle	Standard (041)	As observed from the top of a $75\\text{ m}$ high lighthouse from the sea-level, the angles of depression of two ships are $30^\\circ$ and $45^\\circ$. If one ship is exactly behind the other on the same side of the lighthouse, find the distance between the two ships. (Take $\\sqrt{3} = 1.732$)	[{"step": "Accurate diagram with observer altitude $75\\\\text{ m}$ and angles $30^\\\\circ, 45^\\\\circ$", "marks": 1.0}, {"step": "In $\\\\Delta ABC$: $\\\\tan 45^\\\\circ = 75/x \\\\implies x = 75\\\\text{ m}$", "marks": 1.0}, {"step": "In $\\\\Delta ABD$: $\\\\tan 30^\\\\circ = 75/(x + d) \\\\implies x + d = 75\\\\sqrt{3}$", "marks": 1.5}, {"step": "Compute distance $d = 75(\\\\sqrt{3} - 1) = 75(0.732) = 54.9\\\\text{ m}$", "marks": 1.5}]	5.0	PYQ	Problem Solving	Board Standard	09b9b31f181b246379aff98dd25e611368e46fdd71e2d9efb61a32ce2162382d	["CBSE Board 2024", "CBSE Board 2023"]	Let $AB = 75\\text{ m}$ be the lighthouse. Let the two ships be at $C$ and $D$ on the same horizontal line from base $B$. In right $\\Delta ABC$: $\\tan 45^\\circ = \\frac{AB}{BC} \\implies 1 = \\frac{75}{BC} \\implies BC = 75\\text{ m}$. In right $\\Delta ABD$: $\\tan 30^\\circ = \\frac{AB}{BD} \\implies \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} = \\frac{75}{BC + CD} \\implies 75 + CD = 75\\sqrt{3} \\implies CD = 75(\\sqrt{3} - 1)$. Using $\\sqrt{3} = 1.732$: $CD = 75(0.732) = 54.9\\text{ m}$. The distance between the ships is $54.9\\text{ m}$.	t	\N	\N	f	MATH	All India
PYQ_2024_BASIC_SET1_Q03	M10_CH07	Distance of a Point from Origin	Basic	The distance of the point $P(3, -4)$ from the origin $(0, 0)$ is:\r\n(A) $3$\r\n(B) $4$\r\n(C) $5$\r\n(D) $7$	[{"step": "Apply distance from origin formula: d = sqrt(x^2 + y^2)", "marks": 0.5}, {"step": "Compute sqrt(3^2 + (-4)^2) = sqrt(9 + 16) = sqrt(25) = 5 units (Option C)", "marks": 0.5}]	1.0	Objective	Applying	Easy	\N	["CBSE_2024", "Basic_241", "Set_1", "Section_A"]	Distance from the origin $O(0, 0)$ to point $P(x, y)$ is:\r\n$$OP = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}$$\r\n$$OP = \\sqrt{3^2 + (-4)^2} = \\sqrt{9 + 16} = \\sqrt{25} = 5\\text{ units}.$$\r\nOption **(C)** is correct.	t	2024	Basic Set 1	t	MATH	All India
CBSE_2024_CH11_01	M10_CH11	Area of Segment of a Circle	Standard (041)	A chord of a circle of radius $10\\text{ cm}$ subtends a right angle at the centre. Find the area of the corresponding minor segment. (Use $\\pi = 3.14$)	[{"step": "Formula: $\\\\text{Area of sector} = \\\\frac{\\\\theta}{360^\\\\circ} \\\\pi r^2$", "marks": 0.5}, {"step": "Compute sector area $= \\\\frac{90^\\\\circ}{360^\\\\circ} \\\\cdot 3.14 \\\\cdot 100 = 78.5\\\\text{ cm}^2$", "marks": 1.0}, {"step": "Compute $\\\\Delta AOB$ area $= \\\\frac{1}{2} \\\\cdot 10 \\\\cdot 10 = 50\\\\text{ cm}^2$", "marks": 0.5}, {"step": "Segment area $= 78.5 - 50 = 28.5\\\\text{ cm}^2$", "marks": 1.0}]	3.0	PYQ	Procedural	Board Standard	66f785b96b23254108cb2281e96ba1400014181b01a3d51e9d36971c49799828	["CBSE Board 2024", "CBSE Board 2022"]	Radius $r = 10\\text{ cm}$, $\\theta = 90^\\circ$. $\\text{Area of minor sector} = \\frac{90^\\circ}{360^\\circ} \\cdot \\pi r^2 = \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot 3.14 \\cdot 100 = 78.5\\text{ cm}^2$. $\\text{Area of } \\Delta AOB = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot r \\cdot r \\cdot \\sin 90^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 10 \\cdot 10 = 50\\text{ cm}^2$. $\\text{Area of minor segment} = \\text{Sector Area} - \\text{Triangle Area} = 78.5 - 50 = 28.5\\text{ cm}^2$.	t	\N	\N	f	MATH	All India
CBSE_2024_CH13_01	M10_CH13	Missing Frequencies in Grouped Median	Standard (041)	The median of the following data is $525$. Find the values of $x$ and $y$, if the total frequency is $100$:\nClasses: $0\\text{-}100, 100\\text{-}200, 200\\text{-}300, 300\\text{-}400, 400\\text{-}500, 500\\text{-}600, 600\\text{-}700, 700\\text{-}800, 800\\text{-}900, 900\\text{-}1000$.\nFrequencies: $2, 5, x, 12, 17, 20, y, 9, 7, 4$.	[{"step": "Cumulative frequency table calculation and sum equation $x + y = 24$", "marks": 1.5}, {"step": "Identify median class $500\\\\text{-}600$ since median is $525$", "marks": 0.5}, {"step": "Apply median formula: $525 = 500 + \\\\left(\\\\frac{50 - (36+x)}{20}\\\\right) \\\\cdot 100$", "marks": 1.5}, {"step": "Solve: $x = 9$ and $y = 15$", "marks": 1.5}]	5.0	PYQ	Problem Solving	HOTS	0a2d4e0d50396af59ea5ed70167a84a878486ba489d020c9e94cc1d630d3d4cf	["CBSE Board 2024", "NCERT Exemplar"]	Total frequency $N = 100 \\implies 76 + x + y = 100 \\implies x + y = 24$. Since median is $525$, the median class is $500\\text{-}600$. Here $l = 500, f = 20, cf = 36 + x, h = 100$. $\\text{Median} = l + \\left(\\frac{N/2 - cf}{f}\\right) \\cdot h \\implies 525 = 500 + \\left(\\frac{50 - (36 + x)}{20}\\right) \\cdot 100 \\implies 25 = (14 - x) \\cdot 5 \\implies 5 = 14 - x \\implies x = 9$. Since $x + y = 24$, $y = 24 - 9 = 15$.	t	\N	\N	f	MATH	All India
CBSE_2024_CH14_01	M10_CH14	Playing Cards Probability	Standard (041)	One card is drawn from a well-shuffled deck of $52$ cards. Find the probability of getting:\n(i) A king of red colour\n(ii) A face card\n(iii) A red face card	[{"step": "Total outcomes $n(S) = 52$", "marks": 0.5}, {"step": "(i) Number of red kings $= 2 \\\\implies P = 2/52 = 1/26$", "marks": 0.75}, {"step": "(ii) Total face cards $= 12 \\\\implies P = 12/52 = 3/13$", "marks": 1.0}, {"step": "(iii) Red face cards $= 6 \\\\implies P = 6/52 = 3/26$", "marks": 0.75}]	3.0	PYQ	Procedural	Board Standard	63beb95d0c1184ad666d5d86da41f7ee5100e677d3f20b8a7490c7d2025e2409	["CBSE Board 2024", "CBSE Board 2021"]	Total number of possible outcomes $= 52$.\n(i) Red kings: Hearts king and Diamonds king $= 2$. $P(\\text{red king}) = \\frac{2}{52} = \\frac{1}{26}$.\n(ii) Face cards: $4\\text{ Kings} + 4\\text{ Queens} + 4\\text{ Jacks} = 12$. $P(\\text{face card}) = \\frac{12}{52} = \\frac{3}{13}$.\n(iii) Red face cards: $6$ (Kings, Queens, Jacks of Hearts and Diamonds). $P(\\text{red face card}) = \\frac{6}{52} = \\frac{3}{26}$.	t	\N	\N	f	MATH	All India
M10_CH10_BAS_203	M10_CH04	Concentric Circle Simple	Basic	Find length of chord of outer circle of radius 10 cm which touches inner circle of radius 6 cm.	{"rubric": "Half chord = sqrt(100-36) = 8 cm. Chord = 16 cm."}	2.0	Cat_B	Apply	Easy	\N	[]	\N	t	\N	\N	f	MATH	All India
CBSE_2024_CH10_01	M10_CH10	Tangents from an External Point	Standard (041)	Prove that the lengths of tangents drawn from an external point to a circle are equal.	[{"step": "Correct diagram with circle $(O, r)$, external point $P$, and contact points $A, B$", "marks": 0.5}, {"step": "Given, To Prove, and Construction (join $OA, OB, OP$)", "marks": 0.5}, {"step": "Prove $\\\\Delta OAP \\\\cong \\\\Delta OBP$ using RHS criterion ($OA=OB=r$, $OP=OP$, $\\\\angle OAP = \\\\angle OBP = 90^\\\\circ$)", "marks": 1.5}, {"step": "Conclude $PA = PB$ by CPCTC", "marks": 0.5}]	3.0	PYQ	Recall & Proof	Board Standard	5b1e69516dee2165f656be4d6b74fc2b8e884b2f29a52f9a85caa97f2e0e1541	["CBSE Board 2024", "Official SQP 2024-25"]	Given: A circle with centre $O$ and tangents $PA$ and $PB$ from external point $P$.\nTo Prove: $PA = PB$.\nConstruction: Join $OA, OB$, and $OP$.\nProof: Since tangent is perpendicular to radius at the point of contact, $\\angle OAP = \\angle OBP = 90^\\circ$.\nIn right $\\Delta OAP$ and right $\\Delta OBP$:\n$OA = OB$ (Radii of the same circle)\n$OP = OP$ (Common hypotenuse)\n$\\angle OAP = \\angle OBP = 90^\\circ$\nBy RHS Congruence Criterion, $\\Delta OAP \\cong \\Delta OBP$.\nTherefore, $PA = PB$ (by CPCTC).	t	\N	\N	f	MATH	Sample
CBSE_2024_CH12_01	M10_CH12	Combination of Solids Surface Area	Standard (041)	A solid toy is in the form of a hemisphere surmounted by a right circular cone. The height of the cone is $2\\text{ cm}$ and the diameter of the base is $4\\text{ cm}$. Determine the total volume of the toy. (Take $\\pi = 3.14$)	[{"step": "Determine radius $r = 2\\\\text{ cm}$ and cone height $h = 2\\\\text{ cm}$", "marks": 0.5}, {"step": "Cone volume: $\\\\frac{1}{3}\\\\pi r^2 h = \\\\frac{1}{3} \\\\cdot 3.14 \\\\cdot 4 \\\\cdot 2 = 8.37\\\\text{ cm}^3$", "marks": 1.0}, {"step": "Hemisphere volume: $\\\\frac{2}{3}\\\\pi r^3 = \\\\frac{2}{3} \\\\cdot 3.14 \\\\cdot 8 = 16.75\\\\text{ cm}^3$", "marks": 1.0}, {"step": "Total volume $= 25.12\\\\text{ cm}^3$", "marks": 0.5}]	3.0	PYQ	Procedural	Board Standard	161fd3edbb288e79255e359737240cfd6bb2cdc586635b8dc121fb5794d3eb80	["CBSE Board 2024", "Official SQP 2024-25"]	Radius $r = 4/2 = 2\\text{ cm}$. Height of cone $h = 2\\text{ cm}$. Volume of toy $= \\text{Volume of cone} + \\text{Volume of hemisphere} = \\frac{1}{3}\\pi r^2 h + \\frac{2}{3}\\pi r^3 = \\frac{1}{3}\\pi r^2(h + 2r) = \\frac{1}{3}(3.14)(4)(2 + 4) = \\frac{1}{3}(3.14)(4)(6) = 25.12\\text{ cm}^3$.	t	\N	\N	f	MATH	Sample
PYQ_2024_CH04_01	M10_CH04	Speed Word Problem	Standard (041)	A motor boat whose speed is 18 km/h in still water takes 1 hour more to go 24 km upstream than to return downstream to the same spot. Find the speed of the stream.	[{"step": "Upstream = (18 - x) and downstream = (18 + x) speed declaration", "marks": 1.0}, {"step": "Time difference equation: 24/(18-x) - 24/(18+x) = 1", "marks": 1.5}, {"step": "Simplification to quadratic form: x² + 48x - 324 = 0", "marks": 1.5}, {"step": "Reject x = -54 with reason; conclude stream speed = 6 km/h", "marks": 1.0}]	5.0	PYQ	\N	HOTS	1912715edb7fd89c215790cab3f7fe54d97ab0f2ee3f3d5d6b8a1c3f2f15f88c	["CBSE Board 2024", "Official QB Class 10", "Official SQP 2024-25"]	Let stream speed be x km/h. 24/(18 - x) - 24/(18 + x) = 1 => 48x = 324 - x² => x² + 48x - 324 = 0 => (x + 54)(x - 6) = 0. Discard x = -54 since physical speed cannot be negative. Stream speed = 6 km/h.	t	\N	\N	f	MATH	Sample
PYQ_2024_CH06_01	M10_CH06	BPT Proof	Standard (041)	State and prove Basic Proportionality Theorem (Thales Theorem).	[{"step": "Theorem statement, Given figure with DE || BC", "marks": 1.0}, {"step": "Construction: Join BE, CD; draw EF ⊥ AB, DG ⊥ AC", "marks": 1.0}, {"step": "Area ratio equations on side AB and side AC", "marks": 1.5}, {"step": "Equating areas between parallels to conclude BPT", "marks": 1.5}]	5.0	PYQ	\N	Board Standard	8930ddba6a8b565536dc1a82b0d6602210a581ab845279a7e3a4ea88acdf5a1a	["CBSE Board 2024", "Official SQP 2024-25"]	Statement: If a line is drawn parallel to one side of a triangle intersecting other two sides, it divides them in the same ratio. Area(ADE)/Area(BDE) = AD/DB. Area(ADE)/Area(CDE) = AE/EC. Since ΔBDE and ΔCDE lie on same base DE between same parallels, Area(BDE) = Area(CDE). Therefore, AD/DB = AE/EC.	t	\N	\N	f	MATH	Sample
SST_2020_D_12	SS10_GEO_06	\N	Standard (041)	Classify manufacturing industries on the basis of ownership. Provide one distinct industrial enterprise example for each category.	{"classification": "3 Marks (0.75 Mark per ownership type with valid example)"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	083d3660bdab095be9226a8020824617b5df9ac1416db104ae371c88eb663d54	[]	1. Public Sector: Owned and managed by government agencies (e.g., BHEL, SAIL).\r\n2. Private Sector: Owned and operated by individuals or corporate bodies (e.g., TISCO, Reliance, Bajaj Auto).\r\n3. Joint Sector: Jointly operated by the state and private enterprises (e.g., Oil India Ltd - OIL).\r\n4. Cooperative Sector: Owned and operated by raw material producers, suppliers, and workers sharing profits (e.g., AMUL, sugar mills in Maharashtra).	t	2020	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SST_2020_D_13	SS10_HIS_02	\N	Standard (041)	Why did Mahatma Gandhi decide to launch a nationwide Satyagraha against the proposed Rowlatt Act in 1919? How did the British authorities react to this movement?	{"rowlatt_reasons": "2.5 Marks", "british_repression": "2.5 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	f742529bc50618e7b69cad910373d64b6b6ace009ed50456a73666bd88b46afe	[]	1. Opposition to Rowlatt Act:\r\n- Enacted hastily despite united opposition from Indian legislative council members.\r\n- Granted colonial police the power to repress political activity and detain suspects for two years without judicial trial.\r\n- Violated fundamental principles of natural justice, summarized as "no dalil, no vakil, no appeal".\r\n2. British Reaction:\r\n- Imposed martial law in Punjab under General Dyer.\r\n- Open fired on unarmed crowds at Jallianwala Bagh on April 13, 1919.\r\n- Arrested leaders (Dr. Saifuddin Kitchlew, Dr. Satyapal), subjected satyagrahis to public floggings, and forced citizens to crawl on streets.	t	2020	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SST_2020_D_14	SS10_POL_02	\N	Standard (041)	Evaluate the linguistic reorganization of Indian States after 1947. How did language policy strengthen national unity rather than disintegrate it?	{"reorganization": "2.5 Marks", "language_policy": "2.5 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	664c7bd3dd0255b56836beb1d3b008f5a0a484c8d8a18be82b2a75df51da1154	[]	1. Historical Fears: Leaders initially feared linguistic reorganization would encourage regional separatism and weaken national cohesion.\r\n2. Linguistic Policy Success:\r\n- Reorganizing boundaries along language lines (e.g., Andhra, Maharashtra, Gujarat) accommodated identity demands and reduced friction.\r\n- Hindi was made an official language without imposing it as the sole national language, preserving English for inter-state communication.\r\n- The Eighth Schedule recognized 22 regional languages with equal constitutional protection.\r\n3. Outcome: Language accommodation helped integrate diverse linguistic groups into the federal structure.	t	2020	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SST_2020_D_15	SS10_ECO_04	\N	Standard (041)	Describe the role of Multinational Corporations (MNCs) in integrating markets across the globe. Explain four characteristics of MNC operations.	{"integration": "1 Mark", "characteristics": "4 Marks (1 Mark each)"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	e9094a474fe6ad347518e2a1252135e4857f91ead7a4e879d65b6bece7d9eafd	[]	1. Market Integration: MNCs connect distant national markets by setting up production facilities, transferring technology, and building cross-border supply chains.\r\n2. Four Characteristics:\r\n- Global Sourcing: Raw materials, engineering design, assembly, and customer support are located where factor costs are lowest.\r\n- Foreign Direct Investment: They introduce capital into host countries by acquiring local companies or forming joint ventures.\r\n- Contract Manufacturing: They subcontract labor-intensive assembly to decentralized local suppliers (e.g., garments, footwear).\r\n- Global Distribution: Products are distributed internationally under unified brand names.	t	2020	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SCI_2020_AI_01	S10_CH01	\N	Standard (041)	A clear solution of slaked lime is made by dissolving Ca(OH)2 in an excess of water. This solution is left exposed to air in an open beaker. The surface of the solution will slowly form a white crust due to the formation of:\r\n(A) CaO\r\n(B) CaCO3\r\n(C) Ca(OH)2\r\n(D) CaSO4	{"answer": "1 Mark for Option (B)"}	1.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	ed78fce5ec453f586a9e11cdaab627dddc39086bc488133d6fc6ee150d732441	[]	Correct Option: (B)\r\nCa(OH)2(aq) absorbs atmospheric CO2(g) to precipitate white insoluble calcium carbonate:\r\nCa(OH)2 + CO2 -> CaCO3(s) + H2O.	t	2020	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
