MATH_2018_D_02	M10_CH05	\N	Standard (041)	Find the 10th term of the AP: 2, 7, 12, ...	{"a10_calc": "0.5 Mark", "common_diff": "0.5 Mark"}	1.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	ad191ed4c271fd50076b51cf3e8935d372059f9f727fb7c9ddf9bd1f49d8b0e8	[]	Given AP: 2, 7, 12, ...\r\nFirst term a = 2, common difference d = 7 - 2 = 5.\r\na_n = a + (n - 1)d\r\na_10 = 2 + (10 - 1)*5 = 2 + 9*5 = 2 + 45 = 47.	t	2018	Delhi Set 1	f	MATH	Delhi
MATH_2018_D_03	M10_CH10	\N	Standard (041)	In figure, PQ is a tangent at a point C to a circle with center O. If AB is a diameter and angle CAB = 30 degrees, find angle PCA.	{"pca_calc": "0.5 Mark", "semicircle_angle": "0.5 Mark"}	1.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	5a6b5e7a7c54a97ffdbbcf43bdd8b39905bb262d477069b70cf0dbba0181da55	[]	Given AB is a diameter => Angle ACB = 90 degrees (angle in a semicircle).\r\nIn right triangle ABC:\r\nAngle CBA = 180 - (90 + 30) = 60 degrees.\r\nRadius OC is perpendicular to tangent PQ => Angle OCQ = 90 degrees.\r\nSince OA = OC (radii), triangle OAC is isosceles:\r\nAngle OCA = Angle OAC = 30 degrees.\r\nAngle PCA = 90 - Angle OCA = 90 - 30 = 60 degrees (or by Alternate Segment Theorem: Angle PCA = Angle CBA = 60 degrees).	t	2018	Delhi Set 1	f	MATH	Delhi
MATH_2018_D_04	M10_CH14	\N	Standard (041)	A bag contains 3 red balls and 5 black balls. A ball is drawn at random from the bag. What is the probability that the ball drawn is not red?	{"favorable": "0.5 Mark", "probability": "0.5 Mark"}	1.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	73de73df322fe8c242a9b9b0653c005040a6fda7f29f65682c3fee8f7556db29	[]	Total number of balls n(S) = 3 + 5 = 8.\r\nNumber of balls that are not red (i.e. black) = 5.\r\nP(not red) = 5 / 8.	t	2018	Delhi Set 1	f	MATH	Delhi
MATH_2018_D_05	M10_CH04	\N	Standard (041)	Find the value of k for which the roots of the quadratic equation 2x^2 + kx + 3 = 0 are real and equal.	{"k_values": "1 Mark", "discriminant_setup": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	0b70ea1879d6d5979068eb9b292e75d1df3e55bc8c1b96018e90da4fd4d8cd45	[]	Given: 2x^2 + kx + 3 = 0.\r\nHere a = 2, b = k, c = 3.\r\nFor real and equal roots, Discriminant D = 0:\r\nb^2 - 4ac = 0\r\nk^2 - 4(2)(3) = 0\r\nk^2 - 24 = 0\r\nk^2 = 24\r\nk = ± sqrt(24) = ± 2*sqrt(6).	t	2018	Delhi Set 1	f	MATH	Delhi
MATH_2018_D_06	M10_CH07	\N	Standard (041)	Find the coordinates of a point A, where AB is the diameter of a circle whose center is (2, -3) and B is (1, 4).	{"x_coord": "1 Mark", "y_coord": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	fbc588eeb28d934d575d8158d8ba96913e061f1744a5c7add3b3ef712913b2bc	[]	Let the coordinates of A be (x, y).\r\nCenter C(2, -3) is the midpoint of diameter AB joining A(x, y) and B(1, 4).\r\nBy midpoint formula:\r\n(x + 1) / 2 = 2 => x + 1 = 4 => x = 3.\r\n(y + 4) / 2 = -3 => y + 4 = -6 => y = -10.\r\nThe coordinates of point A are (3, -10).	t	2018	Delhi Set 1	f	MATH	Delhi
MATH_2018_D_07	M10_CH11	\N	Standard (041)	The radii of two circles are 19 cm and 9 cm respectively. Find the radius of the circle which has circumference equal to the sum of the circumferences of the two circles.	{"equation": "1 Mark", "radius_calc": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	996a1e8cb34791fadf4561c605782dfd7f8777691e99a2766abff1a6e90d547d	[]	Let the radii be r1 = 19 cm and r2 = 9 cm.\r\nLet R be the radius of the new circle.\r\nGiven: Circumference of new circle = 2*pi*r1 + 2*pi*r2\r\n2*pi*R = 2*pi*(r1 + r2)\r\nR = r1 + r2 = 19 + 9 = 28 cm.\r\nThe radius of the new circle is 28 cm.	t	2018	Delhi Set 1	f	MATH	Delhi
MATH_2018_D_08	M10_CH03	\N	Standard (041)	Solve for x and y by elimination:\r\n4x + 6/y = 15\r\n6x - 8/y = 14  (y != 0)	{"x_val": "1 Mark", "y_val": "1 Mark", "substitution_u": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	3878c5d1df0ad057cd7d64b484836cab930e59ee4f49ea59a233406aba3578ec	[]	Let 1/y = u. The system becomes:\r\n4x + 6u = 15  --- (1)\r\n6x - 8u = 14  --- (2)\r\n\r\nMultiply (1) by 4 and (2) by 3:\r\n16x + 24u = 60  --- (3)\r\n18x - 24u = 42  --- (4)\r\n\r\nAdd (3) and (4):\r\n34x = 102 => x = 3.\r\n\r\nSubstitute x = 3 into (1):\r\n4(3) + 6u = 15 => 12 + 6u = 15 => 6u = 3 => u = 1/2.\r\nSince u = 1/y:\r\n1/y = 1/2 => y = 2.\r\nSolution: x = 3, y = 2.	t	2018	Delhi Set 1	f	MATH	Delhi
MATH_2018_D_09	M10_CH05	\N	Standard (041)	How many terms of the AP: 24, 21, 18, ... must be taken so that their sum is 78? Explain the double answer.	{"quadratic_n": "1.5 Marks", "double_answer_explanation": "1.5 Marks"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	bf495b3af10736846c5e329d990d7e452dc9aa41e3480169e920061ec02032b1	[]	Given AP: 24, 21, 18, ...\r\nFirst term a = 24, common difference d = 21 - 24 = -3, S_n = 78.\r\nS_n = (n/2)[2a + (n - 1)d]\r\n78 = (n/2)[2(24) + (n - 1)(-3)]\r\n156 = n[48 - 3n + 3] = n[51 - 3n]\r\n156 = 51n - 3n^2\r\n3n^2 - 51n + 156 = 0\r\nDivide by 3:\r\nn^2 - 17n + 52 = 0\r\n(n - 4)(n - 13) = 0\r\n=> n = 4 or n = 13.\r\n\r\nExplanation of double answer:\r\nSince the common difference is negative (d = -3), terms decrease and become negative after the 9th term.\r\nThe sum of the 5th to the 13th terms is zero:\r\na_5 = 12, a_6 = 9, a_7 = 6, a_8 = 3, a_9 = 0, a_10 = -3, a_11 = -6, a_12 = -9, a_13 = -12.\r\nSum of these 9 terms = 0.\r\nHence, S_4 = S_13 = 78.	t	2018	Delhi Set 1	f	MATH	Delhi
MATH_2018_D_10	M10_CH08	\N	Standard (041)	Prove that:\r\n(sin A + cosec A)^2 + (cos A + sec A)^2 = 7 + tan^2 A + cot^2 A	{"expansion": "1 Mark", "identity_substitution": "1 Mark", "reciprocal_simplification": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	395a9e0a1b9f1a335aea40c6f04db3c23a87b01b95fdd9dc4a41286051f5d9ac	[]	LHS = (sin A + cosec A)^2 + (cos A + sec A)^2\r\nExpand both binomial squares:\r\n= (sin^2 A + 2*sin A*cosec A + cosec^2 A) + (cos^2 A + 2*cos A*sec A + sec^2 A)\r\nSince sin A * cosec A = 1 and cos A * sec A = 1:\r\n= sin^2 A + 2(1) + cosec^2 A + cos^2 A + 2(1) + sec^2 A\r\nGroup (sin^2 A + cos^2 A) = 1:\r\n= (sin^2 A + cos^2 A) + 4 + cosec^2 A + sec^2 A\r\n= 1 + 4 + cosec^2 A + sec^2 A\r\n= 5 + cosec^2 A + sec^2 A\r\n\r\nApply identities cosec^2 A = 1 + cot^2 A and sec^2 A = 1 + tan^2 A:\r\n= 5 + (1 + cot^2 A) + (1 + tan^2 A)\r\n= 7 + tan^2 A + cot^2 A = RHS. (Hence Proved)	t	2018	Delhi Set 1	f	MATH	Delhi
MATH_2018_D_11	M10_CH10	\N	Standard (041)	Prove that the lengths of tangents drawn from an external point to a circle are equal.	{"cpct": "1 Mark", "rhs_congruence": "1 Mark", "figure_construction": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	30757d12daf860f43959e1a45490c754f8423dfb74c61b6fe17c8fb0e8b3ac50	[]	Given: A circle with center O, an external point P, and tangents PA and PB touching the circle at A and B.\r\nTo Prove: PA = PB.\r\nConstruction: Join OA, OB, and OP.\r\n\r\nProof:\r\nIn right triangles OAP and OBP:\r\nAngle OAP = Angle OBP = 90 degrees (radius is perpendicular to tangent at point of contact).\r\nOP = OP (common hypotenuse).\r\nOA = OB (radii of the same circle).\r\nTherefore, triangle OAP is congruent to triangle OBP by RHS congruence criterion.\r\nHence, PA = PB by CPCT. (Hence Proved)	t	2018	Delhi Set 1	f	MATH	Delhi
SCI_2020_AI_04	S10_CH06	\N	Standard (041)	Which of the following events does NOT occur in the light-dependent photochemical reaction of photosynthesis in green leaves?\r\n(A) Absorption of light energy by chlorophyll\r\n(B) Photolysis of water into hydrogen and oxygen\r\n(C) Reduction of carbon dioxide to carbohydrates\r\n(D) Conversion of light energy into chemical energy	{"answer": "1 Mark for Option (C)"}	1.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	2267f735664f9d527e4d37eb25582aecae4b34968f0197ff0bf0d91dc3994b25	[]	Correct Option: (C)\r\nReduction of carbon dioxide to carbohydrates is a dark reaction (Calvin cycle) occurring in the stroma, not during the primary photochemical phase.	t	2020	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
