MATH_2019_D_02	M10_CH05	\N	Standard (041)	Find the common difference of an AP in which a_21 - a_7 = 84.	{"expansion": "0.5 Mark", "common_diff": "0.5 Mark"}	1.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	f252945fdc39789d07bf56f9939cd41c3e908dffc2c68238e5258db5f9bea327	[]	For any AP: a_n = a + (n - 1)d\r\na_21 - a_7 = [a + 20d] - [a + 6d] = 14d\r\nGiven: 14d = 84 => d = 84 / 14 = 6.	t	2019	Delhi Set 1	f	MATH	Delhi
MATH_2019_D_03	M10_CH10	\N	Standard (041)	In figure, PA and PB are tangents to the circle with center O such that angle APB = 50 degrees. Find angle OAB.	{"oab_calc": "0.5 Mark", "isosceles_angle": "0.5 Mark"}	1.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	ae8b056015f4bb447b85e95bcbf95327fa9e85f1a2ffadd50f79c15ee41e1e3c	[]	Since lengths of tangents from an external point are equal, PA = PB.\r\nTriangle PAB is isosceles with angle PAB = angle PBA:\r\nAngle PAB = (180 - 50) / 2 = 130 / 2 = 65 degrees.\r\nRadius OA is perpendicular to tangent PA => Angle OAP = 90 degrees.\r\nAngle OAB = Angle OAP - Angle PAB = 90 - 65 = 25 degrees.	t	2019	Delhi Set 1	f	MATH	Delhi
MATH_2019_D_04	M10_CH14	\N	Standard (041)	A card is drawn from a well-shuffled pack of 52 cards. Find the probability of getting a face card of black color.	{"favorable": "0.5 Mark", "probability": "0.5 Mark"}	1.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	e757b37bd665bbc7957698e2e075cbc19314245d9ee54f16cfb1b4e3fe50a361	[]	Total possible outcomes n(S) = 52.\r\nBlack face cards (Kings, Queens, Jacks of Spades and Clubs) = 3 + 3 = 6.\r\nP(black face card) = 6 / 52 = 3 / 26.	t	2019	Delhi Set 1	f	MATH	Delhi
MATH_2019_D_05	M10_CH04	\N	Standard (041)	Find the value of k for which the equation x^2 + k(2x + k - 1) + 2 = 0 has real and equal roots.	{"k_calc": "1 Mark", "rearrangement": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	83bd6717e3f064f0aaac5455abe28dacd11f138b4d23e52ce44687a54c173d9a	[]	Rearrange into standard form ax^2 + bx + c = 0:\r\nx^2 + 2kx + (k^2 - k + 2) = 0.\r\nHere a = 1, b = 2k, c = (k^2 - k + 2).\r\n\r\nFor real and equal roots, Discriminant D = 0:\r\nb^2 - 4ac = 0\r\n(2k)^2 - 4(1)(k^2 - k + 2) = 0\r\n4k^2 - 4k^2 + 4k - 8 = 0\r\n4k - 8 = 0 => 4k = 8 => k = 2.	t	2019	Delhi Set 1	f	MATH	Delhi
MATH_2019_D_06	M10_CH07	\N	Standard (041)	Find the ratio in which P(4, m) divides the line segment joining the points A(2, 3) and B(6, -3). Hence find m.	{"m_value": "1 Mark", "ratio_calc": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	1ac2c5e9de8a14b85514a69f77274486ef111bd33d8484b3de0271e40a94c9d9	[]	Let P divide AB in the ratio k : 1.\r\nUsing Section Formula on x-coordinates:\r\nx = (k*6 + 1*2) / (k + 1) = 4\r\n6k + 2 = 4(k + 1) => 6k + 2 = 4k + 4 => 2k = 2 => k = 1.\r\nThe ratio is 1 : 1 (P is the midpoint).\r\n\r\nNow find m (the y-coordinate):\r\nm = [1*(-3) + 1*(3)] / (1 + 1) = 0 / 2 = 0.\r\nRatio = 1 : 1, m = 0.	t	2019	Delhi Set 1	f	MATH	Delhi
MATH_2019_D_07	M10_CH11	\N	Standard (041)	The short and long hands of a clock are 4 cm and 6 cm long respectively. Find the sum of distances traveled by their tips in 48 hours (Use pi = 3.14).	{"hour_hand": "1 Mark", "minute_hand": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	fc6d71417871d88e0d3447382893b662860272ce86584cdf9acc336ada941b20	[]	1. Short hand (hour hand, length r1 = 4 cm):\r\nIn 12 hours, completes 1 revolution.\r\nIn 48 hours, completes 48 / 12 = 4 revolutions.\r\nDistance d1 = 4 * (2 * pi * r1) = 4 * 2 * 3.14 * 4 = 100.48 cm.\r\n\r\n2. Long hand (minute hand, length r2 = 6 cm):\r\nIn 1 hour, completes 1 revolution.\r\nIn 48 hours, completes 48 revolutions.\r\nDistance d2 = 48 * (2 * pi * r2) = 48 * 2 * 3.14 * 6 = 1808.64 cm.\r\n\r\nTotal distance = d1 + d2 = 100.48 + 1808.64 = 1909.12 cm.	t	2019	Delhi Set 1	f	MATH	Delhi
MATH_2019_D_08	M10_CH03	\N	Standard (041)	Solve for x and y:\r\nx/a + y/b = 2\r\nax - by = a^2 - b^2	{"values": "1.5 Marks", "elimination_setup": "1.5 Marks"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	2c830e00a2aa30294ed5e690c87f32c246160b8ca774416d2ec4065d3498ba94	[]	Equation 1: bx + ay = 2ab   --- (1)\r\nEquation 2: ax - by = a^2 - b^2 --- (2)\r\n\r\nMultiply (1) by b and (2) by a:\r\nb^2*x + ab*y = 2a*b^2       --- (3)\r\na^2*x - ab*y = a^3 - a*b^2   --- (4)\r\n\r\nAdd (3) and (4):\r\n(a^2 + b^2)x = a^3 + a*b^2 = a(a^2 + b^2)\r\nx = a.\r\n\r\nSubstitute x = a into (1):\r\nb(a) + ay = 2ab => ay = ab => y = b.\r\nSolution: x = a, y = b.	t	2019	Delhi Set 1	f	MATH	Delhi
MATH_2019_D_09	M10_CH05	\N	Standard (041)	The sum of the first n terms of an AP is given by S_n = 3n^2 + 4n. Determine the AP and find its 25th term.	{"a25_calc": "1.5 Marks", "ap_series": "1.5 Marks"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	6b8841c00444b9ba16ea7eccca9da51191d95c081308efa3d552468a35f3edc6	[]	Given: S_n = 3n^2 + 4n.\r\nS_1 = a_1 = 3(1)^2 + 4(1) = 3 + 4 = 7.\r\nS_2 = a_1 + a_2 = 3(2)^2 + 4(2) = 12 + 8 = 20.\r\nSecond term a_2 = S_2 - S_1 = 20 - 7 = 13.\r\nCommon difference d = a_2 - a_1 = 13 - 7 = 6.\r\nThe AP is 7, 13, 19, 25, ...\r\n\r\n25th term:\r\na_25 = a + 24d = 7 + 24(6) = 7 + 144 = 151.	t	2019	Delhi Set 1	f	MATH	Delhi
MATH_2019_D_10	M10_CH08	\N	Standard (041)	Prove that:\r\n(1 + cot A + tan A)(sin A - cos A) / (sec^3 A - cosec^3 A) = sin^2 A * cos^2 A	{"conclusion": "1 Mark", "denominator_expansion": "1 Mark", "numerator_simplification": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	483e12f7a5ca02aa76ade2dbf3310537ebba0c5461f264465cf02ccc9b77074d	[]	Numerator = (1 + cos A/sin A + sin A/cos A)(sin A - cos A)\r\n= [(sin A * cos A + cos^2 A + sin^2 A) / (sin A * cos A)] * (sin A - cos A)\r\n= [(1 + sin A * cos A)(sin A - cos A)] / (sin A * cos A)\r\n\r\nDenominator:\r\nsec^3 A - cosec^3 A = 1/cos^3 A - 1/sin^3 A = (sin^3 A - cos^3 A) / (sin^3 A * cos^3 A)\r\nUsing algebraic identity a^3 - b^3 = (a - b)(a^2 + ab + b^2):\r\nsin^3 A - cos^3 A = (sin A - cos A)(sin^2 A + sin A * cos A + cos^2 A) = (sin A - cos A)(1 + sin A * cos A)\r\nSo Denominator = [(sin A - cos A)(1 + sin A * cos A)] / (sin^3 A * cos^3 A)\r\n\r\nNow evaluate LHS = Numerator / Denominator:\r\n= {[(1 + sin A * cos A)(sin A - cos A)] / (sin A * cos A)} * {(sin^3 A * cos^3 A) / [(sin A - cos A)(1 + sin A * cos A)]}\r\n= (sin^3 A * cos^3 A) / (sin A * cos A)\r\n= sin^2 A * cos^2 A = RHS. (Hence Proved)	t	2019	Delhi Set 1	f	MATH	Delhi
MATH_2019_D_11	M10_CH06	\N	Standard (041)	In an equilateral triangle ABC, D is a point on side BC such that BD = 1/4 BC. Prove that 16*AD^2 = 13*AB^2.	{"altitude_setup": "1.5 Marks", "pythagoras_proof": "1.5 Marks"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	71abaec7a36e8f2725da3e2bf2ed6b7dd0347105f4589907da02bc93a028a215	[]	Let the side of equilateral triangle ABC be a.\r\nDraw altitude AM perpendicular to BC.\r\nAltitude of equilateral triangle bisects base: BM = a / 2.\r\nGiven: BD = a / 4.\r\nDM = BM - BD = a/2 - a/4 = a/4.\r\n\r\nIn right triangle ABM:\r\nAM^2 = AB^2 - BM^2 = a^2 - (a/2)^2 = 3a^2 / 4.\r\n\r\nIn right triangle ADM:\r\nAD^2 = AM^2 + DM^2 = 3a^2/4 + (a/4)^2 = 3a^2/4 + a^2/16\r\nAD^2 = (12a^2 + a^2) / 16 = 13a^2 / 16.\r\n16*AD^2 = 13a^2 = 13*AB^2. (Hence Proved)	t	2019	Delhi Set 1	f	MATH	Delhi
SCI_2020_AI_07	S10_CH10	\N	Standard (041)	A convex lens has a focal length of 25 cm. Calculate its optical power with proper SI units. State whether it converges or diverges incident parallel light rays.	{"power": "1.5 Marks", "nature": "0.5 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	104b66779027e8d6c8fa0ba47213ac7b94924b0739ddf4eac4579bb03fcd40d1	[]	f = +25 cm = +0.25 m.\r\nPower P = 1 / f(in meters) = 1 / (+0.25) = +4.0 Dioptres (+4.0 D).\r\nNature: Converging lens (converges parallel incident light rays to the focal plane).	t	2020	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2020_AI_08	S10_CH02	\N	Standard (041)	Name the products formed when brine (concentrated aqueous NaCl) is electrolysed in the chlor-alkali process. Write the balanced equation and state one practical use of each product.	{"uses": "1.5 Marks (0.5M each)", "equation": "1.5 Marks"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	73b3a0329b854be8fc488cc8fc86f5a9f7d8570e365833816f5bdce4d24a2991	[]	1. Chlor-Alkali Equation:\r\n2NaCl(aq) + 2H2O(l) --(Electricity)--> 2NaOH(aq) + Cl2(g) + H2(g).\r\n2. Products and Uses:\r\n- Sodium Hydroxide (NaOH): Soap and detergent manufacturing, paper refining.\r\n- Chlorine Gas (Cl2): Water purification, synthesis of PVC and bleaching powder.\r\n- Hydrogen Gas (H2): Ammonia fertilizer synthesis, fuel for fuel cells.	t	2020	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2020_AI_09	S10_CH06	\N	Standard (041)	Describe the role of the following in the human digestive system:\r\n(a) Gastric Hydrochloric Acid (HCl)\r\n(b) Pancreatic Amylase\r\n(c) Bile salts (Emulsification)	{"hcl": "1 Mark", "bile": "1 Mark", "amylase": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	6be8491689c7a2444244026ce00036877ddef3364809aece0ab1dc41949c1518	[]	(a) Gastric HCl: Creates an acidic pH (~1.5–2.0) required to activate inactive pepsinogen into active pepsin, and kills swallowed bacterial pathogens.\r\n(b) Pancreatic Amylase: Hydrolyzes residual complex starch and glycogen polymers into maltose disaccharides in an alkaline medium.\r\n(c) Bile Salts: Mechanically break large insoluble dietary fat globules into smaller droplets (emulsification), increasing surface area for lipase action.	t	2020	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2020_AI_10	S10_CH10	\N	Standard (041)	A concave lens has a focal length of 15 cm. At what distance should the object from the lens be placed so that it forms an image at 10 cm from the lens? Find the magnification.	{"u_calc": "1 Mark", "formula_sub": "1 Mark", "magnification": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	ae5208458f185ab832a2cb51317779915ce07914cdeda16fa6699ee072f0f510	[]	For a concave lens: f = -15 cm, v = -10 cm (virtual image formed on same side).\r\nLens formula: 1/v - 1/u = 1/f -> 1/u = 1/v - 1/f\r\n1/u = 1/(-10) - 1/(-15) = -1/10 + 1/15 = (-3 + 2)/30 = -1/30.\r\nu = -30 cm.\r\nMagnification m = v / u = (-10) / (-30) = +1/3 = +0.33.\r\nObject should be placed 30 cm in front of the concave lens. Magnification is +0.33 (erect and diminished).	t	2020	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2020_AI_11	S10_CH12	\N	Standard (041)	Explain the following:\r\n(a) Why is an ammeter always connected in series in an electric circuit?\r\n(b) Why is a voltmeter always connected in parallel across circuit components?\r\n(c) What happens if an ammeter is accidentally connected in parallel across a high-voltage supply?	{"ammeter": "1 Mark", "voltmeter": "1 Mark", "short_circuit": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	dd26ef4032991ca3f2447385402f541a951ecef5fd999ec72373fe7dddcf9b99	[]	(a) Ammeter: Possesses near-zero internal electrical resistance; connecting in series ensures full circuit current traverses through it without altering net circuit resistance or current value.\r\n(b) Voltmeter: Possesses exceptionally high internal resistance; connecting in parallel across two nodes measures the potential difference without drawing appreciable branch current.\r\n(c) Parallel Ammeter: Due to its low resistance, it produces a short circuit across the supply, drawing high current that burns out the meter coil and risks blowing circuit fuses.	t	2020	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2020_AI_12	S10_CH15	\N	Standard (041)	Differentiate between a food chain and a food web. Why is a food web considered more stable for ecological equilibrium than an isolated food chain?	{"web_def": "1 Mark", "chain_def": "1 Mark", "stability": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	f35147624c898c21a934299fc9e7b6a39ce19fed96d093d8b9f05cee286cc553	[]	1. Food Chain: A linear, unidirectional sequence tracing the transfer of food energy from autotrophic producers to apex consumers.\r\n2. Food Web: A complex network of interconnected, interlocking food chains representing alternative feeding relationships within a community.\r\n3. Stability: In an isolated food chain, loss of one species breaks the sequence and disrupts dependent predators. In a food web, predators switch to alternative prey species, buffering the ecosystem against collapse.	t	2020	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2020_AI_13	S10_CH03	\N	Standard (041)	(a) Describe the thermite process for joining cracked railway tracks with a balanced chemical equation.\r\n(b) Explain why aluminum cannot be extracted from alumina (Al2O3) by carbon reduction. Describe the electrolytic reduction method used instead.	{"affinity": "1 Mark", "thermite": "2 Marks", "electrolytic": "2 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	9e0f85026b7f1438d913c7767ef0fb07b787d6241e5e6efee16819aced9d3488	[]	(a) Thermite Reaction: Highly exothermic reduction of ferric oxide powder by aluminum powder:\r\nFe2O3(s) + 2Al(s) -> 2Fe(l) + Al2O3(s) + Heat.\r\nThe molten iron produced flows directly into cracks to weld railway tracks.\r\n(b) Aluminum Extraction:\r\n- Al has a higher chemical affinity for oxygen than carbon does, so carbon cannot reduce Al2O3.\r\n- Hall-Héroult Electrolytic Reduction: Purified alumina is dissolved in molten cryolite (Na3AlF6) to lower its melting point and improve conductivity. Electrolysis uses carbon (graphite) anodes and a carbon-lined cathode chamber:\r\nCathode: Al3+ + 3e- -> Al(l)\r\nAnode: C(s) + 2O2- -> CO2(g) + 4e-.	t	2020	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2020_AI_14	S10_CH04	\N	Standard (041)	(a) What is meant by a substitution reaction in carbon compounds? Give the chlorination reaction of methane in sunlight.\r\n(b) What is an addition reaction? Write the industrial application of the addition reaction of vegetable oils with hydrogen, naming the catalyst.\r\n(c) State two physical differences between soaps and synthetic detergents.	{"addition": "2 Marks", "substitution": "1.5 Marks", "soap_detergent": "1.5 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	493c52d0829955ff89d70f964a3fa9ce945e661dcbcf7c30ee03f862f0440605	[]	(a) Substitution: A reaction in which one or more hydrogen atoms in a saturated hydrocarbon are replaced atom-by-atom by another functional group or halogen:\r\nCH4 + Cl2 --(Sunlight)--> CH3Cl + HCl.\r\n(b) Addition Reaction: Unsaturated hydrocarbons add hydrogen atoms across double or triple bonds in the presence of a nickel (Ni) or palladium (Pd) catalyst to yield saturated compounds.\r\nApplication: Hydrogenation of unsaturated liquid vegetable oils into solid vegetable ghee (vanaspati).\r\n(c) Differences:\r\n1. Soaps are sodium/potassium salts of long-chain fatty acids; detergents are sodium salts of alkyl benzene sulphonic acids.\r\n2. Soaps form insoluble curds (scum) in hard water; synthetic detergents clean effectively in both hard and soft water.	t	2020	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
PYQ_2024_BASIC_SET1_Q04	M10_CH08	Standard Trigonometric Value Evaluation	Basic	The value of $\\tan 45^\\circ + \\cos 60^\\circ$ is:\r\n(A) $1$\r\n(B) $\\frac{3}{2}$\r\n(C) $\\frac{1}{2}$\r\n(D) $2$	[{"step": "Substitute standard values: tan 45 = 1 and cos 60 = 1/2", "marks": 0.5}, {"step": "Compute 1 + 1/2 = 3/2 (Option B)", "marks": 0.5}]	1.0	Objective	Remembering	Easy	\N	["CBSE_2024", "Basic_241", "Set_1", "Section_A"]	From the standard trigonometric table:\r\n$$\\tan 45^\\circ = 1, \\quad \\cos 60^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2}$$\r\n$$\\tan 45^\\circ + \\cos 60^\\circ = 1 + \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{3}{2}.$$\r\nOption **(B)** is correct.	t	2024	Basic Set 1	t	MATH	All India
SCI_2019_D_07	S10_CH10	\N	Standard (041)	Why do stars twinkle in the night sky, whereas planets do not twinkle? Explain briefly.	{"stars": "1 Mark", "planets": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	08bbdd32a63641b439729ae6bc33af1106f68ebadad111706185d66e0673b8a6	[]	1. Stars: Being distant point sources of light, their rays pass through dynamic layers of the atmosphere with fluctuating refractive indices, causing continuous shifts in apparent position and light intensity (twinkling).\r\n2. Planets: Located much closer to Earth, planets act as extended arrays of multiple point light sources. The variations from individual points average out to zero, resulting in a steady illumination.	t	2019	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
SCI_2019_D_08	S10_CH02	\N	Standard (041)	(a) How is Plaster of Paris chemically prepared from gypsum? Write the balanced chemical equation and mention the essential temperature condition.\r\n(b) What happens when Plaster of Paris is left exposed to moisture?	{"moisture": "1 Mark", "preparation": "2 Marks"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	ef6863c8101d0e456d5954995aa8a9d6e4c0bdd7482241e0a5bfe02d49cb82b4	[]	(a) Preparation: Gypsum is heated carefully at 373 K (100°C):\r\nCaSO4·2H2O --(373 K)--> CaSO4·0.5H2O + 1.5H2O.\r\nTemperature must not exceed 373 K, or dead burnt plaster (anhydrous CaSO4) forms.\r\n(b) Moisture Exposure: POP absorbs water and re-hydrates into hard crystalline gypsum:\r\nCaSO4·0.5H2O + 1.5H2O -> CaSO4·2H2O.	t	2019	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
SCI_2019_D_09	S10_CH06	\N	Standard (041)	Differentiate between xylem and phloem transport tissues in vascular plants based on: (a) substances transported, (b) direction of flow, (c) energy requirement (ATP).	{"energy": "1 Mark", "direction": "1 Mark", "substances": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	4aaead47ffce7375cf43f5b6037f7bc693c9d6f1d304a88cf8592057ebd635ee	[]	1. Substances Transported: Xylem transports water and dissolved mineral ions; Phloem transports synthesized organic photosynthates (sucrose, amino acids).\r\n2. Direction of Flow: Xylem flow is strictly unidirectional (upward from roots to foliage); Phloem transport is bidirectional (source to sink).\r\n3. Energy Requirement: Xylem relies largely on physical forces (transpiration pull, root pressure) without direct metabolic ATP use; Phloem translocation actively consumes ATP to build osmotic pressure gradients.	t	2019	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
SCI_2019_D_10	S10_CH10	\N	Standard (041)	A 2.0 cm tall object is placed perpendicular to the principal axis of a convex lens of focal length 10 cm. The distance of the object from the lens is 15 cm. Find the nature, position, and size of the image formed.	{"size": "1 Mark", "nature": "1 Mark", "position": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	392c70eea958e95af2ee5cadcf4b98ce1593e4ad9fc8748b1b34abd63672a1c3	[]	Given: h = +2.0 cm, f = +10 cm, u = -15 cm.\r\nLens formula: 1/v - 1/u = 1/f -> 1/v = 1/10 + 1/(-15) = (3 - 2)/30 = 1/30 -> v = +30 cm.\r\nMagnification m = v / u = (+30) / (-15) = -2.0.\r\nHeight h' = m * h = -2.0 * 2.0 cm = -4.0 cm.\r\nImage formed 30 cm on the opposite side of the lens; it is real, inverted, and magnified (4.0 cm high).	t	2019	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
SCI_2019_D_11	S10_CH12	\N	Standard (041)	Two electric bulbs rated 60 W, 220 V and 100 W, 220 V are connected in parallel to an electric mains supply of 220 V. Calculate the total current drawn from the line.	{"total": "1 Mark for 0.73 A", "resistances_currents": "2 Marks"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	45b6a8d584cff5fa8c5ce2b97caf5b6db64682f220a25dbdb4c0c9a15879e287	[]	Resistance of bulb 1: R1 = V^2 / P1 = (220)^2 / 60 = 48400 / 60 = 806.67 ohms.\r\nResistance of bulb 2: R2 = V^2 / P2 = (220)^2 / 100 = 48400 / 100 = 484 ohms.\r\nCurrent in bulb 1: I1 = P1 / V = 60 / 220 = 0.272 A.\r\nCurrent in bulb 2: I2 = P2 / V = 100 / 220 = 0.454 A.\r\nTotal current I = I1 + I2 = (60 + 100) / 220 = 160 / 220 = 0.727 A (approx 0.73 A).	t	2019	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
SCI_2019_D_12	S10_CH15	\N	Standard (041)	Explain how pesticide residues enter food chains. Why do apex predators (such as humans) accumulate the highest concentration of toxic residues?	{"entry": "1.5 Marks", "biomagnification": "1.5 Marks"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	8506447e6ddc22eeb71e954bd8e3cdcad7620cacb22ef46afb55c2af5a6eb19f	[]	1. Entry: Synthetic pesticides and insecticides sprayed onto agricultural fields wash into soil and aquatic reservoirs, where they are absorbed by terrestrial plant roots and aquatic producers.\r\n2. Biomagnification: These synthetic chemical toxins are non-biodegradable and cannot be metabolized or excreted. As biomass passes sequentially along the food chain, the concentration of toxins compounds at each trophic tier, reaching its peak in top predators.	t	2019	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
SCI_2019_D_13	S10_CH03	\N	Standard (041)	(a) Write electron dot structures for sodium, oxygen, and chlorine atoms.\r\n(b) Show the formation of Na2O and NaCl by the transfer of electrons.\r\n(c) State three general properties of ionic compounds.	{"dots": "1.5 Marks", "transfer": "2 Marks", "properties": "1.5 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	ae6052399a300323d8e2d0581e035f6c9bef36c47911c3766b5f6d1f78382847	[]	(a) Electron dots: Na (one dot: •), O (six dots: :Ö:), Cl (seven dots: :C̈l:•).\r\n(b) Electron Transfer:\r\n- Na2O: Two Na atoms each donate 1 electron: 2Na -> 2Na+ + 2e-; Oxygen accepts both: O + 2e- -> O2-. Yields (Na+)2[O]2-.\r\n- NaCl: Na -> Na+ + e-; Cl + e- -> Cl-. Yields [Na+][Cl]-.\r\n(c) Properties:\r\n1. High melting and boiling points due to strong inter-ionic electrostatic forces.\r\n2. Solid, hard, and brittle in crystalline structure.\r\n3. Conduct electricity in molten state and aqueous solution, but insulate in solid form.	t	2019	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
SCI_2019_D_14	S10_CH04	\N	Standard (041)	(a) Give reasons why carbon forms an unusually large variety of compounds.\r\n(b) Distinguish between soaps and synthetic detergents with respect to their cleansing action in hard water.\r\n(c) What happens when ethanoic acid reacts with absolute ethanol in the presence of an acid catalyst? Write the balanced equation.	{"hard_water": "2 Marks", "esterification": "1.5 Marks", "carbon_uniqueness": "1.5 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	ee44221e1a787f6110ac1f4466a38341e1723c1e4af92ae8ebf358ce21a15376	[]	(a) Catenation (capacity to form long stable C-C covalent chains and rings) and Tetravalency (ability to bind with 4 other monovalent or multivalent atoms).\r\n(b) Hard Water Cleansing:\r\n- Soaps react with Ca2+ and Mg2+ salts in hard water to form insoluble curds (scum), wasting soap and reducing lather.\r\n- Synthetic detergents form soluble calcium and magnesium sulfonates, maintaining effective cleaning performance without forming scum.\r\n(c) Esterification:\r\nCH3COOH + C2H5OH --(Conc H2SO4)--> CH3COOC2H5 + H2O (Sweet-smelling ethyl ethanoate ester).	t	2019	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
SCI_2020_AI_15	S10_CH13	\N	Standard (041)	(a) What is a short-circuit and how does overloading occur in domestic electric circuits?\r\n(b) Explain the function of a safety electric fuse in preventing domestic fires.\r\n(c) Why is electrical earthing necessary for metallic domestic appliances such as refrigerators, toasters, and irons?	{"fuse": "1.5 Marks", "earthing": "1.5 Marks", "short_overload": "2 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	6e7e34a84279f62bc546acede2c6684b18d881c4707c9235fc47bbd63be7649c	[]	(a) Short-Circuit & Overloading:\r\n- Short-Circuit: Occurs when insulation frays or faults allow live and neutral wires to make direct physical contact, lowering circuit resistance and producing an immediate surge of high current.\r\n- Overloading: Occurs when too many high-power electrical appliances (heaters, air conditioners, geysers) are switched on simultaneously on a single circuit socket, drawing current beyond the rated capacity of the supply wires.\r\n(b) Safety Fuse: Contains a fuse wire made of an alloy with high resistivity and low melting point. When excess current passes through, Joule heating (H = I^2*R*t) melts the wire, opening the circuit and stopping current flow.\r\n(c) Electrical Earthing: Provides a low-resistance path from metallic appliance casings directly into the ground. Any current leaking to the metal chassis flows harmlessly to earth, tripping circuit breakers and protecting users from electric shocks.	t	2020	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SST_2020_AI_01	SS10_HIS_01	\N	Standard (041)	Who said: 'When France sneezes, the rest of Europe catches cold'?\r\n(A) Giuseppe Mazzini\r\n(B) Duke Metternich\r\n(C) Otto von Bismarck\r\n(D) Napoleon Bonaparte	{"answer": "1 Mark for Option (B)"}	1.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	22dc87fd1517badbdb9153ff2b976924c7055c242d34193c7c5cd6fed715a73e	[]	Correct Option: (B)\r\nAustrian Chancellor Duke Metternich made this remark regarding the spread of revolutionary ideas from France across European monarchies.	t	2020	All India Set 1	f	SST	All India
