SCI_2024_D_Q05	S10_CH01	S10_CH01	Standard (041)	Write a balanced chemical equation with physical state symbols for the reaction between quicklime and water. Classify it as exothermic or endothermic.	{"eq": "1 Mark for balanced equation with states", "type": "1 Mark for exothermic classification and reasoning"}	2.0	Board Exam	Procedural	Board Standard	16c1629387b39ad18dc60a4bc53efe8274a14c36a9a680b21198071deab49116	["CBSE 2024 • Delhi (Delhi Set 1)"]	CaO(s) + H2O(l) -> Ca(OH)2(aq) + Heat\nExothermic: A substantial amount of thermal energy is liberated, causing the solution to boil.	t	2024	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
SCI_2024_D_Q06	S10_CH07	S10_CH07	Standard (041)	Name the plant hormones responsible for: (a) Phototropic curvature in shoots, (b) Stomatal closure during drought stress.	{"a": "1 Mark for Auxin", "b": "1 Mark for Abscisic Acid"}	2.0	Board Exam	Procedural	Board Standard	022650f9b4a634733fa61ae3b976b57139b5bd46c230b535a81890262692d04d	["CBSE 2024 • Delhi (Delhi Set 1)"]	(a) Auxin: Synthesized at shoot tips; diffuses to shaded side promoting cell elongation.\n(b) Abscisic Acid (ABA): Natural growth inhibitor that closes stomata during water stress.	t	2024	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
SCI_2024_D_Q07	S10_CH10	S10_CH10	Standard (041)	A ray of light traveling in air enters obliquely into water. Does the ray bend towards the normal or away? Give the physical reason.	{"reason": "1 Mark for optical density and speed reduction", "direction": "1 Mark for towards normal"}	2.0	Board Exam	Procedural	Board Standard	c2a8226bc8c754a737ce71f063d49a8887f8f3c23e1439d7b7e9a864a20fc2a8	["CBSE 2024 • Delhi (Delhi Set 1)"]	The ray bends TOWARDS the normal.\nReason: Water is optically denser than air (n_water = 1.33 > n_air = 1.00). Light slows down when entering water, causing the wavefront to bend towards the normal.	t	2024	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
SCI_2024_D_Q08	S10_CH02	S10_CH02	Standard (041)	Explain why Plaster of Paris must be stored in a moisture-proof container. Write the chemical equation for its reaction with water and state one medical use.	{"eq": "1 Mark for balanced rehydration", "use": "1 Mark for medical use", "reason": "1 Mark for storage reason"}	3.0	Board Exam	Procedural	Board Standard	12ccde38901b19428afd82f07e0ba545aae633bd4ba81f7d67d2326ac9876d9f	["CBSE 2024 • Delhi (Delhi Set 1)"]	1. Storage: POP absorbs atmospheric moisture and sets into hard crystalline gypsum, losing its molding capability.\n2. Equation: CaSO4·1/2H2O + 1.5H2O -> CaSO4·2H2O (Gypsum).\n3. Medical Use: Used to immobilize and align fractured bones.	t	2024	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
SCI_2024_D_Q09	S10_CH06	S10_CH06	Standard (041)	Trace the three cellular metabolic pathways by which pyruvate is broken down to release energy in living cells.	{"path1": "1 Mark for anaerobic fermentation", "path2": "1 Mark for lactic acid pathway", "path3": "1 Mark for aerobic oxidation"}	3.0	Board Exam	Procedural	Board Standard	6d8f2c6b997d4e4366145523b0b73463d58efb006e7f1fe3fe1c3455e9484baa	["CBSE 2024 • Delhi (Delhi Set 1)"]	1. In Yeast (Absence of O2): Pyruvate -> Ethanol (2C) + CO2 + 2 ATP.\n2. In Muscle cells (Lack of O2): Pyruvate -> Lactic Acid (3C) + 2 ATP (causes muscle cramps).\n3. In Mitochondria (Presence of O2): Pyruvate + O2 -> 6CO2 + 6H2O + 38 ATP.	t	2024	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
SCI_2024_D_Q10	S10_CH10	S10_CH10	Standard (041)	An object 4 cm in size is placed 25 cm in front of a concave mirror of focal length 15 cm. Find the distance from the mirror at which a screen should be placed to obtain a sharp image, and calculate image size.	{"v": "1 Mark for v = -37.5 cm", "size": "1 Mark for h' = -6 cm & nature", "mirror_formula": "1 Mark for formula & substitution"}	3.0	Board Exam	Procedural	Board Standard	d34ae55e2cac822a03d84f4e796f942b94b7c8e799ef4ff0ce244e5cd9286434	["CBSE 2024 • Delhi (Delhi Set 1)"]	u = -25 cm, f = -15 cm, h = +4 cm.\n1/v = 1/f - 1/u = 1/(-15) - 1/(-25) = -2/75 -> v = -37.5 cm.\nm = -v/u = -(-37.5)/(-25) = -1.5.\nh' = m * h = -1.5 * 4 cm = -6 cm.\nScreen placed at 37.5 cm in front of mirror; image is real, inverted, magnified (-6 cm).	t	2024	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
SCI_2024_D_Q11	S10_CH12	S10_CH12	Standard (041)	State Joule's law of heating. Derive H = I^2 R t. Calculate the heat generated by a 20-ohm electric heater drawing 5 A for 30 seconds.	{"calc": "1.5 Marks for 15,000 J with correct units", "law_deriv": "1.5 Marks for statement and derivation"}	3.0	Board Exam	Procedural	Board Standard	d2941d90c5f5418d8046582c78f7872ed4148194ec61927a55041773afec86c1	["CBSE 2024 • Delhi (Delhi Set 1)"]	1. Joule's Law: Heat produced in a resistor is directly proportional to I^2, R, and t.\n2. Derivation: W = V * Q = V * I * t. By Ohm's law V = IR, so W = (IR) * I * t = I^2 R t = H.\n3. Calculation: H = (5)^2 * 20 * 30 = 25 * 600 = 15,000 Joules (15 kJ).	t	2024	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
SCI_2024_D_Q12	S10_CH15	S10_CH15	Standard (041)	If 20,000 J of solar energy falls on green plant foliage, calculate the energy available to a hawk at the 4th trophic level (Grass -> Grasshopper -> Frog -> Hawk) using Lindeman's 10% law.	{"step1": "1 Mark for 1% solar capture (200 J)", "step2_4": "2 Marks for applying 10% consecutively to get 0.2 J"}	3.0	Board Exam	Procedural	Board Standard	dd136b285c8e23ed14ed76bc6826dfe48bf9751e58fe7b32b98c6cf6dbdb559f	["CBSE 2024 • Delhi (Delhi Set 1)"]	1. Plants (T1): Capture 1% of solar energy = 1% of 20,000 J = 200 J.\n2. Grasshopper (T2): 10% of 200 J = 20 J.\n3. Frog (T3): 10% of 20 J = 2 J.\n4. Hawk (T4): 10% of 2 J = 0.2 Joules.	t	2024	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
SCI_2024_D_Q13	S10_CH04	S10_CH04	Standard (041)	(a) Explain the mechanism of micelle formation during the cleansing action of soap.\n(b) Differentiate between ethanol and ethanoic acid using a chemical test. Write the chemical equation.	{"test": "2 Marks for test and balanced equation", "micelle": "3 Marks for structure and mechanism"}	5.0	Board Exam	Procedural	Board Standard	fa5a80ae5002c9e665302fa057c9d66a93b4960397c460ccb7432f264bbf2893	["CBSE 2024 • Delhi (Delhi Set 1)"]	(a) Soap molecule (C17H35COONa) has a polar hydrophilic ionic head (-COO-Na+) and a non-polar hydrophobic hydrocarbon tail. In water, tails dissolve into the central oily dirt droplet while heads point outward into water, forming a spherical micelle that stays suspended as an emulsion and is rinsed away.\n(b) Add NaHCO3 solution: Ethanoic acid produces brisk effervescence of CO2 gas that turns lime water milky. Ethanol does not react.\nCH3COOH + NaHCO3 -> CH3COONa + H2O + CO2	t	2024	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
SCI_2024_D_Q14	S10_CH08	S10_CH08	Standard (041)	(a) Explain the events of double fertilization and triple fusion in flowering plants.\n(b) What are the structural and functional differences between self-pollination and cross-pollination?	{"part_a": "3 Marks for syngamy and triple fusion", "part_b": "2 Marks for 2 valid distinctions"}	5.0	Board Exam	Procedural	Board Standard	397bb86fb0e8e0456e54694722c625e274a01c62f670e3fb51a77af51b6480f2	["CBSE 2024 • Delhi (Delhi Set 1)"]	(a) Pollen tube releases two male gametes into the embryo sac:\n1. Syngamy: 1st male gamete (n) + egg cell (n) -> 2n Zygote (develops into embryo).\n2. Triple Fusion: 2nd male gamete (n) + 2 polar nuclei (2n) -> 3n Primary Endosperm Nucleus (develops into nutrient endosperm).\nTwo fertilizations occur simultaneously -> Double Fertilization.\n(b) Self-pollination transfers pollen from anther to stigma of same flower or same plant; cross-pollination transfers pollen between different plants of the same species, requiring external agents (wind, insects) and introducing genetic variation.	t	2024	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
SCI_2024_D_Q15	S10_CH13	S10_CH13	Standard (041)	(a) State Fleming's Left-Hand Rule.\n(b) Describe the magnetic field pattern produced inside and outside a current-carrying solenoid with key traits.\n(c) What is the function of the earth wire in domestic electrical installations?	{"rule": "2 Marks for rule", "earth": "1 Mark for safety function", "solenoid": "2 Marks for field traits"}	5.0	Board Exam	Procedural	Board Standard	9b394c2e5aa98885811df90fa312e6b93046a692c4a573dad8f48ce210c2f4c3	["CBSE 2024 • Delhi (Delhi Set 1)"]	(a) Stretch thumb, forefinger, and middle finger mutually perpendicular: Forefinger points in magnetic field direction, middle finger in current direction, and thumb indicates mechanical force/motion on the conductor.\n(b) Inside: Field lines are straight, parallel, and equidistant (uniform magnetic field). Outside: Resembles a magnetic dipole / bar magnet emerging from North and entering South.\n(c) Earth wire provides a low-resistance path to ground for leakage currents from metallic appliance bodies, preventing electric shocks by tripping the circuit fuse/MCB.	t	2024	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
SST_2024_D_Q01	SS10_HIS_01	SS10_HIS_01	Standard (041)	Which treaty recognized Greece as an independent sovereign nation?\n(A) Treaty of Vienna of 1815\n(B) Treaty of Constantinople of 1832\n(C) Treaty of Versailles of 1871\n(D) Treaty of Paris of 1856	{"answer": "1 Mark for option (B)"}	1.0	Board Exam	Procedural	Board Standard	45dca6a3d7f9d417226d163257d64203b6d990ddc3041e6caee93e36393aa61f	["CBSE 2024 • Delhi (Delhi Set 1)"]	Correct Option: (B)\nThe Treaty of Constantinople of 1832 formally recognized Greece as an independent nation.	t	2024	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SST_2024_D_Q02	SS10_HIS_02	SS10_HIS_02	Standard (041)	Under which colonial legislation were plantation workers in Assam prohibited from leaving tea gardens without permission?\n(A) Rowlatt Act of 1919\n(B) Vernacular Press Act of 1878\n(C) Inland Emigration Act of 1859\n(D) Arms Act of 1878	{"answer": "1 Mark for option (C)"}	1.0	Board Exam	Procedural	Board Standard	d21d366fdb96443efa98422f19aa6c52a288345fdfa97aca1583c6712246a82b	["CBSE 2024 • Delhi (Delhi Set 1)"]	Correct Option: (C)\nThe Inland Emigration Act of 1859 prevented tea garden workers from leaving plantations without authorization.	t	2024	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SST_2024_D_Q03	SS10_GEO_01	SS10_GEO_01	Standard (041)	Which soil develops deep cracks during hot weather, aiding in proper soil aeration?\n(A) Alluvial Soil\n(B) Black Soil\n(C) Laterite Soil\n(D) Red Soil	{"answer": "1 Mark for option (B)"}	1.0	Board Exam	Procedural	Board Standard	37ac89c76578e9e991a671cb5388abbe254feca09db24201d2c840d303bbe100	["CBSE 2024 • Delhi (Delhi Set 1)"]	Correct Option: (B)\nBlack soil (Regur) has high clay content and develops deep fissures when dried, facilitating aeration.	t	2024	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SST_2024_D_Q04	SS10_POL_01	SS10_POL_01	Standard (041)	A system of 'checks and balances' is an essential characteristic of:\n(A) Horizontal division of power\n(B) Vertical federal division of power\n(C) Majoritarian unitary governance\n(D) Community Government	{"answer": "1 Mark for option (A)"}	1.0	Board Exam	Procedural	Board Standard	28bcc2be7008d92ff45c11c0262e52f85d1c7ef0670084c2d733f8c72ad2a88c	["CBSE 2024 • Delhi (Delhi Set 1)"]	Correct Option: (A)\nHorizontal power sharing places the Legislature, Executive, and Judiciary at the same level so they check each other.	t	2024	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SST_2024_D_Q05	SS10_ECO_01	SS10_ECO_01	Standard (041)	Why do different persons have different notions of development? Explain with two suitable real-life examples.	{"examples": "1 Mark for two valid contrasting examples", "explanation": "1 Mark for developmental divergence reason"}	2.0	Board Exam	Procedural	Board Standard	d623e189d0802f688d10c1aaf81190db19d6ddbfd4df5b529931831e991962c8	["CBSE 2024 • Delhi (Delhi Set 1)"]	People have different developmental goals because their life situations and aspirations differ:\n1. An industrialist may want high dams to generate electricity, while displaced tribals desire check-dams to preserve farmland.\n2. An educated unemployed youth desires a white-collar job, while a landless rural laborer seeks higher daily wages and local work.	t	2024	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SCI_2018_AI_03	S10_CH03	\N	Standard (041)	An element reacts with oxygen to give a compound with a high melting point. This compound is also soluble in water. The element is likely to be:\r\n(A) Calcium\r\n(B) Carbon\r\n(C) Silicon\r\n(D) Iron	{"answer": "1 Mark for Option (A)"}	1.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	e217d23ccd2941ca6ed8066ca3b77f30fb5dd70d7d036d7198c81e0f928bc2a8	[]	Correct Option: (A)\r\nCalcium (Ca) forms calcium oxide (CaO), an ionic compound with a high melting point that dissolves in water to form slaked lime: Ca(OH)2.	t	2018	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SST_2024_D_Q06	SS10_POL_02	SS10_POL_02	Standard (041)	Distinguish between 'Coming Together' and 'Holding Together' federations with one country example for each.	{"coming": "1 Mark for Coming Together definition and example", "holding": "1 Mark for Holding Together definition and example"}	2.0	Board Exam	Procedural	Board Standard	b7295c7c4b6d8d8bbb593987c5b6465bd7e9b5fa426b7374f3d5b034723fda72	["CBSE 2024 • Delhi (Delhi Set 1)"]	1. Coming Together: Independent states join to form a larger union to pool sovereignty and retain equal power (e.g., USA, Australia).\n2. Holding Together: A large nation divides governance between central and regional authorities, giving more power to the center (e.g., India, Spain, Belgium).	t	2024	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SST_2024_D_Q07	SS10_GEO_02	SS10_GEO_02	Standard (041)	Name the two most important beverage crops grown in India. State the climatic conditions required for the growth of tea.	{"crops": "1 Mark for Tea and Coffee", "climate": "1 Mark for temperature and rainfall requirements"}	2.0	Board Exam	Procedural	Board Standard	e6f5233929caa1927c0079c46170984380b2a6dc4ffda494adac8429b47d9ede	["CBSE 2024 • Delhi (Delhi Set 1)"]	1. Two beverage crops: Tea and Coffee.\n2. Tea climatic requirements: Warm, humid, frost-free climate throughout the year; abundant rainfall (150–300 cm) evenly distributed, and deep, well-drained loamy soil rich in humus.	t	2024	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SST_2024_D_Q08	SS10_HIS_01	SS10_HIS_01	Standard (041)	Explain the role played by Giuseppe Mazzini, Count Cavour, and Giuseppe Garibaldi in the unification of Italy.	{"cavour": "1 Mark", "mazzini": "1 Mark", "garibaldi": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	Procedural	Board Standard	3bf444608eb62dcf0fd3cdcec0207e25219614f60ab6adb851847f9324a35265	["CBSE 2024 • Delhi (Delhi Set 1)"]	1. Giuseppe Mazzini (The Ideologue): Founded 'Young Italy' to promote a unified democratic Italian republic.\n2. Count Camillo de Cavour (The Diplomat): Prime Minister of Sardinia-Piedmont who formed an alliance with France to defeat Austria in 1859, unifying northern Italy.\n3. Giuseppe Garibaldi (The Sword): Led the 'Red Shirts' in the Expedition of the Thousand in 1860, liberating southern Italy.	t	2024	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SST_2024_D_Q09	SS10_ECO_02	SS10_ECO_02	Standard (041)	What is disguised unemployment? Why is it prevalent in the agricultural sector of India? Suggest one remedy.	{"def": "1 Mark for definition", "reason": "1 Mark for rural context", "remedy": "1 Mark for valid solution"}	3.0	Board Exam	Procedural	Board Standard	d4291c6d522f93a1e7ab9c3a91946e6c17a47cedee7fea3224b9ddb0d5c7d6f9	["CBSE 2024 • Delhi (Delhi Set 1)"]	1. Definition: A situation where more people work on a farm than needed; removing surplus workers does not reduce total agricultural output.\n2. Reason: Lack of non-farm employment in rural areas forces all family members to work on small, fragmented plots.\n3. Remedy: Establish agro-processing industries, rural transport infrastructure, and promote rural tourism.	t	2024	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SST_2024_D_Q10	SS10_POL_03	SS10_POL_03	Standard (041)	State three constitutional provisions that make India a secular state.	{"point1": "1 Mark", "point2": "1 Mark", "point3": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	Procedural	Board Standard	8b15d1896129ed42b7f37abb362f9fc435a2cac8de92604e9eec8df52763e463	["CBSE 2024 • Delhi (Delhi Set 1)"]	1. No Official Religion: The Indian Constitution does not grant official status to any single religion.\n2. Freedom of Religion: Article 25 guarantees all citizens the right to profess, practice, and propagate any religion.\n3. Non-Discrimination: Article 15 prohibits discrimination on grounds of religion in public employment and access.	t	2024	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SST_2024_D_Q11	SS10_ECO_03	SS10_ECO_03	Standard (041)	Describe the organization and working mechanism of Self-Help Groups (SHGs) for rural women.	{"org": "1 Mark", "bank": "1 Mark", "loans": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	Procedural	Board Standard	cc024c3d328a87cada35aaafc516fa67f62be0b39c3bf175e19c45aea7482165	["CBSE 2024 • Delhi (Delhi Set 1)"]	1. Organization: Consists of 15–20 members from a neighborhood who contribute monthly savings (Rs. 25–100 per member).\n2. Internal Lending: Members take small short-term loans from pooled savings at modest interest rates without collateral.\n3. Bank Linkage: After 1–2 years of consistent savings, commercial banks sanction formal collateral-free loans to the group.	t	2024	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SST_2024_D_Q12	SS10_GEO_06	SS10_GEO_06	Standard (041)	Why is the iron and steel industry concentrated mainly in the Chota Nagpur plateau region?	{"power": "1 Mark", "logistics": "1 Mark", "raw_materials": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	Procedural	Board Standard	695a59304845ae34083d1249635084239e4d898c6fcb0411a50ec060e1197737	["CBSE 2024 • Delhi (Delhi Set 1)"]	1. Raw Material Proximity: High-grade hematite iron ore, coking coal (Jharia, Bokaro), and limestone flux are available in close proximity.\n2. Water & Power: Damodar Valley network provides reliable cooling water and thermal power.\n3. Transport & Labor: Dense railway network and low-cost labor from Bihar, Jharkhand, and Odisha support heavy operations.	t	2024	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SST_2024_D_Q13	SS10_HIS_02	SS10_HIS_02	Standard (041)	How did the Non-Cooperation Movement unfold in cities, countryside, and plantations? Why did Mahatma Gandhi call off the movement in 1922?	{"cities": "1 Mark", "countryside": "1 Mark", "plantations": "1 Mark", "chauri_chaura": "2 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	Procedural	Board Standard	375518efa67e1d64bdebf5d010f04787ddd6960bea6a58f2c05316bec8362b99	["CBSE 2024 • Delhi (Delhi Set 1)"]	1. Cities: Middle-class boycott of British schools, courts, and foreign cloth. Picketing of liquor shops.\n2. Countryside: Peasants in Awadh under Baba Ramchandra protested begar (unpaid forced labor) and talukdar rents; forest dwellers in Andhra protested grazing bans under Alluri Sitaram Raju.\n3. Plantations: Assam workers defied the 1859 Inland Emigration Act to leave plantations and return home.\n4. Withdrawal: In February 1922, a violent mob burned a police station at Chauri Chaura, killing 22 policemen. Gandhi called off the movement because non-violent discipline was breached.	t	2024	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SST_2024_D_Q14	SS10_POL_02	SS10_POL_02	Standard (041)	Describe the key constitutional provisions introduced by the 73rd and 74th Amendments of 1992 to make decentralization effective in India.	{"five_points": "5 Marks (1 Mark for each distinct amendment clause under headings)"}	5.0	Board Exam	Procedural	Board Standard	66490ee5155945d8f0278ea086a71e4d6fcdaa0c608090f47a57281c6f035caa	["CBSE 2024 • Delhi (Delhi Set 1)"]	1. Mandatory Regular Elections: Elections to local bodies every five years are constitutionally compulsory.\n2. Social Group Reservations: Reserved seats for Scheduled Castes and Scheduled Tribes proportional to population.\n3. Women's Reservation: At least one-third (33%) of all seats and chairperson positions are reserved for women.\n4. State Election Commission: Independent state commissions conduct transparent local elections.\n5. Revenue Sharing: State governments must share revenues and powers with local bodies via State Finance Commissions.	t	2024	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SST_2024_D_Q15	SS10_ECO_04	SS10_ECO_04	Standard (041)	What are Multinational Corporations (MNCs)? Explain the three major ways through which MNCs control production in other nations.	{"def": "1 Mark for definition", "three_ways": "4 Marks (1.33 Marks per strategy with example)"}	5.0	Board Exam	Procedural	Board Standard	0f027c1a3d9a6ea67971ff94fab7596ff232bcfb297f4183cedadccf3898cb0a	["CBSE 2024 • Delhi (Delhi Set 1)"]	1. Definition: An enterprise that owns or controls production in more than one nation.\n2. Three Ways of Control:\n- Buying Local Companies: Acquiring established domestic companies to access existing distribution networks (e.g., Cargill Foods purchasing Parakh Foods).\n- Joint Ventures: Partnering with domestic firms to supply capital and advanced machinery (e.g., Ford Motors partnering with Mahindra).\n- Placing Contracts: Subcontracting production to decentralized local manufacturers (garments, footwear, toys) and selling under global brand names.	t	2024	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SST_2020_AI_08	SS10_HIS_01	\N	Standard (041)	Explain the role played by women in the nationalist movements and liberal revolutions in 19th-century Europe.	{"suffrage": "1 Mark", "frankfurt": "1 Mark", "organizations": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	8a3fb5609ccde28c6ed070947e5586cd3db09899241e9cac624916e6ab4fd0ea	[]	1. Active Organizational Participation: Women formed political associations, founded newspapers, and participated in mass rallies and political demonstrations.\r\n2. Frankurt Parliament Mobilization: During the 1848 Frankfurt Assembly at St. Paul's Church, women participated actively, though they were admitted to the visitors' gallery only as observers.\r\n3. Suffrage Agitation: Despite participating in nationalist struggles, women were denied equal suffrage rights and had to mount separate feminist suffrage movements across Europe.	t	2020	All India Set 1	f	SST	All India
SST_2020_AI_09	SS10_ECO_02	\N	Standard (041)	'The unorganized sector leaves workers vulnerable to exploitation.' Justify this statement with three arguments.	{"wages": "1 Mark", "benefits": "1 Mark", "security": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	4238395308b4e86ec19eac279e3708bcca9a39df544a95dbd797cd1471b2e9c2	[]	1. Low Wages & Irregular Remuneration: Wages are low and often lower than statutory minimum wage limits, with no overtime compensation.\r\n2. Absence of Job Security: Employment is informal; workers can be terminated without notice or severance pay during slow seasons.\r\n3. Lack of Social Security: No provision for paid leave, medical benefits, pensions, or safe working environments.	t	2020	All India Set 1	f	SST	All India
SST_2020_AI_10	SS10_POL_03	\N	Standard (041)	Why is religion alone not a viable basis for political mobilization in a democracy? State three reasons.	{"distraction": "1 Mark", "polarization": "1 Mark", "heterogeneity": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	d6d8c79373d3aa4965ffe7f8099511354d5c4ffad760b510f8490c2f0741c9ce	[]	1. Social Division: Organizing politics around religion polarizes citizens and fuels inter-communal conflict.\r\n2. Internal Diversity: Religious communities are not homogeneous; internal caste, class, gender, and linguistic differences mean interests vary within the same faith.\r\n3. Compromises Core Governance: Emphasizing religious identities diverts democratic attention from economic development, education, and healthcare.	t	2020	All India Set 1	f	SST	All India
SST_2020_AI_11	SS10_ECO_03	\N	Standard (041)	Why do poor rural households still depend heavily on informal sources of credit despite the presence of commercial banks? State three reasons.	{"absence": "1 Mark", "collateral": "1 Mark", "flexibility": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	61666bd0780bbae9ac02c66161463cc72a14d8b2f87d7f2cbb5e5def7a446dfc	[]	1. Lack of Collateral: Commercial banks require tangible assets (land titles, house deeds) as loan security, which poor rural households typically do not possess.\r\n2. Absence of Banking Infrastructure: Rural branches remain absent in remote villages, whereas local moneylenders are physically accessible.\r\n3. Flexible Lending: Moneylenders advance loans on personal familiarity without demanding income paperwork, and permit repayment roll-overs without immediate asset foreclosures.	t	2020	All India Set 1	f	SST	All India
SST_2020_AI_12	SS10_GEO_06	\N	Standard (041)	Explain the importance of the manufacturing sector as the backbone of economic development in India.	{"trade": "1 Mark", "employment": "1 Mark", "agriculture": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	173bd883e31f273ba5cbd2b4b6e21270fb4a38ee29bd84ea3ed54c5107a559a6	[]	1. Modernizing Agriculture: Manufactures tractors, harvesters, tube-wells, and chemical fertilizers that raise agricultural productivity.\r\n2. Reducing Employment Dependence: Creates secondary and tertiary sector employment, absorbing surplus labor from agriculture.\r\n3. Expanding Trade & Foreign Exchange: Exporting manufactured goods broadens trade horizons and brings in foreign exchange revenue.	t	2020	All India Set 1	f	SST	All India
SST_2020_AI_13	SS10_HIS_02	\N	Standard (041)	Explain the differences between the Non-Cooperation Movement (1920–1922) and the Civil Disobedience Movement (1930–1934) in terms of objectives, methods, and mass participation.	{"methods": "1.5 Marks", "objectives": "2 Marks", "participation": "1.5 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	43f8717dcd181f1a0f55b7910877833ea20155e090803457d7ff3eb4a02a16d9	[]	1. Objectives:\r\n- Non-Cooperation sought redressing of the Punjab and Khilafat wrongs along with Swaraj within the Empire by withdrawing cooperation from British institutions.\r\n- Civil Disobedience sought complete national independence ("Purna Swaraj") and explicitly demanded the defiance and breaking of colonial laws.\r\n2. Methods:\r\n- Non-Cooperation focused on the passive surrender of titles, boycotts of schools, courts, and foreign goods, and picketing of liquor shops.\r\n- Civil Disobedience involved active violation of laws: manufacturing salt at Dandi, refusing to pay land revenue and chaukidari taxes, and entering reserved forests.\r\n3. Mass Participation:\r\n- Non-Cooperation saw large-scale middle-class and Muslim participation under the Congress-Khilafat alliance.\r\n- Civil Disobedience saw higher participation from business elites and women who picketed salt depots, while Muslim participation was comparatively lower due to earlier communal distance.	t	2020	All India Set 1	f	SST	All India
SST_2020_AI_14	SS10_POL_02	\N	Standard (041)	'The real success of federalism in India is attributed to the nature of democratic politics in our country.' Examine this statement.	{"five_arguments": "5 Marks (1 Mark per distinct argument)"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	463cf92bb6d8dafe45cf84bf6be6f76ce3315625c3d1b299c1b930cf22bc273d	[]	1. Shared Democratic Values: Federalism succeeded in India because respect for regional diversity, linguistic accommodation, and the desire to live together became shared political ideals.\r\n2. Creation of Linguistic States: Accommodating linguistic boundaries (e.g., Telugu, Kannada, Marathi states) prevented Balkanization and strengthened cohesion.\r\n3. Balanced Language Policy: Avoiding the imposition of Hindi on non-Hindi states preserved inter-state cooperation.\r\n4. Coalition Governance: The post-1990 era of coalition governments ended single-party dominance and fostered power-sharing between regional and national parties.\r\n5. Independent Judicial Review: An autonomous Supreme Court mediated center-state disputes without bias.	t	2020	All India Set 1	f	SST	All India
SCI_2024_AI_01	S10_CH01	\N	Standard (041)	When aqueous solutions of barium chloride and sodium sulphate are mixed together, what is observed?\r\n(A) A white precipitate of barium sulphate is formed\r\n(B) A yellow precipitate of lead iodide is formed\r\n(C) A pungent suffocating gas is evolved\r\n(D) The solution turns dark blue	{"answer": "1 Mark for Option (A)"}	1.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	8777cda7e5a1f3474e25e2375136c05e9b5bff44eec76f3391f514bfac406132	[]	Correct Option: (A)\r\nBaCl2(aq) + Na2SO4(aq) -> BaSO4(s) [White precipitate] + 2NaCl(aq).	t	2024	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
