SCI_2024_AI_05	S10_CH07	\N	Standard (041)	Trace the sequence of events that occur when a bright light is focused on human eyes. Name the pathway involved.	{"pathway": "0.5 Mark", "sequence": "1.5 Marks"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	ca4de950be22583107ac15c67c7ffc304af4780a6264a5cdb9df2920058ffa12	[]	Sequence: Receptor (Photoreceptors in Retina) -> Sensory Neuron -> Brain (Midbrain/Relay Center) -> Motor Neuron -> Effector (Iris circular muscles contract to constrict pupil). Pathway: Pupillary Reflex Arc.	t	2024	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2024_AI_06	S10_CH08	\N	Standard (041)	Name the primary male and female sex hormones in humans. State one secondary sexual characteristic regulated by each.	{"male": "1 Mark", "female": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	a06860816c16bb2198df73ac7097b2c31c5986ff04b4baffba38eebdf7fd4a90	[]	1. Male: Testosterone (stimulates facial/body hair growth, deepening of voice).\r\n2. Female: Estrogen (regulates breast development, onset of menstrual cycle).	t	2024	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2024_AI_07	S10_CH09	\N	Standard (041)	Why do Mendel monohybrid crosses in garden pea plants result in only tall plants in F1 generation, but both tall and dwarf plants in F2?	{"f1": "1 Mark for dominance", "f2": "1 Mark for segregation"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	ba9a522acfd66bfc6ad47b735ed1db23da479f7373f9734ce1a056ff2f8eeaca	[]	In F1 (Tt), the dominant tall allele (T) completely masks the recessive dwarf allele (t) according to the Law of Dominance. In F2, self-pollination (Tt x Tt) allows recessive alleles to pair (tt) with a 25% probability, expressing the dwarf phenotype.	t	2024	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2024_AI_08	S10_CH10	\N	Standard (041)	A convex lens of focal length 20 cm produces a real, inverted image at a distance of 40 cm from the optical center. Find the object distance and magnification.	{"m": "1 Mark", "u": "1 Mark", "formula": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	e6d61c6c616230bca202a723e6ca77ba163eb80de23181350d60d34786dfbc16	[]	Given: f = +20 cm, v = +40 cm.\r\nUsing lens formula: 1/u = 1/v - 1/f = 1/40 - 1/20 = -1/40 -> u = -40 cm.\r\nMagnification m = v / u = +40 / (-40) = -1.0.\r\nObject is at 2F1 (40 cm in front), forming a real, inverted image of the same size at 2F2.	t	2024	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2024_AI_09	S10_CH11	\N	Standard (041)	A person cannot see objects clearly beyond 2 meters. Identify the eye defect, state two causes, and determine the power of corrective lens required.	{"power": "1.5 Marks", "name_causes": "1.5 Marks"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	7d490a33983ebca82fe1627b6c56624df0d8bc4d41bd67cc152ca829b726606e	[]	1. Defect: Myopia (Near-sightedness).\r\n2. Causes: (i) Excessive curvature of eye lens, (ii) Elongation of the eyeball.\r\n3. Lens prescription: u = -infinity, v = -2 m -> f = -2 m.\r\nPower P = 1/f = 1/(-2) = -0.5 D (Concave lens of power -0.5 D).	t	2024	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2024_AI_10	S10_CH12	\N	Standard (041)	Three resistors of 4 ohms, 6 ohms, and 12 ohms are connected in parallel across a 6 V battery. Calculate: (a) equivalent circuit resistance, (b) total circuit current.	{"i": "1.5 Marks for 3 A", "rp": "1.5 Marks for 2 ohms"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	237eb2d5659985643fe96c5069b20f73e9861dd0269173754c76a06dee84ced6	[]	(a) 1/Rp = 1/4 + 1/6 + 1/12 = (3 + 2 + 1)/12 = 6/12 = 1/2 -> Rp = 2 ohms.\r\n(b) Total current I = V / Rp = 6 V / 2 ohms = 3.0 Amperes.	t	2024	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2024_AI_11	S10_CH13	\N	Standard (041)	What is an electromagnet? How does it differ from a permanent magnet? State two methods to increase its magnetic strength.	{"methods": "1.5 Marks", "definition_diff": "1.5 Marks"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	d7aa9d2eb11d832e70decdb3f096bc182fbbe0c332a63160d673ca5a01ad4f3a	[]	1. Definition: A temporary magnet consisting of a soft iron core placed inside a current-carrying solenoid coil.\r\n2. Differences: Its magnetic field ceases when current is switched off, and its polarity can be reversed by reversing current direction.\r\n3. Increasing strength: (i) Increase the magnitude of electric current, (ii) Increase the number of turns in the solenoid coil.	t	2024	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2024_AI_12	S10_CH15	\N	Standard (041)	What is biological magnification? Explain with an aquatic food chain example how non-biodegradable pesticides reach maximum concentrations in apex consumers.	{"def": "1.5 Marks", "chain": "1.5 Marks"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	afe32d50263fbe73b8b8bd98028c307596921ab9ff0506e59034ec09fc61d26b	[]	1. Definition: The phenomenon where non-biodegradable toxic chemicals accumulate in progressively higher concentrations at each successive trophic level.\r\n2. Aquatic Example: Water (0.02 ppm) -> Phytoplankton (0.04 ppm) -> Small Fish (0.5 ppm) -> Large Fish (2.0 ppm) -> Fish-eating Bird (25 ppm).\r\nBecause these compounds cannot be metabolized or excreted by organisms, they concentrate in fatty tissues up the chain.	t	2024	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2018_AI_04	S10_CH06	\N	Standard (041)	In amoeba, food is ingested with the help of temporary finger-like extensions of the cell surface called:\r\n(A) Cilia\r\n(B) Pseudopodia\r\n(C) Flagella\r\n(D) Tentacles	{"answer": "1 Mark for Option (B)"}	1.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	7f0d33b11775fa94443a211e4dab2ba1af53033db61c94517cd18bc64a2bfdd8	[]	Correct Option: (B)\r\nAmoeba ingests food using pseudopodia, which fuse over the food particle forming a food-vacuole.	t	2018	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2024_AI_13	S10_CH03	\N	Standard (041)	(a) Differentiate between roasting and calcination with balanced equations using zinc ores.\r\n(b) How is refined copper obtained from blister copper by electrolytic refining? Name the anode, cathode, and electrolyte.	{"part_a": "2.5 Marks", "part_b": "2.5 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	4cf99990e2b31e8a013c0cf7bdde49e89c6fac93c719a5b1b9a1be4d2b601efd	[]	(a) Roasting: Heating sulphide ore in excess air: 2ZnS + 3O2 -> 2ZnO + 2SO2.\r\nCalcination: Heating carbonate ore in limited air: ZnCO3 -> ZnO + CO2.\r\n(b) Electrolytic Refining:\r\n- Anode: Impure blister copper block\r\n- Cathode: Pure thin strip of copper\r\n- Electrolyte: Acidified copper sulphate solution (CuSO4 + dilute H2SO4)\r\nPure copper dissolves from the anode and plates onto the cathode while anode impurities drop as anode mud.	t	2024	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2024_AI_14	S10_CH04	\N	Standard (041)	(a) What is an esterification reaction? Write the balanced equation for the synthesis of ethyl ethanoate.\r\n(b) What happens when an ester reacts with aqueous sodium hydroxide? Write the equation and state the common name of this reaction.\r\n(c) Explain why carbon forms covalent bonds rather than ionic bonds.	{"ester": "2 Marks", "covalent_oct": "1.5 Marks", "saponification": "1.5 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	d472095524783ae2c975244e33f8adbaf39f08eeb3f6aca69acfab3ad94702cb	[]	(a) Heating a carboxylic acid with an alcohol in the presence of conc. H2SO4 catalyst produces a sweet-smelling ester:\r\nCH3COOH + C2H5OH --(Conc H2SO4)--> CH3COOC2H5 + H2O.\r\n(b) Saponification: Alkaline hydrolysis breaks the ester down into sodium ethanoate (soap) and ethanol:\r\nCH3COOC2H5 + NaOH -> CH3COONa + C2H5OH.\r\n(c) Carbon cannot gain 4 electrons to form C4- because 6 nuclear protons cannot stably hold 10 electrons; it cannot lose 4 electrons to form C4+ due to prohibitive ionization enthalpy. Therefore, carbon achieves an octet by sharing electrons.	t	2024	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2024_AI_15	S10_CH06	\N	Standard (041)	(a) Explain the three physiological stages of urine formation inside a human nephron.\r\n(b) Differentiate between arteries and veins based on: (i) direction of blood flow, (ii) wall thickness, (iii) presence of valves.	{"nephron": "3 Marks", "circulatory": "2 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	dfdb1f88fa2e0a24f66adac7e01c980b8baceb772fc2d94fb45b5a75305e29fd	[]	(a) Stages:\r\n1. Glomerular Filtration: High blood pressure forces liquid plasma (water, glucose, urea, salts) across glomerulus walls into Bowman capsule lumen.\r\n2. Selective Reabsorption: Useful substances (glucose, amino acids, vital salts, water) are reabsorbed along the tubular nephron back into capillary blood.\r\n3. Tubular Secretion: Extra ions (H+, K+, creatinine) are actively discharged into collecting tubule fluid to form final urine.\r\n(b) Differences:\r\n1. Blood Flow: Arteries carry blood away from heart; veins return blood toward heart.\r\n2. Wall Thickness: Arteries possess thick, elastic muscular walls; veins have thin, less muscular walls.\r\n3. Valves: Arteries lack valves; veins contain internal pocket valves to prevent backward pooling.	t	2024	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SST_2024_AI_01	SS10_HIS_01	\N	Standard (041)	Who was proclaimed the German Emperor in a ceremony held at Versailles in January 1871?\r\n(A) Kaiser William I\r\n(B) Otto von Bismarck\r\n(C) Friedrich Wilhelm IV\r\n(D) King Victor Emmanuel II	{"answer": "1 Mark for Option (A)"}	1.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	735a213b8e96586fa5a0d983773c1138bb0fd936b8fb38d774f9f87442679cac	[]	Correct Option: (A)\r\nKaiser William I of Prussia was proclaimed German Emperor in the Hall of Mirrors at Versailles on January 18, 1871.	t	2024	All India Set 1	f	SST	All India
