SST_2024_AI_08	SS10_HIS_01	\N	Standard (041)	Explain the role of Otto von Bismarck in the unification of Germany.	{"wars": "1 Mark", "architect": "1 Mark", "culmination": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	5f0532c3f14d4809143d3489d881a9cd2c180f717a9fed117673e82cc4ace201	[]	1. Architect of Realpolitik: Prussian Chief Minister Otto von Bismarck orchestrated unification through the Prussian bureaucracy and military.\r\n2. Three Wars in Seven Years: Directed strategic wars against Denmark (1864), Austria (1866), and France (1870), defeating each.\r\n3. Proclamation: Concluded the process by proclaiming Prussian King William I as German Emperor in Versailles in 1871.	t	2024	All India Set 1	f	SST	All India
SST_2024_AI_09	SS10_ECO_02	\N	Standard (041)	Differentiate between organized and unorganized economic sectors in India based on: (i) employment terms, (ii) job security, (iii) government regulations.	{"rules": "1 Mark", "terms": "1 Mark", "security": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	69e7635e8c99d6a4b0afa85207b4565cf183dc896363437cc844b08f32acf8a5	[]	1. Employment Terms: Organized sector has fixed working hours and overtime pay; unorganized sector has long, erratic hours without overtime remuneration.\r\n2. Job Security: Organized employees enjoy formal appointment contracts and protection from arbitrary termination; unorganized workers face hire-and-fire insecurity.\r\n3. Regulations: Organized firms comply with the Factories Act and Minimum Wages Act; unorganized units operate outside registered government oversight.	t	2024	All India Set 1	f	SST	All India
SST_2024_AI_10	SS10_POL_03	\N	Standard (041)	How does gender division manifest in everyday Indian society? State three ways women face discrimination or disadvantage.	{"wages": "1 Mark", "carework": "1 Mark", "literacy": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	99be7f84b7920bc7449ed57e7c7327e8510087e1269030bca4ffc27415b48546	[]	1. Literacy Disparity: The male literacy rate (~82%) substantially exceeds the female rate (~65%), as families often prioritize boys higher education.\r\n2. Unpaid Care Work: Women perform substantial household and care labor that is economically unvalued and uncompensated.\r\n3. Unequal Wage Compensation: In agriculture, construction, and sports, female workers are frequently paid lower wages than men for identical work.	t	2024	All India Set 1	f	SST	All India
SST_2024_AI_11	SS10_ECO_03	\N	Standard (041)	Explain the role of the Reserve Bank of India (RBI) in supervising the commercial banking system in India.	{"crr": "1 Mark", "priority": "1 Mark", "reporting": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	1b1734553e6c5251f22b11ee1454704be8e0721dd3e539d271260caa045bf7c0	[]	1. Cash Reserve Supervision: RBI ensures banks maintain the mandatory minimum Cash Reserve Ratio (CRR) out of total deposits.\r\n2. Credit Distribution Oversight: Verifies that banks do not advance loans solely to profitable businesses, but also to small borrowers, marginal farmers, and small enterprises.\r\n3. Periodic Audits: Banks must periodically submit balance sheets and lending rates to the RBI for audit compliance.	t	2024	All India Set 1	f	SST	All India
SST_2024_AI_12	SS10_GEO_06	\N	Standard (041)	Why is the textile industry unique in the Indian economy? State three reasons.	{"forex": "1 Mark", "employment": "1 Mark", "selfreliance": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	4669f3c03e89295c7aed6d7e83d306b4d69960f6f2e17d1fd4997072c75e0bc3	[]	1. Self-Reliance: It is the only industrial sector completely self-reliant and value-chain complete—from raw fiber to finished garments.\r\n2. Industrial Output & Employment: Contributes significantly to industrial production and is the second-largest employer after agriculture.\r\n3. Foreign Exchange Earnings: Generates substantial foreign exchange earnings without needing imported primary raw materials.	t	2024	All India Set 1	f	SST	All India
SST_2024_AI_13	SS10_HIS_02	\N	Standard (041)	Describe the participation of rich peasants and poor peasants in the Civil Disobedience Movement. Why were the poor peasants reluctant to rejoin after 1931?	{"poor": "1.5 Marks", "rich": "2 Marks", "1931_gap": "1.5 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	25ceb590d91eff448f875bb7bc6fbecb0c4bd03af3dac0ec1a1f411aec8acc13	[]	1. Rich Peasants (Patidars of Gujarat and Jats of UP):\r\n- Commercial producers hit hard by trade depression and collapsing cash crop prices.\r\n- Could not pay fixed government land revenue, organizing boycotts as active Congress supporters.\r\n2. Poor Peasants (Small tenants and laborers):\r\n- Cultivated rented plots; collapsed crop prices prevented cash rent payments to landlords.\r\n- Sought rent remittance ("no-rent" campaigns) rather than solely revenue reductions.\r\n3. Disillusionment after 1931:\r\n- Congress was hesitant to support "no-rent" campaigns out of concern for alienating rich landlords.\r\n- When the movement was suspended in 1931 without revenue adjustments and resumed in 1932, poorer tenant participation dropped.	t	2024	All India Set 1	f	SST	All India
SST_2024_AI_14	SS10_POL_02	\N	Standard (041)	Explain the structure and functioning of the Panchayati Raj System in rural India across its three tiers.	{"gram": "2 Marks", "zilla": "1.5 Marks", "samiti": "1.5 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	dabd0454ff9c0a9eb7763fb5eb816173eed356b0bce481a79d77b1556c3a2388	[]	1. Gram Panchayat (Village Level):\r\n- Executive committee consisting of ward members (Panchs) and a village president (Sarpanch) directly elected by adult residents.\r\n- Directly accountable to the Gram Sabha (all adult voters), which reviews annual budgets and monitors work.\r\n2. Panchayat Samiti (Block Level):\r\n- Intermediate body formed by pooling several Gram Panchayats.\r\n- Members are elected by local representatives, Block Development Officers (BDO), and local legislators.\r\n3. Zilla Parishad (District Level):\r\n- Apex district-level governance body; coordinates developmental planning and state fund allocations across all blocks.\r\n- Chaired by an elected Zilla Parishad Chairperson, with district MPs, MLAs, and administrative heads as members.	t	2024	All India Set 1	f	SST	All India
SST_2024_AI_15	SS10_ECO_04	\N	Standard (041)	What are Special Economic Zones (SEZs)? State their primary objectives and explain three incentives offered to foreign companies to invest in them.	{"def_obj": "2 Marks", "incentives": "3 Marks (1 Mark each)"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	586324b1a4b056a491ffe9b1f2a7f6f540ae179d26e61c3c9eed75619d81ab8b	[]	1. Definition: Specially demarcated industrial enclaves designed to provide infrastructure and attract Foreign Direct Investment (FDI).\r\n2. Core Objectives: Boost exports, foster technology transfer, and expand domestic job opportunities.\r\n3. Three Incentives:\r\n- World-Class Physical Infrastructure: Continuous electricity, high-speed telecommunications, water supply, and direct highway/port connectivity.\r\n- Tax Holidays: Complete income-tax exemption on export earnings for the initial 5 operational years.\r\n- Labor Law Flexibility: Exemptions from standard bureaucratic labor regulations and simplified single-window environmental and corporate clearances.	t	2024	All India Set 1	f	SST	All India
SCI_2017_D_03	S10_CH03	\N	Standard (041)	Which of the following metals has such a low melting point that it melts when kept on the palm of your hand?\r\n(A) Sodium\r\n(B) Gallium\r\n(C) Potassium\r\n(D) Magnesium	{"answer": "1 Mark for Option (B)"}	1.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	fa228b406d56d5a772a6491ff33355f71b92334494ebb9ce2846b6eeec8956d3	[]	Correct Option: (B)\r\nGallium (and Caesium) has an extremely low melting point (~30°C, 303 K) and melts from human body heat when held on the palm.	t	2017	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
