SCI_2023_D_05	S10_CH01	\N	Standard (041)	Decomposition reactions require energy in the form of heat, light, or electricity to break bonds. Write one balanced equation for each form of energy.	{"equations": "2 Marks (1 Mark each for any two fully balanced reactions with reaction conditions)"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	20ddf76ae1a480a4757ad308b5445a0b8fa0430bd07ef64b19d422a934a69c02	[]	1. Thermal: CaCO3(s) --(Heat)--> CaO(s) + CO2(g)\r\n2. Photochemical: 2AgCl(s) --(Sunlight)--> 2Ag(s) + Cl2(g)\r\n3. Electrolytic: 2H2O(l) --(Electricity)--> 2H2(g) + O2(g)	t	2023	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
SCI_2023_D_06	S10_CH07	\N	Standard (041)	Distinguish between a sensory neuron and a motor neuron. State the role of each in a typical spinal reflex arc.	{"motor": "1 Mark", "sensory": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	efa48475a3e2a89af053b785fc28575735ded757b56739fbd9ea6fe3b5bac15a	[]	1. Sensory Neuron: Conducts sensory nerve impulses from peripheral receptors toward the central nervous system (spinal cord).\r\n2. Motor Neuron: Carries generated response impulses from the central nervous system to peripheral effector organs (muscles/glands).	t	2023	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
SCI_2023_D_07	S10_CH10	\N	Standard (041)	The absolute refractive indices of water and glass are 4/3 and 3/2 respectively. If light speed in glass is 2 x 10^8 m/s, compute light speed in: (i) vacuum, (ii) water.	{"water": "1 Mark", "vacuum": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	acf9bfda08c30781894f7ea788f4d3b67d0e8507b2dce0e0aa61e3047cae8a6a	[]	(i) n_glass = c / v_glass -> 3/2 = c / (2 x 10^8) -> c = 3.0 x 10^8 m/s.\r\n(ii) n_water = c / v_water -> 4/3 = (3.0 x 10^8) / v_water -> v_water = (9/4) x 10^8 = 2.25 x 10^8 m/s.	t	2023	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
SCI_2023_D_08	S10_CH02	\N	Standard (041)	(a) Write the chemical formula of bleaching powder.\r\n(b) Write the balanced equation for its commercial preparation.\r\n(c) State two practical applications of bleaching powder.	{"uses": "1 Mark", "formula": "0.5 Mark", "equation": "1.5 Marks"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	b32af534b8461e7daed59aac205f0ca3c80d9962fd06e2bd23af1fcb631c3ee3	[]	(a) Chemical formula: CaOCl2 (Calcium oxychloride).\r\n(b) Synthesis: Passing dry chlorine gas over dry slaked lime:\r\nCa(OH)2 + Cl2 -> CaOCl2 + H2O.\r\n(c) Applications: 1. Bleaching cellulose fibers in textile manufacturing; 2. Chemical disinfectant in civic water purification.	t	2023	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
SCI_2023_D_09	S10_CH06	\N	Standard (041)	Describe the mechanism of human kidney filtration under: (a) Glomerular filtration, (b) Selective tubular reabsorption.	{"filtration": "1.5 Marks", "reabsorption": "1.5 Marks"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	7bb61d7b1ed87c01204a64d1088c7124eae07ff06fa1cfd55518c5c7d35b8c3e	[]	(a) Glomerular Filtration: High hydraulic pressure across glomerulus capillaries forces water, mineral ions, glucose, amino acids, and urea into Bowman capsule lumen, filtering roughly 180 liters per day.\r\n(b) Selective Reabsorption: Along proximal convoluted tubules and Henle loops, glucose, amino acids, vital electrolytes, and 99% of water return to surrounding blood capillaries.	t	2023	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
SCI_2023_D_10	S10_CH10	\N	Standard (041)	An object 5 cm high is placed 20 cm from a converging lens of focal length 10 cm. Determine the image location, magnification, and image nature.	{"size": "1 Mark", "nature": "1 Mark", "position": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	3f35d98344bbd92d01cb113f14a7ce24a3ac36cb7141da8b838d0c523a40b558	[]	Given: h = +5 cm, u = -20 cm, f = +10 cm.\r\nUsing lens formula: 1/v - 1/u = 1/f -> 1/v = 1/10 + 1/(-20) = 1/20 -> v = +20 cm.\r\nMagnification: m = v / u = +20 / (-20) = -1.0.\r\nHeight: h' = m * h = -1.0 * 5 cm = -5 cm.\r\nImage is real, inverted, identical in height (5 cm), located 20 cm on the opposite side of the lens.	t	2023	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
SCI_2023_D_11	S10_CH12	\N	Standard (041)	A resistance wire R is powered by voltage V. If the wire is cut into two equal halves and wired in parallel across the same voltage, determine the ratio of initial to final power dissipation.	{"ratio": "1 Mark for 1:4", "derivation": "2 Marks"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	fc31cbb5e7870c1093b61d0b55437355bdd6822328b79456d1c4eebf9d519fa4	[]	1. Original power: P1 = V^2 / R.\r\n2. Halved wires: Each half has resistance R/2. Connected in parallel: 1/Rp = 1/(R/2) + 1/(R/2) = 4/R -> Rp = R/4.\r\n3. New power: P2 = V^2 / Rp = V^2 / (R/4) = 4 * (V^2 / R) = 4 * P1.\r\n4. Ratio P1 : P2 = 1 : 4.	t	2023	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
SCI_2023_D_12	S10_CH15	\N	Standard (041)	Why is stratospheric ozone depletion hazardous to the biosphere? Identify the synthetic chemical responsible and summarize the 1987 global treaty.	{"cfcs": "1 Mark", "hazard": "1 Mark", "treaty": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	39e5eb0296457d50dc24cd6fdbaa3a03c657e4fd624ea0e1c039eb01781ad191	[]	1. Ecological Hazard: Ozone filters high-energy ultraviolet (UV-B) rays. Depletion increases human skin cancers, cataracts, and impairs oceanic phytoplankton productivity.\r\n2. Causative Agent: Chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs), whose photochemically cleaved chlorine atoms catalytically destroy ozone molecules.\r\n3. Treaty: The 1987 Montreal Protocol signed under UNEP froze global CFC production at 1986 baseline thresholds.	t	2023	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
SCI_2023_D_13	S10_CH04	\N	Standard (041)	(a) What are structural isomers? Draw two structural isomers of butane (C4H10).\r\n(b) Write balanced chemical reactions for: (i) Complete combustion of ethanol, (ii) Reaction of ethanoic acid with sodium carbonate.\r\n(c) Explain why covalent organic molecules show low melting and boiling points.	{"isomers": "2 Marks", "reactions": "2 Marks", "intermolecular": "1 Mark"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	f732fba0b7ab894af43c953790c706ba20049139cb35407bfda85075029a0882	[]	(a) Structural isomers are chemical compounds sharing an identical molecular formula but exhibiting different structural atomic configurations.\r\nIsomers of C4H10:\r\n1. n-butane: CH3-CH2-CH2-CH3\r\n2. 2-methylpropane (isobutane): CH3-CH(CH3)-CH3\r\n(b) Reactions:\r\n(i) C2H5OH + 3O2 -> 2CO2 + 3H2O + Thermal Energy + Light\r\n(ii) 2CH3COOH + Na2CO3 -> 2CH3COONa + H2O + CO2(g)\r\n(c) Covalent molecules are bonded by weak intermolecular dispersion forces; minimal thermal energy is required to overcome these during phase transitions.	t	2023	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
SCI_2023_D_14	S10_CH08	\N	Standard (041)	(a) Describe the physiological functions of: (i) Placenta, (ii) Fallopian tube, (iii) Testes.\r\n(b) Differentiate between barrier and surgical methods of contraception with one advantage for each.	{"organs": "3 Marks", "contraception": "2 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	ca7d8c759c9126e989b03b9a4e100c72f8f72803eedc914335bb06f161763910	[]	(a) Organs:\r\n(i) Placenta: Provides a high surface area for glucose, amino acids, and oxygen diffusion from mother to fetus, and removes fetal metabolic waste (urea, CO2).\r\n(ii) Fallopian Tube (Oviduct): Serves as the site of fertilization and transports the developing zygote toward the uterus.\r\n(iii) Testes: Produces spermatozoa and synthesizes testosterone.\r\n(b) Contraception:\r\n1. Barrier Methods (e.g., Condoms): Block physical gamete union while offering protection against sexually transmitted infections (STIs).\r\n2. Surgical Methods (Vasectomy/Tubectomy): Sever and ligate gamete transport ducts, providing permanent contraception.	t	2023	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
SCI_2023_D_15	S10_CH13	\N	Standard (041)	(a) Describe the magnetic field pattern produced around a straight current-carrying conductor.\r\n(b) How does magnetic field intensity depend on: (i) Current magnitude, (ii) Distance from conductor?\r\n(c) State the rule used to determine the orientation of magnetic field lines around a current-carrying wire.	{"rule": "1 Mark", "factors": "2 Marks", "pattern": "2 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	3a400e05f199d360bdc0565318b924762e7037a6437dd512b9e9f08d4ea857ad	[]	(a) Magnetic field lines form concentric circular loops centered along the wire axis in perpendicular geometric planes.\r\n(b) (i) Directly proportional to electric current: B proportional to I.\r\n(ii) Inversely proportional to radial distance: B proportional to 1/r.\r\n(c) Right-Hand Thumb Rule: Grip the current-carrying wire with your right hand with the thumb extended in the direction of conventional current; your curled fingers point along the magnetic field loops.	t	2023	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
