SST_2023_D_08	SS10_HIS_01	\N	Standard (041)	Highlight three core geopolitical outcomes established by the Congress of Vienna in 1815.	{"bourbon": "1 Mark", "buffers": "1 Mark", "territory": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	b748ac81f077eecd498c28a198c183dd7f8d6ab5111cbb9d6adcf4219f501fe8	[]	1. Bourbon Restoration: Restored the Bourbon monarchy to power in France to re-establish conservative order.\r\n2. Buffer States: Established fortified border states surrounding France to check territorial expansion (e.g., Netherlands annexed Belgium).\r\n3. Territorial Redistribution: Awarded Prussia regions along its western border, Austria gained northern Italy (Lombardy-Venetia), and Russia was given parts of Poland.	t	2023	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SST_2023_D_09	SS10_ECO_02	\N	Standard (041)	Why is the tertiary service sector expanding faster than primary and secondary sectors in India? Provide three reasons.	{"basic": "1 Mark", "incomes": "1 Mark", "interdependence": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	ba6c0b011f42d707354a0acba59f6851469fe6a2ea6e36f0a5efbe66883291a3	[]	1. Basic Services: Government provision of public infrastructure (hospitals, schools, courts, police, banking) is required for modern civil administration.\r\n2. Intersectoral Demand: Growth in agriculture and industry creates derived demand for transport, logistics, storage, and retail.\r\n3. Income Elasticity: Increasing personal disposable incomes stimulate demand for consumer services like tourism, private schooling, dining, and IT services.	t	2023	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SST_2023_D_10	SS10_POL_03	\N	Standard (041)	Describe three forms in which communalism influences electoral politics in India.	{"beliefs": "1 Mark", "violence": "1 Mark", "mobilization": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	6e860b81a65d698981095e7de53445332206ff3ab00c4898bacf9f337964f817	[]	1. Everyday Beliefs: Religious prejudices, stereotypical biases, and convictions regarding one community's cultural superiority over others.\r\n2. Electoral Mobilization: Leveraging religious icons, emotional rhetoric, and clerical appeals to polarize voting groups.\r\n3. Communal Violence: Outbreaks of organized rioting, state-level disturbances, and sectarian clashes during elections.	t	2023	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SST_2023_D_11	SS10_ECO_03	\N	Standard (041)	Define 'terms of credit'. List four essential components of terms of credit.	{"definition": "1 Mark", "four_elements": "2 Marks (0.5M each)"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	9ec12872f03f0681029c246aa3a9feaf16bd6b6a610f12b87d24265d702c078e	[]	Terms of credit encompass the contractually binding conditions under which a financial lender issues a loan.\r\nFour components:\r\n1. Interest Rate: The annual percentage rate charged on the principal sum.\r\n2. Collateral: Physical security (land titles, house deeds, gold) held by the lender until full repayment.\r\n3. Documentation: Proof of identity, income verification, and employment status.\r\n4. Mode of Repayment: Tenure, frequency, and payment mechanism of installments.	t	2023	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SST_2023_D_12	SS10_GEO_06	\N	Standard (041)	State three measures to control industrial pollution and reduce ecological degradation in India.	{"air": "1 Mark", "water": "1 Mark", "solids": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	1e222ddbabcd34dee00100877f1d7764168583c0eb26af913bf892e833e171e6	[]	1. Wastewater Treatment: Treating industrial effluents through mechanical sedimentation, biological aeration, and chemical recycling before discharge.\r\n2. Atmospheric Controls: Installing electrostatic precipitators, scrubbers, and particulate baghouse filters on smokestacks, and substituting coal with natural gas.\r\n3. Solid Waste Recycling: Using coal fly ash in brick making and repurposing blast-furnace slag in cement production.	t	2023	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SST_2023_D_13	SS10_HIS_02	\N	Standard (041)	Why was the salt tax chosen by Mahatma Gandhi to spearhead the Civil Disobedience Movement? Detail the historic Salt March of 1930.	{"symbolism": "2 Marks", "march_history": "3 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	120650985c6f932e5e8f7e7276725fc5ad32513c60a84f1bf1b181c9494489be	[]	1. Strategic Symbolism: Salt was an everyday necessity consumed equally by rich and poor. The British state monopoly on its manufacture and the tax burden symbolized colonial exploitation across social divisions.\r\n2. The Historic Dandi March:\r\n- On March 12, 1930, Gandhi departed Sabarmati Ashram with 78 verified satyagrahis.\r\n- The march covered 240 miles over 24 days across Gujarat.\r\n- On April 6, 1930, Gandhi reached Dandi, boiled seawater, broke the salt monopoly, and initiated the nationwide movement.	t	2023	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SST_2023_D_14	SS10_POL_02	\N	Standard (041)	Explain the division of legislative powers between the Union and the State Governments across the three constitutional lists.	{"state": "1.5 Marks", "union": "1.5 Marks", "residuary": "0.5 Mark", "concurrent": "1.5 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	1e4fdee24a344af022af5616a4d08cf3db44293d7a78429f4a5edb7e4d0f703d	[]	1. Union List: Covers subjects requiring uniform national policy: Defense, Foreign Affairs, Banking, Communications, and Currency. Only the Union Parliament can legislate.\r\n2. State List: Covers matters of local and state interest: Police, Trade, Commerce, Agriculture, and Irrigation. State Assemblies have exclusive legislative authority.\r\n3. Concurrent List: Covers common interests: Education, Forests, Trade Unions, Marriage, and Succession. Both Union and States can legislate; in case of conflict, Union law prevails.\r\n4. Residuary Subjects: Any subject not enumerated in the three lists (e.g., Cyber Law) falls under exclusive Union jurisdiction.	t	2023	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
PYQ_2024_AI_SET1_Q01	M10_CH01	Co-prime Product Relation	Standard	If two positive integers $a$ and $b$ are co-prime, then $\\text{HCF}(a, b)$ is:\r\n(A) $0$\r\n(B) $1$\r\n(C) $a b$\r\n(D) $a + b$	[{"step": "State standard definition: two numbers are co-prime if their only common positive factor is 1", "marks": 0.5}, {"step": "Conclude HCF(a, b) = 1 and select Option (B)", "marks": 0.5}]	1.0	Objective	Remembering	Easy	\N	["CBSE_2024", "All_India_Set_1", "Section_A"]	By mathematical definition, two positive integers $a$ and $b$ are termed co-prime (or mutually prime) if and only if their greatest common divisor is $1$:\r\n$$\\text{HCF}(a, b) = 1$$\r\nTherefore, the correct option is **(B)**.	t	2024	All India Set 1	t	MATH	All India
SST_2023_D_15	SS10_ECO_04	\N	Standard (041)	What is globalization? Explain three positive impacts and two negative consequences of economic globalization in India.	{"negatives": "1.5 Marks", "positives": "2.5 Marks", "definition": "1 Mark"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	3847ce06cd44d7fc349f3c679c1f14390330e3b0dcda6f69e750e4ca03c633d0	[]	1. Definition: The rapid cross-border integration of national markets, goods, services, technologies, and human capital.\r\n2. Positive Impacts:\r\n- Consumer Benefits: Wider selection of consumer goods at competitive prices.\r\n- Direct Investment: Increased foreign direct investment (FDI) in automobiles, electronics, and IT services.\r\n- Domestic Growth: Several Indian corporations (Tata Motors, Infosys, Ranbaxy) expanded into global multinationals.\r\n3. Negative Consequences:\r\n- Local Business Vulnerability: Small-scale domestic producers (batteries, plastics, toys) faced factory closures due to cheaper imports.\r\n- Job Insecurity: Increased reliance on informal, uncontracted labor contracts.	t	2023	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
