SCI_2023_AI_04	S10_CH06	\N	Standard (041)	In which part of the human alimentary canal is water absorbed from undigested food material?\r\n(A) Stomach\r\n(B) Small intestine\r\n(C) Large intestine\r\n(D) Esophagus	{"answer": "1 Mark for Option (C)"}	1.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	949031c3802e42a1efb65cb5f9cafd4767999c5083198f6385f35e438b71d68a	[]	Correct Option: (C)\r\nThe walls of the large intestine absorb residual water and essential mineral salts from unabsorbed waste.	t	2023	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2023_AI_05	S10_CH01	\N	Standard (041)	Define rancidity. State two practical methods used in everyday domestic and industrial food preservation to prevent rancidity.	{"methods": "1 Mark", "definition": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	8a161a39efb6d32520fe5fc149570ef458dff26d987716f9cae0ef3277c090de	[]	1. Definition: The oxidation of fats and oils present in food items when exposed to air, producing an unpleasant odor, altered taste, and toxic peroxides.\r\n2. Prevention: (i) Packaging fatty foods in nitrogen gas flushes (e.g., potato chips), (ii) Storing food in airtight containers away from direct light, or adding antioxidants like BHA/BHT.	t	2023	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2023_AI_06	S10_CH07	\N	Standard (041)	How does feedback mechanism regulate the release of hormones in the human body? Explain with insulin secretion as an example.	{"example": "1 Mark", "feedback": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	50a34f838cd21ae833fd4d770e0fd8f05f74fb0412c4cd786e115be03980e33a	[]	The timing and amount of hormone released are controlled by built-in feedback loops. When blood glucose levels rise after digestion, pancreatic islet cells detect this surge and secrete insulin to facilitate cellular glucose uptake. As blood glucose falls back to normal baseline, insulin secretion is down-regulated.	t	2023	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2023_AI_07	S10_CH10	\N	Standard (041)	State Snell's law of refraction. Write the mathematical expression for the refractive index of medium 2 with respect to medium 1 in terms of light velocities.	{"law": "1 Mark", "expression": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	7cd991bed010e7d4ff71738b7c1ba2751f710b93273a9877ff7f69585519ed53	[]	1. Snell's Law: The ratio of the sine of the angle of incidence to the sine of the angle of refraction is a constant for a given pair of optical media: sin(i) / sin(r) = constant.\r\n2. Velocity Relation: n_21 = Speed of light in medium 1 (v1) / Speed of light in medium 2 (v2).	t	2023	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2023_AI_08	S10_CH02	\N	Standard (041)	What is water of crystallization? Give the chemical formula and common name of two crystalline salts that lose water of crystallization on heating.	{"salts": "2 Marks (1 Mark each)", "definition": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	4ae797831dec18be4b217cef1da7dc4fe53d8f9d9880d9257c390d70e661c53a	[]	1. Definition: The fixed number of water molecules chemically bonded in one formula unit of a crystalline salt.\r\n2. Two Salts:\r\n- Blue Vitriol (Copper Sulphate Pentahydrate): CuSO4·5H2O; turns white anhydrous CuSO4 upon losing 5 water molecules.\r\n- Washing Soda (Sodium Carbonate Decahydrate): Na2CO3·10H2O; loses water molecules on exposure to air/heat to form monohydrate.	t	2023	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2023_AI_09	S10_CH06	\N	Standard (041)	Differentiate between aerobic respiration and anaerobic respiration with respect to: (i) site of breakdown, (ii) end products, (iii) ATP yield per glucose molecule.	{"site": "1 Mark", "yield": "1 Mark", "products": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	5db02af9cd9764cd0732c4e2882df244bfa0d7bb81d8f74e1aa9a35883476908	[]	1. Site: Aerobic occurs partially in cytoplasm and completes in mitochondria; anaerobic occurs solely in the cytoplasm.\r\n2. End Products: Aerobic yields CO2, H2O, and high energy; anaerobic yields ethanol + CO2 (yeast) or lactic acid (muscle cells).\r\n3. ATP Yield: Aerobic yields 36–38 ATP molecules; anaerobic yields only 2 ATP molecules per glucose molecule.	t	2023	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2023_AI_10	S10_CH10	\N	Standard (041)	A concave mirror produces a three times magnified real image of an object placed at 10 cm in front of it. Find the image distance and focal length.	{"f": "1.5 Marks", "v": "1.5 Marks"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	091a3518ba71f57e78d85073c2b3866c8d64466f52f244e3d37c0f83a53a2667	[]	Given: u = -10 cm, m = -3 (real and inverted).\r\nMagnification: m = -v / u -> -3 = -v / (-10) -> v = -30 cm.\r\nMirror Formula: 1/f = 1/v + 1/u = 1/(-30) + 1/(-10) = (-1 - 3)/30 = -4/30 = -2/15.\r\nf = -15/2 = -7.5 cm.\r\nImage is formed 30 cm in front of mirror; focal length is 7.5 cm.	t	2023	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2023_AI_11	S10_CH12	\N	Standard (041)	Explain why an electric bulb filament is made of tungsten and why bulbs are filled with chemically inactive nitrogen and argon gases.	{"gases": "1.5 Marks", "tungsten": "1.5 Marks"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	55ca8887141f604aef6d09ae70383f16bfc0908c9f2a6296b37bc5a73cb498b7	[]	1. Tungsten Filament: Tungsten possesses an exceptionally high melting point (3380 deg C) and high resistivity, allowing it to glow white-hot without melting.\r\n2. Inactive Gas Filling: Nitrogen and argon are chemically inert gases that prevent the hot tungsten filament from oxidizing and volatilizing, significantly extending bulb lifespan.	t	2023	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2018_D_01	S10_CH01	\N	Standard (041)	A student adds a few drops of universal indicator to a solution of dilute ethanoic acid. The colour produced on the indicator paper will be:\r\n(A) Dark red\r\n(B) Orange-yellow\r\n(C) Green\r\n(D) Dark blue	{"answer": "1 Mark for Option (B)"}	1.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	01cdd9b822539f82406373046141fce803f3cbc9142c6e96517c9e35801908a3	[]	Correct Option: (B)\r\nDilute ethanoic acid is a weak organic acid with a pH between 3 and 5, producing an orange-yellow colour on universal indicator paper.	t	2018	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
SCI_2023_AI_12	S10_CH15	\N	Standard (041)	What are decomposers? Name two groups of decomposers and explain their essential role in cycling nutrients in an ecosystem.	{"role": "1 Mark", "groups": "1 Mark", "definition": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	fce680e910a806ddad97bc98642c9fbf377c23478eb83fd093eff2d1e1069143	[]	1. Definition: Microorganisms that biochemically break down complex organic compounds found in dead plants and animal carcasses into simpler inorganic substances.\r\n2. Groups: Bacteria and Fungi.\r\n3. Ecological Role: They return recycled mineral elements (nitrogen, carbon, phosphorus) into soil and water reservoirs, enabling renewed absorption by autotrophic producers.	t	2023	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2023_AI_13	S10_CH04	\N	Standard (041)	(a) What is a homologous series of carbon compounds? State any two distinguishing traits.\r\n(b) Write the structural formula of propanone and ethanoic acid.\r\n(c) Give chemical tests to distinguish between saturated and unsaturated hydrocarbons.	{"test": "1.5 Marks", "series": "2 Marks", "structures": "1.5 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	78799c78019c673eda7bbd34c1b9305c63475b51af73a7ae83c2ff2962d85a36	[]	(a) A series of structurally related organic compounds containing the same functional group where adjacent members differ by a -CH2- unit (14 unified mass units).\r\nTraits: Constant chemical reactivity; graded variation in physical properties (boiling point, solubility).\r\n(b) Structural Formulas:\r\n- Propanone: CH3-CO-CH3 (acetone).\r\n- Ethanoic acid: CH3-COOH.\r\n(c) Bromine Water Test: Unsaturated hydrocarbons (alkenes/alkynes) decolorize reddish-brown bromine water; saturated alkanes do not react in the dark.	t	2023	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2023_AI_14	S10_CH08	\N	Standard (041)	(a) Describe the process of vegetative propagation in plants. State two practical advantages of this method.\r\n(b) Trace the path of sperm transport in the human male reproductive system from formation to ejaculation.	{"sperm_path": "2.5 Marks", "propagation": "2.5 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	bfeb45da0df2474471e62e9e7f7ab1f928f484da9320be3474f535e493e47332	[]	(a) Vegetative Propagation: A form of asexual reproduction where new plantlets develop from vegetative structures (roots, stems, leaves) without seeds or spores.\r\nAdvantages: Produces seedless cultivars (bananas, grapes, rose); offspring retain identical genetic characteristics of the parent plant.\r\n(b) Sperm Pathway:\r\nSeminiferous Tubules (Testes) -> Epididymis (maturation/storage) -> Vas Deferens -> merges with Seminal Vesicle and Prostate Gland secretions -> Urethra -> Penis.	t	2023	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2023_AI_15	S10_CH13	\N	Standard (041)	(a) State the principle of an electric motor.\r\n(b) What is the function of the split-ring commutator and carbon brushes in a direct-current motor?\r\n(c) State three key factors that govern the strength of the induced magnetic force on a conductor in a magnetic field.	{"factors": "1.5 Marks", "principle": "1.5 Marks", "commutator_brush": "2 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	7c7a5792794802e26466f929fde86529c0ec6dd9dd9949e649b153d840c6f1d5	[]	(a) Principle: A current-carrying coil placed perpendicular to a magnetic field experiences equal and opposite mechanical forces along its sides, producing continuous rotational torque (Fleming's Left-Hand Rule).\r\n(b) Split-Ring Commutator: Reverses the direction of electric current flow through the rotating armature every half-turn, maintaining unidirectional torque.\r\nCarbon Brushes: Maintain sliding electrical contact with the commutator rings to deliver current without twisting external wires.\r\n(c) Factors: (i) Magnetic field intensity (B), (ii) Current magnitude (I), (iii) Conductor length (L) (F = B I L sin(theta)).	t	2023	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
