SCI_2022_D_07	S10_CH12	\N	Standard (041)	A uniform metal wire of resistance 20 ohms is cut into 5 equal parts. These 5 parts are then connected in parallel. Determine the equivalent resistance of this parallel combination.	{"parallel": "1 Mark for 0.8 ohm", "part_res": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	f35989b1850c0b9ec77ae15107dd39ffa6cc4f772945d6d0dfbce3aa0e85e8f0	[]	Each part has resistance r = 20 / 5 = 4 ohms.\r\nConnected in parallel: 1/Rp = 1/4 + 1/4 + 1/4 + 1/4 + 1/4 = 5/4.\r\nRp = 4 / 5 = 0.8 ohms.	t	2022	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
SCI_2022_D_08	S10_CH04	\N	Standard (041)	Explain the two unique properties of carbon that account for the formation of a very large number of carbon compounds.	{"catenation": "1.5 Marks", "tetravalency": "1.5 Marks"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	d2bf0378c8974933eb38d81f87c8d6b4ececf59282940b3061e787f6dbec2087	[]	1. Catenation: The unique ability of carbon atoms to form strong, stable covalent bonds with other carbon atoms, producing long linear chains, branched networks, and rings.\r\n2. Tetravalency: With a valency of four, carbon can form covalent bonds with four other atoms of carbon, hydrogen, oxygen, nitrogen, sulfur, or halogens.	t	2022	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
SCI_2022_D_09	S10_CH08	\N	Standard (041)	Trace the sequence of physiological and anatomical events that occur in a flower from the moment pollen lands on the stigma until seed development begins.	{"seed": "1 Mark", "germination": "1 Mark", "fertilization": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	9d3010e96c65b2b7ba267c71880d94252e1ebc6bab064c1e26fff2bc332a94bf	[]	1. Pollen Germination: Stigma secretes sugary fluid; pollen grain germinates and develops a pollen tube.\r\n2. Chemotropic Growth: Pollen tube grows through the style toward the ovary, guided by chemical signals.\r\n3. Syngamy & Triple Fusion: Pollen tube penetrates ovule through micropyle; one male gamete fuses with egg (zygote -> embryo), and the second fuses with polar nuclei (triploid endosperm).\r\n4. Seed Transformation: The fertilized ovule hardens into a seed while ovary ripens into a fruit.	t	2022	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
SCI_2022_D_10	S10_CH09	\N	Standard (041)	A pure tall pea plant (TT) with round seeds (RR) is crossed with a pure dwarf plant (tt) with wrinkled seeds (rr).\r\n(a) What are the phenotype and genotype of the F1 generation?\r\n(b) State the dihybrid phenotypic ratio observed in the F2 generation resulting from self-pollination of F1.	{"f1": "1.5 Marks", "f2": "1.5 Marks"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	0ade301c17e1181917cc7e88d9e5af4aaf241a63b4e0cb025ede7196de70048b	[]	(a) F1 Genotype: TtRr (all heterozygous).\r\n    F1 Phenotype: Tall plants with round seeds (100%).\r\n(b) F2 Phenotypic Ratio (Mendel Law of Independent Assortment):\r\n    9 Tall-Round : 3 Tall-Wrinkled : 3 Dwarf-Round : 1 Dwarf-Wrinkled (9:3:3:1).	t	2022	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
CBSE_PRAC_STD_Q01	M10_CH01	LCM and HCF Exponent Invariance	Standard	If two positive integers $a$ and $b$ are expressible in the form $a = p^3 q^2$ and $b = p q^3$, where $p$ and $q$ are prime numbers, then $[\\text{LCM}(a, b) / \\text{HCF}(a, b)]$ is equal to:\r\n(A) $p^2 q$\r\n(B) $p^2 / q$\r\n(C) $p q$\r\n(D) $p^3 q^3$	[{"step": "Compute HCF(a, b) = p^1 * q^2", "marks": 0.5}, {"step": "Compute LCM(a, b) = p^3 * q^3; Ratio = (p^3 * q^3)/(p * q^2) = p^2 * q (Select Option A)", "marks": 0.5}]	1.0	Objective	Analysing	Medium	\N	["CBSE_Practice_Paper", "Standard_041", "Section_A"]	Given $a = p^3 q^2$ and $b = p q^3$.\r\n• $\\text{HCF}(a, b) = p^{\\min(3, 1)} \\times q^{\\min(2, 3)} = p^1 q^2$\r\n• $\\text{LCM}(a, b) = p^{\\max(3, 1)} \\times q^{\\max(2, 3)} = p^3 q^3$\r\n\r\n$$\\frac{\\text{LCM}(a, b)}{\\text{HCF}(a, b)} = \\frac{p^3 q^3}{p q^2} = p^{3 - 1} q^{3 - 2} = p^2 q.$$\r\nTherefore, Option **(A)** is correct.	t	2024	Official Practice Paper	t	MATH	All India
CBSE_PRAC_STD_Q02	M10_CH02	Parabola Intersections and Sign of Coefficients	Standard	The graph of a quadratic polynomial $y = ax^2 + bx + c$ opens upwards and intersects the $x$-axis at two distinct positive coordinates. Which of the following is true?\r\n(A) $a > 0, b > 0, c > 0$\r\n(B) $a > 0, b < 0, c > 0$\r\n(C) $a < 0, b > 0, c < 0$\r\n(D) $a > 0, b < 0, c < 0$	[{"step": "Graph opens upwards => a > 0", "marks": 0.5}, {"step": "Both zeroes positive => sum (-b/a) > 0 => b < 0, and product (c/a) > 0 => c > 0 (Option B)", "marks": 0.5}]	1.0	Objective	Analysing	Hard	\N	["CBSE_Practice_Paper", "Standard_041", "Section_A"]	1. Because the parabola opens upwards, $a > 0$.\r\n2. The zeroes $\\alpha, \\beta$ are both positive real numbers ($\\alpha > 0, \\beta > 0$):\r\n   • Sum of zeroes: $\\alpha + \\beta = -\\frac{b}{a} > 0 \\implies b < 0$ (since $a > 0$).\r\n   • Product of zeroes: $\\alpha \\beta = \\frac{c}{a} > 0 \\implies c > 0$ (since $a > 0$).\r\nThus, $a > 0, b < 0, c > 0$. Option **(B)** is correct.	t	2024	Official Practice Paper	t	MATH	All India
CBSE_PRAC_STD_Q03	M10_CH03	No Solution Line Ratio Parameter	Standard	The pair of linear equations $kx + 3y + 1 = 0$ and $2x + y + 3 = 0$ represent parallel lines if $k$ equals:\r\n(A) $6$\r\n(B) $2/3$\r\n(C) $-6$\r\n(D) $3/2$	[{"step": "State parallel condition: a1/a2 = b1/b2 != c1/c2", "marks": 0.5}, {"step": "Substitute k/2 = 3/1 => k = 6 and verify 1/3 != 3 (Option A)", "marks": 0.5}]	1.0	Objective	Understanding	Easy	\N	["CBSE_Practice_Paper", "Standard_041", "Section_A"]	For two lines to be parallel, their coefficients must satisfy:\r\n$$\\frac{a_1}{a_2} = \\frac{b_1}{b_2} \\neq \\frac{c_1}{c_2}$$\r\n$$\\frac{k}{2} = \\frac{3}{1} \\neq \\frac{1}{3}$$\r\nFrom the first equality:\r\n$$k = 2 \\times 3 = 6.$$\r\nSince $\\frac{3}{1} \\neq \\frac{1}{3}$, the lines are strictly parallel with no solution.\r\nOption **(A)** is correct.	t	2024	Official Practice Paper	t	MATH	All India
CBSE_PRAC_STD_Q04	M10_CH08	Secant Tangent Sum and Reciprocal Relation	Standard	If $\\sec \\theta + \\tan \\theta = x$, then the value of $\\tan \\theta$ is:\r\n(A) $\\frac{x^2 + 1}{2x}$\r\n(B) $\\frac{x^2 - 1}{2x}$\r\n(C) $\\frac{x^2 - 1}{x}$\r\n(D) $\\frac{2x}{x^2 - 1}$	[{"step": "Use identity sec^2 theta - tan^2 theta = 1 => sec theta - tan theta = 1/x", "marks": 0.5}, {"step": "Subtract (sec theta - tan theta) from (sec theta + tan theta) to isolate 2*tan theta = x - 1/x = (x^2 - 1)/x => tan theta = (x^2 - 1)/(2x) (Option B)", "marks": 0.5}]	1.0	Objective	Applying	Medium	\N	["CBSE_Practice_Paper", "Standard_041", "Section_A"]	We know:\r\n$$\\sec^2 \\theta - \\tan^2 \\theta = 1$$\r\n$$(\\sec \\theta + \\tan \\theta)(\\sec \\theta - \\tan \\theta) = 1$$\r\nGiven $\\sec \\theta + \\tan \\theta = x$, it follows that:\r\n$$\\sec \\theta - \\tan \\theta = \\frac{1}{x}$$\r\nSubtracting the two equations:\r\n$$(\\sec \\theta + \\tan \\theta) - (\\sec \\theta - \\tan \\theta) = x - \\frac{1}{x}$$\r\n$$2\\tan \\theta = \\frac{x^2 - 1}{x} \\implies \\tan \\theta = \\frac{x^2 - 1}{2x}.$$\r\nTherefore, Option **(B)** is correct.	t	2024	Official Practice Paper	t	MATH	All India
CBSE_PRAC_STD_Q05	M10_CH07	Equidistant Locus Relation between Variables	Standard	Find a relation between $x$ and $y$ such that the point $P(x, y)$ is equidistant from the points $A(3, 6)$ and $B(-3, 4)$.	[{"step": "State PA^2 = PB^2 => (x - 3)^2 + (y - 6)^2 = (x - (-3))^2 + (y - 4)^2", "marks": 1.0}, {"step": "Expand and simplify: -6x - 12y + 45 = 6x - 8y + 25 => 12x + 4y - 20 = 0 => 3x + y - 5 = 0", "marks": 1.0}]	2.0	Short Answer	Applying	Medium	\N	["CBSE_Practice_Paper", "Standard_041", "Section_B"]	Since point $P(x, y)$ is equidistant from $A(3, 6)$ and $B(-3, 4)$:\r\n$$PA = PB \\implies PA^2 = PB^2$$\r\nUsing the distance formula:\r\n$$(x - 3)^2 + (y - 6)^2 = (x + 3)^2 + (y - 4)^2$$\r\n$$(x^2 - 6x + 9) + (y^2 - 12y + 36) = (x^2 + 6x + 9) + (y^2 - 8y + 16)$$\r\n$$-6x - 12y + 45 = 6x - 8y + 25$$\r\nTranspose terms:\r\n$$12x + 4y = 20$$\r\nDividing by 4:\r\n$$3x + y - 5 = 0.$$\r\nThis is the required linear relation.	t	2024	Official Practice Paper	t	MATH	All India
CBSE_PRAC_STD_Q06	M10_CH06	Triangle Ratio of Similar Sub-Triangles	Standard	In $\\Delta ABC$, $D$ and $E$ are points on sides $AB$ and $AC$ respectively such that $DE \\parallel BC$. If $AD = 2\\text{ cm}, AB = 6\\text{ cm},$ and $DE = 3\\text{ cm}$, find the length of $BC$.	[{"step": "Prove Delta ADE ~ Delta ABC by AA similarity (angle ADE = angle B, angle AED = angle C)", "marks": 1.0}, {"step": "Set ratio AD / AB = DE / BC => 2 / 6 = 3 / BC => 1/3 = 3 / BC => BC = 9 cm", "marks": 1.0}]	2.0	Short Answer	Applying	Easy	\N	["CBSE_Practice_Paper", "Standard_041", "Section_B"]	In $\\Delta ADE$ and $\\Delta ABC$:\r\n$$\\angle ADE = \\angle ABC \\quad (\\text{Corresponding angles, as } DE \\parallel BC)$$\r\n$$\\angle DAE = \\angle BAC \\quad (\\text{Common angle})$$\r\nBy AA similarity:\r\n$$\\Delta ADE \\sim \\Delta ABC$$\r\nRatio of corresponding sides:\r\n$$\\frac{AD}{AB} = \\frac{DE}{BC}$$\r\n$$\\frac{2}{6} = \\frac{3}{BC}$$\r\n$$\\frac{1}{3} = \\frac{3}{BC} \\implies BC = 3 \\times 3 = 9\\text{ cm}.$$	t	2024	Official Practice Paper	t	MATH	All India
SCI_2022_D_11	S10_CH13	\N	Standard (041)	State three distinguishing characteristics of magnetic field lines. Why do two magnetic field lines never cross each other?	{"traits": "1.5 Marks", "intersection": "1.5 Marks"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	6a879656af8e7691cafabe1473943e1ffcab64cc783c2b765be5a24f59557675	[]	1. Characteristics:\r\n- Continuous closed loops emerging from North pole and entering South pole outside the magnet (South to North inside).\r\n- Field line density reflects magnetic field strength (closer lines represent stronger field).\r\n- Never intersect.\r\n2. Non-Intersection Principle:\r\nIf two field lines crossed, a magnetic compass placed at the intersection point would point in two different directions simultaneously, which is physically impossible.	t	2022	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
CBSE_PRAC_STD_Q07	M10_CH14	Calendar Probability Leap Year Days	Standard	Find the probability that a leap year selected at random contains $53$ Sundays.	[{"step": "Total days in leap year = 366 = 52 full weeks + 2 extra days", "marks": 1.0}, {"step": "List 7 pairs of extra days; favorable pairs containing Sunday: (Sat, Sun) and (Sun, Mon) => P = 2/7", "marks": 1.0}]	2.0	Short Answer	Understanding	Easy	\N	["CBSE_Practice_Paper", "Standard_041", "Section_B"]	A leap year contains $366\\text{ days}$.\r\n$$366\\text{ days} = 52\\text{ weeks} + 2\\text{ odd days}$$\r\n$52$ complete weeks give $52$ Sundays. The 53rd Sunday depends on the $2$ extra consecutive days:\r\nSample space of $2$ consecutive days ($7$ equally likely pairs):\r\n$$S = \\{(\\text{Sun, Mon}), (\\text{Mon, Tue}), (\\text{Tue, Wed}), (\\text{Wed, Thu}), (\\text{Thu, Fri}), (\\text{Fri, Sat}), (\\text{Sat, Sun})\\}$$\r\nPairs containing Sunday: $\\{(\\text{Sun, Mon}), (\\text{Sat, Sun})\\} \\implies n(E) = 2$.\r\n$$P(53\\text{ Sundays}) = \\frac{2}{7}.$$	t	2024	Official Practice Paper	t	MATH	All India
CBSE_PRAC_STD_Q08	M10_CH05	Ratio of Sum of Terms to Ratio of nth Terms	Standard	The ratio of the sums of $m$ and $n$ terms of an A.P. is $m^2 : n^2$. Show that the ratio of their $m^{\\text{th}}$ and $n^{\\text{th}}$ terms is $(2m - 1) : (2n - 1)$.	[{"step": "Set Sm / Sn = (m/2)[2a + (m - 1)d] / (n/2)[2a + (n - 1)d] = m^2 / n^2", "marks": 1.0}, {"step": "Simplify to [2a + (m - 1)d] / [2a + (n - 1)d] = m / n", "marks": 1.0}, {"step": "Replace m by 2m - 1 and n by 2n - 1 to obtain [a + (m - 1)d] / [a + (n - 1)d] = (2m - 1)/(2n - 1)", "marks": 1.0}]	3.0	Core Proofs	Analysing	Hard	\N	["CBSE_Practice_Paper", "Standard_041", "Section_C"]	Given:\r\n$$\\frac{S_m}{S_n} = \\frac{\\frac{m}{2}[2a + (m - 1)d]}{\\frac{n}{2}[2a + (n - 1)d]} = \\frac{m^2}{n^2}$$\r\n$$\\frac{2a + (m - 1)d}{2a + (n - 1)d} = \\frac{m}{n} \\quad \\text{--- (1)}$$\r\n\r\nTo find the ratio of the $m^{\\text{th}}$ and $n^{\\text{th}}$ terms:\r\n$$\\frac{a_m}{a_n} = \\frac{a + (m - 1)d}{a + (n - 1)d} = \\frac{2a + 2(m - 1)d}{2a + 2(n - 1)d} = \\frac{2a + [(2m - 1) - 1]d}{2a + [(2n - 1) - 1]d}$$\r\n\r\nReplacing $m$ with $(2m - 1)$ and $n$ with $(2n - 1)$ in equation (1):\r\n$$\\frac{2a + [(2m - 1) - 1]d}{2a + [(2n - 1) - 1]d} = \\frac{2m - 1}{2n - 1}$$\r\n$$\\frac{a_m}{a_n} = \\frac{2m - 1}{2n - 1}.$$\r\nHence proved. $\\blacksquare$	t	2024	Official Practice Paper	t	MATH	All India
CBSE_PRAC_STD_Q09	M10_CH10	Opposite Sides of Circumscribed Quadrilateral Supplementary Angles	Standard	Prove that opposite sides of a quadrilateral circumscribing a circle subtend supplementary angles at the centre of the circle.	[{"step": "Draw quadrilateral ABCD circumscribing circle O touching at P, Q, R, S; join OA, OB, OC, OD and OP, OQ, OR, OS", "marks": 1.0}, {"step": "Prove pairs of adjacent subtended angles equal by RHS congruence (angle 1 = angle 2, angle 3 = angle 4, etc.)", "marks": 1.0}, {"step": "Sum all angles around centre to 360 deg: 2(angle 1 + angle 4 + angle 5 + angle 8) = 360 deg => angle AOB + angle COD = 180 deg", "marks": 1.0}]	3.0	Core Proofs	Analysing	Hard	\N	["CBSE_Practice_Paper", "Standard_041", "Section_C"]	Let quadrilateral $ABCD$ circumscribe a circle with centre $O$, touching sides $AB, BC, CD, DA$ at $P, Q, R, S$ respectively.\r\nJoin $OP, OQ, OR, OS$ and vertices $OA, OB, OC, OD$.\r\n\r\nIn $\\Delta AOP$ and $\\Delta AOS$:\r\n• $AP = AS$ (Tangents from external point $A$)\r\n• $OP = OS$ (Radii)\r\n• $OA = OA$ (Common)\r\n$\\Delta AOP \\cong \\Delta AOS \\implies \\angle 1 = \\angle 8$.\r\nSimilarly:\r\n$$\\angle 2 = \\angle 3, \\quad \\angle 4 = \\angle 5, \\quad \\angle 6 = \\angle 7$$\r\n\r\nThe complete angle around centre $O$ is $360^\\circ$:\r\n$$\\angle 1 + \\angle 2 + \\angle 3 + \\angle 4 + \\angle 5 + \\angle 6 + \\angle 7 + \\angle 8 = 360^\\circ$$\r\n$$2(\\angle 1 + \\angle 2 + \\angle 5 + \\angle 6) = 360^\\circ$$\r\n$$(\\angle 1 + \\angle 2) + (\\angle 5 + \\angle 6) = 180^\\circ$$\r\n$$\\angle AOB + \\angle COD = 180^\\circ.$$\r\nSimilarly, $\\angle BOC + \\angle AOD = 180^\\circ$.\r\nHence proved. $\\blacksquare$	t	2024	Official Practice Paper	t	MATH	All India
CBSE_PRAC_STD_Q10	M10_CH08	Trigonometric Identity Proof with Cubic Terms	Standard	Prove the following identity:\r\n$$\\frac{\\sin \\theta - 2\\sin^3 \\theta}{2\\cos^3 \\theta - \\cos \\theta} = \\tan \\theta$$	[{"step": "Factor sin theta from numerator and cos theta from denominator: (sin theta)(1 - 2*sin^2 theta) / [(cos theta)(2*cos^2 theta - 1)]", "marks": 1.0}, {"step": "Substitute 1 = sin^2 theta + cos^2 theta in numerator and denominator", "marks": 1.0}, {"step": "Simplify to (sin theta)(cos^2 theta - sin^2 theta) / [(cos theta)(cos^2 theta - sin^2 theta)] = tan theta", "marks": 1.0}]	3.0	Core Proofs	Applying	Medium	\N	["CBSE_Practice_Paper", "Standard_041", "Section_C"]	$$\\text{LHS} = \\frac{\\sin \\theta - 2\\sin^3 \\theta}{2\\cos^3 \\theta - \\cos \\theta} = \\frac{\\sin \\theta (1 - 2\\sin^2 \\theta)}{\\cos \\theta (2\\cos^2 \\theta - 1)}$$\r\nSubstitute $1 = \\sin^2 \\theta + \\cos^2 \\theta$:\r\n$$= \\frac{\\sin \\theta [(\\sin^2 \\theta + \\cos^2 \\theta) - 2\\sin^2 \\theta]}{\\cos \\theta [2\\cos^2 \\theta - (\\sin^2 \\theta + \\cos^2 \\theta)]}$$\r\n$$= \\frac{\\sin \\theta (\\cos^2 \\theta - \\sin^2 \\theta)}{\\cos \\theta (\\cos^2 \\theta - \\sin^2 \\theta)}$$\r\n$$= \\frac{\\sin \\theta}{\\cos \\theta} = \\tan \\theta = \\text{RHS}.$$\r\nHence proved. $\\blacksquare$	t	2024	Official Practice Paper	t	MATH	All India
CBSE_PRAC_STD_Q11	M10_CH13	Median Evaluation of Grouped Table	Standard	Calculate the median for the following frequency distribution:\r\n$$\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|c|c|}\r\n\\hline\r\n\\text{Height (in cm)} & 135-140 & 140-145 & 145-150 & 150-155 & 155-160 \\\\\r\n\\hline\r\n\\text{Number of Girls} & 4 & 7 & 18 & 11 & 10 \\\\\r\n\\hline\r\n\\end{array}$$	[{"step": "Build cumulative frequency table: 4, 11, 29, 40, 50 (N = 50)", "marks": 1.0}, {"step": "Identify median class N/2 = 25 => class 145-150 (l = 145, cf = 11, f = 18, h = 5)", "marks": 1.0}, {"step": "Median = 145 + [(25 - 11)/18] * 5 = 145 + (14/18)*5 = 145 + 3.89 = 148.89 cm", "marks": 1.0}]	3.0	Core Proofs	Applying	Medium	\N	["CBSE_Practice_Paper", "Standard_041", "Section_C"]	Cumulative Frequency:\r\n• $135-140$: $f = 4, cf = 4$\r\n• $140-145$: $f = 7, cf = 11$\r\n• $145-150$: $f = 18, cf = 29$\r\n• $150-155$: $f = 11, cf = 40$\r\n• $155-160$: $f = 10, cf = 50$\r\n\r\nTotal frequency $N = 50 \\implies \\frac{N}{2} = 25$.\r\nMedian class is **$145-150$**:\r\n• $l = 145$\r\n• $cf = 11$\r\n• $f = 18$\r\n• $h = 5$\r\n\r\n$$\\text{Median} = l + \\left( \\frac{\\frac{N}{2} - cf}{f} \\right) \\times h$$\r\n$$\\text{Median} = 145 + \\left( \\frac{25 - 11}{18} \\right) \\times 5 = 145 + \\frac{14 \\times 5}{18} = 145 + \\frac{70}{18} \\approx 145 + 3.89 = 148.89\\text{ cm}.$$	t	2024	Official Practice Paper	t	MATH	All India
SCI_2022_D_12	S10_CH15	\N	Standard (041)	Distinguish between biodegradable and non-biodegradable domestic substances with two examples of each. Explain how non-biodegradable waste damages terrestrial and aquatic ecosystems.	{"damage": "1.5 Marks", "distinction": "1.5 Marks"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	cafd64def4d0eb50616cd4b46a8e855fad36bb3ba773f1bb2f1dea510fd58884	[]	1. Definitions: Biodegradable materials decompose naturally via microbial enzymatic action (e.g., fruit peels, paper); non-biodegradable substances cannot be degraded by microorganisms (e.g., plastic bottles, glass).\r\n2. Ecosystem Damage: Non-biodegradable plastics clog urban drains causing flooding, leach endocrine-disrupting chemicals into soil, and cause fatal intestinal impaction when ingested by marine fauna.	t	2022	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
CBSE_PRAC_STD_Q12	M10_CH04	Right Triangle Perimeter and Hypotenuse Quadratic Model	Standard	The hypotenuse of a right-angled triangle is $6\\text{ m}$ more than twice the shortest side. If the third side is $2\\text{ m}$ less than the hypotenuse, find the sides of the triangle.	[{"step": "Let shortest side be x. Hypotenuse = 2x + 6; third side = (2x + 6) - 2 = 2x + 4", "marks": 1.0}, {"step": "Apply Pythagoras Theorem: x^2 + (2x + 4)^2 = (2x + 6)^2", "marks": 1.5}, {"step": "Expand: x^2 + 4x^2 + 16x + 16 = 4x^2 + 24x + 36 => x^2 - 8x - 20 = 0", "marks": 1.0}, {"step": "Factorise (x - 10)(x + 2) = 0; discard x = -2; sides are 10 m, 24 m, and 26 m", "marks": 1.5}]	5.0	Word Problems	Analysing	Hard	\N	["CBSE_Practice_Paper", "Standard_041", "Section_D"]	Let the shortest side of the right triangle be $x\\text{ meters}$.\r\n• Hypotenuse $= (2x + 6)\\text{ m}$.\r\n• Third side $= (2x + 6) - 2 = (2x + 4)\\text{ m}$.\r\n\r\nBy Pythagoras Theorem:\r\n$$(\\text{Hypotenuse})^2 = (\\text{Shortest Side})^2 + (\\text{Third Side})^2$$\r\n$$(2x + 6)^2 = x^2 + (2x + 4)^2$$\r\n$$4x^2 + 24x + 36 = x^2 + 4x^2 + 16x + 16$$\r\n$$x^2 - 8x - 20 = 0$$\r\nFactorising:\r\n$$x^2 - 10x + 2x - 20 = 0$$\r\n$$(x - 10)(x + 2) = 0 \\implies x = 10 \\text{ or } x = -2$$\r\nSince length cannot be negative, $x = -2$ is discarded.\r\n• Shortest side $= 10\\text{ m}$.\r\n• Third side $= 2(10) + 4 = 24\\text{ m}$.\r\n• Hypotenuse $= 2(10) + 6 = 26\\text{ m}$.	t	2024	Official Practice Paper	t	MATH	All India
CBSE_PRAC_STD_Q13	M10_CH09	Two Pillars of Equal Height on Road Width Model	Standard	Two poles of equal heights are standing opposite each other on either side of the road, which is $80\\text{ m}$ wide. From a point between them on the road, the angles of elevation of the top of the poles are $60^\\circ$ and $30^\\circ$ respectively. Find the height of the poles and the distances of the point from the poles. (Take $\\sqrt{3} = 1.732$)	[{"step": "Let height of poles be h; point on road divides 80 m into x and (80 - x)", "marks": 1.0}, {"step": "In Delta 1: tan 60 = h / x => h = x*sqrt(3)", "marks": 1.0}, {"step": "In Delta 2: tan 30 = h / (80 - x) => 1/sqrt(3) = (x*sqrt(3))/(80 - x)", "marks": 1.5}, {"step": "Solve: 80 - x = 3x => 4x = 80 => x = 20 m; other distance = 60 m", "marks": 1.0}, {"step": "Height h = 20*sqrt(3) = 34.64 m", "marks": 0.5}]	5.0	Word Problems	Analysing	Hard	\N	["CBSE_Practice_Paper", "Standard_041", "Section_D"]	Let $AB$ and $CD$ be the two poles of equal height $h$.\r\nThe road width is $BD = 80\\text{ m}$.\r\nLet $P$ be the observation point on the road such that $BP = x\\text{ m}$ and $PD = (80 - x)\\text{ m}$.\r\n$\\angle APB = 60^\\circ$ and $\\angle CPD = 30^\\circ$.\r\n\r\n1. In right $\\Delta ABP$:\r\n$$\\tan 60^\\circ = \\frac{h}{x} \\implies \\sqrt{3} = \\frac{h}{x} \\implies h = x\\sqrt{3} \\quad \\text{--- (1)}$$\r\n\r\n2. In right $\\Delta CDP$:\r\n$$\\tan 30^\\circ = \\frac{h}{80 - x} \\implies \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} = \\frac{h}{80 - x} \\implies h = \\frac{80 - x}{\\sqrt{3}} \\quad \\text{--- (2)}$$\r\n\r\nEquating (1) and (2):\r\n$$x\\sqrt{3} = \\frac{80 - x}{\\sqrt{3}}$$\r\n$$3x = 80 - x$$\r\n$$4x = 80 \\implies x = 20\\text{ m}.$$\r\nDistance from the first pole $= 20\\text{ m}$.\r\nDistance from the second pole $= 80 - 20 = 60\\text{ m}$.\r\nHeight of the poles:\r\n$$h = 20\\sqrt{3} = 20 \\times 1.732 = 34.64\\text{ meters}.$$	t	2024	Official Practice Paper	t	MATH	All India
CBSE_PRAC_STD_Q14	M10_CH07	Sports Day Grid Coordinate Distance and Section Flag Model	Standard	To conduct Sports Day activities in a rectangular shaped school ground $ABCD$, lines have been drawn with chalk powder at a distance of $1\\text{ m}$ each. $100$ flower pots have been placed at a distance of $1\\text{ m}$ from each other along $AD$. Niharika runs $\\frac{1}{4}^{\\text{th}}$ the distance $AD$ on the 2nd line and posts a green flag. Preet runs $\\frac{1}{5}^{\\text{th}}$ the distance $AD$ on the 8th line and posts a red flag.\r\n(i) Find the coordinates of the green flag ($G$) and the red flag ($R$). (1 Mark)\r\n(ii) What is the distance between both flags? (1.5 Marks)\r\n(iii) If Rashmi has to post a blue flag exactly halfway between the line segment joining the two flags, where should she post it? (1.5 Marks)	[{"step": "(i) G = (2, (1/4)*100) = (2, 25); R = (8, (1/5)*100) = (8, 20)", "marks": 1.0}, {"step": "(ii) Distance GR = sqrt((8 - 2)^2 + (20 - 25)^2) = sqrt(36 + 25) = sqrt(61) meters", "marks": 1.5}, {"step": "(iii) Midpoint = ((2 + 8)/2, (25 + 20)/2) = (5, 22.5) => 5th line at 22.5 m", "marks": 1.5}]	4.0	Case Study	Applying	Medium	\N	["CBSE_Practice_Paper", "Standard_041", "Section_E", "Case_Study"]	(i) Total distance $AD = 100\\text{ m}$.\r\n• Niharika’s position $G$: $x = 2$, $y = \\frac{1}{4} \\times 100 = 25 \\implies G(2, 25)$.\r\n• Preet’s position $R$: $x = 8$, $y = \\frac{1}{5} \\times 100 = 20 \\implies R(8, 20)$.\r\n\r\n(ii) Distance between $G$ and $R$:\r\n$$GR = \\sqrt{(8 - 2)^2 + (20 - 25)^2} = \\sqrt{6^2 + (-5)^2} = \\sqrt{36 + 25} = \\sqrt{61}\\text{ meters} \\approx 7.81\\text{ m}.$$\r\n\r\n(iii) Halfway position (Midpoint of $GR$):\r\n$$M = \\left(\\frac{x_1 + x_2}{2}, \\frac{y_1 + y_2}{2}\\right) = \\left(\\frac{2 + 8}{2}, \\frac{25 + 20}{2}\\right) = (5, 22.5).$$\r\nRashmi should post the blue flag on the **5th line** at a distance of **$22.5\\text{ m}$**. 	t	2024	Official Practice Paper	t	MATH	All India
CBSE_PRAC_STD_Q15	M10_CH12	Cylindrical Circus Tent with Conical Roof Canvas Model	Standard	A circus tent is cylindrical up to a height of $3\\text{ m}$ and conical above it. The total height of the tent is $13.5\\text{ m}$ and the radius of its base is $14\\text{ m}$.\r\n(i) Find the slant height of the conical roof. (1.5 Marks)\r\n(ii) Find the total area of the canvas required to make the tent. (Take $\\pi = \\frac{22}{7}$) (2 Marks)\r\n(iii) If $1\\text{ m}^2$ of canvas costs ₹$100$, find the total cost of canvas used. (0.5 Marks)	[{"step": "(i) Conical height H_cone = 13.5 - 3 = 10.5 m; Slant height l = sqrt((10.5)^2 + 14^2) = sqrt(110.25 + 196) = sqrt(306.25) = 17.5 m", "marks": 1.5}, {"step": "(ii) Total Canvas = Cylinder CSA + Conical CSA = 2*pi*r*h + pi*r*l = pi*r(2h + l) = (22/7)*14*(2(3) + 17.5) = 44 * 23.5 = 1034 m^2", "marks": 2.0}, {"step": "(iii) Cost = 1034 * 100 = ₹1,03,400", "marks": 0.5}]	4.0	Case Study	Applying	Medium	\N	["CBSE_Practice_Paper", "Standard_041", "Section_E", "Case_Study"]	Given:\r\n• Base radius $r = 14\\text{ m}$\r\n• Height of cylinder $h = 3\\text{ m}$\r\n• Total height $= 13.5\\text{ m} \\implies$ Height of conical roof $H_{\\text{cone}} = 13.5 - 3 = 10.5\\text{ m}$\r\n\r\n(i) Slant height of the cone ($l$):\r\n$$l = \\sqrt{r^2 + H_{\\text{cone}}^2} = \\sqrt{14^2 + (10.5)^2} = \\sqrt{196 + 110.25} = \\sqrt{306.25} = 17.5\\text{ m}.$$\r\n\r\n(ii) Total canvas area required:\r\n$$\\text{Area} = \\text{CSA of Cylinder} + \\text{CSA of Cone}$$\r\n$$= 2\\pi r h + \\pi r l = \\pi r (2h + l)$$\r\n$$= \\frac{22}{7} \\times 14 \\times [2(3) + 17.5]$$\r\n$$= 44 \\times [6 + 17.5] = 44 \\times 23.5 = 1034\\text{ m}^2.$$\r\n\r\n(iii) Total cost at ₹$100\\text{ per m}^2$:\r\n$$\\text{Cost} = 1034 \\times 100 = ₹1,03,400.$$	t	2024	Official Practice Paper	t	MATH	All India
