CBSE_SQP_2025_Q06	M10_CH10	Tangent Segment Perimeter Relation	Standard	A quadrilateral $ABCD$ is drawn to circumscribe a circle. Prove that $AB + CD = AD + BC$.	[{"step": "State equal tangent lengths: AP = AS, BP = BQ, CR = CQ, DR = DS", "marks": 1.0}, {"step": "Sum all 4 equations: (AP + BP) + (CR + DR) = (AS + DS) + (BQ + CQ) => AB + CD = AD + BC", "marks": 1.0}]	2.0	Short Answer	Analysing	Medium	\N	["CBSE_SQP_2025", "Official_Sample_Paper", "Section_B"]	Let the circle touch sides $AB, BC, CD, DA$ at $P, Q, R, S$ respectively.\r\nBy Theorem 10.2:\r\n$$AP = AS \\quad \\text{--- (1)}$$\r\n$$BP = BQ \\quad \\text{--- (2)}$$\r\n$$CR = CQ \\quad \\text{--- (3)}$$\r\n$$DR = DS \\quad \\text{--- (4)}$$\r\nAdding (1), (2), (3), and (4):\r\n$$(AP + BP) + (CR + DR) = (AS + DS) + (BQ + CQ)$$\r\n$$AB + CD = AD + BC.$$\r\nHence proved. $\\blacksquare$	t	2025	Official SQP	t	MATH	All India
SST_2022_D_05	SS10_HIS_02	\N	Standard (041)	Explain the circumstances that compelled Mahatma Gandhi to call off the Non-Cooperation Movement in February 1922.	{"reason": "1 Mark", "incident": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	cfd7ea6239131a408b0e7e424299839809381fb3a78e4ff69fdf1f18c436e670	[]	In February 1922, a peaceful demonstration turned violent at Chauri Chaura (Gorakhpur, UP), leading to the torching of a police station and the death of 22 policemen. Believing satyagrahis were not yet disciplined in non-violence, Gandhi suspended the movement.	t	2022	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SST_2022_D_06	SS10_POL_04	\N	Standard (041)	Distinguish between a National Political Party and a Recognized State Political Party in India on the basis of Election Commission criteria.	{"state": "1 Mark", "national": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	e4330295643f425a09368a63782aa63af9fe222288de599425ed1dc88eacf728	[]	1. National Party: Must secure at least 6% of valid votes in Lok Sabha elections or Assembly elections in four states, and win at least 4 Lok Sabha seats.\r\n2. State Party: Must secure at least 6% of total votes in a state Legislative Assembly election and win at least 2 Assembly seats.	t	2022	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SST_2022_D_07	SS10_ECO_03	\N	Standard (041)	Why do rural moneylenders charge high interest rates while commercial banks offer cheaper loans? State two institutional reasons.	{"rbi": "1 Mark", "collateral": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	bc17668ee10070122da58ec4cd3cdd79079887bc31461d4dded3c1dab9731dd8	[]	1. Lack of Collateral & Default Risk: Informal moneylenders advance loans without formal assets or income records, absorbing higher default risk and charging 30–60% interest.\r\n2. Regulatory Oversight: Commercial banks operate under RBI interest caps and statutory capital reserves, reducing rates.	t	2022	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SST_2022_D_08	SS10_HIS_02	\N	Standard (041)	How did the First World War create new economic and political crises in colonial India? Explain three factors.	{"taxation": "1 Mark", "inflation": "1 Mark", "recruitment": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	d752efb65db21de1952717e2ee09ea230dfc9b7f6a45a8b4921ecc2adb241221	[]	1. War Expenditure & Taxation: The British colonial government increased defense expenditures, funded by war loans and increased customs duties and income taxes.\r\n2. Price Inflation: Commodity prices doubled between 1913 and 1918, causing economic distress for common citizens.\r\n3. Forced Military Recruitment: Forced conscription in rural districts generated widespread discontent among villagers.	t	2022	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SST_2022_D_09	SS10_GEO_06	\N	Standard (041)	Why is the IT and electronics software industry crucial for India socio-economic growth? Highlight three contributions.	{"forex": "1 Mark", "employment": "1 Mark", "urbanization": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	6b424c4f46cbbbaee507d3a130bc38106cd293975fa7f98923567c0203f4cf54	[]	1. Employment Generation: Employed over 4 million professionals, with women comprising over 30% of the workforce.\r\n2. Foreign Exchange Revenue: Serves as a major export earner through software exports and global enterprise support.\r\n3. Tier-2 Urbanization: Stimulated infrastructure development in tech hubs beyond tier-1 cities (Pune, Hyderabad, Kochi, Indore).	t	2022	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SST_2022_D_10	SS10_POL_04	\N	Standard (041)	Describe the challenges faced by political parties in India regarding: (i) Lack of internal democracy, (ii) Dynastic succession, (iii) Money and muscle power.	{"dynastic": "1 Mark", "internal": "1 Mark", "money_muscle": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	9377c7b9ea842fb146891e7725907d36ac3aacb8818a0394fd55a77377d051c2	[]	1. Lack of Internal Democracy: Decisions are concentrated among central leaders; parties rarely hold regular organizational elections or maintain open membership registers.\r\n2. Dynastic Succession: Top leadership posts are often passed down within single families, limiting meritocratic advancement.\r\n3. Money & Muscle Power: Parties increasingly nominate wealthy candidates or individuals with criminal backgrounds to secure election funding and win votes.	t	2022	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SST_2022_D_11	SS10_ECO_04	\N	Standard (041)	How have improvements in information and communication technology (IT) accelerated the process of globalization? Illustrate with two examples.	{"examples": "1.5 Marks", "tech_role": "1.5 Marks"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	438e396334ff645e43d81ff165ba6a693f12deb3e47f1cb2101c5d6a23952b10	[]	1. Real-time Communication: Fiber-optic cables, mobile telephony, and satellite internet allow immediate information transfer, remote work coordination, and access to international pricing.\r\n2. Global Banking & Ordering: E-commerce platforms allow cross-border transactions and ordering. Example: A London publication company coordinates graphic design and editorial proofreading with Bangalore IT firms in real time.	t	2022	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
CBSE_SQP_2025_Q07	M10_CH14	Complementary Event Number Cards Calculation	Standard	A box contains $90$ discs numbered from $1$ to $90$. One disc is drawn at random. Find the probability that it bears:\r\n(i) A two-digit number.\r\n(ii) A number divisible by $5$.	[{"step": "Total outcomes n(S) = 90", "marks": 0.5}, {"step": "(i) Two-digit numbers: 10 to 90 => n = 81 => P = 81/90 = 9/10", "marks": 0.75}, {"step": "(ii) Multiples of 5: 5, 10, ..., 90 => n = 18 => P = 18/90 = 1/5", "marks": 0.75}]	2.0	Short Answer	Understanding	Easy	\N	["CBSE_SQP_2025", "Official_Sample_Paper", "Section_B"]	Total number of discs $n(S) = 90$.\r\n\r\n(i) Single digit numbers are $1$ through $9$ ($9$ discs).\r\n$$\\text{Two-digit numbers} = 90 - 9 = 81$$\r\n$$P(\\text{Two-digit number}) = \\frac{81}{90} = \\frac{9}{10}.$$\r\n\r\n(ii) Numbers divisible by $5$:\r\n$$E_2 = \\{5, 10, 15, \\dots, 90\\} \\implies n(E_2) = \\frac{90}{5} = 18$$\r\n$$P(\\text{Divisible by } 5) = \\frac{18}{90} = \\frac{1}{5}.$$	t	2025	Official SQP	t	MATH	All India
SST_2022_D_12	SS10_GEO_07	\N	Standard (041)	Explain why roadways hold an advantage over railways in India. State three reasons.	{"capital": "1 Mark", "topography": "1 Mark", "door_to_door": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	c7552ebaeb83aab4d96af8d85d1ac2481f7a1e79eb27306aeaa95be2aa6f2249	[]	1. Lower Construction Capital: Constructing roads requires less capital expenditure per kilometer compared to railway lines.\r\n2. Topographical Adaptability: Roadways navigate steeper gradients and rugged terrain (such as the Himalayas) where laying rail lines is impractical.\r\n3. Door-to-Door Logistics: Roads provide direct door-to-door transit, reducing transshipment handling costs for perishable agricultural products.	t	2022	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SST_2022_D_13	SS10_HIS_02	\N	Standard (041)	How did cultural processes, symbols, folklore, and icons instill a sense of collective belonging among Indians during the national independence movement?	{"flags": "1.5 Marks", "hymns": "1 Mark", "folklore": "1 Mark", "bharatmata": "1.5 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	96de0bd24cf0fd2ec36b532cfea214c7a6de3fc0f64f7261a08f0eb8dcfbd1dc	[]	1. Image of Bharat Mata: First created by Bankim Chandra Chattopadhyay and painted by Abanindranath Tagore; represented an ascetic, composed mother figure inspiring devotion.\r\n2. National Song & Hymns: 'Vande Mataram' included in 'Anandamath' became a common rallying anthem during Swadeshi demonstrations.\r\n3. Revival of Regional Folklore: Nationalists collected folk ballads, legends, and tales (e.g., Rabindranath Tagore in Bengal, Natesa Sastri in Madras) to restore pride in traditional heritage.\r\n4. Nationalist Flags: The Swadeshi tricolor (red, green, yellow with eight lotuses) and Gandhi's 1921 Swaraj flag (red, green, white with spinning wheel) symbolized defiance and unity.	t	2022	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SST_2022_D_14	SS10_POL_05	\N	Standard (041)	Evaluate why democracy is considered a superior form of government compared to any autocratic, monarchical, or dictatorial regime.	{"five_points": "5 Marks (1 Mark each for distinct dimension)"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	ded992431bee9fbca6bce6951de6540a60fbca7a7384c74618f31076fe59dc10	[]	1. Promotes Citizen Equality: Recognizes fundamental rights and equal voting weight regardless of caste, gender, or wealth.\r\n2. Enhances Individual Dignity: Protects personal freedoms and minority identities through constitutional safeguards.\r\n3. Improves Decision Quality: Deliberation and debate across legislative chambers reduce arbitrary policymaking.\r\n4. Accommodation of Social Diversity: Institutional mechanisms negotiate internal social, ethnic, and religious conflicts peacefully.\r\n5. Mechanism to Correct Errors: Public critique, judicial review, and regular elections allow leadership adjustments without systemic collapse.	t	2022	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SCI_2018_D_03	S10_CH03	\N	Standard (041)	Which metal among the following is the poorest conductor of heat?\r\n(A) Silver\r\n(B) Copper\r\n(C) Lead\r\n(D) Aluminium	{"answer": "1 Mark for Option (C)"}	1.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	65373022a03b924e116c9f207cbc72f0b8b4e3ae7f3da7f19a31b0651205fd7d	[]	Correct Option: (C)\r\nLead (Pb) and Mercury (Hg) are comparatively the poorest conductors of heat among metals (Silver and Copper are the best).	t	2018	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
SST_2022_D_15	SS10_ECO_04	\N	Standard (041)	What was the New Economic Policy (NEP) introduced in India in 1991? Explain its three pillars: Liberalization, Privatization, and Globalization (LPG).	{"lpg": "4 Marks (1.33 Marks per pillar)", "context": "1 Mark"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	75a174f5281dcd9b4906ec95d51c21af5a5e2e794eed9de70c26527bf2da97f7	[]	1. Background: In July 1991, India enacted structural reforms to manage a balance-of-payments crisis and reduce industrial stagnation.\r\n2. The Three LPG Pillars:\r\n- Liberalization: Dismantled industrial licensing ("License Raj"), eased import restrictions, and relaxed quotas.\r\n- Privatization: Disinvested government equity in non-strategic public sector enterprises (PSUs) and opened closed sectors to private investment.\r\n- Globalization: Reduced customs tariffs, opened sectors to Foreign Direct Investment (FDI), and unified currency conversion mechanisms.	t	2022	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SST_2020_AI_15	SS10_ECO_04	\N	Standard (041)	Explain five negative impacts of globalization on domestic industries, small producers, and unorganized workers in India.	{"five_negatives": "5 Marks (1 Mark per distinct argument)"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	d64102d20717c0a2b9e078e43397d6f9b26b441f6c504ba452067d80b6e262aa	[]	1. Closure of Small Manufacturing Units: Cheap manufactured imports (batteries, plastic toys, glassware) forced thousands of small domestic enterprises to shut down.\r\n2. Increased Job Insecurity: Global competition led employers to replace permanent staff with temporary, casual, or subcontracted labor to minimize costs.\r\n3. Deterioration of Labor Standards: Competitive export pressures led to compromised working conditions, mandatory overtime, and evasion of statutory welfare laws.\r\n4. Agricultural Distress: Developing country farmers struggled to compete against subsidized agricultural produce imported from developed economies.\r\n5. Regional Economic Disparities: Foreign direct investments and infrastructural advancements concentrated in wealthy urban states, worsening rural-urban inequality.	t	2020	All India Set 1	f	SST	All India
CBSE_SQP_2025_Q01	M10_CH01	Prime Exponents in Factorization	Standard	If $a = 2^3 \\times 3$, $b = 2 \\times 3 \\times 5$, $c = 3^n \\times 5$ and $\\text{LCM}(a, b, c) = 2^3 \\times 3^2 \\times 5$, then the value of $n$ is:\r\n(A) $1$\r\n(B) $2$\r\n(C) $3$\r\n(D) $4$	[{"step": "State rule: power of 3 in LCM is maximum of powers of 3 in a, b, c", "marks": 0.5}, {"step": "max(1, 1, n) = 2 => n = 2 (Option B)", "marks": 0.5}]	1.0	Objective	Analysing	Medium	\N	["CBSE_SQP_2025", "Official_Sample_Paper", "Section_A"]	The prime factor powers of $3$ in the given numbers are:\r\n• In $a$: power of $3$ is $1$.\r\n• In $b$: power of $3$ is $1$.\r\n• In $c$: power of $3$ is $n$.\r\n\r\nThe LCM takes the highest power of each prime factor:\r\n$$\\text{Power of } 3 \\text{ in LCM} = \\max(1, 1, n) = 2 \\implies n = 2.$$\r\nTherefore, Option **(B)** is correct.	t	2025	Official SQP	t	MATH	All India
CBSE_SQP_2025_Q02	M10_CH07	Collinear Points and Section Relation	Standard	The point on the $x$-axis which divides the line segment joining $(2, -3)$ and $(5, 6)$ in the ratio $1 : 2$ has coordinates:\r\n(A) $(3, 0)$\r\n(B) $(0, 3)$\r\n(C) $(1, 0)$\r\n(D) $(0, 0)$	[{"step": "Apply section formula for x = (1(5) + 2(2))/(1 + 2) = (5 + 4)/3 = 3", "marks": 0.5}, {"step": "y = (1(6) + 2(-3))/(1 + 2) = (6 - 6)/3 = 0; conclude (3, 0) (Option A)", "marks": 0.5}]	1.0	Objective	Applying	Easy	\N	["CBSE_SQP_2025", "Official_Sample_Paper", "Section_A"]	Using Section Formula with $m_1 : m_2 = 1 : 2$, $(x_1, y_1) = (2, -3)$ and $(x_2, y_2) = (5, 6)$:\r\n$$x = \\frac{1(5) + 2(2)}{1 + 2} = \\frac{5 + 4}{3} = \\frac{9}{3} = 3$$\r\n$$y = \\frac{1(6) + 2(-3)}{1 + 2} = \\frac{6 - 6}{3} = 0$$\r\nCoordinates are $(3, 0)$. Option **(A)** is correct.	t	2025	Official SQP	t	MATH	All India
CBSE_SQP_2025_Q03	M10_CH06	Perimeter Ratio of Similar Triangles	Standard	If $\\Delta ABC \\sim \\Delta DEF$ such that $2AB = DE$ and $BC = 8\\text{ cm}$, then the length of $EF$ is:\r\n(A) $16\\text{ cm}$\r\n(B) $12\\text{ cm}$\r\n(C) $4\\text{ cm}$\r\n(D) $8\\text{ cm}$	[{"step": "State similarity ratio: AB / DE = BC / EF", "marks": 0.5}, {"step": "Substitute AB / (2AB) = 8 / EF => 1/2 = 8 / EF => EF = 16 cm (Option A)", "marks": 0.5}]	1.0	Objective	Understanding	Easy	\N	["CBSE_SQP_2025", "Official_Sample_Paper", "Section_A"]	Since $\\Delta ABC \\sim \\Delta DEF$:\r\n$$\\frac{AB}{DE} = \\frac{BC}{EF}$$\r\nGiven $DE = 2AB \\implies \\frac{AB}{DE} = \\frac{1}{2}$:\r\n$$\\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{8}{EF} \\implies EF = 16\\text{ cm}.$$\r\nOption **(A)** is correct.	t	2025	Official SQP	t	MATH	All India
CBSE_SQP_2025_Q04	M10_CH08	Sine Cosine Sum Ratio Identity	Standard	If $\\sin \\theta + \\cos \\theta = \\sqrt{2}\\cos \\theta$, then the value of $\\tan \\theta$ is:\r\n(A) $\\sqrt{2} - 1$\r\n(B) $\\sqrt{2} + 1$\r\n(C) $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}$\r\n(D) $\\sqrt{3}$	[{"step": "Divide both sides by cos theta: tan theta + 1 = sqrt(2)", "marks": 0.5}, {"step": "tan theta = sqrt(2) - 1 (Select Option A)", "marks": 0.5}]	1.0	Objective	Applying	Easy	\N	["CBSE_SQP_2025", "Official_Sample_Paper", "Section_A"]	Given:\r\n$$\\sin \\theta + \\cos \\theta = \\sqrt{2}\\cos \\theta$$\r\nDividing throughout by $\\cos \\theta$:\r\n$$\\frac{\\sin \\theta}{\\cos \\theta} + \\frac{\\cos \\theta}{\\cos \\theta} = \\sqrt{2}\\frac{\\cos \\theta}{\\cos \\theta}$$\r\n$$\\tan \\theta + 1 = \\sqrt{2} \\implies \\tan \\theta = \\sqrt{2} - 1.$$\r\nOption **(A)** is correct.	t	2025	Official SQP	t	MATH	All India
CBSE_SQP_2025_Q05	M10_CH03	System Consistency and Infinitely Many Solutions	Standard	Find the values of $a$ and $b$ for which the pair of linear equations $2x + 3y = 7$ and $(a + b)x + (2a - b)y = 21$ has infinitely many solutions.	[{"step": "State condition: a1/a2 = b1/b2 = c1/c2 => 2/(a+b) = 3/(2a-b) = 7/21 = 1/3", "marks": 1.0}, {"step": "Solve: a + b = 6 and 2a - b = 9 => 3a = 15 => a = 5, b = 1", "marks": 1.0}]	2.0	Short Answer	Applying	Medium	\N	["CBSE_SQP_2025", "Official_Sample_Paper", "Section_B"]	For infinitely many solutions:\r\n$$\\frac{a_1}{a_2} = \\frac{b_1}{b_2} = \\frac{c_1}{c_2}$$\r\n$$\\frac{2}{a + b} = \\frac{3}{2a - b} = \\frac{7}{21} = \\frac{1}{3}$$\r\n• $\\frac{2}{a + b} = \\frac{1}{3} \\implies a + b = 6 \\quad \\text{--- (1)}$\r\n• $\\frac{3}{2a - b} = \\frac{1}{3} \\implies 2a - b = 9 \\quad \\text{--- (2)}$\r\n\r\nAdding (1) and (2):\r\n$$3a = 15 \\implies a = 5$$\r\nSubstitute $a = 5$ into (1):\r\n$$5 + b = 6 \\implies b = 1.$$\r\nValues are $a = 5$ and $b = 1$.	t	2025	Official SQP	t	MATH	All India
SCI_2019_D_01	S10_CH01	\N	Standard (041)	When silver chloride is exposed to sunlight for a long duration, it turns grey due to:\r\n(A) The formation of silver by decomposition of silver chloride\r\n(B) Sublimation of silver chloride\r\n(C) Evolution of chlorine gas from silver chloride\r\n(D) Oxidation of silver chloride	{"answer": "1 Mark for Option (A)"}	1.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	4323dd57b071db6b98bc0d21022a9c0f2c9859c58300a28dfcf999f520ca4c6b	[]	Correct Option: (A)\r\n2AgCl(s) --(Sunlight)--> 2Ag(s) [Grey metallic silver] + Cl2(g).	t	2019	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
SCI_2019_D_02	S10_CH02	\N	Standard (041)	Which of the following salts does NOT contain water of crystallization?\r\n(A) Blue vitriol\r\n(B) Baking soda\r\n(C) Washing soda\r\n(D) Gypsum	{"answer": "1 Mark for Option (B)"}	1.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	53db19965933849a8a390f25de6317935c3640c6bee04ce03c1a9d99a557d9d9	[]	Correct Option: (B)\r\nBaking soda is anhydrous sodium hydrogen carbonate (NaHCO3), whereas Blue vitriol (CuSO4·5H2O), Washing soda (Na2CO3·10H2O), and Gypsum (CaSO4·2H2O) all contain water of crystallization.	t	2019	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
CBSE_SQP_2025_Q08	M10_CH01	Proof of Root 3 Irrationality	Standard	Prove that $\\sqrt{3}$ is an irrational number by contradiction.	[{"step": "Assume sqrt(3) = a/b with coprime integers a, b (b != 0)", "marks": 0.5}, {"step": "3b^2 = a^2 => 3 divides a^2 => 3 divides a (a = 3c)", "marks": 1.0}, {"step": "3b^2 = 9c^2 => b^2 = 3c^2 => 3 divides b", "marks": 1.0}, {"step": "State contradiction to coprimality and conclude sqrt(3) is irrational", "marks": 0.5}]	3.0	Core Proofs	Analysing	Medium	\N	["CBSE_SQP_2025", "Official_Sample_Paper", "Section_C"]	Assume to the contrary that $\\sqrt{3}$ is rational.\r\nThen $\\sqrt{3} = \\frac{a}{b}$ where $a$ and $b$ are coprime integers ($b \\neq 0$).\r\nSquaring both sides:\r\n$$3 = \\frac{a^2}{b^2} \\implies 3b^2 = a^2 \\quad \\text{--- (1)}$$\r\nSince $3$ divides $3b^2$, $3$ divides $a^2$, which implies **$3$ divides $a$**.\r\nLet $a = 3c$ for some integer $c$.\r\nSubstituting into (1):\r\n$$3b^2 = (3c)^2 = 9c^2 \\implies b^2 = 3c^2$$\r\nThis implies **$3$ divides $b$**.\r\nThus, both $a$ and $b$ share a common factor of $3$, contradicting the premise that $a$ and $b$ are coprime.\r\nHence, $\\sqrt{3}$ is irrational. $\\blacksquare$	t	2025	Official SQP	t	MATH	All India
CBSE_SQP_2025_Q09	M10_CH02	Quadratic Zeroes Algebraic Verification	Standard	Find the zeroes of the quadratic polynomial $6x^2 - 3 - 7x$ and verify the relationship between the zeroes and the coefficients.	[{"step": "Rearrange to 6x^2 - 7x - 3 = 0 and factorise to (2x - 3)(3x + 1) = 0 => zeroes are 3/2 and -1/3", "marks": 1.0}, {"step": "Verify sum: 3/2 + (-1/3) = 7/6 = -b/a = -(-7)/6", "marks": 1.0}, {"step": "Verify product: (3/2)*(-1/3) = -1/2 = c/a = -3/6", "marks": 1.0}]	3.0	Core Proofs	Applying	Easy	\N	["CBSE_SQP_2025", "Official_Sample_Paper", "Section_C"]	Rewrite in standard form:\r\n$$P(x) = 6x^2 - 7x - 3 = 0$$\r\nSplitting the middle term:\r\n$$6x^2 - 9x + 2x - 3 = 0$$\r\n$$3x(2x - 3) + 1(2x - 3) = 0$$\r\n$$(2x - 3)(3x + 1) = 0 \\implies \\alpha = \\frac{3}{2}, \\quad \\beta = -\\frac{1}{3}.$$\r\n\r\nVerification:\r\n• Sum of zeroes:\r\n$$\\alpha + \\beta = \\frac{3}{2} - \\frac{1}{3} = \\frac{9 - 2}{6} = \\frac{7}{6}$$\r\n$$-\\frac{b}{a} = -\\frac{-7}{6} = \\frac{7}{6} \\quad (\\text{Verified})$$\r\n• Product of zeroes:\r\n$$\\alpha \\beta = \\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right) = -\\frac{1}{2}$$\r\n$$\\frac{c}{a} = \\frac{-3}{6} = -\\frac{1}{2} \\quad (\\text{Verified}).$$	t	2025	Official SQP	t	MATH	All India
CBSE_SQP_2025_Q10	M10_CH08	Trigonometric Square Root Rationalisation	Standard	Prove that:\r\n$$\\sqrt{\\frac{1 + \\sin A}{1 - \\sin A}} = \\sec A + \\tan A$$	[{"step": "Multiply numerator and denominator by (1 + sin A): sqrt((1 + sin A)^2 / (1 - sin^2 A))", "marks": 1.0}, {"step": "Simplify denominator to cos^2 A: sqrt((1 + sin A)^2 / cos^2 A) = (1 + sin A)/cos A", "marks": 1.0}, {"step": "Split terms: 1/cos A + sin A/cos A = sec A + tan A", "marks": 1.0}]	3.0	Core Proofs	Applying	Easy	\N	["CBSE_SQP_2025", "Official_Sample_Paper", "Section_C"]	$$\\text{LHS} = \\sqrt{\\frac{1 + \\sin A}{1 - \\sin A}}$$\r\nMultiplying numerator and denominator inside the square root by $(1 + \\sin A)$:\r\n$$= \\sqrt{\\frac{(1 + \\sin A)(1 + \\sin A)}{(1 - \\sin A)(1 + \\sin A)}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{(1 + \\sin A)^2}{1 - \\sin^2 A}}$$\r\nSince $1 - \\sin^2 A = \\cos^2 A$:\r\n$$= \\sqrt{\\frac{(1 + \\sin A)^2}{\\cos^2 A}} = \\frac{1 + \\sin A}{\\cos A}$$\r\n$$= \\frac{1}{\\cos A} + \\frac{\\sin A}{\\cos A} = \\sec A + \\tan A = \\text{RHS}.$$\r\nHence proved. $\\blacksquare$	t	2025	Official SQP	t	MATH	All India
CBSE_SQP_2025_Q11	M10_CH13	Continuous Distribution Mode Evaluation	Standard	The following table shows the marks obtained by $100$ students in a class:\r\n$$\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|c|c|}\r\n\\hline\r\n\\text{Marks} & 0-20 & 20-40 & 40-60 & 60-80 & 80-100 \\\\\r\n\\hline\r\n\\text{No. of Students} & 10 & 35 & 52 & 61 & 38 \\\\\r\n\\hline\r\n\\end{array}$$\r\nFind the mode of the data.	[{"step": "Identify modal class 60-80 with highest frequency f1 = 61 (l = 60, f0 = 52, f2 = 38, h = 20)", "marks": 1.0}, {"step": "Apply formula: Mode = 60 + [(61 - 52)/(2(61) - 52 - 38)] * 20", "marks": 1.0}, {"step": "Calculate: 60 + (9 / (122 - 90)) * 20 = 60 + (9/32)*20 = 60 + 5.625 = 65.63 marks", "marks": 1.0}]	3.0	Core Proofs	Applying	Medium	\N	["CBSE_SQP_2025", "Official_Sample_Paper", "Section_C"]	The maximum frequency is $61$, corresponding to modal class **$60-80$**:\r\n• Lower limit $l = 60$\r\n• $f_1 = 61$\r\n• $f_0 = 52$\r\n• $f_2 = 38$\r\n• Class size $h = 20$\r\n\r\n$$\\text{Mode} = l + \\left(\\frac{f_1 - f_0}{2f_1 - f_0 - f_2}\\right) \\times h$$\r\n$$\\text{Mode} = 60 + \\left(\\frac{61 - 52}{2(61) - 52 - 38}\\right) \\times 20$$\r\n$$= 60 + \\left(\\frac{9}{122 - 90}\\right) \\times 20 = 60 + \\left(\\frac{9}{32}\\right) \\times 20 = 60 + \\frac{45}{8} = 60 + 5.625 = 65.63\\text{ marks}.$$	t	2025	Official SQP	t	MATH	All India
CBSE_SQP_2025_Q12	M10_CH09	Lighthouse Angles of Depression Moving Target Model	Standard	A straight highway leads to the foot of a tower. A man standing at the top of the tower observes a car at an angle of depression of $30^\\circ$, which is approaching the foot of the tower with a uniform speed. Six seconds later, the angle of depression of the car is found to be $60^\\circ$. Find the time taken by the car to reach the foot of the tower from this point.	[{"step": "Let tower height be h; car initial distance x1 = h*sqrt(3), final distance x2 = h/sqrt(3)", "marks": 1.5}, {"step": "Distance covered in 6 seconds: d = x1 - x2 = h*sqrt(3) - h/sqrt(3) = (2h)/sqrt(3)", "marks": 1.5}, {"step": "Speed v = d / 6 = (2h)/(6*sqrt(3)) = h/(3*sqrt(3))", "marks": 1.0}, {"step": "Time to cover remaining distance x2: t = x2 / v = (h/sqrt(3)) / (h/(3*sqrt(3))) = 3 seconds", "marks": 1.0}]	5.0	Word Problems	Analysing	Hard	\N	["CBSE_SQP_2025", "Official_Sample_Paper", "Section_D"]	Let $AB$ be the tower of height $h$. Let $C$ and $D$ be the two positions of the car.\r\n• Initial angle of depression at $C$ is $30^\\circ \\implies \\angle ACB = 30^\\circ$.\r\n• Angle of depression $6\\text{ seconds}$ later at $D$ is $60^\\circ \\implies \\angle ADB = 60^\\circ$.\r\n\r\n1. In right $\\Delta ABD$:\r\n$$\\tan 60^\\circ = \\frac{h}{BD} \\implies \\sqrt{3} = \\frac{h}{BD} \\implies BD = \\frac{h}{\\sqrt{3}}$$\r\n\r\n2. In right $\\Delta ABC$:\r\n$$\\tan 30^\\circ = \\frac{h}{BC} \\implies \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} = \\frac{h}{BC} \\implies BC = h\\sqrt{3}$$\r\n\r\n3. Distance covered in $6\\text{ seconds}$ ($CD$):\r\n$$CD = BC - BD = h\\sqrt{3} - \\frac{h}{\\sqrt{3}} = \\frac{3h - h}{\\sqrt{3}} = \\frac{2h}{\\sqrt{3}}$$\r\nSpeed of the car:\r\n$$v = \\frac{\\text{Distance}}{\\text{Time}} = \\frac{\\frac{2h}{\\sqrt{3}}}{6} = \\frac{h}{3\\sqrt{3}}\\text{ m/s}$$\r\n\r\n4. Time taken to reach the tower foot from $D$:\r\n$$t = \\frac{\\text{Distance } BD}{\\text{Speed } v} = \\frac{\\frac{h}{\\sqrt{3}}}{\\frac{h}{3\\sqrt{3}}} = \\frac{h}{\\sqrt{3}} \\times \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{h} = 3\\text{ seconds}.$$	t	2025	Official SQP	t	MATH	All India
