SCI_2022_AI_06	S10_CH08	\N	Standard (041)	Differentiate between binary fission and fragmentation with an example of an organism undergoing each.	{"fission": "1 Mark", "fragmentation": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	1306d53a6ea60ea8a1fdef3ea0ff60a77d094ba9b60bbd454a9aa70621c187df	[]	1. Binary Fission: A parent single-celled organism divides into two approximately equal daughter cells (e.g., Amoeba, Leishmania).\r\n2. Fragmentation: A multicellular organism with simple organization breaks into smaller pieces upon maturation, each piece growing into a complete individual (e.g., Spirogyra).	t	2022	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2022_AI_07	S10_CH12	\N	Standard (041)	Why are coils of electric toasters and electric irons made of an alloy rather than a pure metal? State two reasons.	{"oxidation": "1 Mark", "resistivity": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	ce412abc871b101c39bbf6b41a694ab3937d84d5216968c2ac4a6d96fc9b5f1d	[]	1. Higher Resistivity: Alloys have higher resistivity than their constituent pure metals, producing more heat per unit length.\r\n2. Resistance to Oxidation: Alloys do not oxidize or burn easily at elevated operational temperatures.	t	2022	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2022_AI_08	S10_CH04	\N	Standard (041)	What are covalent bonds? Explain why covalent compounds: (i) have low melting and boiling points, (ii) are poor conductors of electricity.	{"mp_bp": "1 Mark", "definition": "1 Mark", "conductivity": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	f43df039c8b18ff367e192e77b3b4f9adc7fd19dae441e84c5b71f4aec5c5ad1	[]	1. Definition: Chemical bonds formed by the mutual sharing of valence electron pairs between atoms.\r\n2. (i) Low MP/BP: Intermolecular forces between covalent molecules are weak, requiring minimal thermal energy to overcome during phase changes.\r\n   (ii) Poor Conductivity: Covalent compounds do not form free electrons or ions, so they cannot transport electric charge.	t	2022	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2022_AI_09	S10_CH08	\N	Standard (041)	Trace the changes an ovary and an ovule undergo after fertilization in angiosperm plants.	{"ovary": "1 Mark", "ovule": "1 Mark", "zygote": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	95f4c3c8187f856ff1843bb90383b5df0db89f4ee08c23dc9d979ee8d2019949	[]	1. Zygote to Embryo: The fertilized egg cell (zygote) divides mitotically to form an embryo within the ovule.\r\n2. Ovule to Seed: The primary endosperm nucleus develops into nutritional endosperm; the integuments of the ovule harden into a protective seed coat, forming the seed.\r\n3. Ovary to Fruit: The ovary walls thicken and ripen into the pericarp and fruit tissue, while petals and sepals wither and drop.	t	2022	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2022_AI_10	S10_CH09	\N	Standard (041)	Explain the chromosomal mechanism of sex determination in human beings with the help of a genetic cross diagram.	{"cross": "1.5 Marks", "mechanism": "1.5 Marks"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	2c3755c2057518c1a28d06245ab7ab81c870f9e2249eebd8c748ebd2ecfd2a07	[]	Human somatic cells have 23 pairs of chromosomes, including 22 pairs of autosomes and 1 pair of sex chromosomes.\r\n- Females possess XX sex chromosomes and produce one type of egg (22 + X).\r\n- Males possess XY sex chromosomes and produce two types of sperm in equal numbers: 50% carrying X (22 + X) and 50% carrying Y (22 + Y).\r\nWhen an X-carrying sperm fertilizes an egg, the zygote is XX (female). When a Y-carrying sperm fertilizes an egg, the zygote is XY (male).	t	2022	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2022_AI_11	S10_CH13	\N	Standard (041)	Describe an experiment using a compass needle to demonstrate that a magnetic field is produced around a straight current-carrying wire (Oersted experiment).	{"setup": "1 Mark", "inference": "1 Mark", "observation": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	668fc56adefd72cec078981f2122b13fc1235cdcacaeda35a3f03356cf34c3a4	[]	1. Setup: Place a straight copper wire parallel to and directly above a magnetic compass needle connected in circuit with a battery and key.\r\n2. Observation: When the circuit key is closed and current flows from South to North, the North pole of the compass needle deflects toward the West (SNOW rule).\r\n3. Deduction: Reversing current direction reverses the compass deflection; opening the switch returns the needle to the magnetic meridian, proving moving electric charges induce a magnetic field.	t	2022	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2022_AI_12	S10_CH15	\N	Standard (041)	Explain why food chains in terrestrial ecosystems typically consist of only 3 to 4 trophic levels.	{"ten_percent": "1.5 Marks", "energy_limitation": "1.5 Marks"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	0f98215b35d3f4b066e5d613137953a575e52c880028880aab02423fa4524e06	[]	According to Lindeman 10% law, only about 10% of the chemical energy stored in organic matter at each trophic level is transferred to the next level; approximately 90% is dissipated as metabolic heat and waste.\r\nBeyond 3 to 4 trophic transfers, the remaining available energy is insufficient to sustain viable populations of higher apex carnivores.	t	2022	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2022_AI_13	S10_CH12	\N	Standard (041)	(a) Derive the formula for equivalent resistance of two resistors R1 and R2 connected in parallel.\r\n(b) An electric lamp of resistance 20 ohms and a conductor of 4 ohms resistance are connected in series to a 6 V battery. Calculate: (i) total resistance, (ii) total circuit current, (iii) potential difference across the electric lamp.	{"numerical": "2.5 Marks", "derivation": "2.5 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	91a8ba20703f07187191f10a6fb5a3b642ca105eb8b0d2ed66b0eb240479d395	[]	(a) In parallel, voltage V across each resistor is identical, while total current I = I1 + I2.\r\nBy Ohm law: I1 = V / R1, I2 = V / R2.\r\nV / Rp = V / R1 + V / R2 -> 1 / Rp = 1 / R1 + 1 / R2.\r\n(b) Numerical:\r\n(i) Total series resistance Req = 20 + 4 = 24 ohms.\r\n(ii) Total current I = V / Req = 6 V / 24 ohms = 0.25 A.\r\n(iii) Potential difference across lamp V_lamp = I * R_lamp = 0.25 A * 20 ohms = 5.0 V.	t	2022	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
