SCI_2020_D_09	S10_CH06	\N	Standard (041)	Describe the mechanism of blood circulation in humans. Why is it termed 'double circulation'? What is its functional advantage in warm-blooded birds and mammals?	{"advantage": "1.5 Marks", "double_circ": "1.5 Marks"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	a4920611c3d53f979a3a62b03106e39a65d14eb8b693fabc524895c18f829094	[]	1. Double Circulation: Blood passes through the heart twice during each complete circuit of the body (Pulmonary circulation: Right ventricle -> Lungs -> Left atrium; Systemic circulation: Left ventricle -> Body -> Right atrium).\r\n2. Advantage: Complete separation of oxygenated and deoxygenated blood supports efficient oxygen supply, meeting high energy demands to maintain a constant body temperature.	t	2020	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
SCI_2020_D_10	S10_CH10	\N	Standard (041)	An object is placed at a distance of 12 cm in front of a concave mirror of focal length 15 cm. Find the image position, magnification, and describe its nature.	{"nature": "1 Mark", "position": "1 Mark", "magnification": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	084b842681259e4ab0c4e48514a3503553f47138197fe4ba64c8c2b4205bc4de	[]	Given: u = -12 cm, f = -15 cm.\r\nUsing mirror formula: 1/f = 1/v + 1/u\r\n-> 1/v = 1/f - 1/u = 1/(-15) - 1/(-12) = -1/15 + 1/12 = (-4 + 5)/60 = +1/60.\r\nv = +60 cm.\r\nMagnification: m = -v / u = -(+60) / (-12) = +5.0.\r\nImage is formed 60 cm behind the mirror; it is virtual, erect, and magnified 5 times.	t	2020	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
