SST_2019_D_06	SS10_POL_02	\N	Standard (041)	Distinguish between 'Coming Together' and 'Holding Together' federations with one country example of each.	{"coming": "1 Mark", "holding": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	fa74373b23046027dbeb71005eaa91e16cc6d82df77400da8369854bc18af987	[]	1. Coming Together: Independent sovereign states voluntarily form a larger federation to enhance security while pooling sovereignty (e.g., USA, Australia, Switzerland).\r\n2. Holding Together: A large sovereign country divides administrative authority between central governance and regional state units (e.g., India, Spain, Belgium).	t	2019	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SST_2019_D_07	SS10_GEO_02	\N	Standard (041)	What is 'Primitive Subsistence Farming' (slash and burn agriculture)? State two of its characteristics.	{"definition": "1 Mark", "characteristics": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	be8c28e17e5dd8b5afd56df9edf4c4f037dfd6f480b89f02caf97048d1af49d1	[]	1. Definition: Farming practiced on small patches of cleared forest land using primitive hand tools (hoe, dao, digging sticks) relying on family labor.\r\n2. Characteristics: (i) Relies on monsoon rainfall and natural soil fertility; (ii) Low productivity since farmers use no synthetic chemical fertilizers or irrigation machinery.	t	2019	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SST_2019_D_08	SS10_HIS_01	\N	Standard (041)	Explain three provisions of the Napoleonic Civil Code of 1804 that dismantled feudalism across Western Europe.	{"equality": "1 Mark", "property": "1 Mark", "feudalism": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	e1a6625ea0317f09414a9b2ef5ef3f39a039504d406bdc01f45a86844a9c061c	[]	1. Abolished Birth Privileges: Terminated feudal aristocratic exemptions and established legal equality for all male citizens.\r\n2. Property Rights: Secured the legal right to private property and standardized contract enforcement.\r\n3. Administrative Uniformity: Eliminated the feudal manorial dues system, freed peasants from serfdom, and abolished guild restrictions in towns.	t	2019	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SST_2019_D_09	SS10_ECO_02	\N	Standard (041)	Explain the differences between primary, secondary, and tertiary economic sectors with an example of an economic activity for each.	{"primary": "1 Mark", "tertiary": "1 Mark", "secondary": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	e1f61b8fa6917ef63b65cb41b621aa287b710d655cc1394f1e79a48e3fa8fcb9	[]	1. Primary Sector: Direct exploitation of natural ecological resources (e.g., Agriculture, Forestry, Fishing, Dairy farming).\r\n2. Secondary Sector: Industrial processing transforming primary raw commodities into manufactured consumer goods (e.g., Cotton textile weaving, Steel smelting).\r\n3. Tertiary Sector: Support services that facilitate production, logistics, and exchange (e.g., Transport, Banking, Telecommunications, Warehousing).	t	2019	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SST_2019_D_10	SS10_POL_03	\N	Standard (041)	Explain three ways through which women in India face discrimination and gender disadvantage in daily social and economic life.	{"literacy": "1 Mark", "sex_ratio": "1 Mark", "unpaid_labor": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	71655b359d3c7d2324749c219f4d320b341d375fce18e1fcf6087b00e937839d	[]	1. Educational Disparity: Female literacy (~65%) remains lower than male literacy (~82%) due to domestic work burdens and differential parental expenditure.\r\n2. Unpaid Domestic Labor: Household upkeep, cooking, and childcare performed by women are not accounted for as economic production in national income.\r\n3. Adverse Child Sex Ratio: Cultural preference for male offspring has resulted in female feticide and skewed child sex ratios across several regions.	t	2019	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SST_2019_D_11	SS10_ECO_03	\N	Standard (041)	What is collateral? Why do commercial banks demand collateral before sanctioning personal or commercial loans?	{"purpose": "1.5 Marks", "definition": "1.5 Marks"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	3814a8c457033edc53a5b1dda31475e03a272416ee7305cc74972dd715136a94	[]	1. Definition: An asset owned by the borrower (land, house, vehicle, livestock, bank deposits) pledged to the lender as a financial guarantee until the loan is fully repaid.\r\n2. Institutional Necessity: Protects the bank against borrower default. If the borrower fails to service the loan, the bank has the legal right to liquidate the collateral asset to recover its capital.	t	2019	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SST_2019_D_12	SS10_GEO_06	\N	Standard (041)	Why is the iron and steel industry referred to as a 'basic' or 'heavy' industry? State three reasons.	{"basic": "1 Mark", "raw_materials": "1 Mark", "finished_output": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	59dbaf817b9abd2abcd9a7e710814188b30f6c5771d3d26ba3fc7d548e38030c	[]	1. Feeds Other Manufacturing: It supplies essential machinery, tools, and raw metal feedstocks required by other manufacturing sectors (heavy engineering, automobiles, construction).\r\n2. Heavy Raw Materials: Relies on bulky, heavy, weight-losing raw materials (iron ore, coking coal, limestone).\r\n3. Bulky Finished Outputs: The finished products (beams, rails, sheets) are heavy and incur significant transportation costs.	t	2019	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SST_2019_D_13	SS10_HIS_02	\N	Standard (041)	Describe the course of the Non-Cooperation Movement in the countryside among: (a) Peasants of Awadh, (b) Tribal forest dwellers of the Gudem Hills in Andhra Pradesh.	{"awadh": "2.5 Marks", "gudem": "2.5 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	8c5434a9c4a3d4a9f810e0fd508a0b6207230345e33294b8474b7bd7fdb9e902	[]	(a) Awadh Peasants:\r\n- Organized under Baba Ramchandra against oppressive talukdars and landlords demanding extortionate rents and unpaid labor (begar).\r\n- Formed the Oudh Kisan Sabha in 1920 with Jawaharlal Nehru, setting up village branches and mounting social boycotts (nai-dhobi bandhs).\r\n(b) Gudem Hills Tribals:\r\n- Led by Alluri Sitaram Raju against colonial forest enclosures that barred cattle grazing and fuelwood collection.\r\n- Blended Khadi advocacy with armed guerrilla resistance, attacking police stations until Raju was captured and executed in 1924.	t	2019	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SST_2019_D_14	SS10_POL_02	\N	Standard (041)	Explain the constitutional provisions introduced by the 73rd Constitutional Amendment Act of 1992 to institutionalize local self-governance in India.	{"five_provisions": "5 Marks (1 Mark each for distinct provision)"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	cec5101d41854df11d9a2a3892787b600b7c26aaa313ae82b2106326d50f9df2	[]	1. Mandatory Periodic Elections: Mandated regular local panchayat elections every five years.\r\n2. Seat Reservations: Reserved seats for Scheduled Castes (SC) and Scheduled Tribes (ST) in proportion to their local population.\r\n3. Gender Representation: Mandated that at least one-third (33%) of all elected seats and leadership positions be reserved for women.\r\n4. State Election Commission: Created independent State Election Commissions to administer and monitor municipal and panchayat elections.\r\n5. Revenue Sharing: Required state governments to share statutory revenue and governing functions with local bodies via State Finance Commissions.	t	2019	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SST_2019_D_15	SS10_ECO_04	\N	Standard (041)	What is globalization? Explain three factors that have enabled globalization and two areas where government policy can support fair globalization.	{"factors": "2.5 Marks", "definition": "1 Mark", "fair_policies": "1.5 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	3aeada86129d93eac0f77cdd581694c9c5a305a6bcd24cbaaa8d5c0000515052	[]	1. Definition: The rapid cross-border economic integration and interconnectedness of goods, services, technologies, and labor.\r\n2. Three Enabling Factors:\r\n- Telecommunications & IT: Satellite links, mobile connectivity, and internet networks enable real-time operational coordination.\r\n- Container Logistics: Standardized intermodal shipping containers reduce long-distance freight handling time and expenses.\r\n- Trade Liberalization: Systematic reductions in import tariffs, duties, and licensing restrictions.\r\n3. Fair Policy Measures:\r\n- Enforce statutory labor protections to guarantee fair wages and safe working conditions for factory workers.\r\n- Provide targeted credit and technology upgrades to shield small-scale domestic producers from predatory import dumping.	t	2019	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SCI_2019_AI_01	S10_CH01	\N	Standard (041)	When copper powder is heated strongly in a china dish, its reddish-brown surface becomes coated with a black substance. The black substance is:\r\n(A) Cu2O\r\n(B) CuO\r\n(C) Cu(OH)2\r\n(D) CuCO3	{"answer": "1 Mark for Option (B)"}	1.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	f2685380a6f5935931f4491f5e68731aaf2cbfd134e0b29da91a8ee94c1cf3f0	[]	Correct Option: (B)\r\nCopper reacts with atmospheric oxygen upon heating to form black copper(II) oxide:\r\n2Cu(s) + O2(g) -> 2CuO(s) [Black].	t	2019	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2019_AI_02	S10_CH02	\N	Standard (041)	Which of the following acids is present in stinging nettle leaves and causes burning pain upon contact?\r\n(A) Oxalic acid\r\n(B) Methanoic acid\r\n(C) Citric acid\r\n(D) Tartaric acid	{"answer": "1 Mark for Option (B)"}	1.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	0a63240b21687026d9ca107b43be9a1ea0df0e93eb642566503a7b473a9a8977	[]	Correct Option: (B)\r\nStinging hair of nettle leaves injects methanoic acid (formic acid, HCOOH), causing a burning sensation.	t	2019	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2019_AI_03	S10_CH03	\N	Standard (041)	Which of the following metals does NOT react with either cold water or hot water, but reacts with steam to evolve hydrogen?\r\n(A) Sodium\r\n(B) Calcium\r\n(C) Iron\r\n(D) Copper	{"answer": "1 Mark for Option (C)"}	1.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	871022c0a82fa04455904f6596746f489b46ab09bed48bd45dc5cb01d5ca8930	[]	Correct Option: (C)\r\nIron (Fe), zinc (Zn), and aluminum (Al) react only with steam to produce metal oxides and hydrogen gas:\r\n3Fe(s) + 4H2O(g) -> Fe3O4(s) + 4H2(g).	t	2019	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2019_AI_04	S10_CH06	\N	Standard (041)	The opening and closing of the stomatal pore is primarily regulated by:\r\n(A) Atmospheric oxygen concentration\r\n(B) Temperature of the leaf\r\n(C) Water content in guard cells\r\n(D) Chlorophyll concentration in stomata	{"answer": "1 Mark for Option (C)"}	1.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	2b79154f5a4779f5f18f5b3bff7bdb5a5b30fb85a68c6b9429586a735116b497	[]	Correct Option: (C)\r\nGuard cells swell and curve outward when water flows into them, opening the pore; they shrink and straighten when losing water, closing it.	t	2019	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2019_AI_05	S10_CH01	\N	Standard (041)	Why does the colour of copper sulphate solution change from blue to light green when an iron nail is dipped in it? Write the balanced chemical equation.	{"equation": "1 Mark", "explanation": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	5381f4878e34f14ecf10fd508ace0a6a7fabc3e7a18429f744b12b884a3bfd7c	[]	Iron is more reactive than copper and displaces it from aqueous copper sulphate, forming light green ferrous sulphate (FeSO4) while reddish-brown metallic copper deposits on the nail:\r\nFe(s) + CuSO4(aq) [Blue] -> FeSO4(aq) [Light Green] + Cu(s).	t	2019	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2019_AI_07	S10_CH10	\N	Standard (041)	State the two laws of reflection of light. Does light reflection obey these laws when reflecting from rough or irregular surfaces?	{"laws": "1 Mark", "diffuse": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	248f194c6fafb5bd41e6f953cf609626990a2ca2c195fbf3d8d9f780c9d9d62d	[]	1. Laws:\r\n(i) The incident ray, the reflected ray, and the normal to the reflecting surface at the point of incidence all lie in the same plane.\r\n(ii) The angle of incidence equals the angle of reflection (angle i = angle r).\r\n2. Irregular Reflection: Yes, the laws of reflection hold strictly at every single microscopic point on an irregular surface; diffuse scattering occurs because the microscopic normal varies across the rough surface.	t	2019	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2019_AI_08	S10_CH02	\N	Standard (041)	Equal lengths of magnesium ribbons are taken in test tubes A and B. Hydrochloric acid (HCl) is added to test tube A, while acetic acid (CH3COOH) of equal concentration is added to test tube B.\r\n(a) In which test tube will effervescence occur more vigorously and why?\r\n(b) Write the balanced chemical equation for the reaction occurring in test tube A.	{"equation": "1 Mark", "vigor_reason": "2 Marks"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	42d40e5894c0d18fc745833acbf4f349dcffe3d81d20adf791632b1a57853cf6	[]	(a) Test tube A exhibits significantly more vigorous effervescence.\r\nReason: Hydrochloric acid (HCl) is a strong mineral acid that completely dissociates in water, providing a higher concentration of H+ ions. Acetic acid (CH3COOH) is a weak organic acid that dissociates only partially, resulting in a slower reaction rate.\r\n(b) Equation for Test Tube A:\r\nMg(s) + 2HCl(aq) -> MgCl2(aq) + H2(g).	t	2019	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2019_AI_09	S10_CH06	\N	Standard (041)	Explain the role of the following components during the human breathing cycle:\r\n(a) Diaphragm during inhalation\r\n(b) Alveoli in lungs\r\n(c) Hemoglobin respiratory pigment in erythrocytes	{"alveoli": "1 Mark", "diaphragm": "1 Mark", "hemoglobin": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	960d4de1d92f5e56a18244a036e076704d6395c2be51ed4d1ab621588c5abb76	[]	(a) Diaphragm during inhalation: Contracts and flattens downward, expanding thoracic cavity volume and lowering intra-pulmonary pressure so atmospheric air rushes into the lungs.\r\n(b) Alveoli: Balloon-like thin-walled air sacs enveloped by dense capillary networks that provide an extensive surface area (~80 m^2) for gas diffusion between air and blood.\r\n(c) Hemoglobin: Iron-containing respiratory pigment with high chemical affinity for oxygen; binds O2 in pulmonary capillaries to form oxyhemoglobin and transports it to respiring tissues.	t	2019	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2019_AI_10	S10_CH10	\N	Standard (041)	A convex lens forms a real and inverted image of a needle at a distance of 50 cm from it. Where is the needle placed in front of the convex lens if the image is equal to the size of the needle? Also, find the power of the lens.	{"needle_pos": "1.5 Marks", "power_calc": "1.5 Marks"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	c43d5ec55c2a497caa86fb13b11118d21b37c28bd4db57453b70629ed159d253	[]	Given: v = +50 cm, image is real, inverted, and same size (m = -1).\r\nMagnification m = v / u -> -1 = +50 / u -> u = -50 cm.\r\nThe needle is placed 50 cm in front of the lens (at 2F1, so 2f = 50 cm -> f = +25 cm = +0.25 m).\r\nPower P = 1 / f(in meters) = 1 / (+0.25 m) = +4.0 Dioptres (+4.0 D).	t	2019	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2019_AI_11	S10_CH12	\N	Standard (041)	A cylindrical conductor of length L and uniform cross-sectional area A has resistance R. What will be the resistance of another conductor of the same material having length 2L and cross-sectional area A/2?	{"final": "1 Mark for 4R", "derivation": "2 Marks"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	10abcd8f47c1ecd1e51dd1e05f345cc8898dc00aa344189ea6b5267e2f8d0221	[]	Original conductor: R = rho * (L / A).\r\nNew conductor parameters: L' = 2L, A' = A / 2.\r\nR' = rho * (L' / A') = rho * (2L / (A / 2)) = rho * (4L / A) = 4 * [rho * (L / A)] = 4 * R.\r\nThe resistance of the new conductor will be 4 times the initial resistance (4R).	t	2019	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2019_AI_12	S10_CH15	\N	Standard (041)	Why is the flow of energy in an ecosystem always unidirectional? Explain why energy captured by autotrophs does not revert to the solar input.	{"thermodynamics": "1.5 Marks", "unidirectional": "1.5 Marks"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	4bc6976835e0ff7062b6e765f41814f8a1e9c4925a510204b06e4a66b21f0021	[]	1. Unidirectional Principle: Solar radiant energy captured by green autotrophs is fixed into chemical bond energy. When herbivores consume plants, energy transfers forward to higher carnivores. Energy cannot flow backward from consumer to producer, nor can dissipated metabolic heat return to the sun.\r\n2. Thermodynamic Loss: At every trophic transition, roughly 90% of energy is lost to entropy and respiration (Lindeman 10% law). The energy dispersed as heat cannot be recaptured by photosynthetic machinery.	t	2019	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2019_AI_13	S10_CH03	\N	Standard (041)	(a) What is the reactivity series of metals? Arrange Cu, Fe, Zn, and Mg in decreasing order of reactivity.\r\n(b) Describe an experiment with observations when zinc metal granules are added to aqueous copper sulphate solution.\r\n(c) State two conditions necessary for the rusting of iron.	{"rusting_factors": "1.5 Marks", "zinc_experiment": "2 Marks", "reactivity_order": "1.5 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	ead49c39c25167d23546cb69ba30c4f2f3e8726b59398fd541d9f2afa6f9fe70	[]	(a) Reactivity Series: A vertical arrangement of metals in decreasing order of their chemical reactivity.\r\nDecreasing order: Mg > Zn > Fe > Cu.\r\n(b) Zinc Granules in CuSO4:\r\nZn(s) + CuSO4(aq) -> ZnSO4(aq) + Cu(s).\r\nObservations: The dark blue copper sulphate solution becomes colourless, and reddish-brown copper deposits on the zinc granules.\r\n(c) Rusting Conditions: (i) Presence of oxygen (air), (ii) Presence of moisture (water vapor). Both must be present simultaneously.	t	2019	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2019_AI_14	S10_CH04	\N	Standard (041)	(a) What is meant by denatured alcohol? Why is ethanol denatured for commercial distribution?\r\n(b) Write balanced chemical equations for:\r\n(i) Reaction of ethanol with sodium metal\r\n(ii) Dehydration of ethanol by heating with concentrated sulphuric acid at 443 K\r\n(c) State two common commercial applications of ethanol.	{"uses": "1.5 Marks", "denatured": "1.5 Marks", "equations": "2 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	1eab62a309c73919057f51f64a3c0c807164fb292fee258a70134bcc3dbba470	[]	(a) Denatured Alcohol: Ethanol mixed with poisonous adulterants (such as methanol, pyridine, or copper sulphate dye) to make it unfit for human consumption.\r\nPurpose: Prevents industrial-tax-exempt alcohol from being diverted for recreational drinking.\r\n(b) Chemical Equations:\r\n(i) 2C2H5OH + 2Na -> 2C2H5ONa (Sodium ethoxide) + H2(g)\r\n(ii) C2H5OH --(Conc H2SO4, 443 K)--> CH2=CH2 (Ethene) + H2O.\r\n(c) Applications: 1. Industrial solvent for paints, varnishes, and perfumes; 2. Antiseptic in tinctures, cough syrups, and medical hand sanitizers.	t	2019	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SST_2025_SQP_02	SS10_HIS_02	\N	Standard (041)	Who wrote the famous book 'Hind Swaraj' in 1909?\r\n(A) Jawaharlal Nehru\r\n(B) Mahatma Gandhi\r\n(C) Subhas Chandra Bose\r\n(D) Bal Gangadhar Tilak	{"answer": "1 Mark for Option (B)"}	1.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	008cdb7df63925f0a257ee48f76c591927ac9d401e219a3d6eae9c060331d1b2	[]	Correct Option: (B)\r\nMahatma Gandhi wrote Hind Swaraj in 1909.	t	2025	Official SQP	f	SST	All India
SCI_2019_AI_15	S10_CH13	\N	Standard (041)	(a) Explain the underlying physical principle of a domestic electric generator.\r\n(b) What modification in design converts an AC generator into a DC generator?\r\n(c) What are the standard colour codes for Live, Neutral, and Earth insulation wires in domestic electrical circuits?	{"colour_codes": "1.5 Marks", "dc_splitring": "1.5 Marks", "generator_principle": "2 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	d59fb0c9d4be4efb2b17c15d57b33a0d449721078c50ee77f6b51fdc5f2d9027	[]	(a) Principle: Electromagnetic Induction (Faraday Law). When a closed armature coil is rotated mechanically in a stationary magnetic field, changing magnetic flux induces an electric current in the coil.\r\n(b) Generator Modification: Replace continuous slip rings with a split-ring commutator. The commutator segments switch contact with the stationary carbon brushes every half turn, converting alternating output into unidirectional direct current (DC).\r\n(c) Colour Codes:\r\n- Live wire: Red (or Brown)\r\n- Neutral wire: Black (or Light Blue)\r\n- Earth wire: Green (or Green with Yellow stripes).	t	2019	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SST_2019_AI_01	SS10_HIS_01	\N	Standard (041)	Which Prussian King was proclaimed German Emperor at the Hall of Mirrors in the Palace of Versailles in January 1871?\r\n(A) William I\r\n(B) Friedrich Wilhelm IV\r\n(C) Otto von Bismarck\r\n(D) Napoleon III	{"answer": "1 Mark for Option (A)"}	1.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	a34a1c27155d9e7053de63ee71b8e628db106662813fc612a07dee5b2a482cd1	[]	Correct Option: (A)\r\nKing William I of Prussia was proclaimed Emperor of united Germany on January 18, 1871.	t	2019	All India Set 1	f	SST	All India
SST_2019_AI_02	SS10_HIS_02	\N	Standard (041)	In which city did Mahatma Gandhi establish his Sabarmati Ashram after returning to India from South Africa?\r\n(A) Surat\r\n(B) Ahmedabad\r\n(C) Rajkot\r\n(D) Porbandar	{"answer": "1 Mark for Option (B)"}	1.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	50b711b0f3ebbaa4510defbba91cd5657badc68b3296550bfbd92d284827169d	[]	Correct Option: (B)\r\nMahatma Gandhi founded the Sabarmati Ashram in Ahmedabad, Gujarat, in 1917.	t	2019	All India Set 1	f	SST	All India
SST_2019_AI_03	SS10_GEO_01	\N	Standard (041)	What term is used to describe land left uncultivated for more than five agricultural years?\r\n(A) Current fallow\r\n(B) Fallow other than current fallow\r\n(C) Culturable waste land\r\n(D) Barren and waste land	{"answer": "1 Mark for Option (C)"}	1.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	2f8878a50da17d708fc383b959966bf2f404e858dbf78fdbadc73fef3366d3e5	[]	Correct Option: (C)\r\nCulturable waste land refers to agricultural land left unploughed and uncultivated for more than five consecutive agricultural seasons.	t	2019	All India Set 1	f	SST	All India
SST_2019_AI_04	SS10_POL_01	\N	Standard (041)	What is the ethnic composition of the capital city of Brussels in Belgium?\r\n(A) 80% French-speaking and 20% Dutch-speaking\r\n(B) 80% Dutch-speaking and 20% French-speaking\r\n(C) 50% French-speaking and 50% Dutch-speaking\r\n(D) 90% German-speaking and 10% Flemish	{"answer": "1 Mark for Option (A)"}	1.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	07fa2d0f5bc3f269f94c46ea645754ebb690bcb0567974f848f8efa0951b75f2	[]	Correct Option: (A)\r\nIn the capital city Brussels, 80% of the residents speak French while 20% speak Dutch (the inverse of the national linguistic proportion).	t	2019	All India Set 1	f	SST	All India
SST_2019_AI_05	SS10_ECO_01	\N	Standard (041)	What is meant by Per Capita Income? Why do national averages alone fail to reflect the true standard of living of citizens in a country?	{"definition": "1 Mark", "disparities": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	69c72e36c2d6f244f62c2ed2b03c88d29f6bb6d4052e7508cdd5db4b7dfd4b1f	[]	1. Definition: The total national income of a country divided by its total population (Average Income).\r\n2. Limitation: Averages conceal disparities in income distribution. A high average per capita income can exist in a society where a few wealthy families hold most assets while the majority live in poverty.	t	2019	All India Set 1	f	SST	All India
SST_2019_AI_06	SS10_POL_02	\N	Standard (041)	State two differences between a Unitary system of government and a Federal system of government.	{"difference1": "1 Mark", "difference2": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	2b6803e57508afdaa0dd7a6486bb8c2bd87681663ca1e76f6d7b9524a39aed8a	[]	1. Levels of Power: A unitary system concentrates all constitutional authority in a single central government; a federal system divides sovereign authority between central and regional state tiers.\r\n2. Subordination: In a unitary state, sub-units are subordinate to the center and subject to unilateral dissolution; in a federation, state governments hold constitutionally protected powers and are directly accountable to the electorate.	t	2019	All India Set 1	f	SST	All India
SST_2019_AI_07	SS10_GEO_02	\N	Standard (041)	Differentiate between subsistence farming and commercial farming on the basis of: (a) purpose of cultivation, (b) use of modern inputs.	{"inputs": "1 Mark", "purpose": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	3a56dfb20914efb721c0d6ace9d7686f685651f7fd89240fad63b86c5af3219e	[]	1. Purpose: Subsistence farming produces food primarily for family consumption; commercial farming cultivates crops for sale in domestic and international markets.\r\n2. Inputs: Subsistence relies on traditional tools, family labor, and organic compost; commercial uses capital-intensive inputs like HYV seeds, synthetic fertilizers, chemical pesticides, and mechanized tractors.	t	2019	All India Set 1	f	SST	All India
SST_2019_AI_08	SS10_HIS_01	\N	Standard (041)	Explain the political and economic conditions that led to the emergence of the middle class in 18th- and 19th-century Europe.	{"ideology": "1 Mark", "industrial": "1 Mark", "professionals": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	a32ccccb6ab0ff1f28b00e1ec576655969f564652c117686cfa907ff7af9fd6c	[]	1. Commercial Industrialization: The expansion of overseas trade and mechanized factory production generated wealth for merchant traders, bankers, and factory owners.\r\n2. Professional Growth: Industrial expansion stimulated demand for educated professionals—doctors, lawyers, professors, and civil administrators.\r\n3. Ideological Shift: Educated middle classes rejected aristocratic birth privileges and advocated for meritocracy, constitutional representation, and free market trade.	t	2019	All India Set 1	f	SST	All India
SST_2019_AI_09	SS10_ECO_02	\N	Standard (041)	Why is the employment generation in the secondary and tertiary sectors lagging behind the growth of GDP in India? Explain with three reasons.	{"rural_trap": "1 Mark", "capital_tech": "1 Mark", "tertiary_skill": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	f8ef1c11880e4ccc1725901d34bf9a1efe376581a5f7219edfe6340050b79446	[]	1. Capital-Intensive Industrialization: Secondary sector manufacturing expansion relies on automation and machinery rather than labor-intensive methods.\r\n2. High-Skill Tertiary Jobs: Growth in the tertiary sector is driven by IT, finance, and telecommunications, which require specialized higher education, limiting absorption of unskilled workers.\r\n3. Agricultural Underemployment: Lack of non-farm rural job opportunities traps surplus labor on small agricultural holdings (disguised unemployment).	t	2019	All India Set 1	f	SST	All India
SST_2019_AI_10	SS10_POL_03	\N	Standard (041)	State three constitutional mechanisms through which secularism is practiced and guaranteed in the Republic of India.	{"freedom": "1 Mark", "no_official": "1 Mark", "non_discrimination": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	aa092af1a02c67b4685111377c216a0e568e358d32d8771c99f71834455b3624	[]	1. No State Religion: The Indian Constitution does not designate an official religion, maintaining neutrality toward all faiths.\r\n2. Freedom of Conscience: Article 25 guarantees all citizens the right to profess, practice, and propagate any religion.\r\n3. Prohibition of Discrimination: Article 15 prohibits state discrimination on grounds of religion in public institutions, employment, and civic access.	t	2019	All India Set 1	f	SST	All India
SST_2019_AI_11	SS10_ECO_03	\N	Standard (041)	Explain the role of Self-Help Groups (SHGs) in empowering rural women and overcoming collateral requirements.	{"social": "1 Mark", "collateral": "1 Mark", "self_reliance": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	efbfc67436d42fd3ca321e5575369d3498f3bb8741641f1a16ee483b2de21ce4	[]	1. Collateral-Free Loans: SHGs provide small loans from pooled savings without requiring land titles or property deeds, helping women avoid moneylenders.\r\n2. Economic Self-Reliance: Group loans support income-generating activities such as tailoring, handlooms, livestock rearing, and pottery.\r\n3. Social Empowerment: Regular monthly meetings provide a platform to discuss civic issues, public health, nutrition, and domestic violence.	t	2019	All India Set 1	f	SST	All India
SST_2019_AI_12	SS10_GEO_06	\N	Standard (041)	Why is the cotton textile industry concentrated in the states of Maharashtra and Gujarat? Give three geographical and economic reasons.	{"port": "1 Mark", "climate": "1 Mark", "raw_cotton": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	d2341e61c4d5f1744964644ff262a52beb4811cc1eb9383b3ec68c45f3a03d73	[]	1. Raw Material Proximity: The black cotton soil (regur) of the Deccan plateau provides an abundant, reliable supply of raw cotton.\r\n2. Humid Coastal Climate: Coastal humidity prevents cotton yarn from snapping during high-speed spinning in mills.\r\n3. Port and Logistics: Mumbai and Kandla ports offer container infrastructure for exporting yarn and importing long-staple cotton.	t	2019	All India Set 1	f	SST	All India
SST_2019_AI_13	SS10_HIS_02	\N	Standard (041)	Describe the course of the Salt March from Sabarmati to Dandi. Explain why the defiance of the Salt Law became the catalyst for the Civil Disobedience Movement.	{"catalyst": "2.5 Marks", "march_details": "2.5 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	6773fa2dd93a5e2f0e4264927a8ae0c2c4248e086316c0ebcf7f0274aeaca70a	[]	1. The Historic Dandi March:\r\n- On March 12, 1930, Mahatma Gandhi set out from Sabarmati Ashram with 78 verified satyagrahis.\r\n- The march covered 240 miles over 24 days through rural Gujarat.\r\n- On April 6, 1930, Gandhi reached the coastal village of Dandi, boiled seawater, and manufactured salt in open defiance of British law.\r\n2. Catalyst for Civil Disobedience:\r\n- Salt was an everyday necessity consumed across caste, class, and religious lines; the British monopoly and tax affected all households directly.\r\n- Breaking the salt law demonstrated that colonial authority could be peacefully defied, prompting boycotts of foreign cloth, refusals to pay land revenue, and resignations of village officials across India.	t	2019	All India Set 1	f	SST	All India
SST_2019_AI_14	SS10_POL_02	\N	Standard (041)	Analyze the key differences between the administrative structure of rural local governments (Panchayati Raj) and urban local governments (Municipalities) in India.	{"rural_panchayat": "2.5 Marks", "urban_municipal": "2.5 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	bb39aef5e6b80ffe9ea3bd43c9d2f842dc3aafdeccc8c7c7c31343d0eed86265	[]	1. Rural Local Governance (Panchayati Raj):\r\n- Three-tier hierarchy: Gram Panchayat at the village level, Panchayat Samiti at the block level, and Zilla Parishad at the district level.\r\n- Village bodies are directly monitored by the Gram Sabha (all adult village voters).\r\n- Political executive: Elected Sarpanch (village) and Zilla Parishad Chairperson (district).\r\n2. Urban Local Governance:\r\n- Organized into Municipal Corporations in major cities and Municipal Councils in smaller towns.\r\n- Political executive: Mayor in Municipal Corporations and Municipal Council President in smaller towns.\r\n- Administrative executive: A state-appointed Municipal Commissioner implements decisions.	t	2019	All India Set 1	f	SST	All India
SST_2019_AI_15	SS10_ECO_04	\N	Standard (041)	Evaluate the role of Multinational Corporations (MNCs) in the globalization process. Explain three ways MNCs influence production across different countries.	{"mnc_role": "2 Marks", "three_strategies": "3 Marks (1 Mark each)"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	91b91de7cf6dac71291e8df0c3b42420ba6eb56a14ef748601bc17fa39c8d3b4	[]	1. Role in Globalization: MNCs act as primary drivers of cross-border trade, technology transfer, and capital investment, integrating national markets into global supply networks.\r\n2. Three Production Strategies:\r\n- Direct Acquisitions: Purchasing established domestic enterprises to gain immediate access to local factories, distribution systems, and retail footprints (e.g., Cargill Foods acquiring Parakh Foods).\r\n- Joint Ventures: Partnering with local companies to supply modern production machinery and capital in exchange for local operational know-how (e.g., Ford Motors partnering with Mahindra).\r\n- Contract Manufacturing: Placing manufacturing orders with decentralized local suppliers in developing countries for labor-intensive goods (e.g., Nike and Zara subcontracting footwear and garments).	t	2019	All India Set 1	f	SST	All India
SCI_2018_D_05	S10_CH01	\N	Standard (041)	Write a balanced chemical equation for the reaction that occurs when ferrous sulphate crystals (FeSO4·7H2O) are heated strongly in a dry boiling tube. State two observations.	{"equation": "1 Mark", "observations": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	8ff79a7ef83e0e5e34ac6833abfbf3c24db2d7f364b1b262532dea31401124ed	[]	1. Equation:\r\n2FeSO4(s) --(Heat)--> Fe2O3(s) + SO2(g) + SO3(g).\r\n2. Observations:\r\n- Green crystals turn into a reddish-brown solid residue (ferric oxide, Fe2O3).\r\n- Choking, pungent fumes of burning sulphur gases (SO2 and SO3) are evolved.	t	2018	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
SCI_2018_D_06	S10_CH07	\N	Standard (041)	What is hydrotropism? Design an experiment to demonstrate hydrotropism in plant roots.	{"definition": "1 Mark", "experiment": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	820bec1c856705fb5150b9363154e89705dc2ae4e1ad458119c58a979a738ead	[]	1. Hydrotropism: Directional growth movement of plant roots toward a moisture gradient.\r\n2. Experiment: Place moist sawdust in a porous sieve suspended horizontally. Place germinating bean seeds in the sawdust.\r\nObservation: As radicles emerge from the bottom perforations, they curve back upward toward the moist sawdust rather than growing downward with gravity, demonstrating positive hydrotropism.	t	2018	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
SCI_2018_D_07	S10_CH10	\N	Standard (041)	Why is a convex mirror commonly used as a rear-view (wing) mirror in motor vehicles? State two optical reasons.	{"erect": "1 Mark", "field_of_view": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	eeb2b10d5aae2997dde1f9f46cddf5a22b1b417dcb27837e4f969fad38b5f575	[]	1. Erect Images: A convex mirror always produces an erect, albeit diminished, image of trailing traffic regardless of distance.\r\n2. Wider Field of View: Being curved outwards, convex mirrors capture a significantly wider field of view than a flat plane mirror.	t	2018	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
SCI_2018_D_08	S10_CH02	\N	Standard (041)	(a) What is the chlor-alkali process? Why is it called so?\r\n(b) Name the gases liberated at the anode and the cathode during this electrolysis.\r\n(c) Write the balanced chemical equation representing the complete reaction.	{"gases": "1 Mark", "equation": "1 Mark", "definition_name": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	9b8cccd97574a5e1f7da3d7bf78c90367b10f5faa904ae00798c78848c6ed873	[]	(a) Chlor-Alkali Process: The electrolysis of an aqueous concentrated solution of sodium chloride (brine). It is named chlor-alkali because of the co-products formed: chlorine (chlor) and sodium hydroxide (an alkali).\r\n(b) Gases Liberated:\r\n- Anode: Chlorine gas (Cl2).\r\n- Cathode: Hydrogen gas (H2).\r\n(c) Balanced Equation:\r\n2NaCl(aq) + 2H2O(l) --(Electricity)--> 2NaOH(aq) + Cl2(g) + H2(g).	t	2018	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
SCI_2018_D_09	S10_CH06	\N	Standard (041)	Describe the mechanism of transport of water and mineral salts from the soil to the leaves of tall plants under: (a) Root pressure, (b) Transpirational pull.	{"root_pressure": "1.5 Marks", "transpiration_pull": "1.5 Marks"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	cb42886023ff07c3577f08961f8f77eac43b517b52e7b371783adea9b6edd90f	[]	(a) Root Pressure: Active transport of mineral ions into root hair cells creates a hypertonic gradient; water flows in osmotically from the soil, generating positive hydrostatic pressure that pushes water into basal xylem vessels.\r\n(b) Transpirational Pull: Continuous evaporation of water molecules from stomata creates suction tension across xylem conduits. Because water molecules have high cohesion and adhesion, water is pulled upward through continuous xylem columns to heights exceeding 20 meters.	t	2018	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
SCI_2018_D_10	S10_CH10	\N	Standard (041)	An object 4 cm in height is placed at 15 cm in front of a concave mirror of focal length 10 cm. At what distance from the mirror should a screen be placed to obtain a sharp image? Calculate the size of the image.	{"v_calc": "1.5 Marks", "size_nature": "1.5 Marks"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	b673e1465635ef56580e8dab953e7c3f24ca7f02ade92b70769efde1cdb2d831	[]	Given: h = +4 cm, u = -15 cm, f = -10 cm.\r\nMirror Formula: 1/f = 1/v + 1/u -> 1/v = 1/f - 1/u\r\n1/v = 1/(-10) - 1/(-15) = -1/10 + 1/15 = (-3 + 2)/30 = -1/30.\r\nv = -30 cm (Screen must be placed 30 cm in front of the mirror).\r\nMagnification m = -v / u = -(-30) / (-15) = -2.0.\r\nHeight h' = m * h = -2.0 * 4 cm = -8 cm.\r\nImage is real, inverted, magnified, and 8 cm tall.	t	2018	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
SCI_2018_D_11	S10_CH12	\N	Standard (041)	State Joule's Law of heating. An electric iron of resistance 20 ohms takes a current of 5 A. Calculate the thermal energy developed in 30 seconds in Joules and calories (1 cal = 4.18 J).	{"law": "1 Mark", "joules": "1 Mark for 15,000 J", "calories": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	83b5ff16d45bfc1d3b07f4c3298e46c68d72a508c397dde5507e83e268bc13d1	[]	1. Joule's Law: The heat generated in a resistor is directly proportional to the square of current (I^2), resistance (R), and time (t): H = I^2 * R * t.\r\n2. Heat in Joules:\r\nH = (5)^2 * 20 * 30 = 25 * 600 = 15,000 Joules (15 kJ).\r\n3. Heat in Calories:\r\nH_cal = 15000 / 4.18 = 3,588.5 calories.	t	2018	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
SCI_2018_D_12	S10_CH15	\N	Standard (041)	What is the role of decomposers in an ecosystem? What would happen if all decomposers were eliminated completely from the biosphere?	{"role": "1.5 Marks", "consequences": "1.5 Marks"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	80b4b8252e5f8eba193a44da72eeefb99cbaa91d4243f7841d64d595172e26f7	[]	1. Role: Decomposers (bacteria and saprophytic fungi) break down complex organic matter from dead plants and animals into simple inorganic nutrients (carbon, nitrogen, phosphorus) returned to the soil and atmosphere.\r\n2. Elimination Consequences: Dead biomass would accumulate, nutrient recycling would halt, soil fertility would deplete, and autotrophic plant synthesis would collapse.	t	2018	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
SCI_2018_D_13	S10_CH03	\N	Standard (041)	(a) Distinguish between minerals and ores. Name one ore of mercury and one ore of copper.\r\n(b) Explain the chemical extraction of mercury from its cinnabar ore with balanced equations.\r\n(c) How are metals located at the top of the reactivity series (such as Na and Al) extracted from their ores?	{"minerals_ores": "1.5 Marks", "mercury_roasting": "2 Marks", "reactive_electrolytic": "1.5 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	0bc9d2de217dedd7de587d8cc74a8744b37e8952627be7cd1da0258c9ba59bd2	[]	(a) Distinction: Minerals are naturally occurring inorganic substances containing metals; Ores are minerals from which metals can be extracted profitably.\r\nMercury ore: Cinnabar (HgS); Copper ore: Copper glance (Cu2S) or Copper pyrites (CuFeS2).\r\n(b) Extraction of Mercury:\r\n1. Roasting: 2HgS(s) + 3O2(g) --(Heat)--> 2HgO(s) + 2SO2(g).\r\n2. Thermal Reduction: 2HgO(s) --(Heat)--> 2Hg(l) + O2(g).\r\n(c) Reactive Metals: High chemical affinity for oxygen prevents extraction via carbon reduction; they are extracted by electrolytic reduction of their molten chloride salts (e.g., molten NaCl at cathode: Na+ + e- -> Na).	t	2018	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
SCI_2017_D_04	S10_CH06	\N	Standard (041)	Which digestive enzyme present in human saliva initiates the chemical breakdown of dietary starch into maltose?\r\n(A) Pepsin\r\n(B) Salivary amylase (Ptyalin)\r\n(C) Trypsin\r\n(D) Lipase	{"answer": "1 Mark for Option (B)"}	1.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	d0231ae68b0c1429418ddd55595ca9b75a47489c5f2ea1610348eaa94bdb0b2c	[]	Correct Option: (B)\r\nSalivary amylase hydrolyzes complex insoluble starch into maltose disaccharides in the mouth (pH ~6.8).	t	2017	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
SCI_2018_D_14	S10_CH04	\N	Standard (041)	(a) What are micelles? Explain how micelles form during the cleansing action of soap in water.\r\n(b) Why are detergents preferred over soaps for washing clothes in hard water?\r\n(c) Write the chemical equation for the saponification reaction.	{"micelle": "2 Marks", "hard_water": "1.5 Marks", "saponification": "1.5 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	712fed5c654badd272711151f334fd840e7dad25b3ae1b8715fbe19e66f91296	[]	(a) Micelles: Spherical molecular clusters formed by amphipathic soap molecules (e.g., C17H35COONa) in water. Hydrophobic hydrocarbon tails dissolve into central oily dirt, while hydrophilic ionic heads (-COO- Na+) project radially outward into water, forming a stable emulsion washed away during rinsing.\r\n(b) Hard Water: Soaps precipitate as insoluble curd (scum) when reacting with Ca2+ and Mg2+ ions; synthetic detergents form soluble sulfonates, preserving lather and cleaning power.\r\n(c) Saponification:\r\nCH3COOC2H5 + NaOH -> CH3COONa (Soap) + C2H5OH\r\n(or Glyceryl ester + 3NaOH -> Glycerol + 3 Soap molecules).	t	2018	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
SCI_2018_D_15	S10_CH13	\N	Standard (041)	(a) What is electromagnetic induction?\r\n(b) Describe an activity using two coils wound on a cylindrical non-conducting roll to demonstrate induced current in a secondary coil when primary current is switched on and off.\r\n(c) State Fleming's Right-Hand Rule.	{"rule": "1.5 Marks", "activity": "2 Marks", "definition": "1.5 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	6de24dcf1d456700d9e79fbbcef1dde88fc49a2546acb3d2844e26917605aa3a	[]	(a) Electromagnetic Induction: The phenomenon of inducing an electromotive force (and current) in a closed conductor due to a change in the magnetic flux linked with it.\r\n(b) Activity:\r\n- Wind Coil-1 (Primary, ~100 turns connected to a battery and plug key) and Coil-2 (Secondary, ~50 turns connected to a sensitive galvanometer) on a cylindrical cardboard roll.\r\n- Observations: Closing the key causes a momentary deflection in the galvanometer; opening the key causes a momentary deflection in the opposite direction; steady current causes zero deflection.\r\n(c) Fleming's Right-Hand Rule: Stretch the thumb, forefinger, and middle finger mutually perpendicular: forefinger indicates magnetic field direction, thumb indicates motion of the conductor, and middle finger indicates the induced current direction.	t	2018	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
SST_2018_D_01	SS10_HIS_01	\N	Standard (041)	Who among the following was described by Duke Metternich as 'the most dangerous enemy of our social order'?\r\n(A) Lord Byron\r\n(B) Giuseppe Mazzini\r\n(C) Count Cavour\r\n(D) Giuseppe Garibaldi	{"answer": "1 Mark for Option (B)"}	1.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	3a8229db1a922c8339c899d5ed39966936f7c66abddeb94bc28302b133decf3f	[]	Correct Option: (B)\r\nDuke Metternich described Giuseppe Mazzini as the most dangerous enemy of conservative monarchical order in Europe.	t	2018	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
