SST_2018_D_03	SS10_GEO_01	\N	Standard (041)	Which soil type is formed by intense leaching due to heavy tropical rainfall and is common across the Western Ghats and Kerala?\r\n(A) Alluvial Soil\r\n(B) Black Soil\r\n(C) Laterite Soil\r\n(D) Arid Soil	{"answer": "1 Mark for Option (C)"}	1.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	58010abe1b70cd2d4789451ac8206beff884bdc2aeb51ae758a18d4b9a0b7dbe	[]	Correct Option: (C)\r\nLaterite soil develops in regions with high temperatures and heavy tropical rainfall, characterized by intense silica and lime leaching.	t	2018	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SST_2018_D_04	SS10_POL_01	\N	Standard (041)	A democratic system where powers are shared among governments at different levels (such as Central, State, and Local) is called:\r\n(A) Horizontal distribution of power\r\n(B) Federal or vertical division of power\r\n(C) Community governance\r\n(D) Majoritarian coalition	{"answer": "1 Mark for Option (B)"}	1.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	74c10cb67af5f577035e280d076c221386f932bd0d3a9e9c5bac22056059f666	[]	Correct Option: (B)\r\nVertical division of power distributes authority hierarchically between higher and lower tiers of government within a federation.	t	2018	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SST_2018_D_05	SS10_ECO_01	\N	Standard (041)	State two developmental goals of a landless rural agricultural labourer in India.	{"goal1": "1 Mark", "goal2": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	4205427c9375e5b2c4d0d49183e1f992bdb59f699519f9ac05dccc81c0df2850	[]	1. More days of work and higher daily wages.\r\n2. Quality basic education for their children in local schools without caste discrimination.	t	2018	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SST_2018_D_06	SS10_POL_02	\N	Standard (041)	Mention any two differences between a Holding Together federation and a Coming Together federation.	{"diff1": "1 Mark", "diff2": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	0446b28a2e53fc8962912e45bc0ea99d096969241d2f0ab76c8fe4c30f055bcf	[]	1. Formation: Coming Together involves independent states pooling sovereignty to form a larger union (e.g., USA); Holding Together involves a single large nation dividing power between central and state units (e.g., India).\r\n2. State Parity: States in Coming Together federations typically enjoy equal powers; in Holding Together federations, central authority is stronger and some states may have special constitutional arrangements.	t	2018	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SST_2018_D_07	SS10_GEO_02	\N	Standard (041)	What is 'Rabi' cropping season? Name two major Rabi crops grown in the north-western parts of India.	{"crops": "1 Mark", "season": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	18a8ae7c82348443d0e6db9e7f7b35efb9e724697f6e54922d50efb6e67f32f7	[]	1. Rabi Season: Crops sown in winter (October to December) and harvested in spring and early summer (April to June).\r\n2. Major Crops: Wheat and Mustard (or Barley and Gram).	t	2018	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SST_2018_D_08	SS10_HIS_01	\N	Standard (041)	Describe the role played by Giuseppe Garibaldi and his armed volunteers ('Red Shirts') in the unification of Italy.	{"handover": "1 Mark", "expedition": "1 Mark", "leadership": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	9e1b7fb798c2f903b80559c57e1d4c32dcc794296c952ffc5a0b780e319b4f3b	[]	1. Leadership: Giuseppe Garibaldi organized an armed civilian volunteer force known as the Red Shirts.\r\n2. Expedition of the Thousand: In 1860, he led his volunteers into southern Italy and the Kingdom of the Two Sicilies.\r\n3. Peasant Mobilization: Gained the support of local peasants to expel the Spanish Bourbon rulers, handing liberated southern provinces to King Victor Emmanuel II to unify the country.	t	2018	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
MATH_2022_D_06	M10_CH05	\N	Standard (041)	Which term of the AP: 27, 24, 21, ... is zero?	{"n_calc": "1 Mark", "formula": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	68d6a3db8850eb377940fb03f9aef9936009dbc181f9708d076a91a4cf90e5d1	[]	Given AP: 27, 24, 21, ...\r\nFirst term a = 27, common difference d = 24 - 27 = -3.\r\nLet the nth term be 0:\r\na_n = a + (n - 1)d = 0\r\n27 + (n - 1)(-3) = 0\r\n-3(n - 1) = -27\r\nn - 1 = 9 => n = 10.\r\nThe 10th term of the AP is zero.	t	2022	Delhi Set 1	f	MATH	Delhi
SST_2018_D_09	SS10_ECO_02	\N	Standard (041)	Explain how public sector enterprises contribute to balanced economic development and social justice in India.	{"regional": "1 Mark", "subsidies": "1 Mark", "infrastructure": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	a57a3c0f2ff2b442e347a404267e827bf514eb8d0be991c70da9bad1a7d9ad1f	[]	1. Capital-Intensive Infrastructure: The government finances strategic infrastructure with high capital requirements and long gestation periods (e.g., railways, thermal power, bridges).\r\n2. Regional Industrial Growth: Locating steel plants and heavy industrial units in underdeveloped backward districts generates local jobs and reduces regional disparities.\r\n3. Subsidized Public Goods: Providing essential goods (subsidized foodgrains through PDS, free healthcare, drinking water) safeguards low-income populations from private price gouging.	t	2018	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SST_2018_D_10	SS10_POL_03	\N	Standard (041)	State three arguments to support the constitutional reservation of seats for women in elected local government bodies.	{"priorities": "1 Mark", "empowerment": "1 Mark", "representation": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	2bda60956a8b90a675affc6945f10cff175e9bc2c89e3ba48c80103839f24bf5	[]	1. Fair Representation: Women constitute nearly half the population but were historically under-represented in political decision-making.\r\n2. Voice on Grassroots Concerns: Elected women representatives prioritize community welfare issues such as drinking water, primary schools, and sanitation.\r\n3. Social Empowerment: Holding official panchayat and municipal offices challenges traditional patriarchal barriers in rural and urban society.	t	2018	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SST_2018_D_11	SS10_ECO_03	\N	Standard (041)	Why is cheap and affordable credit essential for a country's economic development? State three reasons.	{"purchasing": "1 Mark", "agriculture": "1 Mark", "entrepreneurship": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	4baf3240c9924969567266819cfb55eb011196e7dec9443ad276e51b51acfe1b	[]	1. Encourages Entrepreneurship: Low interest rates allow small entrepreneurs, artisans, and farmers to borrow without falling into high-interest debt traps.\r\n2. Expands Agricultural Investment: Affordable credit enables small farmers to invest in HYV seeds, fertilizers, and irrigation, raising output.\r\n3. Boosts Purchasing Power: Lower debt servicing burdens leave households with disposable income to spend on healthcare, education, and housing.	t	2018	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SST_2018_D_12	SS10_GEO_06	\N	Standard (041)	Explain any three factors that influence the geographical location of an industrial manufacturing unit.	{"power": "1 Mark", "market_labor": "1 Mark", "raw_material": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	8e42a91d8f0ee625e8eff8648eb9303735f2663b0d5c09b68e4202e3a8c2505a	[]	1. Availability of Raw Materials: Heavy or weight-losing industries locate near mineral deposits or crop belts to minimize transit costs.\r\n2. Power & Water Supply: Continuous electricity grids and large water bodies are required for industrial processing and plant cooling.\r\n3. Labor & Market Access: Proximity to dense population centers provides skilled/unskilled labor and an accessible market for finished products.	t	2018	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SST_2018_D_13	SS10_HIS_02	\N	Standard (041)	Explain why Mahatma Gandhi launched the Non-Cooperation Movement in 1920. How did the movement unfold in Indian cities and towns?	{"reasons": "2.5 Marks", "urban_course": "2.5 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	bce25e2a87319701e4441bb1b094a80f75f073bfcc0c17f324736ead41e8616d	[]	1. Reasons for Launch:\r\n- Deep resentment over the Rowlatt Act and the Jallianwala Bagh massacre of 1919.\r\n- Betrayal of wartime promises of constitutional self-rule.\r\n- The Khilafat movement, providing an opportunity to unify Hindu and Muslim national aspirations.\r\n2. Urban Mobilization:\r\n- Thousands of students and teachers left government-controlled schools and colleges.\r\n- Lawyers withdrew from practice in colonial courts.\r\n- Foreign textiles were boycotted and burned in public bonfires; imports of foreign cloth halved between 1921 and 1922.\r\n- Liquor shops were picketed, reducing colonial excise revenues.	t	2018	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SST_2018_D_14	SS10_POL_02	\N	Standard (041)	'Decentralization in India was strengthened significantly by the 1992 constitutional amendments.' Discuss five institutional changes brought about by these amendments.	{"five_changes": "5 Marks (1 Mark each for distinct provision)"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	9ac4ea2fb6d36673b228685affcf4165adf8971f568bc62b100a2c04432a37a5	[]	1. Mandatory Periodic Elections: Elections to rural and urban local bodies every five years are constitutionally compulsory.\r\n2. Proportional Reservations: Reserved seats for Scheduled Castes and Scheduled Tribes in proportion to population.\r\n3. Women's Reservation: At least one-third (33%) of all seats and executive posts are reserved for women.\r\n4. Independent State Election Commissions: Established to conduct free and regular local elections.\r\n5. Revenue Sharing: State governments are constitutionally required to transfer specific governing powers and revenue streams to local bodies via State Finance Commissions.	t	2018	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SST_2018_D_15	SS10_ECO_04	\N	Standard (041)	What is globalization? Explain three factors that have enabled globalization and two ways the government can ensure globalization is fair.	{"enablers": "2.5 Marks", "definition": "1 Mark", "fair_measures": "1.5 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	e2e7120aeaab6ab0a56c94e94f332147ba588d8a3b3acebe0e6731ac439e1aec	[]	1. Definition: The rapid cross-border economic integration of markets, goods, services, technologies, and capital.\r\n2. Enabling Factors:\r\n- Telecommunications & IT: Satellite links, mobile connectivity, and the internet allow instantaneous global communication.\r\n- Container Shipping: Standardized containers reduce intermodal freight transit times and handling costs.\r\n- Trade Deregulation: Systematically lowering import tariffs and quotas enables cross-border movement of commodities.\r\n3. Fair Globalization Measures:\r\n- Enforce labor laws to guarantee fair wages, reasonable working hours, and workplace safety for workers.\r\n- Protect small domestic producers with transitional tariffs and subsidized credit until they can compete internationally.	t	2018	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SCI_2018_AI_01	S10_CH01	\N	Standard (041)	A piece of granular zinc was dropped into copper sulphate solution taken in a test tube. The initial blue color of the solution gradually turned colorless and:\r\n(A) White precipitate was formed\r\n(B) Reddish-brown particles settled at the bottom\r\n(C) Black residue was observed\r\n(D) Yellow solution was produced	{"answer": "1 Mark for Option (B)"}	1.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	850b6e499d4b1f6c9f9f60d99c1b14056f3c2324585b12334ab9065974089fbb	[]	Correct Option: (B)\r\nZn(s) + CuSO4(aq) [Blue] -> ZnSO4(aq) [Colorless] + Cu(s) [Reddish-brown deposit].	t	2018	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2018_AI_02	S10_CH02	\N	Standard (041)	The pH of a sample of water collected from a contaminated pond was found to be 5.0. To make this water suitable for the survival of aquatic aquatic organisms, which substance should be added?\r\n(A) Common salt\r\n(B) Quicklime (Calcium oxide)\r\n(C) Hydrochloric acid\r\n(D) Vinegar	{"answer": "1 Mark for Option (B)"}	1.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	282dfd5bbefa94284f5039a34bb996ea664ed05a3d1273829d75387e729efb92	[]	Correct Option: (B)\r\nPond water at pH 5.0 is acidic. Adding basic quicklime (CaO) neutralizes the acid, bringing the pH closer to neutral (7.0–7.8).	t	2018	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2018_AI_05	S10_CH01	\N	Standard (041)	Identify the type of chemical reaction in each of the following:\r\n(a) Magnesium ribbon burns in air with a dazzling white flame.\r\n(b) Heating of lead nitrate crystals yielding brown fumes of nitrogen dioxide.	{"reaction_a": "1 Mark", "reaction_b": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	d4e7adc497ce46152ca4aa5274f8bbead118853e429fe015c552b5b485967141	[]	(a) Combination reaction (and exothermic reaction): 2Mg(s) + O2(g) -> 2MgO(s).\r\n(b) Thermal decomposition reaction: 2Pb(NO3)2(s) --(Heat)--> 2PbO(s) + 4NO2(g) + O2(g).	t	2018	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2018_AI_06	S10_CH07	\N	Standard (041)	Differentiate between involuntary actions and reflex actions with an example of each.	{"reflex": "1 Mark", "involuntary": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	31d07ce71dc52527ff18657a727b1940f54c9751873f95034767c97c35e49d64	[]	1. Involuntary Actions: Controlled by the medulla and midbrain in the brain without conscious intent; happen continuously and automatically (e.g., heartbeat, peristalsis, salivation).\r\n2. Reflex Actions: Sudden, rapid, involuntary responses to a specific peripheral sensory stimulus, routed predominantly through the spinal cord (e.g., withdrawing hand instantly from a hot object).	t	2018	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2018_AI_07	S10_CH10	\N	Standard (041)	Why does a ray of light bend when it travels obliquely from one optical medium to another? State the physical factor determining the extent of bending.	{"cause": "1 Mark", "factor": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	25a7a91fd70b060bcadd6d8e5e945acc60ef877b21d6a6c22c2018494ed45a9d	[]	1. Cause of Bending: Light travels at different propagation speeds in different optical media. As a wavefront strikes the interface obliquely, one side slows down or speeds up before the other, altering propagation direction.\r\n2. Physical Factor: Optical density / absolute refractive index of the two media (governed by n = c / v).	t	2018	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2018_AI_08	S10_CH02	\N	Standard (041)	A metal carbonate 'X' on reacting with dilute hydrochloric acid produces effervescence of gas 'Y'. Gas 'Y' turns lime water milky.\r\n(a) Identify 'X' and 'Y'.\r\n(b) Write balanced chemical equations for: (i) reaction of 'X' with dilute HCl, (ii) reaction of 'Y' with lime water.	{"equations": "2 Marks (1 Mark each)", "identification": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	fdc04185fd580947a318b586893aa722aeb6e099fad1a34469b35fbf26eeb22e	[]	(a) Identification: Metal carbonate 'X' is Calcium carbonate (CaCO3), and Gas 'Y' is Carbon dioxide (CO2).\r\n(b) Chemical Equations:\r\n(i) CaCO3(s) + 2HCl(aq) -> CaCl2(aq) + H2O(l) + CO2(g)\r\n(ii) Ca(OH)2(aq) + CO2(g) -> CaCO3(s) [White precipitate / milky] + H2O(l).	t	2018	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2018_AI_09	S10_CH06	\N	Standard (041)	Explain the importance of the following in the human digestive system:\r\n(a) Bile juice in fat emulsification\r\n(b) Trypsin secreted by pancreas\r\n(c) Villi present in the lining of small intestine	{"bile": "1 Mark", "villi": "1 Mark", "trypsin": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	c010b46878ede29f20c6ed96dc41566717ecb0831aaae06462b4ab9655c446b3	[]	(a) Bile Juice: Contains no digestive enzymes but contains bile salts that break down large lipid globules into small droplets (emulsification), expanding surface area for lipase action while neutralizing acidic chyme.\r\n(b) Trypsin: Proteolytic enzyme operating in an alkaline pH that digests proteins into peptones and peptides.\r\n(c) Villi: Countless microscopic finger-like projections richly supplied with blood capillaries that dramatically increase the surface area for nutrient absorption.	t	2018	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2018_AI_10	S10_CH10	\N	Standard (041)	An object is placed at a distance of 10 cm in front of a convex lens of focal length 15 cm. Find the position, size, and nature of the image formed.	{"v_calc": "1.5 Marks", "magnification_nature": "1.5 Marks"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	fdfef5e0f6b41cbc7ed38793f35b70d410eec751c49e35bc85a4814372f8f874	[]	Given: u = -10 cm, f = +15 cm.\r\nLens formula: 1/v - 1/u = 1/f -> 1/v = 1/15 + 1/(-10) = (2 - 3)/30 = -1/30.\r\nv = -30 cm.\r\nMagnification m = v / u = (-30) / (-10) = +3.0.\r\nImage is formed 30 cm on the same side as the object (in front of the lens); it is virtual, erect, and magnified 3 times.	t	2018	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2018_AI_11	S10_CH12	\N	Standard (041)	A 9 V battery is connected in series with resistors of 0.2 ohm, 0.3 ohm, 0.4 ohm, 0.5 ohm, and 12 ohms. How much current flows through the 12-ohm resistor?	{"series_req": "1.5 Marks", "current_calc": "1.5 Marks"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	0b070af053accc1692c73db3e5833c23782df6d0b320b2892035796fbabf90f1	[]	Total series resistance:\r\nReq = 0.2 + 0.3 + 0.4 + 0.5 + 12 = 13.4 ohms.\r\nIn a series circuit, current remains identical across every component:\r\nI = V / Req = 9 V / 13.4 ohms = 0.6716 A (approx 0.67 A).\r\nCurrent through the 12-ohm resistor is 0.67 A.	t	2018	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2018_AI_12	S10_CH15	\N	Standard (041)	Distinguish between autotrophs, heterotrophs, and decomposers. State one example of each.	{"autotroph": "1 Mark", "decomposer": "1 Mark", "heterotroph": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	3ecd236d0eed2d3d662354c504947aefbdc667dbff93fd1ef8b22c1187552698	[]	1. Autotrophs: Organisms that synthesize organic carbohydrates from simple inorganic CO2 and water using solar photons via chlorophyll (e.g., green plants, cyanobacteria).\r\n2. Heterotrophs: Organisms that depend directly or indirectly on autotrophic organic compounds for nutrition (e.g., deer, humans, tigers).\r\n3. Decomposers: Microorganisms that biochemically decompose complex dead plant and animal matter, recycling nutrients into soil and water (e.g., bacteria, fungi).	t	2018	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2018_AI_13	S10_CH03	\N	Standard (041)	(a) Describe the electrolytic refining of copper with a neat schematic description, specifying the anode, cathode, and electrolyte.\r\n(b) What happens to soluble and insoluble impurities during electrolytic refining?	{"setup": "2.5 Marks", "reactions": "1.5 Marks", "impurities": "1 Mark"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	7b29f131bfc0994c715b80e873227b2dc5933542fa491e8cfba3934549eeaa9d	[]	(a) Setup for Electrolytic Refining of Copper:\r\n- Anode: Slab of impure copper metal.\r\n- Cathode: Thin sheet of pure copper metal.\r\n- Electrolyte: Acidified copper sulphate solution (CuSO4 + dilute H2SO4).\r\nReaction on current passage:\r\nAt Anode: Cu(s) -> Cu2+(aq) + 2e- (impure Cu dissolves).\r\nAt Cathode: Cu2+(aq) + 2e- -> Cu(s) (pure Cu deposits).\r\n(b) Behavior of Impurities:\r\n- Soluble impurities go into the aqueous electrolyte solution.\r\n- Insoluble impurities (silver, gold, platinum) settle at the base of the tank beneath the anode as anode mud.	t	2018	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SST_2018_AI_09	SS10_ECO_02	\N	Standard (041)	Differentiate between open unemployment and disguised unemployment in India with an example of each.	{"open": "1.5 Marks", "disguised": "1.5 Marks"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	16bd445a1cbcbb649662301158825395db6c7934be2406344a90719fb9e5bb20	[]	1. Open Unemployment: A situation where an individual is able and willing to work at prevailing wages, but cannot find any job (e.g., an educated graduate searching for employment).\r\n2. Disguised Unemployment: A situation where more people are engaged in an activity than required; withdrawing the surplus workers does not reduce total production (e.g., eight members of a farming family cultivating a plot that only requires four workers).	t	2018	All India Set 1	f	SST	All India
SCI_2018_AI_14	S10_CH04	\N	Standard (041)	(a) What is an ester? How is an ester formed? Write the chemical equation for the reaction of ethanoic acid with ethanol.\r\n(b) How does an ester react with sodium hydroxide? State the name and practical importance of this reaction.\r\n(c) Write one test to distinguish ethanol from ethanoic acid.	{"test": "1.5 Marks", "ester": "2 Marks", "saponification": "1.5 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	1941fe08a7b1098382e357480b3a5a230cbcbf7bd0101a015ef87cfe8a7d6090	[]	(a) Ester: Organic compounds characterized by a pleasant fruity smell, formed by reacting a carboxylic acid with an alcohol in the presence of conc. H2SO4:\r\nCH3COOH + C2H5OH --(Conc H2SO4)--> CH3COOC2H5 (Ethyl ethanoate) + H2O.\r\n(b) Reaction with NaOH (Saponification):\r\nCH3COOC2H5 + NaOH -> CH3COONa (Soap) + C2H5OH.\r\nImportance: Used commercially in soap manufacturing.\r\n(c) Distinguishing Test: Add NaHCO3 solution; ethanoic acid produces brisk effervescence of CO2 gas that turns lime water milky, whereas ethanol exhibits no reaction.	t	2018	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2018_AI_15	S10_CH13	\N	Standard (041)	(a) State the principle of an electric generator.\r\n(b) Draw a schematic circuit diagram of a domestic electric circuit showing main fuse, electric meter, distribution board, live, neutral, and earth wires.\r\n(c) Why are different appliances in domestic electrical wiring always connected in parallel rather than series?	{"principle": "1.5 Marks", "parallel_reasons": "1.5 Marks", "wiring_description": "2 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	c41af381bf2e0b6bc43daab4dd38c8288dd87f58b054b45269ae19888611a248	[]	(a) Principle: Electromagnetic Induction (Faraday Law). When an armature coil is rotated in a magnetic field, the flux through the coil changes, inducing an electromotive force (EMF) and current.\r\n(b) Circuit Components: Service cable -> Electricity board fuse -> Electricity meter -> Main switch (distribution box with MCBs/fuses) -> Parallel appliance lines with 3 wires: Live (220 V), Neutral (0 V), Earth.\r\n(c) Parallel Connection Advantages:\r\n1. Each appliance receives the full rated potential difference (220 V).\r\n2. Each appliance operates on an independent switch; failure or switching off of one appliance does not break the circuit for other units.\r\n3. Total equivalent resistance is reduced, allowing required current for heavy appliances.	t	2018	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SST_2018_AI_01	SS10_HIS_01	\N	Standard (041)	Who among the following was the architect of German unification process and carried it out with the help of the Prussian army and bureaucracy?\r\n(A) Otto von Bismarck\r\n(B) Kaiser William I\r\n(C) Count Cavour\r\n(D) Giuseppe Garibaldi	{"answer": "1 Mark for Option (A)"}	1.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	0687be7938ffc7769bc864032581e5137f2c7474a0fb7616fc72d71407c810da	[]	Correct Option: (A)\r\nChief Minister Otto von Bismarck was the architect of German national unification.	t	2018	All India Set 1	f	SST	All India
SST_2018_AI_02	SS10_HIS_02	\N	Standard (041)	In which city of India was the Rowlatt Satyagraha launched by Mahatma Gandhi in 1919 against the infamous Rowlatt Act?\r\n(A) Bombay\r\n(B) Amritsar\r\n(C) Calcutta\r\n(D) Nationwide strike called across all major cities	{"answer": "1 Mark for Option (D)"}	1.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	ae1c3b108de4d116d9084dbae693c210e122789474d7d91f7ca7429b756aacfe	[]	Correct Option: (D)\r\nMahatma Gandhi organized a nationwide hartal on April 6, 1919, starting simultaneous civil demonstrations across the country.	t	2018	All India Set 1	f	SST	All India
SST_2018_AI_03	SS10_GEO_01	\N	Standard (041)	Which of the following geographical regions of India is predominantly covered with Black Soil (Regur soil)?\r\n(A) The Northern Plains\r\n(B) The Deccan Trap region (Basalt)\r\n(C) The Coastal Plains\r\n(D) The Thar Desert	{"answer": "1 Mark for Option (B)"}	1.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	f35fec4a4a8da3c1aae4b5f109cc9688b2d2435cca1e18a03f550dd620c22f82	[]	Correct Option: (B)\r\nBlack soil covers the volcanic basaltic Deccan Trap region across Maharashtra, Saurashtra, Malwa, and Madhya Pradesh.	t	2018	All India Set 1	f	SST	All India
SST_2018_AI_04	SS10_POL_01	\N	Standard (041)	In Sri Lanka, an Act was passed in 1956 to establish Sinhala as the sole official language, disregarding which minority community language?\r\n(A) Tamil\r\n(B) English\r\n(C) Dutch\r\n(D) Hindi	{"answer": "1 Mark for Option (A)"}	1.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	4e4016cd9f36bb784290d13e1a8b1ff4370867d258e3b982f72c812affae3928	[]	Correct Option: (A)\r\nThe 1956 Official Language Act disregarded the language of the Sri Lankan Tamils.	t	2018	All India Set 1	f	SST	All India
SST_2018_AI_05	SS10_ECO_01	\N	Standard (041)	Explain why developmental goals of different categories of persons may sometimes be conflicting. Illustrate with an example of an industrial dam.	{"conflict": "1 Mark", "dam_example": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	5f59012fca8aab772be63882a9d4a24bb2ea6ee4f2d8f8123719120f028ab06d	[]	What is developmental for one person may cause hardship for another.\r\nExample: An industrialist desires mega hydroelectric dams to ensure a consistent power supply for factories, but the dam submerges agricultural land and forces the displacement of local tribal communities and small farmers.	t	2018	All India Set 1	f	SST	All India
SST_2018_AI_06	SS10_POL_02	\N	Standard (041)	What is the role of the State Election Commission (SEC) in the Indian federal system? When was it constitutionally established?	{"role": "1 Mark", "establishment": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	9cec3177ab862eca3c66812ae5d1dd535846fb99fcb4caac1a60b83672aa773c	[]	1. Role: An independent statutory body created in every State to conduct, supervise, and monitor free and fair elections to rural Panchayats and urban Municipalities.\r\n2. Establishment: Formally created through the 73rd and 74th Constitutional Amendment Acts of 1992.	t	2018	All India Set 1	f	SST	All India
SST_2018_AI_07	SS10_GEO_02	\N	Standard (041)	Mention any two characteristics of Plantation Agriculture in India. Name two plantation crops.	{"crops": "1 Mark", "traits": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	127ad5417119ca558a2b753ceaa8be52782ebd85cd33bcfe014c704e99f437da	[]	1. Characteristics:\r\n- A single commercial cash crop is grown over an extensive estate of land.\r\n- Capital-intensive farming that uses advanced processing machinery and relies on migrant wage labor.\r\n2. Crops: Tea, Coffee, Rubber, or Sugarcane.	t	2018	All India Set 1	f	SST	All India
SST_2018_AI_08	SS10_HIS_01	\N	Standard (041)	How did the Greek War of Independence mobilize nationalist feelings among the educated elite across Europe? Explain three factors.	{"cradle": "1 Mark", "ottoman": "1 Mark", "byron_treaty": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	547cf70e2f3482ba8f9770364aee22bf4df19103529515bb4ed9d484aa609cdf	[]	1. Cultural Cradle of Civilization: Greece was revered by European intellectuals, artists, and poets as the foundational cradle of European classical civilization.\r\n2. Struggle Against Ottoman Rule: European sympathies grew for fellow Christians struggling against Muslim Ottoman imperial control.\r\n3. European Volunteers: Poets and artists (e.g., Lord Byron, who raised funds and died in the struggle) rallied European public support until the 1832 Treaty of Constantinople recognized Greek independence.	t	2018	All India Set 1	f	SST	All India
SST_2018_AI_10	SS10_POL_03	\N	Standard (041)	State three factors that determine the outcomes of social divisions in a democratic political system.	{"leaders": "1 Mark", "government": "1 Mark", "identities": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	ca74119c11e62a6dbe57e9346894c326e33c86cb5306d5718f1e6872dcab0dfb	[]	1. Perception of Identities: If people perceive their identities in singular, exclusive terms, accommodation is difficult; if perceived as complementary to national identity, harmony is preserved.\r\n2. Political Demands by Leaders: How political representatives articulate community demands—whether demands remain within the constitutional framework or infringe upon other communities.\r\n3. Government Reaction: How the administration responds—willingness to share power with marginalized groups preserves national unity, whereas suppression fuels separatism.	t	2018	All India Set 1	f	SST	All India
SST_2018_AI_11	SS10_ECO_03	\N	Standard (041)	Why is it necessary for commercial banks and formal financial institutions to increase their lending in rural India? State three reasons.	{"growth": "1 Mark", "debttrap": "1 Mark", "moneylenders": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	c28323792c110bb45434a9c34d2db25694912ad6ceba704f29a156ee67a36142	[]	1. Protect Borrowers from Exploitation: Reduces rural dependence on informal moneylenders who charge usurious interest rates (30–60%).\r\n2. Prevent Debt Traps: Lower interest rates ensure repayment without forcing land sales or perpetual debt.\r\n3. Drive Agricultural Modernization: Affordable institutional credit allows small farmers to invest in tubewells, seeds, and farm machinery, boosting rural income.	t	2018	All India Set 1	f	SST	All India
SST_2018_AI_12	SS10_GEO_06	\N	Standard (041)	Classify manufacturing industries on the basis of capital investment. State the investment threshold for small-scale industries in India.	{"large_scale": "1.5 Marks", "small_scale": "1.5 Marks"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	5a4de207f530570b1345fe47d7e030c042e8b106f4cd39f2fdaf27c74e0540e2	[]	1. Small-Scale Industries: Industrial manufacturing units where maximum capital investment in plant and machinery does not exceed the statutory threshold of Rupees One Crore (Rs. 1 crore).\r\n2. Large-Scale Industries: Industrial units where capital investment exceeds Rupees One Crore (e.g., integrated steel plants, petrochemical complexes, automotive factories).	t	2018	All India Set 1	f	SST	All India
SST_2018_AI_13	SS10_HIS_02	\N	Standard (041)	How did the Civil Disobedience Movement differ from the Non-Cooperation Movement? Why did business classes participate enthusiastically in the Civil Disobedience Movement?	{"differences": "2.5 Marks", "business_class": "2.5 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	2077dde80f69fb46f8c8062b1929a5ce67afd309c502e8eed6cc490ba650daa3	[]	1. Core Differences:\r\n- Objective: Non-Cooperation (1920–22) aimed at Swaraj via passive non-cooperation; Civil Disobedience (1930–34) aimed at Purna Swaraj via active defiance of colonial laws (breaking salt laws, refusing chaukidari taxes).\r\n- Methods: Non-Cooperation boycotted foreign cloth and municipal institutions; Civil Disobedience involved open violation of administrative decrees.\r\n2. Business Community Participation:\r\n- Indian merchants had profited during WWI and resisted colonial policies that restricted their business expansion.\r\n- Opposed the overvalued rupee-sterling exchange ratio that encouraged British imports.\r\n- Demanded high import tariffs on manufactured goods and supported the boycott of foreign textiles to protect domestic industries.	t	2018	All India Set 1	f	SST	All India
SST_2018_AI_14	SS10_POL_02	\N	Standard (041)	Explain how powers are distributed between the Union Government and the State Governments under the Indian Constitution across the three lists.	{"state": "1.5 Marks", "union": "1.5 Marks", "residuary": "0.5 Mark", "concurrent": "1.5 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	0c3e4bd15609ba7801f38affe9fa1ad536d9691d422644259734a4ce1feed77a	[]	1. Union List:\r\n- Contains subjects of national importance: Defense, Foreign Affairs, Banking, Communications, and Currency.\r\n- Only the Union Parliament has exclusive power to enact legislation, ensuring nationwide policy uniformity.\r\n2. State List:\r\n- Contains matters of state and regional importance: Police, Trade, Commerce, Agriculture, and Irrigation.\r\n- State Legislatures hold exclusive legislative power over these areas.\r\n3. Concurrent List:\r\n- Contains subjects of common interest to both levels: Education, Forests, Trade Unions, Marriage, and Succession.\r\n- Both Parliament and State Legislatures can pass laws; in the event of conflict, Union law prevails.\r\n4. Residuary Subjects: Any subject that emerged after the drafting of the Constitution (e.g., Cyber Law, Computer Software) falls under exclusive Union jurisdiction.	t	2018	All India Set 1	f	SST	All India
SST_2018_AI_15	SS10_ECO_04	\N	Standard (041)	What are Multinational Corporations (MNCs)? Explain three ways through which MNCs control production in other countries, and state one benefit to local companies.	{"benefit": "1 Mark", "definition": "1 Mark", "three_ways": "3 Marks (1 Mark each)"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	5db2f96db3c7d7a5f5969c12d92e4badfe3728c9b7d7b0fe7c73dd65719fcaf1	[]	1. Definition: An enterprise that owns, controls, and manages production facilities in more than one sovereign nation.\r\n2. Three Ways of Controlling Production:\r\n- Direct Acquisitions: Purchasing domestic firms to immediately acquire established production lines and distribution networks (e.g., Cargill Foods acquiring Parakh Foods).\r\n- Joint Ventures: Partnering with domestic firms, supplying modern machinery and technology while utilizing local manufacturing facilities.\r\n- Subcontracting / Contract Manufacturing: Placing assembly orders with small domestic manufacturers for finished goods (footwear, garments, toys) sold under the MNC brand name.\r\n3. Benefit to Local Companies:\r\n- Influx of foreign capital to purchase advanced manufacturing equipment.\r\n- Technology transfer introducing modern management and production methods.	t	2018	All India Set 1	f	SST	All India
SCI_2017_D_01	S10_CH01	\N	Standard (041)	When potassium iodide solution is added to a test tube containing lead nitrate solution, a precipitate is formed. What is the colour of the precipitate and its chemical formula?\r\n(A) White, PbI2\r\n(B) Yellow, PbI2\r\n(C) Red, Pb(NO3)2\r\n(D) Black, KI	{"answer": "1 Mark for Option (B)"}	1.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	d13f76043193a2c8203e2fdd4ad1874d39d03a41fc79414a5c37bbfe137b6c63	[]	Correct Option: (B)\r\nPb(NO3)2(aq) + 2KI(aq) -> PbI2(s) [Bright yellow precipitate] + 2KNO3(aq).	t	2017	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
SCI_2017_D_02	S10_CH02	\N	Standard (041)	A drop of universal indicator solution is added to a sample of pure distilled water. The colour observed will be:\r\n(A) Red\r\n(B) Green\r\n(C) Blue\r\n(D) Violet	{"answer": "1 Mark for Option (B)"}	1.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	ded6ac05ed59a9475a5e22cb2054bf337ec4ef9c6a85d1c0af1db8fff8256bff	[]	Correct Option: (B)\r\nPure distilled water has a neutral pH of 7.0, producing a distinct green colour on universal indicator paper.	t	2017	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
SCI_2025_SQP_04	S10_CH10	\N	Standard (041)	A convex lens has a focal length of 20 cm. Its optical power is:\r\n(A) +2.0 D\r\n(B) -2.0 D\r\n(C) +5.0 D\r\n(D) -5.0 D	{"answer": "1 Mark for Option (C)"}	1.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	4040c0095bf43c12627f28e2333f491cc843741be4c2236056e5c2d2a8cbdb93	[]	Correct Option: (C)\r\nP = 1 / f(m) = 1 / (+0.20 m) = +5.0 D.	t	2025	Official SQP	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2017_D_05	S10_CH01	\N	Standard (041)	Why do potato chips manufacturers flush bags of chips with nitrogen gas? Explain the phenomenon prevented.	{"nitrogen": "1 Mark", "rancidity": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	2be58ba8e5d21d27cd6e9f4efa91745d736387584ee8d9af0c8c29149a05e9c5	[]	1. Phenomenon: Rancidity—the aerial oxidation of fats and oils in fried foods resulting in foul odors, off-tastes, and toxic peroxides.\r\n2. Nitrogen Flushing: Nitrogen is an unreactive inert gas that displaces atmospheric oxygen inside the packet, creating an airtight, oxygen-free environment that halts lipid oxidation.	t	2017	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
SCI_2017_D_06	S10_CH07	\N	Standard (041)	State the function of each of the following plant hormones: (a) Gibberellins, (b) Cytokinins.	{"cytokinins": "1 Mark", "gibberellins": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	a20b73b155ccda8fffa67b50ce3f4d8c5664273972f138439e64daf2cc0eb780	[]	(a) Gibberellins: Promote internodal stem elongation, break seed dormancy, and facilitate seed germination.\r\n(b) Cytokinins: Promote rapid cell division (mitosis), particularly in areas of rapid growth like shoot apices, fruits, and seeds, and delay leaf senescence.	t	2017	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
SCI_2017_D_07	S10_CH10	\N	Standard (041)	What is meant by the refractive index of a medium? Why does light travel slower in glass than in air?	{"definition": "1 Mark", "density_velocity": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	ced6b28db7f0bb1c77b0df1538be66256d2e8fd48ff66e82c378e8511fd5d35b	[]	1. Refractive Index: The ratio of the speed of light in vacuum (c) to the speed of light in the given optical medium (v): n = c / v.\r\n2. Velocity Difference: Optical density of glass is greater than that of air (n_glass = 1.5 > n_air = 1.0003). As light wavefronts enter the denser electron-rich molecular lattice of glass, absorption and re-emission slow the effective phase velocity to ~2 x 10^8 m/s.	t	2017	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
SCI_2017_D_08	S10_CH02	\N	Standard (041)	(a) Write the chemical name and formula of washing soda.\r\n(b) How is washing soda produced from sodium chloride via the Solvay process? Write the equations involved.\r\n(c) State one domestic and one industrial use of washing soda.	{"uses": "1 Mark", "equations": "1.5 Marks", "name_formula": "0.5 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	cfafea6de71a19bd5177652f942da56a18fc8cb66f2a59c9e2c32e1c0064f40b	[]	(a) Name & Formula: Sodium carbonate decahydrate, Na2CO3·10H2O.\r\n(b) Equations:\r\n1. NaCl + H2O + CO2 + NH3 -> NH4Cl + NaHCO3\r\n2. 2NaHCO3 --(Heat)--> Na2CO3 + H2O + CO2\r\n3. Na2CO3 + 10H2O -> Na2CO3·10H2O (Recrystallization).\r\n(c) Uses: Domestic: Cleansing agent for laundry. Industrial: Used in glass, soap, and paper manufacturing, and for removing permanent hardness of water.	t	2017	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
SCI_2017_D_09	S10_CH06	\N	Standard (041)	Describe the role of hemoglobin in human respiration. Why is carbon monoxide dangerous when inhaled, even in small concentrations?	{"hemoglobin": "1.5 Marks", "carbon_monoxide": "1.5 Marks"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	9735a442c322d81772fb7f6d435bdec8c416df22fadafdba2b4eec9e0ae5db27	[]	1. Hemoglobin Function: Iron-containing respiratory pigment in erythrocytes with high affinity for O2; binds four oxygen molecules in pulmonary alveoli to form oxyhemoglobin, transporting it to tissues.\r\n2. Carbon Monoxide Hazard: Carbon monoxide has over 200 times greater binding affinity for hemoglobin than oxygen does. It binds preferentially to form carboxyhemoglobin, permanently blocking oxygen transport and causing cellular hypoxia and asphyxiation.	t	2017	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
SCI_2017_D_10	S10_CH10	\N	Standard (041)	A concave mirror has a focal length of 20 cm. At what position in front of the mirror should an object be placed to form a real image magnified twice (2x) the size of the object?	{"u_calc": "1 Mark for u = -30 cm", "mirror_formula": "1 Mark", "magnification_sub": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	0c923f1507d4d2708b921302391dd99b7e0aaa426c9872f4dce5b58a60de958e	[]	Given: f = -20 cm, m = -2 (real, inverted).\r\nMagnification formula: m = -v / u -> -2 = -v / u -> v = 2u.\r\nMirror Formula: 1/f = 1/v + 1/u -> 1/(-20) = 1/(2u) + 1/u = (1 + 2)/(2u) = 3 / (2u).\r\n2u = 3 * (-20) = -60 -> u = -30 cm.\r\nThe object must be placed 30 cm in front of the concave mirror (between C and F).	t	2017	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
SCI_2017_D_11	S10_CH12	\N	Standard (041)	An electric kettle rated 1000 W operates on 220 V mains. Calculate: (a) the current drawn by the kettle, (b) the resistance of its heating element, (c) the energy consumed in 2 hours in kilowatt-hours (kWh).	{"energy": "1 Mark", "current": "1 Mark", "resistance": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	83beee799dbe9bbf1aa0e1b6482399cbd25f2b333b24117cc7edde8e56250af4	[]	Given: P = 1000 W = 1 kW, V = 220 V, t = 2 h.\r\n(a) Current: I = P / V = 1000 / 220 = 4.545 A (approx 4.55 A).\r\n(b) Resistance: R = V^2 / P = (220)^2 / 1000 = 48400 / 1000 = 48.4 ohms.\r\n(c) Energy: E = P * t = 1 kW * 2 h = 2 kWh (2 commercial units).	t	2017	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
SCI_2017_D_12	S10_CH15	\N	Standard (041)	What is the 10% law of energy transfer? Explain how energy diminishes across trophic levels in a terrestrial grassland ecosystem.	{"law": "1.5 Marks", "grassland_example": "1.5 Marks"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	7725a315e3085e51bdfb6f68b2a53486df3ae57c72ea939675543d166ecf6478	[]	1. 10% Law: Proposed by Raymond Lindeman (1942); only approximately 10% of chemical organic energy entered at a trophic level is assimilated into net biomass passed to the subsequent tier.\r\n2. Diminution in Grassland:\r\n- Grass (Producers): 10,000 J\r\n- Deer (Primary Consumers): 1,000 J (90% lost to metabolic respiration and entropy)\r\n- Tiger (Secondary Consumer): 100 J.\r\nDue to cumulative thermodynamic losses, food chains rarely exceed 4 to 5 tiers.	t	2017	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
SCI_2017_D_13	S10_CH03	\N	Standard (041)	(a) Write balanced chemical equations for roasting of zinc blende (ZnS) and calcination of calamine (ZnCO3).\r\n(b) How is zinc oxide reduced to zinc metal? Name the reducing agent used.\r\n(c) What is an alloy? Give the composition and uses of Brass and Bronze.	{"alloys": "2 Marks", "reduction": "1 Mark", "roasting_calcination": "2 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	95825fbe2bf96031890315d2fee9a69cedacf64e44bf5c3780b39fe8aaf23d3a	[]	(a) Equations:\r\n- Roasting: 2ZnS(s) + 3O2(g) --(Heat)--> 2ZnO(s) + 2SO2(g).\r\n- Calcination: ZnCO3(s) --(Heat)--> ZnO(s) + CO2(g).\r\n(b) Reduction: Zinc oxide is heated with carbon (coke) as a reducing agent:\r\nZnO(s) + C(s) -> Zn(s) + CO(g).\r\n(c) Alloys: A homogeneous mixture of two or more metals, or a metal and a non-metal.\r\n- Brass: Copper (Cu 70%) + Zinc (Zn 30%); used in musical instruments and decorative plumbing fixtures.\r\n- Bronze: Copper (Cu 90%) + Tin (Sn 10%); used for casting medals, coins, and statues.	t	2017	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
SCI_2017_AI_02	S10_CH02	\N	Standard (041)	Which of the following compounds is the active ingredient in baking powder that reacts with baking soda to release carbon dioxide gas upon heating?\r\n(A) Acetic acid\r\n(B) Tartaric acid\r\n(C) Citric acid\r\n(D) Oxalic acid	{"answer": "1 Mark for Option (B)"}	1.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	d8d89092149961ab1574adbf9c1cab0e351ed770b8f3472d002ae838ef9658e7	[]	Correct Option: (B)\r\nBaking powder is a mixture of baking soda (NaHCO3) and a mild edible acid like tartaric acid, which neutralizes sodium carbonate and evolves CO2 gas.	t	2017	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2017_D_14	S10_CH04	\N	Standard (041)	(a) What is homologous series? State three characteristics of a homologous series.\r\n(b) Write the molecular formula and structural formula of the 3rd and 4th members of the alkyne series.\r\n(c) What is vinegar? Write one test to identify ethanoic acid.	{"alkynes": "1.5 Marks", "homologous": "2 Marks", "vinegar_test": "1.5 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	3c468348d247c26ddef3466a1abdddc5d75e16ddc9309cc7cc071e9388158ac5	[]	(a) Homologous Series: A group of carbon compounds sharing identical functional groups and regular structural formulas where adjacent members differ by a -CH2- unit (14 unified mass units).\r\nCharacteristics: (i) Common general formula (e.g., CnH2n+2), (ii) Similar chemical reactivity, (iii) Gradual progression in physical melting and boiling points.\r\n(b) Alkynes (General Formula: CnH2n-2):\r\n- 3rd member: Butyne (n=4), C4H6: CH≡C-CH2-CH3 (or CH3-C≡C-CH3).\r\n- 4th member: Pentyne (n=5), C5H8: CH≡C-CH2-CH2-CH3.\r\n(c) Vinegar: 5–8% aqueous solution of ethanoic acid.\r\nTest: Adding sodium bicarbonate (NaHCO3) produces brisk effervescence of CO2 gas that turns lime water milky.	t	2017	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
SCI_2017_D_15	S10_CH13	\N	Standard (041)	(a) State Maxwell's Right-Hand Thumb Rule.\r\n(b) Describe the magnetic field pattern produced by a circular loop carrying electric current.\r\n(c) State three methods by which the strength of magnetic field produced at the center of a circular current-carrying coil can be increased.	{"factors": "2 Marks", "pattern": "1.5 Marks", "thumb_rule": "1.5 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	d4da177efb14161572ddabdfeafda5874c33f5233f3aa83a1281bab9c25f6361	[]	(a) Maxwell's Right-Hand Thumb Rule: Imagine grasping a straight current-carrying wire with your right hand such that your outstretched thumb points along the conventional current direction; the curled fingers indicate the direction of magnetic field lines.\r\n(b) Field of Circular Loop:\r\n- Concentric circular lines surround each wire segment.\r\n- Circles expand into wider arcs as they approach the center.\r\n- At the exact center of the loop, field lines appear as straight, parallel, mutually reinforcing lines perpendicular to the loop plane.\r\n(c) Increasing Center Field Strength:\r\n1. Increase current magnitude (I) traversing the loop (B ∝ I).\r\n2. Increase the number of turns (n) of insulated wire in the coil (B ∝ n).\r\n3. Decrease coil radius (r) (B ∝ 1/r).	t	2017	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
