SST_2017_D_05	SS10_ECO_01	\N	Standard (041)	Define the Human Development Index (HDI). Name the two international components used by UNDP along with per capita income to compute HDI.	{"components": "1 Mark", "definition": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	1e752826d03712367f75a1a9d336d9c14b5a3cb3b219d9e1a22c8ee899d31add	[]	1. HDI: A composite statistical index measuring overall human achievement in socioeconomic dimensions.\r\n2. Two Components:\r\n- Life Expectancy at Birth (health dimension).\r\n- Educational Attainment / Mean & Expected Years of Schooling (education dimension).	t	2017	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SST_2017_D_06	SS10_POL_02	\N	Standard (041)	What are Concurrent List subjects under the Indian Constitution? What happens if there is a conflict between a Union law and a State law on a Concurrent subject?	{"concept": "1 Mark", "conflict": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	fb576698bf6151f611b14a29d1f51dab3e7ac88b096d5ec5d6e224c54d1571db	[]	1. Concurrent List: Subjects of joint interest to both the Union and State levels (e.g., Education, Forests, Trade Unions, Marriage, Civil Procedure).\r\n2. Conflict Resolution: If a conflict arises between Union and State legislation on a Concurrent matter, Article 254 dictates that the Union Parliament law prevails.	t	2017	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SST_2017_D_07	SS10_GEO_02	\N	Standard (041)	Name two major cereal food crops grown in India. State the temperature and rainfall requirements for wheat cultivation.	{"crops": "1 Mark", "conditions": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	ad554ec2828426f21e39dd0ecccca48a6bf897c94517f64a57c8074ebae39c31	[]	1. Cereal Crops: Rice (Paddy) and Wheat.\r\n2. Wheat Cultivation Requirements:\r\n- Cool growing season (winter) with bright sunshine at ripening time.\r\n- Annual rainfall of 50 to 75 cm evenly distributed during the vegetative phase.	t	2017	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SST_2017_D_08	SS10_HIS_01	\N	Standard (041)	Explain the role played by Count Camillo de Cavour in the unification of Italy.	{"austria": "1 Mark", "alliance": "1 Mark", "pragmatism": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	0baa5e29c2bbe4a013f81ec9a511ce5cf0131facb5293c059aa42a03c205f2d6	[]	1. Diplomatic Alliances: As Prime Minister of Sardinia-Piedmont, Cavour engineered a tactical diplomatic alliance with Emperor Napoleon III of France.\r\n2. Defeat of Austria (1859): Led Piedmontese military forces alongside France to defeat the Austrian empire, freeing Lombardy and unifying northern Italy.\r\n3. Administrative Realism: Though neither a republican nor a revolutionary, his political pragmatism created the conditions for southern liberation under Garibaldi and national unification.	t	2017	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SST_2017_D_09	SS10_ECO_02	\N	Standard (041)	Compare the employment conditions in the organized sector with those in the unorganized sector on the basis of: (a) security of job, (b) working hours and overtime, (c) paid leave and healthcare benefits.	{"hours": "1 Mark", "benefits": "1 Mark", "security": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	bae018f6ad3106e6091a2c6e296e4aea97e1a2526231a2f196275e856980b5fc	[]	1. Job Security: Organized employees hold formal employment contracts protected against arbitrary termination; unorganized workers face hire-and-fire instability.\r\n2. Working Hours: Organized employees work regulated hours with mandatory overtime pay; unorganized laborers work long, irregular shifts without overtime compensation.\r\n3. Benefits: Organized workers receive paid leave, provident funds, gratuity, and health insurance; unorganized laborers receive zero paid leave or social security.	t	2017	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SST_2017_D_10	SS10_POL_03	\N	Standard (041)	How does communalism threaten the foundations of democracy in India? State three arguments.	{"disorder": "1 Mark", "electoral": "1 Mark", "pluralism": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	b65faafabc8b878aaf831f67091cc9fb2a7d34e022641b1ebcdac033f06f5809	[]	1. Subverts Pluralism: Promotes the belief that followers of one religion share identical interests while viewing other communities as adversaries, eroding social cohesion.\r\n2. Polarizes Electoral Politics: Encourages vote-bank mobilization based on religious emotionalism rather than civic issues like employment and healthcare.\r\n3. Incites Civil Disorder: Fosters sectarian rioting, displacement, and discrimination against religious minorities, undermining constitutional guarantees.	t	2017	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SST_2017_D_11	SS10_ECO_03	\N	Standard (041)	Explain the role of the Reserve Bank of India (RBI) in supervising the commercial banking sector.	{"crr": "1 Mark", "auditing": "1 Mark", "priority": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	b8ca564773e984eef0fc1ef21f76c6d8e452632ffb065278060f2bd284174611	[]	1. Cash Reserve Maintenance: RBI monitors whether commercial banks maintain the statutory Cash Reserve Ratio (CRR) out of total customer deposits.\r\n2. Equitable Credit Allocation: Ensures banks do not lend exclusively to profit-making corporate enterprises, but also allocate credit to small farmers, micro-enterprises, and rural artisans (Priority Sector Lending).\r\n3. Reporting & Auditing: Mandates periodic reporting of loan disbursements, interest rates, and non-performing assets to preserve banking system solvency.	t	2017	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SST_2017_D_12	SS10_GEO_06	\N	Standard (041)	Why is the iron and steel industry predominantly clustered in the Chota Nagpur plateau region? State three reasons.	{"iron_ore": "1 Mark", "coking_coal": "1 Mark", "logistics_labor": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	d9919f1264697e3362cf23e65486e88b974568d608f9ced03fdaf6dcdc135717	[]	1. Low-Cost Iron Ore: Proximity to high-grade hematite deposits across Jharkhand, Odisha, and Chhattisgarh reduces raw material transit costs.\r\n2. Energy Feedstocks: Close proximity to metallurgical coking coal mines in Jharia, Raniganj, and Bokaro, along with limestone flux deposits.\r\n3. Abundant Labor & Transport: Dense railway networks, water from the Damodar Valley river basin, and low-cost labor from neighboring rural states.	t	2017	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SST_2017_D_13	SS10_HIS_02	\N	Standard (041)	Why did Mahatma Gandhi call off the Civil Disobedience Movement in March 1931? Why did he relaunch it after returning from London?	{"gandhi_irwin": "2.5 Marks", "round_table_relaunch": "2.5 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	b8884575f0c9b744e51dd1717c2dfc07c4d316cd5ea56a76d29f16d873c0c6e0	[]	1. Reason for Calling Off (1931):\r\n- Colonial authorities responded with harsh repression, detaining over 60,000 satyagrahis, including Gandhi, Nehru, and Khan Abdul Ghaffar Khan.\r\n- Industrial workers in Sholapur and peasants in Peshawar attacked municipal structures and police stations following arrests.\r\n- Gandhi signed the Gandhi-Irwin Pact on March 5, 1931, agreeing to suspend Civil Disobedience in exchange for the release of political prisoners and participation in the Second Round Table Conference.\r\n2. Reason for Relaunching (1932):\r\n- The Second Round Table Conference in London deadlocked over separate communal electorates, and British representatives rejected immediate self-rule.\r\n- Upon returning, Gandhi found Congress declared illegal, Nehru and Ghaffar Khan re-arrested, meetings banned, and martial ordinances reimposed, compelling him to relaunch the movement.	t	2017	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SST_2017_D_14	SS10_POL_02	\N	Standard (041)	How is federalism practiced in India? Discuss the three major political experiments that have strengthened Indian federalism in practice.	{"coalitions": "1.5 Marks", "language_policy": "1.5 Marks", "linguistic_states": "2 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	d31845fa094961e50962e7eaf218a3523ae3a95aac090f53df3683458e3c4672	[]	1. Creation of Linguistic States: Reorganizing internal state boundaries along linguistic lines (e.g., Andhra Pradesh, Maharashtra, Karnataka) accommodated sub-national identities and enhanced national cohesion.\r\n2. Progressive Language Policy: Hindi was designated an official language without imposing it as the sole national language, preserving English for inter-state communication and recognizing 22 scheduled languages.\r\n3. Center-State Dynamic & Coalition Governments: The post-1990 era ended single-party central dominance; national parties formed multi-party coalitions with regional state parties, fostering respect for state autonomy.	t	2017	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SST_2017_D_15	SS10_ECO_04	\N	Standard (041)	Evaluate the role of Multinational Corporations (MNCs) in promoting globalization. State three positive contributions and two negative impacts of MNCs on developing host economies.	{"role": "1 Mark", "negatives": "1.5 Marks", "positives": "2.5 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	ce50c2fbac5ac6de5c85fbbbdabb981d026d48b2b879e1469bbef64e6e1f1b80	[]	1. Role in Globalization: MNCs act as primary drivers of cross-border trade, technology transfer, and capital investment, integrating national markets into global supply networks.\r\n2. Positive Contributions:\r\n- Capital Inflow: Deliver foreign direct investment (FDI) into capital-scarce industries.\r\n- Technology Upgrades: Introduce modern manufacturing equipment, managerial practices, and quality standards.\r\n- Global Sourcing: Open export markets for domestic suppliers and subcontractors.\r\n3. Negative Impacts:\r\n- Market Displacement: Well-funded MNCs can out-compete and shutter vulnerable small domestic producers (plastics, toys, batteries).\r\n- Labor Exploitation: Competitive tax and wage incentives may encourage flexible, precarious contract work without statutory protections.	t	2017	Delhi Set 1	f	SST	Delhi
SCI_2017_AI_01	S10_CH01	\N	Standard (041)	When copper metal is added to an aqueous solution of silver nitrate, the solution slowly turns blue because:\r\n(A) Silver is more reactive than copper\r\n(B) Copper displaces silver and forms blue copper(II) nitrate\r\n(C) Silver oxidizes copper to copper oxide\r\n(D) Copper nitrate decomposes in solution	{"answer": "1 Mark for Option (B)"}	1.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	8e2a7248768e2c6a6f3a439d73f8e3967753d0d8360f2307ff17513e0fabb24b	[]	Correct Option: (B)\r\nCopper is more reactive than silver and displaces it from silver nitrate solution:\r\nCu(s) + 2AgNO3(aq) -> Cu(NO3)2(aq) [Blue] + 2Ag(s).	t	2017	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2017_AI_04	S10_CH06	\N	Standard (041)	The enzyme pepsin requires which medium in the human stomach for optimal catalytic activity?\r\n(A) Acidic medium (pH 1.5–2.0)\r\n(B) Alkaline medium (pH 8.5)\r\n(C) Neutral medium (pH 7.0)\r\n(D) Strongly saline medium	{"answer": "1 Mark for Option (A)"}	1.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	bca8a9c8b5ef21828094590108193dbc50eade5edb9a715351a87b6653098e8f	[]	Correct Option: (A)\r\nPepsin requires an acidic medium provided by gastric hydrochloric acid (pH ~1.5–2.0) to digest proteins.	t	2017	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2017_AI_05	S10_CH01	\N	Standard (041)	State two differences between a combination reaction and a decomposition reaction with a balanced equation for each.	{"combination": "1 Mark", "decomposition": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	ac8b5987ab502f5d6286337a4cdff0ba6c759e3d3e3df33fc23f7232bb1a5e6e	[]	1. Combination Reaction: Two or more elements/compounds combine to yield a single product:\r\nCaO(s) + H2O(l) -> Ca(OH)2(aq) + Heat.\r\n2. Decomposition Reaction: A single complex reactant breaks down into two or more simpler products upon application of energy:\r\n2FeSO4(s) --(Heat)--> Fe2O3(s) + SO2(g) + SO3(g).	t	2017	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2017_AI_06	S10_CH07	\N	Standard (041)	Name the endocrine gland associated with each of the following hormones and state the target function of each: (a) Insulin, (b) Adrenaline.	{"insulin": "1 Mark", "adrenaline": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	0f0c0df480dc7ce718cb9dd6c20704295aec6aba7a784dbd6387c156687bce95	[]	(a) Insulin: Secreted by beta cells in islets of Langerhans in the pancreas; regulates blood sugar levels by promoting cellular glucose uptake and glycogen storage.\r\n(b) Adrenaline: Secreted by adrenal glands; prepares the body for emergency "fight or flight" responses by increasing heart rate, breathing rate, and blood flow to skeletal muscles.	t	2017	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2017_AI_07	S10_CH10	\N	Standard (041)	A ray of light traveling in water falls obliquely on a glass slab. Does the light ray bend towards the normal or away from the normal? Justify your answer using optical densities.	{"direction": "1 Mark", "justification": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	5cf3a5f2959560e63fe5144c493fff02cf43c2a5c830c534b09316471e37c7de	[]	The light ray bends TOWARDS the normal.\r\nJustification: Glass has an absolute refractive index of ~1.50, which is optically denser than water (refractive index ~1.33). As light passes into an optically denser medium, its propagation velocity decreases, causing the wavefront to refract toward the normal.	t	2017	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2017_AI_08	S10_CH02	\N	Standard (041)	A dry blue crystal of copper sulphate turns white when heated in a dry test tube, and tiny droplets of liquid appear on the cooler upper walls of the tube.\r\n(a) What causes this colour transition?\r\n(b) Write the chemical equation for this reaction.\r\n(c) What happens when 2–3 drops of water are added back to the white anhydrous powder?	{"reason": "1 Mark", "equation": "1 Mark", "rehydration": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	1a36e476060cbb9f9df1626197b9ddeb7eea73699c63094526fa119d8b031e2e	[]	(a) The colour transition is caused by the loss of water of crystallization. Blue hydrated copper sulphate pentahydrate loses its 5 water molecules to form white anhydrous copper sulphate.\r\n(b) Equation:\r\nCuSO4·5H2O(s) [Blue] --(Heat)--> CuSO4(s) [White] + 5H2O(g).\r\n(c) Adding water restores the hydrated crystalline lattice, returning the solution/crystals to a blue colour.	t	2017	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2017_AI_09	S10_CH06	\N	Standard (041)	Explain the structure and functioning of human nephrons in removing nitrogenous wastes during: (a) Glomerular filtration, (b) Tubular secretion.	{"secretion": "1.5 Marks", "filtration": "1.5 Marks"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	4efd39fb38e4b34ae0ce1e43674970b079bd4c2f3afed1d24b5b60082cc7a1ed	[]	(a) Glomerular Filtration: Blood under high pressure in the afferent renal arteriole enters the glomerular capillary tuft. Water, urea, uric acid, glucose, amino acids, and inorganic salts filter across the fenestrated capillary walls into the Bowman capsule, forming primary filtrate.\r\n(b) Tubular Secretion: Capillary networks surrounding the renal tubule actively transport metabolic wastes, excess H+, K+, and ammonia into the tubular fluid, regulating blood pH and electrolyte balance.	t	2017	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2017_AI_10	S10_CH10	\N	Standard (041)	A convex lens has a focal length of 12 cm. At what distance should an object be placed from the lens to produce a real image three times larger than the object? Also, find the image distance.	{"u_calc": "1 Mark", "v_calc": "1 Mark", "magnification_relation": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	621bfc9c36591b964935044a153d6635dd0f04da683c085ae25a1f03fb269032	[]	Given: f = +12 cm, m = -3 (real, inverted).\r\nMagnification formula: m = v / u -> -3 = v / u -> v = -3u.\r\nLens Formula: 1/v - 1/u = 1/f -> 1/(-3u) - 1/u = 1/12\r\n(-1 - 3)/(3u) = 1/12 -> -4 / (3u) = 1/12\r\n3u = -48 -> u = -16 cm.\r\nImage distance v = -3(-16) = +48 cm.\r\nThe object must be placed 16 cm in front of the lens; the image forms 48 cm on the other side of the lens.	t	2017	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2017_AI_11	S10_CH12	\N	Standard (041)	State Ohm's Law. Draw a circuit diagram to verify Ohm's law experimentally, identifying the placement of the voltmeter, ammeter, and rheostat.	{"law": "1.5 Marks", "circuit_description": "1.5 Marks"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	e81d84c8bedceeb3e21e8d4293491533561b47f0290f7e9c99b6239d28dadc61	[]	1. Ohm's Law: The electric current flowing through a metallic conductor is directly proportional to the potential difference across its ends, provided temperature and other physical conditions remain constant (V = IR).\r\n2. Circuit Diagram Setup: Connect a resistor (or resistance wire) in series with an ammeter, a plug key, a variable resistance (rheostat), and a battery cell. Connect a voltmeter in parallel across the ends of the resistor to measure potential drop.	t	2017	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2017_AI_12	S10_CH15	\N	Standard (041)	Explain why the flow of energy in an ecosystem is unidirectional. What happens to the energy lost as heat at each trophic level?	{"heat_loss": "1.5 Marks", "unidirectional": "1.5 Marks"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	eda9c5edffee6507423a320287134d38ed04823a6154d576fc9e4b0e713ee592	[]	1. Unidirectional Energy Flow: Solar energy captured by green autotrophs is transformed into chemical bond energy and transferred to primary, secondary, and tertiary consumers. Energy cannot flow backwards to earlier trophic levels or back to the sun.\r\n2. Energy Dissipated as Heat: Approximately 90% of energy consumed at each level is lost as metabolic heat during respiration and life processes (Lindeman's 10% law). This heat disperses into the surrounding environment as entropy and cannot be re-captured for photosynthetic work.	t	2017	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2016_D_04	S10_CH06	\N	Standard (041)	Which component of human blood plasma aids in clotting at the site of a cut or vascular injury?\r\n(A) Erythrocytes\r\n(B) Platelets\r\n(C) Neutrophils\r\n(D) Lymph	{"answer": "1 Mark for Option (B)"}	1.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	14bfd13952bdae2c0d25602587914a2b3e098f3a63e49d5008847e45e2a8a044	[]	Correct Option: (B)\r\nBlood platelets (thrombocytes) aggregate and release thromboplastin to form an insoluble fibrin clot, sealing leaks.	t	2016	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
SCI_2017_AI_13	S10_CH03	\N	Standard (041)	(a) Explain the formation of calcium oxide (CaO) by electron transfer between Ca (atomic number 20) and O (atomic number 8).\r\n(b) Why do ionic compounds have high melting points?\r\n(c) Why do ionic compounds conduct electricity in the molten state but not in the solid state?	{"high_mp": "1.5 Marks", "conductivity": "1.5 Marks", "electron_transfer": "2 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	52bde14fa035967db004a77724d0901e7233b8eff1224039969a22bbbd2e4a1c	[]	(a) Electron Transfer:\r\nCa (2,8,8,2) -> Ca2+ + 2e-\r\nO (2,6) + 2e- -> O2-\r\nCa2+ + O2- -> CaO (electrostatic attraction).\r\n(b) High Melting Points: Strong electrostatic forces hold the oppositely charged ions in a rigid crystal lattice; substantial thermal energy is required to overcome these bonds.\r\n(c) Conductivity: In solid state, ions are locked into fixed positions within the crystal lattice and cannot migrate under an electric field. In the molten or aqueous state, electrostatic bonds break, allowing free ions to move toward opposite electrodes.	t	2017	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2017_AI_14	S10_CH04	\N	Standard (041)	(a) What is an addition reaction? Under what conditions do hydrocarbons undergo addition reactions? Give an industrial example.\r\n(b) What is a substitution reaction? Write the equation for the reaction of methane with chlorine in sunlight.\r\n(c) State two properties that make carbon unique.	{"addition": "2 Marks", "uniqueness": "1.5 Marks", "substitution": "1.5 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	c3c312b00a5739728bd1f79c5ab818d6a1c54e0a991a34986d16ae81c0236355	[]	(a) Addition Reaction: Unsaturated hydrocarbons add atoms or groups across double or triple bonds to form saturated compounds.\r\nConditions: High temperature in the presence of nickel (Ni) or palladium (Pd) catalysts.\r\nIndustrial Example: Hydrogenation of unsaturated vegetable oils to produce solid vegetable ghee:\r\nR2C=CR2 + H2 --(Ni catalyst)--> R2CH-CHR2.\r\n(b) Substitution Reaction: A reaction where an atom or group in a molecule is replaced by another atom or group without changing the remaining structure:\r\nCH4 + Cl2 --(Sunlight)--> CH3Cl + HCl.\r\n(c) Carbon Uniqueness:\r\n1. Catenation: The ability to form stable, long-chain covalent bonds with other carbon atoms.\r\n2. Tetravalency: Ability to bond with four other monovalent or multivalent atoms.	t	2017	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2017_AI_15	S10_CH13	\N	Standard (041)	(a) State Fleming's Left-Hand Rule.\r\n(b) Explain the working principle of an electric motor.\r\n(c) What is the function of the commutator and carbon brushes in an electric motor?	{"rule": "1.5 Marks", "principle": "1.5 Marks", "commutator_brushes": "2 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	045b30a89e8bb394afea5a6aa3c2703af128059fadbe41207e7da50753cecf93	[]	(a) Fleming's Left-Hand Rule: Stretch the thumb, forefinger, and middle finger mutually perpendicular: forefinger indicates magnetic field direction, middle finger indicates current direction, and thumb points in the direction of the mechanical force on the conductor.\r\n(b) Motor Working Principle: When a rectangular current-carrying coil is placed in a magnetic field, the opposite arms experience equal and opposite magnetic forces perpendicular to both current and field, generating continuous rotational torque.\r\n(c) Component Functions:\r\n- Commutator (Split Rings): Reverses the direction of current in the armature coil every half-rotation to maintain unidirectional torque.\r\n- Carbon Brushes: Provide smooth, stationary electrical contact with the rotating split rings to supply current from the external power source.	t	2017	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
