SCI_2016_D_05	S10_CH01	\N	Standard (041)	Why is hydrogen peroxide stored in colored bottles in dark places? Write the decomposition reaction.	{"reason": "1 Mark", "equation": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	c8b732e11b959c501adcdd24d48b7c14c9f3e636b1cfe024edd1cf7662c98699	[]	Hydrogen peroxide decomposes slowly into water and oxygen gas when exposed to light photons:\r\n2H2O2(l) --(Light)--> 2H2O(l) + O2(g).\r\nStoring it in dark amber bottles blocks incident light rays and retards photochemical decomposition.	t	2016	Delhi Set 1	f	SCI	Delhi
SST_2017_AI_07	SS10_GEO_02	\N	Standard (041)	Name the two major pulses produced in India. State two reasons why pulse crops are widely grown in crop rotation systems.	{"crops": "1 Mark", "reasons": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	9d8cf07ba49241f94397ca67becf98eed37cedace03fb148892736a9f3e3fd02	[]	1. Major Pulses: Gram and Tur (Arhar).\r\n2. Reasons for Rotation:\r\n- Pulses are leguminous crops whose root nodules contain Rhizobium bacteria that fix atmospheric nitrogen, restoring natural soil fertility.\r\n- They require less moisture and survive under dry conditions, allowing subsequent crops to benefit from rejuvenated soil.	t	2017	All India Set 1	f	SST	All India
SST_2017_AI_08	SS10_HIS_01	\N	Standard (041)	Explain the role played by the Silesian weavers' uprising of 1845 in exposing merchant exploitation during industrial transition in Europe.	{"outcome": "1 Mark", "uprising": "1 Mark", "contractor": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	b07dcf58ae51c9e092def1a622684ececd4b3cc65a3314fd6ec12811d49a5b70	[]	1. Contractor Exploitation: Weavers in Silesia were dependent on merchant contractors who supplied raw cotton yarn and ordered finished textiles, but drastically slashed piece-rate wages.\r\n2. The Uprising: On June 4, 1845, impoverished weavers marched to the contractor's mansion demanding higher compensation; when met with defiance, they broke into warehouses and destroyed finished inventories.\r\n3. Military Repression: The contractor called in the Prussian military, who intervened and shot eleven weavers, highlighting labor unrest in early European industrialization.	t	2017	All India Set 1	f	SST	All India
SST_2017_AI_09	SS10_ECO_02	\N	Standard (041)	Explain three measures that can be adopted to create additional employment opportunities in rural areas of India.	{"transport": "1 Mark", "irrigation": "1 Mark", "agro_processing": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	6be8fea7384c4c02b5fb9599a27f4424c7526611e943b9d9ae3b83092ef67796	[]	1. Irrigation Infrastructure: Constructing canals, check dams, and rural tube-wells allows farmers to grow a second and third crop (multi-cropping), generating agricultural employment year-round.\r\n2. Agro-Processing Units: Setting up dal mills, cold-storage facilities, and honey collection centers in rural clusters creates non-farm jobs for rural youth.\r\n3. Rural Transport & Roads: Expanding rural all-weather roads (PMGSY) connects villages to urban markets, creating transport, trade, and logistics jobs.	t	2017	All India Set 1	f	SST	All India
SST_2017_AI_10	SS10_POL_03	\N	Standard (041)	Explain three ways through which caste inequalities are maintained or challenged in contemporary Indian democratic politics.	{"selection": "1 Mark", "coalitions": "1 Mark", "mobilization": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	23e90794673d4d44fababf862bd7a12db85802a78f8ef6646364deb36a75967f	[]	1. Candidate Selection: Political parties select candidates based on the caste composition of constituencies to appeal to voting blocs.\r\n2. Political Mobilization: Marginalized caste groups (Dalits, OBCs) organize into political parties (e.g., BSP, SP) to demand legislative representation, educational access, and public sector reservations.\r\n3. Cross-Caste Coalitions: Governance requires building broad electoral coalitions across diverse caste groups, encouraging inter-caste political cooperation.	t	2017	All India Set 1	f	SST	All India
SST_2017_AI_11	SS10_ECO_03	\N	Standard (041)	Why is the informal source of credit detrimental to poor borrowers in rural India? State three reasons.	{"coercion": "1 Mark", "debttrap": "1 Mark", "interest": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	64a2af5557a7f0d7f738b9d30f88fabf73689deee23f43928fd9a1cb9e0b882d	[]	1. High Interest Rates: Informal lenders charge interest rates from 30% to 60% annually, far higher than commercial bank rates.\r\n2. Debt Traps: High borrowing costs leave little surplus income from harvest sales, trapping borrowers in cycles of compounding debt.\r\n3. Coercive Collection: Unregulated moneylenders can seize land, livestock, or demand unpaid labor upon default without legal oversight.	t	2017	All India Set 1	f	SST	All India
SST_2017_AI_12	SS10_GEO_06	\N	Standard (041)	Why is the aluminium smelting industry concentrated near sources of cheap bauxite and abundant hydroelectric power? State three reasons.	{"power": "1 Mark", "location": "1 Mark", "raw_material": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	a21d5d2cba5b4741bf3865773fb4caccb37b1e13475e024127c732d8341524c3	[]	1. Heavy Raw Material: Smelting requires roughly 4 to 6 tonnes of bauxite to produce 1 tonne of aluminium, making proximity to ore mines critical to reduce transport costs.\r\n2. Electricity Intensity: Electrolytic reduction of alumina consumes massive amounts of electricity (~18,600 kWh per tonne of metal), requiring dedicated, low-cost power grids.\r\n3. Smelting Economics: Affordable electricity and local raw materials determine plant feasibility, leading smelters to locate near sites like Hirakud and Renukoot.	t	2017	All India Set 1	f	SST	All India
SST_2017_AI_13	SS10_HIS_02	\N	Standard (041)	Explain the participation of women in the Civil Disobedience Movement of 1930. How did this mass participation transform their social perceptions, and what were the limitations of their organizational role in the Congress?	{"limitations": "1.5 Marks", "perceptions": "1.5 Marks", "participation": "2 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	516f6b5469fa0f54399a10ec85831b8aeda0c0792ac10fcfb2bda695fa063caa	[]	1. Women's Participation in 1930:\r\n- Thousands of women left home to participate in salt manufacturing, street rallies, and the picketing of foreign cloth and liquor shops.\r\n- In urban areas, women from high-caste, educated families joined demonstrations, while in rural areas, women from affluent peasant households actively supported satyagraha.\r\n- Many were arrested and served prison sentences.\r\n2. Changing Social Perceptions:\r\n- Participating in public protests challenged orthodox social norms that restricted women to domestic domesticity.\r\n- National service came to be viewed as a civic duty for women.\r\n3. Limitations in Congress Leadership:\r\n- Despite mass participation, women were largely excluded from decision-making committees within the Indian National Congress.\r\n- Mainstream leadership, including Gandhi, still largely saw a woman's primary role as maintaining the home and hearth, keeping their representation in party executive bodies symbolic for decades.	t	2017	All India Set 1	f	SST	All India
SCI_2016_AI_09	S10_CH07	\N	Standard (041)	Draw a diagram of the human brain and label the following parts: (a) Cerebrum, (b) Cerebellum, (c) Medulla oblongata. State one major function of each.	{"medulla": "1 Mark", "cerebrum": "1 Mark", "cerebellum": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	fe3427bc27c7d44ecb5b62a03107b549e0e31afbab51d54da290f099cc68d6c0	[]	1. Cerebrum: Controls voluntary motor actions, thinking, memory, sensory perception, and conscious reasoning.\r\n2. Cerebellum: Coordinates voluntary muscular movements, precision, posture, and equilibrium/balance of the body.\r\n3. Medulla Oblongata: Controls involuntary autonomic reflex actions including blood pressure, salivation, heartbeat, and vomiting.	t	2016	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SST_2017_AI_14	SS10_POL_02	\N	Standard (041)	Analyze the key constitutional differences between a federal system of government and a unitary system of government across five distinct dimensions.	{"five_dimensions": "5 Marks (1 Mark each for distinct dimension)"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	20a11a5d63f0e9a320778ef54c6c8835f3c585e9a64297def1a56e7145302b81	[]	1. Levels of Government: Federal has two or more tiers (Union, State, Local); Unitary has a single central government with subordinate regional units.\r\n2. Division of Powers: Federal powers are constitutionally divided; Unitary central government can alter or withdraw sub-unit powers at will.\r\n3. Constitutional Rigidity: Federal constitutions are rigid and require state ratification for federal amendments; Unitary constitutions can usually be amended by the national parliament alone.\r\n4. Judicial Arbiter: Federal systems rely on an independent Supreme Court to mediate center-state disputes; Unitary judiciaries cannot invalidate central decisions based on federal divisions.\r\n5. Sovereign Autonomy: Federal state governments are directly accountable to the electorate and hold sovereign jurisdiction; Unitary provincial units act as administrative arms of the central executive.	t	2017	All India Set 1	f	SST	All India
SST_2017_AI_15	SS10_ECO_04	\N	Standard (041)	What is meant by Foreign Direct Investment (FDI)? Explain three reasons why multinational corporations invest in India, and two policy conditions enforced by the government to protect domestic industry.	{"fdi_def": "1 Mark", "reasons": "2.5 Marks", "safeguards": "1.5 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	ac41759e7e8a8d3d04e24cf217bb1788ebeead19b9147c8a2e9f4cfd562af720	[]	1. Definition: Investment made by a foreign company to establish business operations, manufacturing plants, or acquire corporate assets in another country.\r\n2. Reasons for Investing in India:\r\n- Large Consumer Market: A rapidly growing middle class with rising purchasing power.\r\n- Low Factor Costs: Availability of skilled engineers and semi-skilled manufacturing labor at lower wage rates compared to developed economies.\r\n- Proximity to Regional Markets: Direct access to South Asian and Middle Eastern export corridors.\r\n3. Government Safeguards:\r\n- Sectoral Caps & Approval Routes: Reserving sensitive sectors (e.g., retail, defense) under mandatory government approval channels.\r\n- Local Sourcing Mandates: Requiring multinational manufacturers to source a percentage of components from domestic small-scale suppliers.	t	2017	All India Set 1	f	SST	All India
SCI_2016_AI_01	S10_CH01	\N	Standard (041)	Name the oxidizing agent in the following reaction:\r\nCuO(s) + H2(g) -> Cu(s) + H2O(l)	{"answer": "1 Mark for identifying CuO with reasoning"}	1.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	693690d8b5daf503765eae8ab3e77aa4c00ada42a874019466b22e7b82e784fd	[]	Copper(II) oxide (CuO) is the oxidizing agent because it supplies oxygen and undergoes reduction to elemental copper (Cu).	t	2016	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2016_AI_02	S10_CH02	\N	Standard (041)	A solution turns red litmus blue. Its pH is likely to be:\r\n(A) 1\r\n(B) 4\r\n(C) 5\r\n(D) 10	{"answer": "1 Mark for Option (D)"}	1.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	78ec2b104ff01dca07eca4823358d68d3ea59c23c837d313c1930cdfb082d689	[]	Correct Option: (D)\r\nBasic solutions turn red litmus blue and possess a pH value strictly greater than 7. Thus, pH = 10.	t	2016	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2016_AI_03	S10_CH06	\N	Standard (041)	Which enzyme present in human saliva initiates the breakdown of starch into maltose sugars?\r\n(A) Pepsin\r\n(B) Salivary Amylase\r\n(C) Trypsin\r\n(D) Lipase	{"answer": "1 Mark for Option (B)"}	1.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	67b7c9219d7a8201b8282678722caf2099b70325c460c8efbfd4a38614ca04eb	[]	Correct Option: (B)\r\nSalivary amylase (ptyalin) catalyzes the hydrolytic cleavage of dietary starch into disaccharide maltose at an optimum pH of ~6.8.	t	2016	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2016_AI_04	S10_CH10	\N	Standard (041)	A ray of light traveling in air enters obliquely into water. Does the light ray bend towards the normal or away from the normal? Why?	{"reason": "0.5 Mark", "direction": "0.5 Mark"}	1.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	f407dc8a656e7b53d960c82b726db9291f9072163c74fa198971b30d8ea64b02	[]	The light ray bends towards the normal. Water is an optically denser medium than air; as light enters water, its propagation speed decreases, causing the ray to refract closer to the normal.	t	2016	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2016_AI_05	S10_CH03	\N	Standard (041)	Write balanced chemical equations for the reactions of:\r\n(a) Iron with steam\r\n(b) Calcium with water	{"iron_steam": "1 Mark", "calcium_water": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	a968f46d342bf1bf401ba0676981e302a84ef107eead7162a216e4acbf01331b	[]	(a) Iron with steam:\r\n3Fe(s) + 4H2O(g) -> Fe3O4(s) + 4H2(g)\r\n(b) Calcium with water:\r\nCa(s) + 2H2O(l) -> Ca(OH)2(aq) + H2(g)	t	2016	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2016_AI_06	S10_CH08	\N	Standard (041)	What is regeneration in organisms? Name two animals that show regeneration.	{"examples": "1 Mark", "definition": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	ec0270aada4bd57bc60f2fc63e41c7750e5b1f104315c022fed6ee1e18661661	[]	1. Regeneration: The biological ability of an organism to give rise to a complete new individual from its severed body parts by proliferation of specialized cells.\r\n2. Examples: Planaria and Hydra.	t	2016	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2016_AI_07	S10_CH11	\N	Standard (041)	Why does the clear sky appear blue? Why does it appear dark to an astronaut in outer space?	{"outer_space": "1 Mark", "blue_scattering": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	532f73b8caa9c5d7f1f54fe3e3f358aaab8ef2ee80db4633ae210418251f11ae	[]	1. Blue Sky: Fine molecules of air and atmospheric particles have sizes smaller than the wavelength of visible light; they scatter shorter wavelengths (blue light) much more strongly than red light (Rayleigh scattering).\r\n2. Astronaut View: In outer space, there is no atmosphere to scatter incident sunlight, so the sky appears completely dark.	t	2016	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2016_AI_08	S10_CH02	\N	Standard (041)	A white powder A is a key constituent of baking powder and is also used as an antacid.\r\n(a) Identify compound A.\r\n(b) Write the chemical equation when A is heated during cooking.\r\n(c) State why tartaric acid is mixed with compound A to make baking powder.	{"equation": "1 Mark", "identity": "1 Mark", "tartaric_role": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	f262de0c940060819c022a41871294cc5421307955709d57d86bdea178395f5a	[]	(a) Compound A is Sodium hydrogen carbonate (NaHCO3 / Sodium bicarbonate).\r\n(b) Thermal decomposition on heating:\r\n2NaHCO3 --(Heat)--> Na2CO3 + H2O + CO2(g)\r\n(c) Tartaric acid neutralizes the basic sodium carbonate (Na2CO3) produced upon heating, preventing the baked item from tasting bitter.	t	2016	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SST_2016_AI_03	SS10_GEO_01	\N	Standard (041)	Which soil type develops deep cracks during hot summer weather, helping in proper aeration of the soil?\r\n(A) Alluvial Soil\r\n(B) Black (Regur) Soil\r\n(C) Laterite Soil\r\n(D) Arid Soil	{"answer": "1 Mark for Option (B)"}	1.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	e6801571d8270916a9049a0e08916b05900c5a8f2942fe970fd9b119d9eb030e	[]	Correct Option: (B)\r\nBlack soil (regur) has high clay content; it shrinks and develops deep fissures during dry hot spells, enabling self-ploughing aeration.	t	2016	All India Set 1	f	SST	All India
SCI_2016_AI_10	S10_CH10	\N	Standard (041)	An object 5 cm tall is placed at a distance of 20 cm in front of a concave mirror of focal length 15 cm. Find the position, nature, and height of the image formed.	{"v_calc": "1.5 Marks", "magnification_height": "1.5 Marks"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	4a95264c2a55dd3c683c742979f0716013ca1075f256f683cad6489b72d28bd5	[]	Given: h = +5 cm, u = -20 cm, f = -15 cm.\r\nUsing mirror formula: 1/v + 1/u = 1/f -> 1/v = 1/f - 1/u\r\n1/v = 1/(-15) - 1/(-20) = -1/15 + 1/20 = (-4 + 3)/60 = -1/60.\r\nv = -60 cm.\r\nThe image is formed 60 cm in front of the mirror (real and inverted).\r\nMagnification: m = -v / u = -(-60) / (-20) = -3.\r\nHeight of image: h' = m * h = -3 * 5 = -15 cm.	t	2016	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2016_AI_11	S10_CH12	\N	Standard (041)	Three resistors of resistances R1 = 5 Ω, R2 = 10 Ω, and R3 = 30 Ω are connected in parallel across a 12 V battery. Calculate:\r\n(a) The equivalent resistance of the network.\r\n(b) The total current drawn from the battery.\r\n(c) The current flowing through each individual resistor.	{"rp_calc": "1 Mark", "total_i": "1 Mark", "branches_i": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	7854097d1d8f3d75f3863599f8006681f4db2f0c8184b87dbbf109f7d7f29d08	[]	(a) Equivalent Resistance (1/Rp):\r\n1/Rp = 1/5 + 1/10 + 1/30 = (6 + 3 + 1)/30 = 10/30 = 1/3.\r\nRp = 3 Ω.\r\n(b) Total Current:\r\nI_total = V / Rp = 12 / 3 = 4 A.\r\n(c) Individual Currents:\r\nI1 = V / R1 = 12 / 5 = 2.4 A\r\nI2 = V / R2 = 12 / 10 = 1.2 A\r\nI3 = V / R3 = 12 / 30 = 0.4 A\r\n(Check: 2.4 + 1.2 + 0.4 = 4.0 A)	t	2016	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2016_AI_12	S10_CH15	\N	Standard (041)	What is biological magnification? Explain with an example of an aquatic food chain how pesticide concentration increases at progressive trophic levels.	{"mechanism": "1 Mark", "definition": "1 Mark", "trophic_chain": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	f8ab254db067f7b8caf5371856696b9a47f6643e0df6f0df54efdda718e1bb1c	[]	1. Biological Magnification: The progressive accumulation of non-biodegradable toxic substances (such as DDT or heavy metals) in organism tissues at successively higher trophic levels.\r\n2. Aquatic Chain Example:\r\nWater (0.000003 ppm) -> Phytoplankton (0.04 ppm) -> Small Fish (0.5 ppm) -> Large Fish (2.0 ppm) -> Fish-eating Birds (25.0 ppm).\r\nBecause toxins cannot be metabolized or excreted, top carnivores absorb and concentrate the cumulative dosage.	t	2016	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2016_AI_13	S10_CH04	\N	Standard (041)	(a) What is a homologous series of carbon compounds? State two characteristic properties.\r\n(b) Write the molecular formula and structural formula of the second and third members of the alkene series.\r\n(c) Explain the cleaning action of soap with reference to micelle formation.	{"alkenes": "1.5 Marks", "micelle": "2 Marks", "homologous": "1.5 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	7ac1295ed986cdba6913f63d1e842cf6d6eb224fe9344f4ec9150e0c00c245d8	[]	(a) Homologous Series: A series of structurally related organic compounds containing the same functional group, where adjacent members differ by a -CH2- unit (molecular mass difference of 14 u).\r\nCharacteristics: Same general formula, gradual gradation in physical properties.\r\n(b) Alkene Series (CnH2n):\r\n- 2nd Member: Propene (C3H6), Structure: CH3-CH=CH2\r\n- 3rd Member: Butene (C4H8), Structure: CH3-CH2-CH=CH2\r\n(c) Micelle Formation:\r\nSoap molecules (RCOO- Na+) have an amphiphilic structure: an ionic hydrophilic head (water-soluble) and a hydrocarbon hydrophobic tail (oil-soluble). In water, tails cluster inward dissolving the oil/grease droplet while ionic heads orient outward toward water, forming spherical micelles that lift dirt into emulsion.	t	2016	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2016_AI_14	S10_CH09	\N	Standard (041)	(a) How did Mendel demonstrate that traits are inherited independently in pea plants? Outline a dihybrid cross between round green and wrinkled yellow seeds.\r\n(b) Explain why acquired traits cannot be passed on to future generations.	{"dihybrid_cross": "3 Marks", "acquired_traits": "2 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	1fbc4fe3ae5f6bf348fad9b50949247260da19fc934e86a9fda403f11148644e	[]	(a) Dihybrid Cross:\r\n- Parents: Pure round yellow (RRYY) × wrinkled green (rryy).\r\n- F1 Generation: All heterozygous round yellow (RrYy).\r\n- Self-pollination of F1 yields F2 phenotypic ratio of 9:3:3:1:\r\n  9 Round Yellow : 3 Round Green : 3 Wrinkled Yellow : 1 Wrinkled Green.\r\nThe emergence of novel recombinant phenotypes demonstrates independent assortment of allele pairs during gamete formation.\r\n(b) Acquired Traits: Changes occurring in somatic non-reproductive tissues (e.g., muscle hypertrophy, scars) do not alter the germ cell DNA/genes, and therefore cannot be inherited.	t	2016	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2016_AI_15	S10_CH13	\N	Standard (041)	(a) What is electromagnetic induction? Name the scientist who discovered it.\r\n(b) State Fleming's Right-Hand Rule used to determine induced current.\r\n(c) What is the difference between direct current (DC) and alternating current (AC)? Why is AC preferred for long-distance transmission?	{"ac_vs_dc": "2 Marks", "induction": "1.5 Marks", "fleming_rule": "1.5 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	507cdff83a6cb20e950e1fa6634a6cbdea671b0ab8e16aca75f4255d7539c579	[]	(a) Electromagnetic Induction: The process by which a varying magnetic flux through a closed conductor induces an electric current. Discovered by Michael Faraday.\r\n(b) Fleming's Right-Hand Rule: Stretch the thumb, forefinger, and central finger of the right hand mutually perpendicular:\r\n- Forefinger: Direction of magnetic field.\r\n- Thumb: Direction of motion/force of conductor.\r\n- Central finger: Direction of induced current.\r\n(c) DC vs AC:\r\n- DC flows unidirectionally with constant polarity.\r\n- AC reverses its direction periodically (50 Hz in India).\r\n- Transmission Advantage: AC voltage can be stepped up efficiently using transformers to high voltages, minimizing I^2*R Joule heating transmission line losses over long distances.	t	2016	All India Set 1	f	SCI	All India
SST_2016_AI_01	SS10_HIS_01	\N	Standard (041)	Who was described by Austrian Chancellor Duke Metternich as "the most dangerous enemy of our social order"?\r\n(A) Otto von Bismarck\r\n(B) Giuseppe Mazzini\r\n(C) Count Camillo de Cavour\r\n(D) Giuseppe Garibaldi	{"answer": "1 Mark for Option (B)"}	1.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	80c1093a1e45e84c07c30cc43e736df3be4f83467727b301cfeb2fde399bbbfb	[]	Correct Option: (B)\r\nDuke Metternich characterized the Italian nationalist revolutionary Giuseppe Mazzini as the foremost threat to monarchical rule in Europe.	t	2016	All India Set 1	f	SST	All India
