SCI_2021_SQP_04	S10_CH10	\N	Standard (041)	Define the power of accommodation of the human eye.	{"definition": "1 Mark"}	1.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	7ef654050c4b24ba14e401f8d72745b05b36cc3277d62132e4283056148cc3c8	[]	The ability of the human eye lens to adjust its focal length using ciliary muscles to focus clearly on both near and distant objects onto the retina.	t	2021	Official SQP	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2021_SQP_05	S10_CH03	\N	Standard (041)	Explain why ionic compounds have high melting and boiling points, and why they conduct electricity in solution but not in solid state.	{"conductivity": "1 Mark", "melting_point": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	2db3e45094cafd6da18444d1cadace4bd28bb72ee55f1c77978981726d46c69e	[]	1. High Melting Points: Strong electrostatic forces of attraction bind oppositely charged cations and anions tightly into a crystalline lattice, requiring high thermal energy to overcome.\r\n2. Electrical Conductivity: In the solid state, ions are locked into fixed lattice points. In aqueous solution, water molecules weaken electrostatic attractions, freeing mobile ions to migrate and conduct electric current.	t	2021	Official SQP	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2021_SQP_06	S10_CH08	\N	Standard (041)	What is regeneration in organisms? State one difference between regeneration and reproduction.	{"definition": "1 Mark", "difference": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	61622c750f8635f0a9b5d861565d8b6e7acb3ade273e96dd3aada45f634ad44c	[]	1. Regeneration: The biological ability of an organism to grow back or reconstruct a whole new body from cut or injured body fragments (e.g., Planaria).\r\n2. Difference: Reproduction is a natural programmed life cycle process to produce progeny, whereas regeneration relies on accidental slicing/damage of the body.	t	2021	Official SQP	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2021_SQP_07	S10_CH10	\N	Standard (041)	Find the focal length of a lens of power -2.0 D. What type of lens is this?	{"lens_type": "1 Mark", "focal_length": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	a04ebf2acaaf9e1a01d544c8ce21195142e14913598f9ba3aa1744f85d081ade	[]	Power P = 1 / f (in meters)\r\nf = 1 / P = 1 / (-2.0) = -0.5 m = -50 cm.\r\nSince the focal length is negative, it is a concave (diverging) lens.	t	2021	Official SQP	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2021_SQP_08	S10_CH02	\N	Standard (041)	A compound X of sodium is used as an antacid in medicine and is also a constituent of baking powder.\r\n(a) Identify compound X and write its chemical formula.\r\n(b) Write the chemical equation representing its thermal decomposition on heating.\r\n(c) Why is tartaric acid mixed with compound X to prepare baking powder?	{"equation": "1 Mark", "identity": "1 Mark", "tartaric_role": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	5aebd84648ca5a4f5b0617e9c368cc951eda42f09ef2b843a0ff2a04768cb6da	[]	(a) Compound X is Sodium hydrogen carbonate (NaHCO3).\r\n(b) Thermal decomposition:\r\n2NaHCO3 --(Heat)--> Na2CO3 + H2O + CO2(g).\r\n(c) On heating, NaHCO3 produces sodium carbonate (Na2CO3) which tastes bitter. Tartaric acid neutralizes the basic sodium carbonate to form harmless sodium tartrate and releases additional CO2 for sponginess.	t	2021	Official SQP	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2021_SQP_09	S10_CH06	\N	Standard (041)	Explain the process of double circulation in human beings. Why is it necessary to separate oxygenated and deoxygenated blood in mammals?	{"mammal_reason": "1 Mark", "double_circulation": "2 Marks"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	eb0524af73305ccdf0b6ead7b3edd812defb9926aaa548cdbc866054c93d0052	[]	1. Double Circulation: Blood flows through the heart twice during a complete circuit of the body:\r\n- Pulmonary circulation: Deoxygenated blood is pumped from right ventricle to lungs for oxygenation and returns to left atrium.\r\n- Systemic circulation: Oxygenated blood from left ventricle is pumped throughout body tissues and returns deoxygenated to right atrium.\r\n2. Separation Necessity: Mammals are warm-blooded (endothermic) and require high cellular energy to maintain constant internal body temperature; separation ensures high oxygen transfer efficiency.	t	2021	Official SQP	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2021_SQP_10	S10_CH10	\N	Standard (041)	An object is placed at a distance of 10 cm in front of a convex lens of focal length 15 cm. Find the position, nature, and magnification of the image formed.	{"v_calc": "1.5 Marks", "magnification_nature": "1.5 Marks"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	85af1bd1a6f9610c627b1e911e02c454235bce20151d8505d383dc1bc77ff0e3	[]	Given: Convex lens f = +15 cm, object u = -10 cm.\r\nUsing lens formula:\r\n1/v - 1/u = 1/f => 1/v = 1/f + 1/u\r\n1/v = 1/15 + 1/(-10) = 1/15 - 1/10 = (2 - 3)/30 = -1/30.\r\nv = -30 cm.\r\nThe image is formed 30 cm in front of the lens on the same side as the object (virtual and erect).\r\n\r\nMagnification:\r\nm = v / u = (-30) / (-10) = +3.\r\nThe image is virtual, erect, and magnified 3 times.	t	2021	Official SQP	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2021_SQP_11	S10_CH12	\N	Standard (041)	Three resistors of resistances 2 Ω, 3 Ω, and 6 Ω are given. Show how you can connect them to give an equivalent resistance of: (a) 4 Ω, (b) 1 Ω.	{"combo_1ohm": "1.5 Marks", "combo_4ohms": "1.5 Marks"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	7cce5f4d713f723e798c7f7607aaaafddfc0aaad0da4c7cf7c567b428fb85d9d	[]	(a) To obtain 4 Ω:\r\nConnect 3 Ω and 6 Ω in parallel, and put the 2 Ω resistor in series with that parallel pair.\r\nParallel branch: Rp = (3 * 6) / (3 + 6) = 18 / 9 = 2 Ω.\r\nTotal resistance = 2 + Rp = 2 + 2 = 4 Ω.\r\n\r\n(b) To obtain 1 Ω:\r\nConnect all three resistors (2 Ω, 3 Ω, and 6 Ω) in parallel.\r\n1/Rp = 1/2 + 1/3 + 1/6 = (3 + 2 + 1) / 6 = 6 / 6 = 1.\r\nRp = 1 Ω.	t	2021	Official SQP	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2021_SQP_12	S10_CH15	\N	Standard (041)	What is an aquatic food chain? Trace a four-step food chain in a freshwater pond ecosystem and calculate the energy reaching the top consumer if producers harvest 10,000 J of energy.	{"chain_trace": "1.5 Marks", "energy_calc": "1.5 Marks"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	eacf7df32f50439452757d22c6de717325cf46a9d4eae87391327bf1cd1dd391	[]	1. Pond Food Chain:\r\nPhytoplankton (Producers: 10,000 J) -> Zooplankton (Primary Consumers: 1,000 J) -> Small Fish (Secondary Consumers: 100 J) -> Large Predatory Fish (Tertiary Consumers: 10 J).\r\n2. Energy Reached:\r\nBy Lindeman's 10% law, the energy available to the tertiary consumer (large fish) = 10 J.	t	2021	Official SQP	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2021_SQP_13	S10_CH03	\N	Standard (041)	(a) Write electron dot structures for calcium (Ca, atomic number 20) and oxygen (O, atomic number 8).\r\n(b) Show the formation of calcium oxide (CaO) by transfer of electrons.\r\n(c) State three differences between metals and non-metals on the basis of their chemical properties.	{"electron_dot": "1.5 Marks", "cao_formation": "1.5 Marks", "chemical_diffs": "2 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	03cf6602fa529f1d8d888e380302cf247b56d3e55d34eb1ffce8fe45b735578c	[]	(a) Electron Dot:\r\n- Ca (2,8,8,2): Ca with 2 valence dots.\r\n- O (2,6): O surrounded by 6 dots.\r\n(b) Formation of CaO:\r\nCa -> Ca^2+ + 2e-\r\nO + 2e- -> O^2-\r\n[Ca^2+][:O:^2-] = CaO.\r\n(c) Chemical differences:\r\n1. Oxides: Metal oxides are basic or amphoteric; non-metal oxides are acidic or neutral.\r\n2. Acid Reaction: Metals displace hydrogen from dilute acids; non-metals do not.\r\n3. Ions: Metals lose electrons forming cations (electropositive); non-metals gain electrons forming anions (electronegative).	t	2021	Official SQP	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2021_SQP_14	S10_CH04	\N	Standard (041)	(a) What is esterification? Write the balanced chemical equation for the reaction between ethanol and ethanoic acid in the presence of concentrated sulfuric acid.\r\n(b) What is saponification? Write the chemical equation for the reaction of an ester with sodium hydroxide.\r\n(c) What are micelles? Explain the cleansing action of soap in water.	{"esterification": "1.5 Marks", "saponification": "1.5 Marks", "micelle_cleansing": "2 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	bd387d424bcb31f6e586b8a902d934a512190617ec69165d5adb6b5e10569891	[]	(a) Esterification: Reaction between a carboxylic acid and an alcohol in the presence of an acid catalyst to produce a fruity-smelling ester:\r\nCH3COOH + C2H5OH --(Conc H2SO4)--> CH3COOC2H5 + H2O.\r\n(b) Saponification: Alkaline hydrolysis of an ester into soap (carboxylate salt) and alcohol:\r\nCH3COOC2H5 + NaOH -> CH3COONa + C2H5OH.\r\n(c) Micelle Action:\r\nSoap molecules have an ionic hydrophilic head (water-soluble) and a long hydrocarbon hydrophobic tail (oil-soluble). In water, tails dissolve in the oil/dirt droplet while ionic heads face outward toward water, creating spherical micelles that lift dirt into suspension.	t	2021	Official SQP	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2021_SQP_15	S10_CH13	\N	Standard (041)	(a) State the Right-Hand Thumb Rule.\r\n(b) Draw the magnetic field lines pattern produced around a straight current-carrying wire.\r\n(c) What is a short circuit? Why does a circuit get overloaded? Explain the role of a safety fuse.	{"pattern": "1 Mark", "thumb_rule": "1.5 Marks", "short_circuit_fuse": "2.5 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	6d04ee89b94e2e2db44df16806f970c34eab9ede671c1edec35f0dec8c961a78	[]	(a) Right-Hand Thumb Rule: Hold the straight conductor in your right hand with the thumb pointing in the direction of conventional current; the curled fingers indicate the circular direction of magnetic field lines.\r\n(b) Pattern: Concentric circles centered on the wire, denser closer to the wire.\r\n(c) Short Circuit & Overloading:\r\n- Short circuit occurs when the insulation of live and neutral wires breaks, bringing them into direct contact, reducing resistance to near zero and causing a surge in current.\r\n- Overloading occurs when too many high-power appliances operate simultaneously on a single circuit.\r\n- Fuse: Contains a low melting point alloy wire that melts via Joule heating (I^2*R) during an overcurrent surge, opening the circuit to prevent fire.	t	2021	Official SQP	f	SCI	All India
