SST_2021_SQP_05	SS10_ECO_01	\N	Standard (041)	Define per capita income. What is its main limitation in comparing development levels of different countries?	{"definition": "1 Mark", "limitation": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	77478172f3a06730b351d6500ae6b303013ba1a6f01f4647309a7ad1f2b454b3	[]	1. Per Capita Income: The total national income of a country divided by its total population.\r\n2. Limitation: It is an average that conceals income disparities; it does not indicate how income is distributed among citizens.	t	2021	Official SQP	f	SST	All India
SST_2021_SQP_06	SS10_POL_02	\N	Standard (041)	Differentiate between 'Coming Together' and 'Holding Together' federations with an example for each.	{"coming_together": "1 Mark", "holding_together": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	93b401ee0dd932ed708d1f62016d94a5043a99dfeab88f1c17b61e81776b786d	[]	1. Coming Together: Independent states come together on their own to form a bigger unit to pool sovereignty and retain identity for security (e.g., USA, Switzerland, Australia).\r\n2. Holding Together: A large country decides to divide its power between constituent states and the national government (e.g., India, Spain, Belgium).	t	2021	Official SQP	f	SST	All India
SST_2021_SQP_07	SS10_GEO_04	\N	Standard (041)	State two differences between Kharif and Rabi cropping seasons in India.	{"rabi": "1 Mark", "kharif": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	1f25284cb542c3ed69a4056ba5d406881d669ccf4d206486f9546cf44e93c111	[]	1. Kharif Season: Sown with onset of monsoon (June–July) and harvested in autumn (September–October). Examples: Rice, Maize, Jowar.\r\n2. Rabi Season: Sown in winter (October–December) and harvested in spring (April–June). Examples: Wheat, Barley, Gram.	t	2021	Official SQP	f	SST	All India
SST_2021_SQP_08	SS10_HIS_01	\N	Standard (041)	Explain the role of Otto von Bismarck in the unification of Germany.	{"three_wars": "1 Mark", "realpolitik": "1 Mark", "emperor_proclamation": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	f96234905191fb5d8f45c8e836565314f8d130c8f8c896286d5133eb7f1af2c6	[]	1. Architect of Unification: Prussian Chief Minister Bismarck led unification using the Prussian army and bureaucracy (policy of "Blood and Iron").\r\n2. Three Wars: Fought three wars over seven years with Denmark, Austria, and France, ending in Prussian victory.\r\n3. Imperial Proclamation: In January 1871, King William I of Prussia was proclaimed German Emperor at Versailles, completing unification.	t	2021	Official SQP	f	SST	All India
SST_2021_SQP_09	SS10_ECO_02	\N	Standard (041)	Explain the concept of disguised unemployment with an example from the agricultural sector.	{"concept": "1 Mark", "example": "1 Mark", "solution": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	5972ad71ab1df21bddc9b7a6f47fc9936154d5ee3a90a7d5054fb0679564ba26	[]	1. Disguised Unemployment: A situation where more people are engaged in work than required; if surplus workers are removed, total output remains unaffected (marginal productivity is zero).\r\n2. Example: An eight-member family working a small two-hectare farm plot that requires only four workers. The extra four are disguisedly unemployed.\r\n3. Solution: Diverting surplus workers to rural infrastructure, agro-processing, or allied cottage industries.	t	2021	Official SQP	f	SST	All India
SST_2021_SQP_10	SS10_POL_03	\N	Standard (041)	State three ways in which communalism can take various forms in Indian politics.	{"violence": "1 Mark", "prejudice": "1 Mark", "mobilization": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	60e3e9414f4983e1a0e1888fceb8f75365f3cb3a0545dd0afb438deca5c0d77f	[]	1. Everyday Prejudices: Religious stereotypes, prejudices, and belief in the superiority of one religion over others.\r\n2. Political Mobilization: Use of sacred religious symbols, religious leaders, and emotional appeals to consolidate vote banks.\r\n3. Communal Violence: Outbreaks of sectarian riots, mob violence, and communal conflicts.	t	2021	Official SQP	f	SST	All India
SST_2021_SQP_11	SS10_ECO_03	\N	Standard (041)	Why is cheap and affordable credit crucial for the country's economic development? State three reasons.	{"enterprise": "1 Mark", "agriculture": "1 Mark", "moneylenders": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	0c9dc379695389841eef2bdb2b9ca953495aa1238e007afd98b679aca1f54be2	[]	1. Boosts Agricultural Productivity: Enables farmers to buy modern inputs (HYV seeds, fertilizers, tubewells) without falling into debt traps.\r\n2. Promotes Small Enterprises: Encourages micro-entrepreneurs and artisans to set up small businesses and create local employment.\r\n3. Reduces Moneylender Exploitation: Protects poor borrowers from high interest rates charged by informal lenders.	t	2021	Official SQP	f	SST	All India
SST_2021_SQP_12	SS10_GEO_07	\N	Standard (041)	'Dense and efficient network of transport is a prerequisite for local, national, and global trade.' Justify this statement with three arguments.	{"global_trade": "1 Mark", "goods_movement": "1 Mark", "market_integration": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	ac2bc3d87cea74e963bcb3e6a8a227c4cd3ca66fca593f4a8311cec400cdac52	[]	1. Movement of Goods: Transports raw materials to manufacturing factories and finished products to consumer markets.\r\n2. Market Integration: Connects rural agricultural surplus areas with urban demand centers.\r\n3. Global Trade: Links hinterlands to seaports and international airports for export and import trade.	t	2021	Official SQP	f	SST	All India
SST_2021_SQP_13	SS10_HIS_02	\N	Standard (041)	How did the Non-Cooperation Movement unfold in cities and towns? Why did it gradually slow down?	{"city_unfolding": "3 Marks", "slowdown_reasons": "2 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	f6734b9ef5536dd557331cbe88dcd4c988b72351166dcc9e974e99fd9e0ce9df	[]	1. Movement in Cities:\r\n- Students left government-controlled schools; teachers and headmasters resigned; lawyers gave up practice.\r\n- Council elections were boycotted in most provinces (except Madras by the Justice Party).\r\n- Foreign goods boycotted, liquor shops picketed, and foreign cloth burned in huge bonfires (cloth imports halved between 1921 and 1922).\r\n2. Reasons for Slowdown:\r\n- Khadi cloth was more expensive than mass-produced British mill cloth, making it unaffordable for poor Indians over time.\r\n- Lack of alternative national institutions: Boycotting British schools and courts left students, teachers, and lawyers with no alternative workplaces, forcing them back.	t	2021	Official SQP	f	SST	All India
SST_2021_SQP_14	SS10_POL_04	\N	Standard (041)	Examine five major challenges faced by political parties in India today.	{"five_challenges": "5 Marks (1 Mark each for distinct challenge)"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	847574969a3d42629b58eca78351baa39fee4abc73b4938f24e5e54688dcf365	[]	1. Lack of Internal Democracy: Authority concentrates in top leaders without organizational elections or member consultation.\r\n2. Dynastic Succession: Leadership positions retained within dominant families rather than awarded on merit.\r\n3. Influence of Money & Muscle Power: Wealthy corporate donors and candidates with criminal records dominate nominations.\r\n4. Absence of Meaningful Choice: Ideological convergence between major parties limits distinct policy alternatives.\r\n5. Factional Defection: Shifting coalition loyalties prioritize personal gain over programmatic governance.	t	2021	Official SQP	f	SST	All India
SCI_2025_SQP_05	S10_CH03	\N	Standard (041)	Give reasons for the following:\r\n(a) Gold and platinum are used to make jewelry.\r\n(b) Copper is used to make hot water tanks rather than steel (an alloy of iron).	{"gold": "1 Mark", "copper": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	5c0df273a0c24dc27792b80ce9972e491ea761fccb8ac11cd5a5a51333e79ebb	[]	(a) Gold and platinum are at the bottom of the reactivity series, highly lustrous, malleable, and do not corrode or tarnish in air/moisture.\r\n(b) Copper does not react with water or steam at any temperature, whereas iron in steel reacts with steam to form rust/iron oxide.	t	2025	Official SQP	f	SCI	All India
SST_2021_SQP_15	SS10_ECO_04	\N	Standard (041)	Explain the role of multinational corporations (MNCs) in integrating markets and production processes across countries. State two positive and two negative impacts of MNCs on the Indian economy.	{"negative": "1.5 Marks", "positive": "1.5 Marks", "integration": "2 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	ff9ac09adfd79454c4336bc435f25d48e944ebba3f150cd7d61e315d18f24adf	[]	1. Integration of Production: MNCs locate production facilities globally to lower manufacturing costs, leverage local labor, and access regional markets.\r\n2. Positive Impacts:\r\n- Capital Investment: Introduces foreign direct investment (FDI) and modern manufacturing machinery.\r\n- Employment & Standards: Generates skilled jobs in IT, automotive, and consumer electronics while raising quality standards.\r\n3. Negative Impacts:\r\n- Market Concentration: Global retail and manufacturing giants can outcompete and displace small domestic producers.\r\n- Job Insecurity: Flexible contract hiring arrangements reduce tenure security and traditional benefits.	t	2021	Official SQP	f	SST	All India
MATH_2022_AI_01	M10_CH04	\N	Standard (041)	Find the values of k for which the quadratic equation 3x^2 + kx + 3 = 0 has real and equal roots.	{"k_values": "0.5 Mark", "discriminant": "0.5 Mark"}	1.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	2572ad57b68a5242ce27e0fc254d7a9f422fb77e202faa6063136e604e7982f8	[]	Given: a = 3, b = k, c = 3.\r\nFor real and equal roots, Discriminant D = 0:\r\nb^2 - 4ac = 0\r\nk^2 - 4(3)(3) = 0\r\nk^2 - 36 = 0 => k^2 = 36 => k = ±6.	t	2022	All India Set 1	f	MATH	All India
MATH_2022_AI_02	M10_CH05	\N	Standard (041)	Which term of the AP: 21, 18, 15, ... is -81?	{"n_calc": "0.5 Mark", "formula": "0.5 Mark"}	1.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	be3501ebf9d8c90bccfd8a9692ee5fd3447aa05889c0a98abd0d68d2e81b66ef	[]	Given AP: 21, 18, 15, ...\r\nHere a = 21, d = 18 - 21 = -3.\r\nLet a_n = -81.\r\na_n = a + (n - 1)d\r\n-81 = 21 + (n - 1)(-3)\r\n-102 = -3(n - 1) => n - 1 = 34 => n = 35.\r\nThe 35th term of the AP is -81.	t	2022	All India Set 1	f	MATH	All India
MATH_2022_AI_03	M10_CH10	\N	Standard (041)	If tangents PA and PB from a point P to a circle with center O are inclined to each other at an angle of 80 degrees, find angle POA.	{"aob_calc": "0.5 Mark", "poa_calc": "0.5 Mark"}	1.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	485635a539682fd1fca2b72094cd67d5973719fe5eb346d389a1230ca8e7f00c	[]	In quadrilateral OAPB:\r\nAngle AOB + Angle APB = 180 degrees (since OA perp PA and OB perp PB).\r\nAngle AOB = 180 - 80 = 100 degrees.\r\nLine OP bisects Angle AOB:\r\nAngle POA = 100 / 2 = 50 degrees.	t	2022	All India Set 1	f	MATH	All India
MATH_2022_AI_04	M10_CH13	\N	Standard (041)	If the mean and mode of a frequency distribution are 28 and 16 respectively, find the median using empirical relationship.	{"formula": "0.5 Mark", "median_calc": "0.5 Mark"}	1.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	b721ab2bc6d81791a92a577e5c6d6bc9878dc2826a80e2637d89a0f9ebcc4159	[]	Empirical relationship:\r\n3 * Median = Mode + 2 * Mean\r\n3 * Median = 16 + 2(28) = 16 + 56 = 72\r\nMedian = 72 / 3 = 24.	t	2022	All India Set 1	f	MATH	All India
MATH_2022_AI_05	M10_CH04	\N	Standard (041)	Solve for x: 4x^2 - 4ax + (a^2 - b^2) = 0.	{"roots": "1 Mark", "factorization": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	2bb1a4d77ecd0a740afec2c8110ffae446070843b54e3fc9cfdf3c095fef3466	[]	Given: 4x^2 - 4ax + (a - b)(a + b) = 0.\r\nSplit the middle term:\r\n4x^2 - 2(a + b)x - 2(a - b)x + (a - b)(a + b) = 0\r\n2x[2x - (a + b)] - (a - b)[2x - (a + b)] = 0\r\n[2x - (a + b)][2x - (a - b)] = 0\r\n=> x = (a + b) / 2 or x = (a - b) / 2.	t	2022	All India Set 1	f	MATH	All India
MATH_2022_AI_06	M10_CH05	\N	Standard (041)	The 4th term of an AP is zero. Prove that its 25th term is triple its 11th term.	{"proof": "1 Mark", "a_in_terms_of_d": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	68fb345b850206f6713af805412063d5deeb2a0cbde384d3581944d056ed77be	[]	Given: a_4 = a + 3d = 0 => a = -3d.\r\n11th term: a_11 = a + 10d = -3d + 10d = 7d.\r\n25th term: a_25 = a + 24d = -3d + 24d = 21d.\r\nNotice: a_25 = 21d = 3 * (7d) = 3 * a_11.\r\nHence, the 25th term is triple the 11th term. (Hence Proved)	t	2022	All India Set 1	f	MATH	All India
MATH_2022_AI_07	M10_CH10	\N	Standard (041)	Prove that the angle between two tangents drawn from an external point to a circle is supplementary to the angle subtended by the line-segment joining the points of contact at the center.	{"quadrilateral_setup": "1 Mark", "supplementary_proof": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	1fc85780f75febf4bd638aba3cccdada742e4035a0af2f2f35b90e88ebfa4e21	[]	Let tangents PA and PB touch the circle at A and B.\r\nRadii OA perp PA and OB perp PB => Angle OAP = 90 degrees and Angle OBP = 90 degrees.\r\nIn quadrilateral OAPB:\r\nAngle APB + Angle AOB + Angle OAP + Angle OBP = 360 degrees\r\nAngle APB + Angle AOB + 90 + 90 = 360\r\nAngle APB + Angle AOB + 180 = 360\r\nAngle APB + Angle AOB = 180 degrees.\r\nHence, the angles are supplementary. (Hence Proved)	t	2022	All India Set 1	f	MATH	All India
MATH_2022_AI_08	M10_CH09	\N	Standard (041)	A 1.5 m tall boy is standing at some distance from a 30 m tall building. The angle of elevation from his eyes to the top of the building increases from 30 degrees to 60 degrees as he walks towards the building. Find the distance he walked towards the building (Take sqrt(3) = 1.732).	{"distance_walked": "1 Mark", "effective_height": "1 Mark", "distance_expressions": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	a79a945498b09d20e1fbbd2cc2e167e0c06dce09eb1dbb9e861f6271242f8584	[]	Height of building AB = 30 m.\r\nHeight of boy = 1.5 m.\r\nEffective height above eye level h = 30 - 1.5 = 28.5 m.\r\nLet initial distance from building be x1 and final distance be x2.\r\n\r\nIn triangle with angle 60 degrees:\r\ntan(60) = 28.5 / x2 => sqrt(3) = 28.5 / x2 => x2 = 28.5 / sqrt(3) m.\r\n\r\nIn triangle with angle 30 degrees:\r\ntan(30) = 28.5 / x1 => 1/sqrt(3) = 28.5 / x1 => x1 = 28.5 * sqrt(3) m.\r\n\r\nDistance walked d = x1 - x2:\r\nd = 28.5*sqrt(3) - 28.5/sqrt(3) = 28.5 * [sqrt(3) - 1/sqrt(3)] = 28.5 * (2 / sqrt(3)) = 57 / sqrt(3) = 19*sqrt(3) m.\r\nd = 19 * 1.732 = 32.908 m ≈ 32.91 m.	t	2022	All India Set 1	f	MATH	All India
MATH_2022_AI_09	M10_CH05	\N	Standard (041)	If the sum of the first n terms of an AP is given by S_n = 2n^2 + 5n, find its nth term and the 20th term.	{"an_and_a20": "1.5 Marks", "sn_minus_sn1": "1.5 Marks"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	7b98d338e0b33610191164ae7eb0fde4332bff625b589f5ea22d6edf22c4290a	[]	Given: S_n = 2n^2 + 5n.\r\nnth term formula: a_n = S_n - S_(n - 1)\r\nS_(n - 1) = 2(n - 1)^2 + 5(n - 1) = 2(n^2 - 2n + 1) + 5n - 5 = 2n^2 - 4n + 2 + 5n - 5 = 2n^2 + n - 3.\r\na_n = (2n^2 + 5n) - (2n^2 + n - 3) = 4n + 3.\r\n\r\n20th term:\r\na_20 = 4(20) + 3 = 80 + 3 = 83.	t	2022	All India Set 1	f	MATH	All India
SCI_2025_SQP_06	S10_CH08	\N	Standard (041)	Differentiate between asexual and sexual reproduction with respect to:\r\n(a) Number of parents involved\r\n(b) Genetic variation in offspring.	{"parents": "1 Mark", "variation": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	677d99e85e6b5db94a32dd20ef02befac41a09fa9f563e385a3d09a932b3d111	[]	(a) Number of parents: Asexual reproduction involves a single parent; sexual reproduction requires two parents of opposite sexes.\r\n(b) Genetic variation: Asexual reproduction produces identical clones with negligible variation; sexual reproduction causes genetic recombination, resulting in significant evolutionary variation.	t	2025	Official SQP	f	SCI	All India
MATH_2022_AI_10	M10_CH10	\N	Standard (041)	In figure, XY and X'Y' are two parallel tangents to a circle with center O and another tangent AB with point of contact C intersecting XY at A and X'Y' at B. Prove that angle AOB = 90 degrees.	{"angle_aob_90": "1.5 Marks", "congruence_proof": "1.5 Marks"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	38f3fc706764893a18e04cc60c040c2157bb6269d59c2f7b96b9cdf5c4dfaf7b	[]	Join OC.\r\nIn right triangles OPA and OCA:\r\nOP = OC (radii), OA = OA (common), AP = AC (tangents from external point A).\r\nTriangle OPA congruent to Triangle OCA (SSS congruence).\r\n=> Angle POA = Angle COA = (1/2) * Angle POC   --- (1)\r\n\r\nSimilarly, Triangle OQB congruent to Triangle OCB:\r\n=> Angle QOB = Angle COB = (1/2) * Angle QOC   --- (2)\r\n\r\nSince POQ is a straight diameter line:\r\nAngle POC + Angle QOC = 180 degrees.\r\nAngle AOB = Angle COA + Angle COB = (1/2)*Angle POC + (1/2)*Angle QOC\r\nAngle AOB = (1/2) * (Angle POC + Angle QOC) = (1/2) * 180 = 90 degrees. (Hence Proved)	t	2022	All India Set 1	f	MATH	All India
MATH_2022_AI_11	M10_CH12	\N	Standard (041)	A solid metallic cuboid of dimensions 9 cm * 8 cm * 2 cm is melted and recast into a number of identical spherical balls each of diameter 2 cm. Find the number of balls formed (Use pi = 22/7).	{"count": "1 Mark", "ball_vol": "1 Mark", "cuboid_vol": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	831ac2b4ff6da166626947270b06f7c84c495e1080f99aa1a880e3158ba7ac8b	[]	Volume of cuboid = l * b * h = 9 * 8 * 2 = 144 cm^3.\r\nDiameter of spherical ball = 2 cm => radius r = 1 cm.\r\nVolume of one ball = (4/3) * pi * r^3 = (4/3) * (22 / 7) * 1^3 = 88 / 21 cm^3.\r\n\r\nNumber of balls:\r\nN = Volume of cuboid / Volume of one ball\r\nN = 144 / (88 / 21) = (144 * 21) / 88 = 3024 / 88 ≈ 34.36.\r\nSince only complete balls can be cast, the number of complete balls is 34.	t	2022	All India Set 1	f	MATH	All India
MATH_2022_AI_12	M10_CH13	\N	Standard (041)	Find the median of the following frequency distribution:\r\nClass:      0-20, 20-40, 40-60, 60-80, 80-100\r\nFrequency:    5,     8,    15,    12,     10	{"cf_table": "1.5 Marks", "median_calc": "1.5 Marks"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	748a7af4e22b45ad94a29dc76f2808bbf80f2a9d080f013fac84391040011280	[]	Cumulative Frequency Table:\r\nClass       f    cf\r\n0-20        5     5\r\n20-40       8    13\r\n40-60      15    28\r\n60-80      12    40\r\n80-100     10    50\r\n\r\nN = 50 => N/2 = 25.\r\ncf just greater than 25 is 28, corresponding to class 40-60.\r\nMedian class = 40-60.\r\nl = 40, h = 20, f = 15, cf = 13.\r\n\r\nMedian = l + [(N/2 - cf) / f] * h\r\nMedian = 40 + [(25 - 13) / 15] * 20\r\nMedian = 40 + [12 / 15] * 20\r\nMedian = 40 + (4/5) * 20 = 40 + 16 = 56.	t	2022	All India Set 1	f	MATH	All India
MATH_2022_AI_13	M10_CH04	\N	Standard (041)	A motor boat whose speed is 15 km/h in still water takes 4 hours 30 minutes to go 30 km downstream and return upstream to the same spot. Find the speed of the stream.	{"conclusion": "1 Mark", "formulation": "2 Marks", "equation_resolution": "2 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	54c7eedc06fb4d01ce6a29adfb6e83ed6ffcf03e259c7eb17a2ab40719f16e2e	[]	Let the speed of the stream be x km/h.\r\nSpeed downstream = (15 + x) km/h.\r\nSpeed upstream = (15 - x) km/h.\r\nTotal time = 4 hours 30 minutes = 4.5 hours = 9/2 hours.\r\n\r\nEquation:\r\n30 / (15 + x) + 30 / (15 - x) = 9/2\r\n30 * [(15 - x) + (15 + x)] / [(15 + x)(15 - x)] = 9/2\r\n30 * 30 / (225 - x^2) = 9/2\r\n900 / (225 - x^2) = 9/2\r\nDivide both sides by 9:\r\n100 / (225 - x^2) = 1/2\r\n200 = 225 - x^2\r\nx^2 = 225 - 200 = 25\r\nx = ±5.\r\n\r\nSince speed of stream cannot be negative, x = -5 is rejected.\r\nSpeed of the stream = 5 km/h.	t	2022	All India Set 1	f	MATH	All India
MATH_2022_AI_14	M10_CH09	\N	Standard (041)	The angle of elevation of an aeroplane from a point on the ground is 60 degrees. After a flight of 30 seconds, the angle of elevation changes to 30 degrees. If the aeroplane is flying at a constant height of 3600*sqrt(3) m, find the speed of the aeroplane in km/h.	{"speed_ms": "1.5 Marks", "speed_kmh": "1.5 Marks", "horizontal_distances": "2 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	6aeca912d308c3edb7739a32d20ee90a173a6eca9fe2351481234ca4b433b7c5	[]	Let the constant altitude be h = 3600*sqrt(3) m.\r\nLet observation point on ground be O.\r\nLet A be the initial position of plane and B be the position after 30 seconds.\r\nDrop perpendiculars AC and BD to ground line OD => AC = BD = 3600*sqrt(3) m.\r\n\r\nIn right triangle OCA (angle 60 degrees):\r\ntan(60) = AC / OC => sqrt(3) = 3600*sqrt(3) / OC => OC = 3600 m.\r\n\r\nIn right triangle ODB (angle 30 degrees):\r\ntan(30) = BD / OD => 1/sqrt(3) = 3600*sqrt(3) / OD => OD = 3600*sqrt(3) * sqrt(3) = 3600 * 3 = 10,800 m.\r\n\r\nDistance AB covered in 30 seconds:\r\nAB = CD = OD - OC = 10,800 - 3600 = 7200 m.\r\n\r\nSpeed of the aeroplane:\r\nSpeed = Distance / Time = 7200 m / 30 s = 240 m/s.\r\nConvert to km/h:\r\nSpeed = 240 * (18 / 5) = 48 * 18 = 864 km/h.\r\nThe speed of the aeroplane is 864 km/h.	t	2022	All India Set 1	f	MATH	All India
MATH_2022_AI_15	M10_CH12	\N	Standard (041)	A solid cylindrical block of radius 6 cm and height 14 cm has a conical cavity drilled out of it. The conical cavity has the same base radius and the same height as the cylinder. Find the total surface area and remaining volume of the solid (Use pi = 22/7).	{"tsa_calc": "2.5 Marks", "remaining_volume": "2.5 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	ce148754fc80bdf6fc676a8c4cac36fc77cb282bcb1a8690a59013cce34f720b	[]	Given: Radius r = 6 cm, height h = 14 cm.\r\nSlant height of the conical cavity:\r\nl = sqrt(r^2 + h^2) = sqrt(6^2 + 14^2) = sqrt(36 + 196) = sqrt(232) = 2*sqrt(58) ≈ 15.23 cm.\r\n\r\n1. Remaining Volume = Volume of cylinder - Volume of cone:\r\nV = pi*r^2*h - (1/3)*pi*r^2*h = (2/3) * pi * r^2 * h\r\nV = (2/3) * (22 / 7) * 6^2 * 14\r\nV = (2/3) * (22 / 7) * 36 * 14 = (2/3) * 22 * 36 * 2 = 2 * 22 * 12 * 2 = 1056 cm^3.\r\n\r\n2. Total Surface Area = CSA of cylinder + Area of top circular base + CSA of inner conical cavity:\r\nTSA = 2*pi*r*h + pi*r^2 + pi*r*l = pi*r(2h + r + l)\r\nTSA = (22 / 7) * 6 * [2(14) + 6 + 15.23]\r\nTSA = (132 / 7) * [28 + 6 + 15.23] = (132 / 7) * 49.23 = 6498.36 / 7 = 928.34 cm^2.	t	2022	All India Set 1	f	MATH	All India
SCI_2025_SQP_01	S10_CH01	\N	Standard (041)	Identify the reducing agent in the following reaction:\r\nFe2O3 + 3CO -> 2Fe + 3CO2	{"answer": "1 Mark for Carbon monoxide (CO) with reasoning"}	1.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	d0c1e91f6f6ba26a51e522727dddb478b91c62629ffad23f6967fd0895d2d6dc	[]	Carbon monoxide (CO) is the reducing agent because it removes oxygen from iron(III) oxide and is itself oxidized to carbon dioxide (CO2).	t	2025	Official SQP	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2025_SQP_02	S10_CH02	\N	Standard (041)	Which of the following salts does not contain water of crystallization?\r\n(A) Blue vitriol\r\n(B) Baking soda\r\n(C) Washing soda\r\n(D) Gypsum	{"answer": "1 Mark for Option (B)"}	1.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	6f3e675033274bc8d6586f65c9b52de506380297adec3909b3cbce17b8c7c034	[]	Correct Option: (B)\r\nBaking soda (NaHCO3) is an anhydrous salt and contains no water of crystallization.	t	2025	Official SQP	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2025_SQP_03	S10_CH06	\N	Standard (041)	In which part of the human alimentary canal is water absorbed from unabsorbed food material?\r\n(A) Small intestine\r\n(B) Large intestine\r\n(C) Stomach\r\n(D) Liver	{"answer": "1 Mark for Option (B)"}	1.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	455764f717ac7316df72b533fdcea39b236f4de6fb9d41c46b429a54b7a2e1b0	[]	Correct Option: (B)\r\nThe large intestine absorbs remaining water and mineral salts from indigestible food residues before egestion.	t	2025	Official SQP	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2025_SQP_07	S10_CH10	\N	Standard (041)	Why do stars twinkle, while planets do not twinkle? Explain in terms of atmospheric refraction.	{"stars": "1 Mark", "planets": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	c1a5e105c12dc1ea26e6f6d24e920513e0476777b3b1e2cb88d1a497a619d8b1	[]	1. Stars: Extremely distant point sources of light; shifting atmospheric air density causes continuous fluctuation in apparent position and intensity (twinkling).\r\n2. Planets: Closer extended disk sources; varying refraction from different points cancels out, producing steady light.	t	2025	Official SQP	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2025_SQP_08	S10_CH02	\N	Standard (041)	A white chemical compound X is prepared by passing chlorine gas over dry slaked lime.\r\n(a) Identify compound X and write its chemical formula.\r\n(b) Write the balanced chemical equation for its formation.\r\n(c) State two important uses of compound X.	{"uses": "1 Mark", "equation": "1 Mark", "identity": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	55eb1b793de30eb01f5c7dd50e22046f791791bc8e93ccd77c30ca2faf440eee	[]	(a) Compound X is Bleaching powder (CaOCl2, Calcium oxychloride).\r\n(b) Ca(OH)2 + Cl2 -> CaOCl2 + H2O.\r\n(c) Uses: Disinfecting municipal drinking water supplies; bleaching cotton and linen in textile mills.	t	2025	Official SQP	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2025_SQP_09	S10_CH06	\N	Standard (041)	Describe the role of the following digestive enzymes in human digestion:\r\n(a) Salivary amylase\r\n(b) Pepsin\r\n(c) Lipase.	{"lipase": "1 Mark", "pepsin": "1 Mark", "salivary": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	8f0afa89e3f1d1a211d20cbeeebf6394616c656e7bf07abc5b02f264904e4b94	[]	(a) Salivary Amylase: Breaks down dietary starch into maltose disaccharide in the mouth at pH ~6.8.\r\n(b) Pepsin: Secreted in gastric juice; hydrolyzes complex dietary proteins into peptones and proteoses in an acidic medium.\r\n(c) Lipase: Secreted in pancreatic and intestinal juice; breaks down emulsified lipid globules into fatty acids and glycerol.	t	2025	Official SQP	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2025_SQP_10	S10_CH10	\N	Standard (041)	A concave mirror has a focal length of 20 cm. At what distance from the mirror should an object be placed so that a real image magnified 3 times is obtained?	{"setup": "1.5 Marks", "u_calc": "1.5 Marks"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	bf374fc3a627efa9eafe7dcf2615311cbe3519bb4c0081f85897b6c11280d98e	[]	Given: Concave mirror f = -20 cm, real image => m = -3.\r\nm = -v / u => -3 = -v / u => v = 3u.\r\nUsing mirror formula:\r\n1/v + 1/u = 1/f => 1/(3u) + 1/u = -1/20\r\n4 / (3u) = -1/20\r\n3u = -80 => u = -80 / 3 = -26.67 cm.\r\nThe object should be placed 26.67 cm in front of the mirror.	t	2025	Official SQP	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2025_SQP_11	S10_CH12	\N	Standard (041)	An electric kettle rated 2 kW operates for 2 hours daily and an electric geyser rated 1.5 kW operates for 1 hour daily. Find the total electricity units (kWh) consumed in the month of September and calculate the total cost at ₹4.50 per unit.	{"cost_calc": "1.5 Marks", "daily_kwh": "1.5 Marks"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	4e765b8e5f0adf9661397520a83e56312d911a9cdbc9796c4c8cd7f85689ec9d	[]	Daily energy consumption:\r\nKettle: E1 = 2 kW * 2 h = 4 kWh.\r\nGeyser: E2 = 1.5 kW * 1 h = 1.5 kWh.\r\nTotal daily energy = 4 + 1.5 = 5.5 kWh.\r\n\r\nSeptember has 30 days:\r\nMonthly energy E = 5.5 * 30 = 165 kWh (units).\r\n\r\nTotal cost:\r\nCost = 165 * ₹4.50 = ₹742.50.	t	2025	Official SQP	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2025_SQP_12	S10_CH15	\N	Standard (041)	Why is energy flow in an ecosystem unidirectional? Explain how pesticides enter a food chain and accumulate in human bodies.	{"unidirectional": "1.5 Marks", "bioaccumulation": "1.5 Marks"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	84a43333a18b87e850638de02af2fa8db85b11a07e8493ca0aef940a03637e0c	[]	1. Unidirectional Energy Flow: Solar radiant energy captured by autotrophs cannot revert to the sun; energy passed to herbivores dissipates as metabolic respiration heat and cannot flow back down trophic levels.\r\n2. Pesticide Accumulation: Chemical pesticides sprayed on crops wash into soil/water, are absorbed by plants, and pass through herbivores to carnivores. Because they are non-biodegradable, they concentrate at successive trophic levels, reaching highest concentrations in apex consumers (humans).	t	2025	Official SQP	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2025_SQP_13	S10_CH03	\N	Standard (041)	(a) What is thermite reaction? Write the balanced chemical equation and mention its application in railways.\r\n(b) Differentiate between roasting and calcination with a balanced equation for each.	{"thermite": "2.5 Marks", "roasting_calcination": "2.5 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	cb5f4c5aafd73e80e9e7ed65432016754789c920a936ceca953713498c70590d	[]	(a) Thermite Reaction: Reduction of iron(III) oxide by aluminum powder:\r\nFe2O3(s) + 2Al(s) -> Al2O3(s) + 2Fe(l) + Heat.\r\nApplication: The molten iron produced is used in situ to weld cracked railway tracks.\r\n(b) Roasting vs Calcination:\r\n- Roasting: Heating sulphide ores in excess air:\r\n2ZnS + 3O2 --(Heat)--> 2ZnO + 2SO2.\r\n- Calcination: Heating carbonate ores in limited/no air:\r\nZnCO3 --(Heat)--> ZnO + CO2.	t	2025	Official SQP	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2025_SQP_14	S10_CH04	\N	Standard (041)	(a) What are soaps chemically? Explain the cleaning action of soap with the help of a labeled micelle diagram.\r\n(b) Why do soaps not work effectively in hard water, whereas synthetic detergents do?	{"hard_water_scum": "2 Marks", "micelle_cleansing": "3 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	75deabc12d2bf4a0f41d46a53563fd0b94663f447c51a9849defa84a5ed8a654	[]	(a) Soaps: Sodium or potassium salts of long-chain fatty acids (e.g., C17H35COONa).\r\nCleansing Action: Soap molecules have an ionic hydrophilic head (water-soluble) and a hydrocarbon hydrophobic tail (oil-soluble). Tails dissolve in the oil/dirt droplet while heads face outward toward water, forming spherical micelles that lift dirt into emulsion.\r\n(b) Hard Water: Contains dissolved Ca^2+ and Mg^2+ salts. Soaps react with these ions to form an insoluble white curd (scum). Detergents do not form insoluble precipitates with calcium and magnesium ions and lather freely.	t	2025	Official SQP	f	SCI	All India
SCI_2025_SQP_15	S10_CH13	\N	Standard (041)	(a) What is an electric motor? State its principle and the function of split rings.\r\n(b) State Fleming's Left-Hand Rule.\r\n(c) What is the function of the earth wire in domestic electric appliances?	{"earth_wire": "1 Mark", "fleming_rule": "1.5 Marks", "motor_split_rings": "2.5 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	fd3f26d06cc20d212cb72e8b05cef1164fb8c21cd10fe28a3b0424672de55f43	[]	(a) Electric Motor: A rotating device that converts electrical energy into mechanical energy.\r\nPrinciple: When a current-carrying coil is placed in a magnetic field, it experiences torque due to magnetic forces.\r\nSplit Rings: Act as a commutator to reverse current direction in the coil arms every half rotation, ensuring continuous unidirectional rotation.\r\n(b) Fleming's Left-Hand Rule: Thumb, forefinger, and central finger stretched mutually perpendicular: Forefinger = Field, Central = Current, Thumb = Force/Motion.\r\n(c) Earth Wire: Connected to a metal plate buried in the earth; provides a low-resistance path for leakage currents from metallic appliance casings, protecting users from fatal shocks.	t	2025	Official SQP	f	SCI	All India
SST_2025_SQP_03	SS10_GEO_01	\N	Standard (041)	In which year was the Rio de Janeiro Earth Summit convened to address global environmental protection?\r\n(A) 1982\r\n(B) 1992\r\n(C) 2002\r\n(D) 2012	{"answer": "1 Mark for Option (B)"}	1.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	c1fbf7896e98b234499936e01a79527f34c346662c9d84c5bd79cdc416d14fcf	[]	Correct Option: (B)\r\nThe United Nations Conference on Environment and Development (Earth Summit) took place in Rio de Janeiro in June 1992.	t	2025	Official SQP	f	SST	All India
SST_2025_SQP_04	SS10_POL_01	\N	Standard (041)	Which ethnic group forms the demographic majority in Sri Lanka?\r\n(A) Sri Lankan Tamils\r\n(B) Sinhala speakers\r\n(C) Indian Tamils\r\n(D) Muslims	{"answer": "1 Mark for Option (B)"}	1.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	2781d3a181ed14c3edfd482a885a1905a4766ba6d941801d48bdde461939a8c4	[]	Correct Option: (B)\r\nSinhala speakers constitute approximately 74% of Sri Lanka's population.	t	2025	Official SQP	f	SST	All India
SST_2025_SQP_05	SS10_ECO_01	\N	Standard (041)	Define Sustainable Development. Why is it crucial for future economic growth?	{"definition": "1 Mark", "importance": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	cbd4ed9f949256ba1426e1e6cdf3f4ce8f6ee5cffab3e6ca6e17a29f5318bbb4	[]	1. Sustainable Development: Economic development that meets current needs without compromising the ability of future generations to meet theirs, while conserving the environment.\r\n2. Crucial Significance: Finite non-renewable resources (fossil fuels, groundwater) are depleting rapidly; unchecked exploitation threatens ecological balance and long-term economic stability.	t	2025	Official SQP	f	SST	All India
SST_2025_SQP_06	SS10_POL_02	\N	Standard (041)	What is meant by the 'Union List' under the Indian federal constitution? Name two subjects included in it.	{"examples": "1 Mark", "definition": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	c331ea421aa3242af134bd172ec7d097792a1e54266170a2ce2fb5d24d3ca688	[]	1. Union List: Matters of national importance requiring uniform policy nationwide; only the central Union Parliament can legislate on these subjects.\r\n2. Examples: Defense, Foreign Affairs, Banking, Currency, and Communications.	t	2025	Official SQP	f	SST	All India
SST_2025_SQP_07	SS10_GEO_04	\N	Standard (041)	State two geographical conditions required for the cultivation of wheat in India.	{"rainfall": "1 Mark", "temperature": "1 Mark"}	2.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	7b05ae9fde424636003f02c63865cf1b5669ddf9e2268a88a0b954e2a6904f97	[]	1. Temperature: Cool growing season with bright sunshine during harvest (ripening).\r\n2. Rainfall: 50 to 75 cm of annual rainfall evenly distributed during the growing period, grown on well-drained fertile loamy or alluvial soils.	t	2025	Official SQP	f	SST	All India
SST_2025_SQP_08	SS10_HIS_01	\N	Standard (041)	Explain any three measures introduced by French Revolutionaries to create a sense of collective identity among the French people.	{"uniform_laws": "1 Mark", "tricolor_flag": "1 Mark", "patrie_citoyen": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	404c4aa1669ac10524d8279720e37575a015472b51f43ef7724ed9c3a84f7c0d	[]	1. The ideas of la patrie (the fatherland) and le citoyen (the citizen) emphasized a united community enjoying equal constitutional rights.\r\n2. A new French tricolor flag replaced the former royal standard.\r\n3. Centralized administration with uniform statutory laws, standardized weights and measures, and abolition of internal customs duties.	t	2025	Official SQP	f	SST	All India
SST_2025_SQP_09	SS10_ECO_02	\N	Standard (041)	Differentiate between public and private sectors in terms of ownership of assets and delivery of services. Give one example of each.	{"public": "1.5 Marks", "private": "1.5 Marks"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	8c5afd9f7d1e26fe314a374443649d7fb1acd32370a1ab64eef5628fc8cb629e	[]	1. Public Sector: Government owns most assets and provides public services; guided by social welfare rather than pure profit (e.g., Indian Railways, BSNL).\r\n2. Private Sector: Ownership of assets and delivery of services is controlled by private individuals or corporations; guided primarily by profit motives (e.g., Tata Motors, Reliance Industries).	t	2025	Official SQP	f	SST	All India
SST_2025_SQP_10	SS10_POL_03	\N	Standard (041)	'Gender division is not based on biology but on social expectations and stereotypes.' Justify this statement with three arguments.	{"political_gap": "1 Mark", "division_labor": "1 Mark", "socio_economic": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	0b6a69b4f624b013105e40ee4ad762c32d11f3fb2042fc80d788b9872d62e211	[]	1. Sexual Division of Labor: Boys and girls are socialized into traditional roles where women handle domestic unpaid labor inside homes and men earn income outside.\r\n2. Political Under-representation: Despite equal citizenship, women hold fewer than 15% of parliamentary seats in India.\r\n3. Educational and Economic Disparities: Female literacy rates and workforce wages lag behind male counterparts due to patriarchal stereotyping.	t	2025	Official SQP	f	SST	All India
SST_2025_SQP_11	SS10_ECO_03	\N	Standard (041)	Differentiate between formal sources of credit and informal sources of credit on the basis of: (a) regulatory supervision, (b) rate of interest, (c) collateral requirements.	{"interest": "1 Mark", "collateral": "1 Mark", "supervision": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	919815de7c0330926b68517893d80c0f5f73a78dbe9cd8ebcb90ba61c787b888	[]	1. Regulatory Supervision: Formal sources (banks, cooperatives) are supervised by the Reserve Bank of India; informal sources (moneylenders, traders) operate without regulatory oversight.\r\n2. Rate of Interest: Formal credit charges lower, regulated interest rates (8–12%); informal credit charges high, compounding rates (36–60%), often causing debt traps.\r\n3. Collateral: Formal lenders require documented asset collateral and paperwork; informal lenders lend on personal familiarity without asset documentation.	t	2025	Official SQP	f	SST	All India
SST_2025_SQP_12	SS10_GEO_06	\N	Standard (041)	Why is the iron and steel industry termed a 'basic' or 'heavy' industry? State two factors influencing its location in the Chota Nagpur Plateau.	{"iron_ore": "1 Mark", "basic_def": "1 Mark", "coal_labor": "1 Mark"}	3.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	db75a0a85d8cc6be502a24eadc16f4c725a0106eaf310ae17bddca153690a282	[]	1. Basic Industry: Provides primary metal feedstocks, structural rods, and machinery for engineering, defense, construction, and transport industries.\r\n2. Chota Nagpur Plateau Concentration:\r\n- Proximity to high-grade iron ore reserves (Odisha, Jharkhand).\r\n- Abundance of coking coal reserves (Jharia, Bokaro), limestone, and local tribal labor.	t	2025	Official SQP	f	SST	All India
SST_2025_SQP_13	SS10_HIS_02	\N	Standard (041)	Explain the role of business classes and commercial groups in the Civil Disobedience Movement. Why did they become less enthusiastic during its second phase after 1931?	{"business_role": "3 Marks", "decline_phase2": "2 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	e143cb9e4d81b24d26e096cf528ea853b2522868cf32148541960dca8a3cb4b4	[]	1. Initial Active Support:\r\n- Industrialists like Purshottamdas Thakurdas and G.D. Birla opposed colonial trade restrictions and import dumping.\r\n- Demanded protection against foreign imports and a rupee-sterling exchange ratio that discouraged imports.\r\n- Formed FICCI (1927), gave financial support, and boycotted foreign goods.\r\n2. Decline in Second Phase:\r\n- Failure of the Second Round Table Conference in London.\r\n- Fear of militant trade union activities, prolonged market disruptions, and growing socialist influence within Congress under Jawaharlal Nehru.	t	2025	Official SQP	f	SST	All India
SST_2025_SQP_14	SS10_POL_04	\N	Standard (041)	Examine five major challenges faced by democratic political parties across the world today.	{"five_challenges": "5 Marks (1 Mark each for distinct challenge)"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	d22eaf398053fdfb3f02f56d929f295f6f0350ab60dd974a0d21ae1aeb6f94f3	[]	1. Lack of Internal Democracy: Authority concentrates in top leaders without organizational elections or member consultation.\r\n2. Dynastic Succession: Leadership positions retained within dominant families rather than awarded on merit.\r\n3. Growing Influence of Money and Muscle Power: Reliance on wealthy corporate donors compromises agendas; nominating candidates with criminal records to secure votes.\r\n4. Absence of Meaningful Choice: Ideological convergence between major parties limits distinct policy alternatives.\r\n5. Factional Defection: Opportunistic shifts in coalition loyalty prioritize personal gain over programmatic governance.	t	2025	Official SQP	f	SST	All India
SST_2025_SQP_15	SS10_ECO_04	\N	Standard (041)	What is globalization? Explain three factors that have enabled globalization, and describe two policy measures governments can implement to make globalization fair.	{"factors": "2 Marks", "definition": "1 Mark", "fair_policies": "2 Marks"}	5.0	Board Exam	\N	Board Standard	f96669bfd2ca060c6decab94dcfb69dee34bc058bcba1cb84046e2ea29be913e	[]	1. Globalization: Cross-border economic integration of markets, goods, services, technologies, and labor.\r\n2. Enabling Factors:\r\n- Telecommunications and digital internet networks enabling real-time global coordination.\r\n- Standardized container logistics reducing freight handling costs and transit times.\r\n- Trade liberalization and reduction of import duties under WTO agreements.\r\n3. Fair Policy Measures:\r\n- Enforce labor laws (fair wages, safe conditions, overtime pay).\r\n- Provide credit and technology upgrades to shield small-scale domestic producers from predatory import dumping.	t	2025	Official SQP	f	SST	All India
